('31a3650f-26ac-57b5-bf1c-494addc31967'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the average lifespan of a red blood cell in circulation?', '["180 days","60 days","120 days","150 days","90 days"]'::jsonb, '120 days', 'RBCs have an average lifespan of approximately 120 days after which they are removed by the reticuloendothelial system.'),
('63b1f625-eb17-5e83-b769-f06d280fa2d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following is the precursor cell that gives rise to all blood cells?', '["Myeloblast","Lymphoblast","Hematopoietic stem cell","Megakaryoblast","Proerythroblast"]'::jsonb, 'Hematopoietic stem cell', 'The pluripotent hematopoietic stem cell (HSC) in bone marrow gives rise to all blood cell lineages.'),
('b7d7d018-4d3c-5b6b-9d38-e93efc02d4da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which enzyme is deficient in RBCs leading to hemolytic anemia upon oxidant stress?', '["Pyruvate kinase","Aldolase","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase","Hexokinase","Phosphofructokinase"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase', 'G6PD deficiency impairs pentose phosphate pathway, reducing NADPH and making RBCs vulnerable to oxidative damage.'),
('9d08a182-8b8e-5ac6-896f-dda825e564b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary function of neutrophils in the immune system?', '["Phagocytosis of bacteria","Antiviral immunity","Antibody production","Parasite defense","Allergen response"]'::jsonb, 'Phagocytosis of bacteria', 'Neutrophils are the most abundant WBCs and the first responders to bacterial infection via phagocytosis.'),
('88730d15-22f4-5470-987b-b5f789eae1b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which white blood cell type is most elevated in parasitic infections?', '["Neutrophil","Eosinophil","Lymphocyte","Monocyte","Basophil"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophil', 'Eosinophils increase in parasitic infections and allergic conditions due to their role in IgE-mediated defense.'),
('4d271473-5c63-5103-ac80-67b92d07559d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which WBC type contains histamine and heparin in its granules?', '["Basophil","Lymphocyte","Eosinophil","Monocyte","Neutrophil"]'::jsonb, 'Basophil', 'Basophils contain histamine and heparin; they mediate allergic and inflammatory responses.'),
('b15799cb-614d-5244-ae21-415203d99428'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the normal platelet count range in healthy adults?', '["1–5 million/microliter","10,000–50,000/microliter","450,000–900,000/microliter","50,000–150,000/microliter","150,000–450,000/microliter"]'::jsonb, '150,000–450,000/microliter', 'The normal platelet count is 150,000–450,000/microliter. Thrombocytopenia is below 150,000.'),
('a02a0385-ff71-5c64-93aa-0ce02ff98078'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary role of platelets in hemostasis?', '["Clot retraction","Fibrin production","Anticoagulation","Formation of platelet plug","Vasodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of platelet plug', 'Platelets adhere to exposed collagen, aggregate, and form the primary hemostatic plug at sites of vascular injury.'),
('9143ba0d-5484-5f82-a33b-687b389c22e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cells produce platelets?', '["Megakaryocytes","Lymphoblasts","Monoblasts","Erythroblasts","Myeloblasts"]'::jsonb, 'Megakaryocytes', 'Megakaryocytes in the bone marrow fragment into thousands of platelets (thrombocytopoiesis).'),
('7f34b689-9a85-583b-99cf-d876512a79bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following is a characteristic feature of RBCs that maximizes oxygen transport?', '["Presence of nucleus","Biconcave disc shape","Abundant mitochondria","High ribosomal content","Cylindrical shape"]'::jsonb, 'Biconcave disc shape', 'The biconcave shape increases surface area-to-volume ratio, facilitating gas exchange and deformability.'),
('259185b2-4aea-59a0-a3a4-c029359d508d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What serum protein is measured to assess iron stores?', '["Ferritin","Albumin","Hemoglobin","Haptoglobin","Transferrin"]'::jsonb, 'Ferritin', 'Serum ferritin reflects total body iron stores; low levels indicate iron deficiency.'),
('d9df0360-d281-5b40-a2c8-e3874090adc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which type of anemia is associated with microcytic hypochromic RBCs?', '["Sickle cell anemia","Aplastic anemia","Iron deficiency anemia","Thalassemia major","Pernicious anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Iron deficiency anemia', 'Iron deficiency leads to impaired hemoglobin synthesis, producing small (microcytic), pale (hypochromic) RBCs.'),
('64339413-180e-5d98-a900-fd0365222b29'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary function of monocytes in the bloodstream?', '["Release histamine","Produce antibodies","Kill parasites","Produce cytokines for B cells","Differentiate into macrophages"]'::jsonb, 'Differentiate into macrophages', 'Monocytes migrate into tissues and differentiate into macrophages, which perform phagocytosis and antigen presentation.'),
('4ba08c8e-b951-5d0a-84b3-6f7834e59f33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the term for an elevated white blood cell count?', '["Leukopenia","Erythrocytosis","Leukemia","Leukocytosis","Thrombocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Leukocytosis', 'Leukocytosis refers to an increased WBC count, commonly seen in infections and inflammation.'),
('52d546f7-81c9-5c22-aa1f-1f3d81b7445a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following cells is classified as a granular leukocyte?', '["Lymphocyte","Macrophage","Plasma cell","Neutrophil","Monocyte"]'::jsonb, 'Neutrophil', 'Granular leukocytes include neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils, characterized by visible cytoplasmic granules.'),
('6ad8c99f-eb80-5921-8ad7-d3b472b1a609'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the normal hemoglobin concentration in adult males?', '["13.5–17.5 g/dL","10–13 g/dL","17–20 g/dL","8–11 g/dL","12–14 g/dL"]'::jsonb, '13.5–17.5 g/dL', 'Normal hemoglobin for adult males is 13.5–17.5 g/dL; for females it is 12.0–15.5 g/dL.'),
('e5039c15-609c-516c-bcac-9191d40f3e85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of aplastic anemia?', '["Elevated serum iron","Pancytopenia with hypocellular bone marrow","Microcytic RBCs","Increased erythropoietin","Splenomegaly"]'::jsonb, 'Pancytopenia with hypocellular bone marrow', 'Aplastic anemia results from bone marrow failure, leading to decreased production of all blood cell lines.'),
('bdd12b95-2d8d-57fa-b839-d5e4b1ddfcdf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary function of thrombopoietin?', '["Stimulates RBC production","Activates complement","Stimulates platelet production","Promotes vasoconstriction","Stimulates WBC production"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates platelet production', 'Thrombopoietin (TPO) is the primary regulator of megakaryocyte development and platelet production.'),
('ef966ecd-6fa1-54d4-b6cb-c8908138c3e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following is an agranulocyte?', '["Neutrophil","Eosinophil","Lymphocyte","Basophil","Mast cell"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocyte', 'Agranulocytes (lymphocytes and monocytes) lack specific cytoplasmic granules visible under light microscopy.'),
('95ecca5f-6869-5f95-bc7f-8077a26cd448'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the normal RBC count range in adult males?', '["2.0–3.5 million/microliter","6.5–8.0 million/microliter","4.0–5.0 million/microliter","4.7–6.1 million/microliter","3.0–4.5 million/microliter"]'::jsonb, '4.7–6.1 million/microliter', 'Adult males have 4.7–6.1 million RBCs/microliter; females have 4.2–5.4 million/microliter.'),
('1f866719-6677-5ab1-874d-429c9be12bd9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following best describes poikilocytosis?', '["Fragmented RBCs","Variation in RBC shape","Variation in RBC color","Immature RBCs in blood","Variation in RBC size"]'::jsonb, 'Variation in RBC shape', 'Poikilocytosis refers to abnormal variation in RBC shape, seen in various anemias.'),
('8d9ed928-2223-551f-a41b-92b9f12e2be8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which iron-containing compound is responsible for oxygen transport in muscles?', '["Ferritin","Hemoglobin","Myoglobin","Cytochrome c","Hemosiderin"]'::jsonb, 'Myoglobin', 'Myoglobin is a heme protein in muscles that stores and transports oxygen, providing a reserve during hypoxia.'),
('39e14b98-3196-5c02-b0d0-d27e828d4095'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following WBC types is most abundant in a normal blood smear?', '["Lymphocyte","Eosinophil","Basophil","Monocyte","Neutrophil"]'::jsonb, 'Neutrophil', 'Neutrophils constitute 50–70% of circulating leukocytes, making them the most abundant WBC type.'),
('c61f9697-537c-5196-bdd8-ef9c51b7e1d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following is a feature of megaloblastic anemia?', '["Microcytic hypochromic RBCs","Normocytic normochromic RBCs","Increased platelet count","Macrocytic RBCs with hypersegmented neutrophils","Basophilic stippling"]'::jsonb, 'Macrocytic RBCs with hypersegmented neutrophils', 'Megaloblastic anemia (B12/folate deficiency) shows macrocytic RBCs and hypersegmented neutrophils.'),
('a03200a0-3d00-54a6-ac61-f39dad6cb52d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What triggers the release of platelets from megakaryocytes?', '["Thrombopoietin binding to c-MPL","IL-3 binding to IL-3R","GM-CSF binding to its receptor","Erythropoietin binding to EPOR","SCF binding to c-KIT"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombopoietin binding to c-MPL', 'Thrombopoietin acts via the c-MPL receptor on megakaryocytes to promote platelet production.'),
('bf517752-3950-5497-8ba9-bc4d4a80f4b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary function of the RBC membrane cytoskeleton?', '["Transport carbon dioxide mediated physiological mechanism","Generate ATP mediated physiological mechanism","Facilitate oxygen binding mediated physiological mechanism","Regulate pH mediated physiological mechanism","Maintain cell deformability and biconcave shape"]'::jsonb, 'Maintain cell deformability and biconcave shape', 'The spectrin-actin cytoskeleton maintains RBC deformability and structural integrity.'),
('74ddf6f1-8399-550d-a2fa-60f921180918'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following conditions would show a left shift in the WBC differential?', '["Viral infection","Acute bacterial infection","Chronic inflammation","Parasitic infection","Allergic reaction"]'::jsonb, 'Acute bacterial infection', 'A left shift indicates increased band neutrophils in blood, typically seen in acute bacterial infections.'),
('bbb9c04c-9e07-5d2d-81dd-e035808899c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the most abundant plasma protein?', '["Fibrinogen","Hemoglobin","Transferrin","Globulin","Albumin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin constitutes about 60% of plasma proteins, maintaining oncotic pressure and transporting substances.'),
('c6b8bf84-b409-5610-848a-3084ddf24b0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which Vitamin is essential for normal RBC maturation and DNA synthesis?', '["Vitamin A","Vitamin C","Vitamin E","Vitamin B12","Vitamin D"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12', 'Vitamin B12 is required for DNA synthesis in erythroblasts; deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia.'),
('269d5b8f-6b7d-5b46-9ec9-4e5a3a3307ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the approximate total blood volume in a 70 kg adult?', '["7 liters","3 liters","4 liters","6 liters","5 liters"]'::jsonb, '5 liters', 'Total blood volume is approximately 5 liters in a 70 kg adult (about 7% of body weight).'),
('88c979d7-0dfc-5115-95f8-82177b8f3c42'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following cells is the precursor to osteoclasts?', '["Eosinophil lineage","Megakaryocyte lineage","Neutrophil lineage","Lymphocyte lineage","Monocyte lineage"]'::jsonb, 'Monocyte lineage', 'Osteoclasts differentiate from the monocyte/macrophage lineage under the influence of RANKL and M-CSF.'),
('b442bf72-d39e-5c51-96b9-4afd23d02b84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following best describes the buffy coat in centrifuged blood?', '["Hemolyzed fraction mediated physiological mechanism","Bottom RBC layer mediated physiological mechanism","Clotted blood layer mediated physiological mechanism","Top plasma layer mediated physiological mechanism","Layer of WBCs and platelets between RBCs and plasma"]'::jsonb, 'Layer of WBCs and platelets between RBCs and plasma', 'The buffy coat is the thin layer of leukocytes and platelets between packed RBCs and plasma after centrifugation.'),
('b7e247ad-9a5d-5a5c-b052-69e0d26a5778'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the principle function of 2,3-BPG in RBCs?', '["Enhance iron absorption","Promote oxygen release from hemoglobin","Increase oxygen affinity of hemoglobin","Generate ATP via glycolysis","Stabilize the RBC membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Promote oxygen release from hemoglobin', '2,3-BPG binds to deoxyhemoglobin, reducing oxygen affinity and facilitating oxygen unloading to tissues.'),
('aa3655b6-1f95-54fc-be61-72ac9b9d39ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which WBC type is elevated in viral infections?', '["Neutrophil","Basophil","Eosinophil","Monocyte","Lymphocyte"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocyte', 'Lymphocytes increase in viral infections (lymphocytosis) as part of the adaptive immune response.'),
('9d42451c-44eb-5d1f-bdd7-b3133d40c424'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following is a nucleated cell found in peripheral blood in significant numbers?', '["Reticulocyte","Platelet","Mature RBC","Erythrocyte","Lymphocyte"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocyte', 'Lymphocytes are nucleated cells normally found in peripheral blood; mature RBCs and platelets lack nuclei.'),
('b2d2b965-cabc-5a82-a2a8-f071b9a8b7a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the main function of albumin in plasma?', '["Iron transport mediated physiological mechanism","Blood coagulation mediated physiological mechanism","Lipid transport mediated physiological mechanism","Antibody-mediated immunity mediated physiological mechanism","Maintaining colloidal osmotic (oncotic) pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Maintaining colloidal osmotic (oncotic) pressure', 'Albumin generates about 75% of plasma oncotic pressure, preventing fluid leakage into tissues.'),
('ee362040-98c4-5d3c-94f4-63d666bf638a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which plasma protein is the primary carrier of iron in the blood?', '["Ferritin","Haptoglobin","Albumin","Ceruloplasmin","Transferrin"]'::jsonb, 'Transferrin', 'Transferrin binds and transports iron from absorption sites to tissues for utilization.'),
('ce139e7d-8a60-514c-997a-e8a40c52a545'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which acute phase protein is significantly elevated in inflammation and measured clinically?', '["Albumin","Fibrinogen","C-reactive protein","Immunoglobulin G","Transferrin"]'::jsonb, 'C-reactive protein', 'CRP is an acute phase reactant synthesized by the liver; it rises dramatically during inflammation.'),
('016b77d5-8d26-5d94-bebd-0ccb3dc47b36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which plasma protein binds free hemoglobin released from hemolyzed RBCs?', '["Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin","Haptoglobin","Albumin","Hemopexin"]'::jsonb, 'Haptoglobin', 'Haptoglobin binds free hemoglobin, preventing renal damage and conserving iron.'),
('3d55be4f-0a2e-567e-ad2b-9dec41c556d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which plasma protein is the primary carrier of copper?', '["Ceruloplasmin","Transferrin","Haptoglobin","Albumin","Fibrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'Ceruloplasmin', 'Ceruloplasmin carries 90% of plasma copper and also has ferroxidase activity.'),
('27ac9704-068f-5375-ba8b-08f1d13e72d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the normal albumin-to-globulin (A:G) ratio in serum?', '["1:1","1.5:1 to 2.5:1","5:1 to 6:1","0.5:1 to 1:1","3:1 to 4:1"]'::jsonb, '1.5:1 to 2.5:1', 'The normal A:G ratio is about 1.5–2.5:1; reversal suggests chronic liver disease or multiple myeloma.'),
('8f5ba2b3-25fb-5401-8498-1bf3cda3a8a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which fraction of plasma proteins is most elevated in multiple myeloma?', '["Gamma globulins","Beta globulins","Alpha-1 globulins","Alpha-2 globulins","Albumin"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma globulins', 'Multiple myeloma involves monoclonal proliferation of plasma cells producing excess gamma globulins (M-protein).'),
('8bbab976-97eb-5a73-b482-e88873c3b0d4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'By which mechanism does albumin contribute to drug transport?', '["Active transport across membranes","Enzymatic degradation","Covalent binding to drugs","Reversible binding as a carrier protein","Receptor-mediated endocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Reversible binding as a carrier protein', 'Albumin binds many drugs reversibly, affecting their distribution, free concentration, and half-life.'),
('b6360ae7-dcd0-5f1b-a434-e7d7abc8d47e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which acute phase protein is a precursor to amyloid A and deposits in secondary amyloidosis?', '["Fibrinogen","Alpha-1 antitrypsin","Haptoglobin","Serum amyloid A","C-reactive protein"]'::jsonb, 'Serum amyloid A', 'Serum amyloid A (SAA) is an acute phase protein whose degradation fragments form AA amyloid fibrils.'),
('791349da-43e6-5274-8f0c-20be777476ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which plasma protein deficiency causes hereditary emphysema due to unchecked neutrophil elastase?', '["Albumin","C-reactive protein","Haptoglobin","Ceruloplasmin","Alpha-1 antitrypsin"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin', 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin inhibits neutrophil elastase; deficiency leads to lung tissue destruction and early-onset emphysema.'),
('af877922-d080-5f54-8afb-0d02232a4d3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which alpha-2 globulin is the major carrier of thyroxine?', '["Haptoglobin","Albumin","Ceruloplasmin","Thyroxine-binding globulin","Transferrin"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroxine-binding globulin', 'TBG is an alpha-2 globulin that transports thyroid hormones (T4 and T3) in the blood.'),
('525d6219-d9e8-507c-8799-8ddf038a5302'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary site of plasma protein synthesis?', '["Liver hepatocytes","Spleen","Bone marrow","Small intestine","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver hepatocytes', 'The liver synthesizes most plasma proteins, including albumin, globulins, fibrinogen, and acute phase proteins.'),
('8fbb7774-d337-5ab2-85f8-7bfc694c4c9c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which protein binds bilirubin for transport to the liver?', '["Ceruloplasmin","Transferrin","Albumin","Haptoglobin","Hemopexin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Unconjugated bilirubin is transported in plasma bound to albumin for delivery to the liver.'),
('3eb88842-9169-55d2-9802-edd83aac1710'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What effect does hypoalbuminemia have on edema formation?', '["Decreased oncotic pressure leading to edema","Increased oncotic pressure reducing edema","Increased hydrostatic pressure","Decreased capillary permeability","No effect on fluid balance"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased oncotic pressure leading to edema', 'Low albumin reduces plasma oncotic pressure, causing fluid to shift into interstitial spaces (edema).'),
('5f027904-541d-56a7-b524-e7c87c40f896'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which acute phase protein acts as a protease inhibitor and is increased in inflammation?', '["Transferrin","Albumin","Haptoglobin","Prealbumin","Alpha-1 antitrypsin"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin', 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin is an acute phase reactant that increases during inflammation to inhibit proteases.'),
('0359ff86-80c9-5263-b48c-db5319c6a179'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which hormone is the primary regulator of erythropoiesis?', '["Testosterone","Cortisol","Erythropoietin","Growth hormone","Thyroxine"]'::jsonb, 'Erythropoietin', 'Erythropoietin (EPO), produced mainly by renal peritubular fibroblasts, stimulates RBC production.'),
('54c2a8ff-309b-5441-81f9-85c61ba01328'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Where is erythropoietin primarily produced in adults?', '["Lung","Bone marrow","Kidney","Spleen","Liver"]'::jsonb, 'Kidney', 'In adults, ~90% of EPO is produced by peritubular interstitial cells in the kidney.'),
('44c88399-9e02-5433-bf5d-65756f39ea58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary stimulus for erythropoietin secretion?', '["Tissue hypoxia","Increased blood pressure","Iron overload","Hyperoxia","High RBC count"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue hypoxia', 'Low tissue oxygen tension upregulates HIF-1alpha, which binds to the EPO gene enhancer to increase EPO synthesis.'),
('fbe0cab3-4bea-542a-aafe-b2934f60737a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which transcription factor is critical for hypoxia-induced EPO expression?', '["HIF-1alpha","GATA-1","NF-kB","PU.1","STAT5"]'::jsonb, 'HIF-1alpha', 'Hypoxia-inducible factor 1α (HIF-1α) stabilizes under low oxygen and drives EPO transcription.'),
('5038bcc3-d5a9-5a63-b507-98ebf2c1ec7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the first morphologically recognizable erythroid precursor?', '["Erythroblast","Hematopoietic stem cell","Proerythroblast","Normoblast","Reticulocyte"]'::jsonb, 'Proerythroblast', 'The proerythroblast is the earliest committed erythroid precursor identifiable in bone marrow.'),
('d4313a0e-eea1-5bea-a2ae-8dbf58296656'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which stage of erythropoiesis loses its nucleus?', '["Proerythroblast","Polychromatophilic normoblast","Reticulocyte","Basophilic normoblast","Orthochromatic normoblast"]'::jsonb, 'Orthochromatic normoblast', 'The orthochromatic normoblast (late normoblast) extrudes its nucleus and becomes a reticulocyte.'),
('0366c981-2885-5143-a5b9-75179dd66b0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is a reticulocyte?', '["RBC precursor with a nucleus","Erythrocyte fragment","Young platelet","Mature RBC","Immature erythrocyte with residual RNA"]'::jsonb, 'Immature erythrocyte with residual RNA', 'Reticulocytes are young RBCs containing remnant ribosomal RNA, visible as a reticular network with special stains.'),
('2be64fd1-a017-5362-adbd-16ceecd04b6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'How long does it take for a reticulocyte to mature into an erythrocyte?', '["5–7 days","1 month","30 minutes","1–2 days","2 weeks"]'::jsonb, '1–2 days', 'Reticulocytes mature in 1–2 days in circulation; they are released early under EPO stimulation.'),
('c357d1e8-3d21-5528-8784-2865b4d26d32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the normal reticulocyte count in adults?', '["0.5–2.5%","3–5%","10–15%","5–10%","0.1–0.3%"]'::jsonb, '0.5–2.5%', 'Normal reticulocyte count is 0.5–2.5% of total RBCs; elevation indicates increased erythropoiesis.'),
('140f93a1-70bb-5ed1-b820-3b0d0694c120'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the reticulocyte production index (RPI) used to assess?', '["Absolute reticulocyte count mediated physiological mechanism","RBC lifespan mediated physiological mechanism","Hemoglobin concentration mediated physiological mechanism","Whether bone marrow response is appropriate for anemia degree","Iron deficiency severity mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Whether bone marrow response is appropriate for anemia degree', 'RPI corrects reticulocyte count for anemia severity and maturation shift, indicating adequate marrow response.'),
('bb65015f-a79d-5a64-8892-081287a4175d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which vitamin is required for the conversion of homocysteine to methionine in erythropoiesis?', '["Vitamin A","Folic acid","Vitamin B6","Vitamin B12","Vitamin C"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12', 'Vitamin B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase, converting homocysteine to methionine, essential for DNA synthesis.'),
('c8116653-389e-5f45-9186-e44f4a3667d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which form of iron is absorbed in the duodenum?', '["Heme-bound iron only","Free elemental iron","Ferric iron","Ferrous iron","Iron-dextran complexes"]'::jsonb, 'Ferrous iron', 'Dietary non-heme ferric iron (Fe3+) is reduced to ferrous (Fe2+) by duodenal cytochrome B (DcytB) for absorption.'),
('5116d114-a1f9-5865-9668-3b8881a4709c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which transporter mediates iron export from enterocytes into the bloodstream?', '["Transferrin receptor","Ferroportin","HFE protein","Hephaestin","DMT1"]'::jsonb, 'Ferroportin', 'Ferroportin is the only known cellular iron exporter; it transports Fe2+ from enterocytes into plasma.'),
('81fbbe99-c36a-525c-8ca4-3d646a853d1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which hormone regulates systemic iron homeostasis by inhibiting ferroportin?', '["Transferrin","Hepcidin","Erythropoietin","HIF-1alpha","Ferritin"]'::jsonb, 'Hepcidin', 'Hepcidin, produced by the liver, binds ferroportin causing its internalization and degradation, reducing iron efflux.'),
('b275bfe6-8174-5370-ad0d-57632ab95fb1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'How does iron deficiency affect erythropoiesis?', '["Causes polycythemia","Increases RBC lifespan","Causes normocytic anemia","Causes macrocytic anemia","Causes microcytic hypochromic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Causes microcytic hypochromic anemia', 'Iron deficiency reduces hemoglobin synthesis, producing small (microcytic) and pale (hypochromic) RBCs.'),
('5fea8695-415f-513e-946f-d85f5560c417'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the storage form of iron in macrophages and hepatocytes?', '["Free Fe2+","Heme iron","Iron-sulfur clusters","Transferrin-bound iron","Ferritin and hemosiderin"]'::jsonb, 'Ferritin and hemosiderin', 'Iron is stored as ferritin (soluble) and hemosiderin (insoluble aggregates) in cells.'),
('39940c73-b109-5740-88dc-b5b7f6ae962c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which protein binds heme for transport?', '["Ceruloplasmin","Albumin","Haptoglobin","Transferrin","Hemopexin"]'::jsonb, 'Hemopexin', 'Hemopexin binds free heme with high affinity, preventing oxidative damage and recycling heme iron.'),
('468b1976-ae5d-57a7-8250-a18880eaa8d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of ceruloplasmin in iron metabolism?', '["Ferroxidase activity converting Fe2+ to Fe3+","Iron storage in liver","Iron reduction for absorption","Iron export from cells","Iron transport in blood"]'::jsonb, 'Ferroxidase activity converting Fe2+ to Fe3+', 'Ceruloplasmin oxidizes Fe2+ to Fe3+, enabling iron loading onto transferrin for transport.'),
('336158b1-bd36-5795-9c9d-62deab2709b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which dietary factor enhances non-heme iron absorption?', '["Phytates in cereals","Oxalates","Calcium","Vitamin C","Tannins in tea"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin C', 'Vitamin C reduces ferric to ferrous iron and forms a chelate, enhancing duodenal iron absorption.'),
('dc99381e-a84e-54bc-9581-2a90c349ff7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the daily iron requirement for an adult male?', '["~1 mg","~0.5 mg","~10 mg","~20 mg","~5 mg"]'::jsonb, '~1 mg', 'Adult males need ~1 mg/day of absorbed iron to balance losses; women of childbearing age need ~1.5–2 mg/day.'),
('0e65470e-6bad-5efd-9660-03cdf42e3024'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What happens to the reticulocyte count after successful treatment of iron deficiency anemia?', '["Remains unchanged","First decreases then stabilizes","Increases","Decreases","Becomes zero"]'::jsonb, 'Increases', 'Iron therapy in iron deficiency anemia produces a reticulocyte peak within 5–10 days.'),
('1d2083e0-13de-5e30-8358-9fb8d9adea7a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which stem cell growth factor is critical for early erythropoiesis?', '["G-CSF","EPO","Thrombopoietin","Stem cell factor","M-CSF"]'::jsonb, 'Stem cell factor', 'SCF (c-kit ligand) acts on early hematopoietic progenitors, including erythroid burst-forming units (BFU-E).'),
('7115aaff-69e1-5568-82b3-ac0080ae24b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which interleukins are important for erythroid progenitor proliferation?', '["IL-2 and IL-4","IL-1 and IL-6","IL-3 and GM-CSF","IL-17 and IL-23","IL-10 and IL-12"]'::jsonb, 'IL-3 and GM-CSF', 'IL-3 and GM-CSF support the proliferation and differentiation of early erythroid progenitors.'),
('3d32a402-f624-56d3-bcc5-b3e80b47cf75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'At which stage of erythropoiesis does hemoglobin synthesis begin?', '["Basophilic normoblast","Polychromatophilic normoblast","Orthochromatic normoblast","Reticulocyte","Proerythroblast"]'::jsonb, 'Basophilic normoblast', 'Hemoglobin synthesis starts at the basophilic normoblast stage and continues through the reticulocyte stage.'),
('96b0753b-91c2-5da3-818e-c01aceb9df1f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the effect of bilateral nephrectomy on erythropoiesis?', '["No change","Polycythemia","Increased RBC lifespan","Increased RBC production","Severely reduced EPO and anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Severely reduced EPO and anemia', 'Bilateral nephrectomy removes the primary EPO source, causing severe anemia unless EPO replacement is given.'),
('34c6f40d-6f5a-5f21-b314-e27212a0d5dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cells are the first responders in the innate immune system?', '["B lymphocytes","Plasma cells","Neutrophils and macrophages","T lymphocytes","Natural killer T cells"]'::jsonb, 'Neutrophils and macrophages', 'Neutrophils and macrophages are the primary phagocytes that rapidly respond to pathogen invasion.'),
('6a0f7d0e-bb77-5f92-9944-bfffbb4c92de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which process describes the coating of pathogens by complement or antibodies to enhance phagocytosis?', '["Lysis","Chemotaxis","Opsonization","Neutralization","Agglutination"]'::jsonb, 'Opsonization', 'Opsonins such as C3b and IgG coat pathogens, enhancing recognition and uptake by phagocytes via Fc and complement receptors.'),
('b28e3a55-ae2a-50ea-9bce-1e1cd563b4a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which complement protein is the most abundant and critical for opsonization?', '["C2","C3","C5a","C4b","C1q"]'::jsonb, 'C3', 'C3 is the central complement component; its cleavage product C3b is a major opsonin.'),
('256c1780-89ee-5166-8f8f-42a094f8f907'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which complement component acts as a potent anaphylatoxin and chemotactic factor?', '["C1q","C4a","C3b","C5a","C2b"]'::jsonb, 'C5a', 'C5a is a potent anaphylatoxin that recruits neutrophils and induces mast cell degranulation.'),
('01f575f5-64c0-53e5-91db-52a30f360601'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary function of NK cells in innate immunity?', '["Produce antibodies","Release histamine","Kill virus-infected and tumor cells","Phagocytose bacteria","Present antigens"]'::jsonb, 'Kill virus-infected and tumor cells', 'NK cells recognize and lyse cells with reduced MHC I expression, typical of viral infection and malignancy.'),
('f79bf9c1-5724-5a72-9ef9-2503b6f1be7d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which receptor on NK cells recognizes MHC class I molecules and inhibits killing?', '["TCR","Fc receptors","BCR","Killer inhibitory receptors","Toll-like receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Killer inhibitory receptors', 'KIRs bind MHC I and transmit inhibitory signals, preventing NK cell activation against healthy cells.'),
('9c575454-7a5c-5568-bf8d-9a9be2034934'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokines are produced by macrophages to mediate inflammation?', '["TGF-beta, IL-10","IFN-gamma, IL-12","IL-2, IL-4, IL-10","IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha","IL-17, IL-22"]'::jsonb, 'IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha', 'Activated macrophages secrete IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-alpha, key pro-inflammatory cytokines.'),
('9c6ddd3b-f994-56a5-8701-7769ac54bf32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which pattern recognition receptor recognizes bacterial lipopolysaccharide?', '["TLR5","TLR3","TLR9","TLR4","TLR2"]'::jsonb, 'TLR4', 'TLR4 binds LPS (gram-negative bacteria) in complex with CD14 and MD-2, triggering innate responses.'),
('a0abdc09-1370-53f6-9a26-e9b233d43d51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which receptor recognizes double-stranded RNA in viral infection?', '["TLR7","TLR8","TLR9","TLR4","TLR3"]'::jsonb, 'TLR3', 'TLR3 recognizes viral dsRNA, a common PAMP during viral replication, triggering type I interferon production.'),
('df7141c0-ce57-5076-a0ee-92df72ce7b32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the function of defensins produced by neutrophils?', '["Complement activation","Antibody production","Direct antimicrobial peptide activity","Cytokine signaling","Opsonization"]'::jsonb, 'Direct antimicrobial peptide activity', 'Defensins are antimicrobial peptides that disrupt microbial membranes, acting as natural antibiotics.'),
('30f1fce9-565a-52e8-b872-3b44d98a0ff8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which plasma protein system is activated by mannose-binding lectin?', '["Lectin pathway of complement","Classical pathway","Coagulation cascade","Kinin system","Alternative pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Lectin pathway of complement', 'Mannose-binding lectin (MBL) binds microbial mannose residues and activates complement via MASP proteases.'),
('e9c9ca0d-fc0a-527e-9b96-73aaaa3c1600'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cell type is the primary source of type I interferons (IFN-alpha/beta)?', '["T cells","Neutrophils","Plasmacytoid dendritic cells","Macrophages","B cells"]'::jsonb, 'Plasmacytoid dendritic cells', 'Plasmacytoid dendritic cells are specialized for high-level production of type I interferons in response to viruses.'),
('a06ba07b-69fa-5d73-8420-f0ae82bb2b6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which complement pathway is antibody-independent and activated by pathogen surfaces?', '["Membrane attack pathway","Terminal pathway","Lectin pathway","Alternative pathway","Classical pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Alternative pathway', 'The alternative pathway is spontaneously activated on microbial surfaces without antibodies.'),
('697511fa-c4e1-549d-8aee-96751e8035c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the membrane attack complex (MAC) composed of?', '["C4b, C2a","C1, C4, C2, C3","C3a, C5a","Factor B, Factor D, Properdin","C5b, C6, C7, C8, C9"]'::jsonb, 'C5b, C6, C7, C8, C9', 'MAC (C5b-9) forms a pore in microbial membranes, causing osmotic lysis.'),
('948db005-91a5-56e9-a3af-5799980b1d5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokine is critical for macrophage activation and killing of intracellular pathogens?', '["IFN-gamma","TGF-beta","IL-10","IL-5","IL-4"]'::jsonb, 'IFN-gamma', 'IFN-gamma (produced by NK cells and Th1 cells) strongly activates macrophages for enhanced killing.'),
('434834ac-003c-5938-93e0-846f19baade8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which leukocyte adhesion molecule is expressed on activated endothelium and binds neutrophils?', '["ICAM-1","L-selectin","PSGL-1","PECAM-1","VCAM-1"]'::jsonb, 'ICAM-1', 'ICAM-1 on endothelium binds neutrophil integrins (LFA-1) during firm adhesion and transmigration.'),
('654e0e2d-56f8-513b-b14c-629631af4c59'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the process of neutrophil migration into tissues called?', '["Opsonization","Diapedesis","Phagocytosis","Chemotaxis","Degranulation"]'::jsonb, 'Diapedesis', 'Diapedesis is the process by which neutrophils squeeze through endothelial junctions to enter tissues.'),
('4fec4eb1-5b96-55e1-b4d4-dd6a6c7ecbba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which NADPH oxidase enzyme is responsible for the respiratory burst in phagocytes?', '["Catalase","Myeloperoxidase","Cytochrome c oxidase","NOX2","Xanthine oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'NOX2', 'Phagocyte NADPH oxidase (NOX2) produces superoxide radicals during the respiratory burst for microbial killing.'),
('4bf9da9d-6735-51b8-b2b5-8b471db8040b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary defect in chronic granulomatous disease?', '["Defective complement system mediated physiological mechanism","Defective NADPH oxidase leading to impaired respiratory burst","Defective antibody production mediated physiological mechanism","Defective chemotaxis mediated physiological mechanism","Defective opsonization mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Defective NADPH oxidase leading to impaired respiratory burst', 'CGD is caused by mutations in NADPH oxidase components, preventing superoxide production and intracellular killing.'),
('1bec3a88-cb5b-5bde-9444-946f3b1c2b20'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokine is a potent chemoattractant for neutrophils?', '["IL-4","IL-2","IL-8","IL-10","IL-12"]'::jsonb, 'IL-8', 'IL-8 is a chemokine that attracts and activates neutrophils at sites of inflammation.'),
('086768e1-fb6a-5e51-8b31-9c6d8a0b9ed9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of C3 convertase in the complement cascade?', '["Cleaves C3 into C3a and C3b","Inhibits complement","Cleaves C5 into C5a and C5b","Activates C1","Cleaves C4 into C4a and C4b"]'::jsonb, 'Cleaves C3 into C3a and C3b', 'C3 convertase is the central enzyme of complement, cleaving C3 to generate opsonins and anaphylatoxins.'),
('3bd19194-c4a8-5223-a927-69e86a73c145'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the alternative pathway C3 convertase?', '["C5b-9","C1qrs","MBL-MASP","C3bBb","C4b2a"]'::jsonb, 'C3bBb', 'C3bBb is the alternative pathway C3 convertase, stabilized by properdin (Factor P).'),
('755d312f-1faf-50ba-b496-68c83adbe96e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which protein inhibits the alternative pathway by inactivating C3b?', '["C4b-binding protein","Decay-accelerating factor","Factor H","Factor I","Protectin"]'::jsonb, 'Factor H', 'Factor H displaces Bb from C3b and acts as a cofactor for Factor I-mediated C3b inactivation.'),
('775a12a3-37a9-5338-938e-395e52c0c9f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which surface protein protects host cells from complement-mediated lysis by inhibiting MAC formation?', '["MCP","DAF","CR1","Factor H","CD59"]'::jsonb, 'CD59', 'CD59 prevents C9 polymerization and MAC insertion on host cell membranes.'),
('528f9834-426d-555c-b76a-3c8bc02304d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary mechanism by which NK cells kill target cells?', '["Phagocytosis","Release of perforin and granzymes","Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity","TNF-alpha secretion","Complement-mediated lysis"]'::jsonb, 'Release of perforin and granzymes', 'NK cells release perforin (forms pores) and granzymes (induce apoptosis) into target cells.'),
('e15898db-fe76-5371-9489-69835bb56020'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Where do B lymphocytes mature?', '["Thymus","Bone marrow","Lymph nodes","Peyer''s patches","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow', 'B cells mature in the bone marrow, unlike T cells which mature in the thymus.'),
('51e23ce3-c4be-560b-bbc2-b7197f4034a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which immunoglobulin is the most abundant in serum?', '["IgA","IgM","IgE","IgD","IgG"]'::jsonb, 'IgG', 'IgG constitutes about 75–80% of serum immunoglobulins and is the main antibody of secondary immune responses.'),
('c8daa3b4-e2fe-548a-8b81-f60dcc0d3c36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which immunoglobulin is the first to be produced in a primary immune response?', '["IgM","IgG","IgA","IgE","IgD"]'::jsonb, 'IgM', 'IgM is the first antibody class produced in a primary immune response; it is a pentamer with high avidity.'),
('b3b75634-3d54-58ef-a090-df6b12476a2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which immunoglobulin can cross the placenta?', '["IgM","IgE","IgD","IgA","IgG"]'::jsonb, 'IgG', 'IgG is the only immunoglobulin that crosses the placenta via FcRn, providing passive immunity to the fetus.'),
('be939eb7-163a-5187-a78e-58dd6a7b0af4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which immunoglobulin is found in secretions (milk, saliva, tears)?', '["IgE","IgA","IgD","IgG","IgM"]'::jsonb, 'IgA', 'Secretory IgA (sIgA) is a dimer with a secretory component, protecting mucosal surfaces.'),
('933f123d-ed76-5097-9764-c7f302c8f066'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which immunoglobulin mediates immediate hypersensitivity reactions by binding to mast cells?', '["IgG","IgE","IgA","IgM","IgD"]'::jsonb, 'IgE', 'IgE binds to Fc-epsilon receptors on mast cells and basophils, triggering degranulation upon allergen cross-linking.'),
('c4e84928-cb57-5123-9fe3-3d7e26aaa315'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the function of IgD on the surface of naive B cells?', '["Mast cell activation mediated physiological mechanism","Acts as a B cell receptor for antigen recognition","Neonatal immunity mediated physiological mechanism","Secreted antibody mediated physiological mechanism","Complement fixation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Acts as a B cell receptor for antigen recognition', 'Surface IgD along with IgM serves as the B cell receptor (BCR) on naive B cells.'),
('2041a21b-8662-5d80-85e5-878d393849ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which process describes the switch from IgM to IgG production during an immune response?', '["Clonal selection","V(D)J recombination","Class switch recombination","Somatic hypermutation","Affinity maturation"]'::jsonb, 'Class switch recombination', 'Class switch recombination changes the antibody heavy chain constant region while preserving antigen specificity.'),
('d7ecdfa0-36cd-5a76-bb3d-d4839c555745'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokine primarily drives class switching to IgE?', '["IL-2","IFN-gamma","TGF-beta","IL-4","IL-10"]'::jsonb, 'IL-4', 'IL-4 from Th2 cells induces B cell class switching to IgE, promoting allergic responses.'),
('e48a058f-7e43-5c63-930b-af03873e52ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which enzyme is critical for class switch recombination and somatic hypermutation?', '["Activation-induced cytidine deaminase","Terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase","RAG1/2","DNA polymerase eta","DNA ligase IV"]'::jsonb, 'Activation-induced cytidine deaminase', 'AID deaminates cytosine in DNA, initiating both class switch recombination and somatic hypermutation.'),
('88650c27-b1bb-5c14-8f40-d07d66eceaa1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary function of plasma cells?', '["Secrete cytokines","Regulate immune responses","Kill infected cells","Secrete large quantities of antibodies","Present antigens"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete large quantities of antibodies', 'Plasma cells are terminally differentiated B lymphocytes specialized for high-rate antibody secretion.'),
('fcbadf1b-d642-5948-94ce-5f10b0856410'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following is the primary antigen-presenting cell for activating naive B cells?', '["Neutrophils mediated physiological mechanism","Dendritic cells and macrophages","NK cells mediated physiological mechanism","Mast cells mediated physiological mechanism","Eosinophils mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Dendritic cells and macrophages', 'Dendritic cells and macrophages present processed antigens to helper T cells, which then activate B cells.'),
('e9b623e9-74b0-576c-a5ef-f9fb364b4009'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of follicular dendritic cells (FDCs)?', '["Display intact antigen to B cells in germinal centers","Phagocytose pathogens mediated physiological mechanism","Secrete antibodies mediated physiological mechanism","Produce complement proteins mediated physiological mechanism","Kill infected cells mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Display intact antigen to B cells in germinal centers', 'FDCs trap immune complexes and display native antigen to B cells, driving affinity maturation.'),
('332ca96e-7ef4-520d-845d-50c509a0e8da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is affinity maturation?', '["Increase in antibody quantity","Switch from T-dependent to T-independent response","Change in antibody class","Decrease in antibody diversity","Increase in antibody affinity over time due to somatic hypermutation and selection"]'::jsonb, 'Increase in antibody affinity over time due to somatic hypermutation and selection', 'Affinity maturation occurs in germinal centers, producing antibodies with progressively higher antigen affinity.'),
('380edc77-490c-51fb-8d34-656251ab6d96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cells give rise to memory B cells?', '["Activated B cells that survive germinal center selection","Naive B cells mediated physiological mechanism","Follicular dendritic cells mediated physiological mechanism","T follicular helper cells mediated physiological mechanism","Plasma cells mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Activated B cells that survive germinal center selection', 'Memory B cells are long-lived cells generated in germinal centers that enable rapid secondary responses.'),
('503e214f-cf1e-5d56-864b-daa2705ce5f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which immunoglobulin forms a pentamer with a J chain?', '["IgD","IgA","IgG","IgE","IgM"]'::jsonb, 'IgM', 'IgM is a pentamer linked by a J (joining) chain, giving high avidity for antigen.'),
('d57998de-17c9-5e76-aab8-50fdf12e4707'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the valency of secretory IgA?', '["5","1","4","3","2"]'::jsonb, '4', 'Secretory IgA is a dimer with 4 antigen-binding sites (tetravalent).'),
('697b94d4-04ce-5fdb-8e6f-e74b921f9b07'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which receptor on B cells recognizes the Fc portion of IgG and regulates B cell activation?', '["CD40","TLR4","Fc-gamma receptor IIB","CD21","BCR"]'::jsonb, 'Fc-gamma receptor IIB', 'FcγRIIB is an inhibitory receptor on B cells that provides negative feedback when IgG immune complexes bind.'),
('cd7a34be-6546-5625-9e67-af0c920e063f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of CD40-CD40L interaction in humoral immunity?', '["Promotes plasma cell death mediated physiological mechanism","Triggers apoptosis mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibits B cell proliferation mediated physiological mechanism","Essential for B cell activation, class switching, and germinal center formation","Induces tolerance mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Essential for B cell activation, class switching, and germinal center formation', 'CD40 on B cells binds CD40L on T cells, delivering critical signals for B cell activation and antibody responses.'),
('0d2ea8a8-e657-5a63-921e-6f8f3d330e3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which X-linked immunodeficiency results from defective CD40L expression?', '["Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Bruton''s agammaglobulinemia","Common variable immunodeficiency","Severe combined immunodeficiency","Hyper-IgM syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Hyper-IgM syndrome', 'X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome is due to CD40L mutation; patients have normal/high IgM but low IgG/IgA/IgE.'),
('34825749-8abf-52ea-8ef4-14670016d6ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary defect in Bruton''s agammaglobulinemia?', '["Defective complement mediated physiological mechanism","Deficiency of Bruton''s tyrosine kinase (BTK) causing B cell maturation arrest","Defective MHC II expression mediated physiological mechanism","Defective RAG1/2 mediated physiological mechanism","Defective CD40L mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Deficiency of Bruton''s tyrosine kinase (BTK) causing B cell maturation arrest', 'BTK mutation blocks pre-B cell maturation, resulting in very low B cells and immunoglobulins.'),
('33cc0624-6175-5e0a-9da0-3e323aa85c9a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which region of the antibody determines its class (isotype)?', '["Constant region of light chain","Fab region","Variable region of light chain","Variable region of heavy chain","Constant region of heavy chain"]'::jsonb, 'Constant region of heavy chain', 'The heavy chain constant region (CH) defines the antibody isotype: mu (IgM), gamma (IgG), alpha (IgA), etc.'),
('38621408-aa42-50a6-a3ac-423fb7d2ef5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which antibody fragment contains the antigen-binding site?', '["Fc","Fab","F(ab'')2","Heavy chain alone","Light chain alone"]'::jsonb, 'Fab', 'The Fab region consists of one constant and one variable domain from both heavy and light chains, binding antigen.'),
('22b3d57c-89cc-5633-bbd5-8d0735140bc8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which complement pathway is activated by IgG and IgM immune complexes?', '["Lectin pathway","Terminal pathway","Alternative pathway","Mannan-binding pathway","Classical pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Classical pathway', 'C1q binds to the Fc region of IgG or IgM in immune complexes, initiating the classical complement pathway.'),
('c2beabc7-bbc7-577c-8cbf-960a971c001a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which antibody subclass is most efficient at opsonization?', '["IgE","IgG2","IgA","IgG1 and IgG3","IgM"]'::jsonb, 'IgG1 and IgG3', 'IgG1 and IgG3 bind Fc-gamma receptors with high affinity, promoting efficient phagocytosis.'),
('8c06af22-2006-51f8-9a67-d447e07e0a64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which T cell subset is essential for helping B cells produce high-affinity antibodies?', '["Th2 cells","Th1 cells","Th17 cells","Regulatory T cells","T follicular helper (Tfh) cells"]'::jsonb, 'T follicular helper (Tfh) cells', 'Tfh cells in germinal centers provide CD40L and IL-21 signals critical for B cell maturation and class switching.'),
('f197bee8-b675-51ab-baec-a2201700f567'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is a hapten?', '["Large immunogenic protein mediated physiological mechanism","Complete antigen mediated physiological mechanism","Small molecule that can elicit an immune response only when conjugated to a carrier","T cell receptor fragment mediated physiological mechanism","Antibody fragment mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Small molecule that can elicit an immune response only when conjugated to a carrier', 'Haptens are small molecules that are immunogenic only when coupled to a carrier protein.'),
('073e739c-0da4-52f2-b57b-3d89edf424de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the function of the J chain in IgM and IgA?', '["Fixes complement","Binds antigen","Determines antibody specificity","Binds Fc receptors","Facilitates polymerization and mucosal transport"]'::jsonb, 'Facilitates polymerization and mucosal transport', 'The J chain links monomer subunits in IgM (pentamer) and IgA (dimer) and facilitates epithelial transport.'),
('4b48631c-b2d1-5423-9c61-84e267d99fd5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which test is used to quantify serum antibody levels to assess humoral immunity?', '["Flow cytometry for B cells mediated physiological mechanism","Complete blood count mediated physiological mechanism","Skin test mediated physiological mechanism","Serum protein electrophoresis and immunoturbidimetry","ELISPOT mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Serum protein electrophoresis and immunoturbidimetry', 'Serum immunoglobulin levels are measured by protein electrophoresis and nephelometry/turbidimetry.'),
('0f1cac64-6855-5195-9fdb-af873ac72a7f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the relationship between somatic hypermutation and B cell selection?', '["Mutations occur only in T cells mediated physiological mechanism","Mutations are selected for increased antigen affinity in germinal centers","Mutations are random and not selected mediated physiological mechanism","Mutations occur in the constant region mediated physiological mechanism","Mutations decrease antibody affinity mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Mutations are selected for increased antigen affinity in germinal centers', 'Somatic hypermutation in variable regions followed by affinity-based selection produces higher-affinity antibodies.'),
('b924fbf3-897e-55e1-8f2b-44317cdaf1da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Where do T lymphocytes undergo maturation?', '["Lymph nodes","Spleen","Liver","Thymus","Bone marrow"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus', 'T cell precursors migrate from bone marrow to the thymus for maturation and selection.'),
('29fee0bd-36ad-5db0-8f1c-8e87078c8a5f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which T cell subset expresses CD4 and acts as a helper cell?', '["CD4+ T cells","Gamma-delta T cells","Regulatory T cells","CD8+ T cells","Natural killer T cells"]'::jsonb, 'CD4+ T cells', 'CD4+ T cells recognize antigen on MHC II and orchestrate immune responses via cytokine secretion.'),
('430d1b5f-b55b-5b0e-9453-2978a9d7de5a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which T cell subset expresses CD8 and is responsible for killing infected cells?', '["T follicular helper cells","CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes","CD4+ T cells","Regulatory T cells","Th17 cells"]'::jsonb, 'CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes', 'CD8+ CTLs recognize peptide on MHC I and directly kill virus-infected or tumor cells.'),
('c1662166-acf0-5853-b399-50355b5794ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which T cell subset suppresses immune responses and prevents autoimmunity?', '["Th17 cells","Th2 cells","Regulatory T cells","Th1 cells","Cytotoxic T cells"]'::jsonb, 'Regulatory T cells', 'Tregs (CD4+FoxP3+) suppress effector T cells and maintain self-tolerance.'),
('1a96f879-8d61-5f0c-9346-6e386e3ffe02'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of MHC class I molecules?', '["Kill infected cells","Secrete cytokines","Present exogenous peptides to CD4+ T cells","Present endogenous peptides to CD8+ T cells","Activate B cells"]'::jsonb, 'Present endogenous peptides to CD8+ T cells', 'MHC I is expressed on all nucleated cells and displays cytosolic peptides to CD8+ CTLs.'),
('2a799b18-45c7-5eb6-9ab4-1b4ba8f5ce90'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of MHC class II molecules?', '["Present endogenous antigens to CD8+ T cells","Present exogenous antigens to CD4+ T cells","Complement activation","Antibody secretion","Lytic killing"]'::jsonb, 'Present exogenous antigens to CD4+ T cells', 'MHC II is expressed on antigen-presenting cells (dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells) for CD4+ T cell activation.'),
('0194748b-9443-55b2-ab63-40ac1722ff03'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which transcription factor is the master regulator of regulatory T cell development?', '["GATA-3","FoxP3","ROR-gamma-t","Bcl-6","T-bet"]'::jsonb, 'FoxP3', 'FoxP3 is the lineage-defining transcription factor for regulatory T cells.'),
('0d76b116-caed-50c8-904f-0ee86e43cb62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokine is critical for Th1 differentiation?', '["IL-4","IL-23","IL-12","IL-6","TGF-beta"]'::jsonb, 'IL-12', 'IL-12 from dendritic cells drives naive CD4+ T cells toward the Th1 lineage, promoting IFN-gamma production.'),
('13e65812-63ca-56d8-b804-0913271fe548'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokine is critical for Th2 differentiation?', '["IL-4","IL-17","IFN-gamma","TGF-beta","IL-12"]'::jsonb, 'IL-4', 'IL-4 drives Th2 differentiation, leading to production of IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 for humoral and allergic responses.'),
('1fca10a4-82e9-5832-80f3-e70116510210'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokine is the hallmark product of Th17 cells?', '["IL-2","IL-17","IL-4","TGF-beta","IFN-gamma"]'::jsonb, 'IL-17', 'Th17 cells produce IL-17, which recruits neutrophils and is important in extracellular bacterial and fungal defense.'),
('e079e91d-e20d-550f-a715-adec495821fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is positive selection in T cell development?', '["Selection of T cells that recognize self-MHC molecules","Elimination of self-reactive T cells","Migration of T cells to periphery","Differentiation into effector cells","Activation of naive T cells"]'::jsonb, 'Selection of T cells that recognize self-MHC molecules', 'Positive selection in thymic cortex retains T cells whose TCRs weakly bind self-MHC, ensuring MHC restriction.'),
('dc90e8a9-92c1-5dbf-8b9e-8e738e62a872'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is negative selection in T cell development?', '["Conversion to regulatory T cells mediated physiological mechanism","Selection for MHC recognition mediated physiological mechanism","Activation by foreign antigen mediated physiological mechanism","Export to periphery mediated physiological mechanism","Elimination of T cells that strongly react to self-antigens"]'::jsonb, 'Elimination of T cells that strongly react to self-antigens', 'Negative selection in the thymic medulla deletes autoreactive T cells to establish central tolerance.'),
('725d1796-aa01-5977-a2f4-0a6236621360'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which molecule on antigen-presenting cells provides co-stimulation for naive T cell activation?', '["MHC II","ICAM-1","CD40","B7","MHC I"]'::jsonb, 'B7', 'CD80/CD86 on APCs bind CD28 on T cells, providing the second signal necessary for naive T cell activation.'),
('d5524b74-685e-5597-bc0e-31ac29e389e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What happens if a T cell receives only signal 1 (TCR stimulation) without co-stimulation?', '["Proliferation","Full activation","Anergy (unresponsiveness) or apoptosis","Differentiation into effector cell","Memory formation"]'::jsonb, 'Anergy (unresponsiveness) or apoptosis', 'TCR stimulation without co-stimulation induces anergy, rendering the T cell functionally unresponsive.'),
('49f6c287-a5e4-59c5-8d3f-61cd8452083e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytotoxic granule protein forms pores in target cell membranes?', '["IFN-gamma","FasL","Granzyme B","Perforin","TNF-alpha"]'::jsonb, 'Perforin', 'Perforin polymerizes to form transmembrane pores, allowing granzymes to enter target cells.'),
('1fefd97a-0c42-5dc1-8221-cdebb84c253b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytotoxic molecule induces apoptosis via caspase activation?', '["TNF-alpha","Granzyme B","IL-2","IFN-gamma","Perforin"]'::jsonb, 'Granzyme B', 'Granzyme B enters target cells via perforin pores and cleaves caspases, triggering apoptosis.'),
('ff88888b-1de8-5141-9d08-42f62966a91b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which transcription factor drives CD8+ T cell effector differentiation?', '["Bcl-6","T-bet and Eomes","GATA-3","ROR-gamma-t","FoxP3"]'::jsonb, 'T-bet and Eomes', 'T-bet and Eomesodermin are key transcription factors for cytotoxic T cell differentiation and function.'),
('8bfdf09f-083a-5a02-b889-edeeded91561'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokine is essential for CD8+ T cell proliferation and memory formation?', '["IL-10","IL-4","IL-2","IL-17","TGF-beta"]'::jsonb, 'IL-2', 'IL-2 is a growth factor for T cells; it drives expansion of antigen-specific CD8+ T cells after activation.'),
('5a580264-3494-5245-a131-d7aad4342cad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of AIRE in T cell tolerance?', '["Induces regulatory T cells mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulates T cell activation mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibits MHC expression mediated physiological mechanism","Promotes B cell development mediated physiological mechanism","Promotes expression of tissue-specific antigens in thymic medulla for negative selection"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes expression of tissue-specific antigens in thymic medulla for negative selection', 'AIRE drives ectopic expression of peripheral tissue antigens in thymic epithelium for deletion of autoreactive T cells.'),
('eca887d1-556e-56dd-a648-2fe11d0e9a79'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cells are the most potent professional antigen-presenting cells?', '["Macrophages","Dendritic cells","B cells","Neutrophils","Eosinophils"]'::jsonb, 'Dendritic cells', 'Dendritic cells are the most efficient APCs, uniquely capable of activating naive T cells.'),
('c70b4c26-0560-50cc-bb09-5fea91f6e175'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is cross-presentation in antigen processing?', '["Cross-linking of BCR","Presentation of endogenous antigen on MHC II","Presentation of exogenous antigen on MHC I to CD8+ T cells","Presentation without MHC","Presentation by B cells only"]'::jsonb, 'Presentation of exogenous antigen on MHC I to CD8+ T cells', 'Cross-presentation allows dendritic cells to present engulfed antigens on MHC I, enabling CD8+ T cell responses.'),
('744e5f9a-1fa4-5ed6-9dcd-ce696dced414'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which chemokine receptor is expressed on Th1 cells and guides them to inflamed tissues?', '["CCR3","CXCR5","CXCR3","CCR4","CCR6"]'::jsonb, 'CXCR3', 'Th1 cells express CXCR3, responding to CXCL9/10/11 at sites of inflammation.'),
('df5eea82-17b2-50e4-bdeb-d3d968c050c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokine is produced by Tregs to suppress immune responses?', '["IL-4","IFN-gamma","TNF-alpha","IL-10 and TGF-beta","IL-17"]'::jsonb, 'IL-10 and TGF-beta', 'Tregs secrete IL-10 and TGF-beta, which suppress effector T cell proliferation and function.'),
('ca35f735-d99c-53e8-9f1b-d34347e2dc9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the consequence of defective MHC II expression (bare lymphocyte syndrome)?', '["High CD8+ T cell count","Autoimmunity","Increased NK cell activity","Excessive antibody production","Severe immunodeficiency with low CD4+ T cells"]'::jsonb, 'Severe immunodeficiency with low CD4+ T cells', 'Bare lymphocyte syndrome (MHC II deficiency) impairs CD4+ T cell development and function.'),
('ee46e3c5-46cc-5d90-82e8-1afcb2be0225'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which receptor on CD8+ T cells binds to MHC I to enhance activation?', '["CD8 binds to alpha-3 domain of MHC I","CD28 binds to B7","CTLA-4 binds to B7","CD4 binds to MHC II","TCR binds to peptide only"]'::jsonb, 'CD8 binds to alpha-3 domain of MHC I', 'The CD8 co-receptor binds the conserved alpha-3 domain of MHC I, stabilizing TCR-peptide-MHC interaction.'),
('d1b6b5f1-4001-5a46-aa7a-5eba0bf94f07'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which type of hypersensitivity reaction is mediated by IgE and mast cells?', '["Type IV","Type V","Type I","Type II","Type III"]'::jsonb, 'Type I', 'Type I hypersensitivity is IgE-mediated, causing mast cell degranulation and symptoms within minutes.'),
('7c2a0e3e-398a-5b0a-834f-cdb6d9a42244'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which type of hypersensitivity involves IgG/IgM binding to cell surface antigens?', '["Type I","Type II","Type V","Type IV","Type III"]'::jsonb, 'Type II', 'Type II hypersensitivity involves antibodies targeting cell surface antigens, leading to opsonization or lysis.'),
('5220e4ee-0463-5908-8409-9f34583b1e82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which type of hypersensitivity is characterized by immune complex deposition?', '["Type II","Type IV","Type I","Type III","Type V"]'::jsonb, 'Type III', 'Type III hypersensitivity results from deposition of antigen-antibody complexes in tissues, causing inflammation.'),
('1be5d542-6a4f-56b3-ab09-72d2118646f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which type of hypersensitivity is delayed and mediated by T cells?', '["Type V","Type IV","Type I","Type III","Type II"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV', 'Type IV hypersensitivity is T cell-mediated, occurring 24–72 hours after antigen exposure.'),
('b51c544a-f0bd-5dc0-b039-00ad1143c6e5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which antibody is responsible for anaphylaxis in type I hypersensitivity?', '["IgG","IgE","IgM","IgA","IgD"]'::jsonb, 'IgE', 'Cross-linking of IgE on mast cells triggers release of histamine and other mediators causing anaphylaxis.'),
('038adf26-1337-522e-945e-c4324cd126e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which drug can cause immune hemolytic anemia as a type II reaction?', '["Aspirin","Omeprazole","Acetaminophen","Penicillin","Metformin"]'::jsonb, 'Penicillin', 'Penicillin can bind to RBC membrane, forming hapten-carrier complexes targeted by IgG, causing hemolysis.'),
('bb737eb3-d36a-53af-9604-8321f73152d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which disease is a classic example of type III hypersensitivity?', '["Asthma","Goodpasture syndrome","Graves disease","Contact dermatitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic lupus erythematosus', 'SLE involves widespread immune complex deposition (DNA-anti-DNA complexes) causing multi-organ damage.'),
('ac9a454a-d842-5e5c-9c1c-45b8f2e80699'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which condition is a classic example of type IV hypersensitivity?', '["Hemolytic disease of newborn","Urticaria","Serum sickness","Anaphylaxis","Tuberculin (PPD) skin test reaction"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberculin (PPD) skin test reaction', 'The PPD skin test for TB is a delayed-type hypersensitivity mediated by memory T cells, peaking at 48-72 hours.'),
('a7d1924c-0be8-5575-a17e-79dbdd69eb69'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary mediator released by mast cells in type I hypersensitivity?', '["Complement C5a","IL-2","Histamine","Perforin","IFN-gamma"]'::jsonb, 'Histamine', 'Histamine is the primary preformed mediator in mast cell granules, causing vasodilation, bronchoconstriction, and itching.'),
('d277f93a-4d3c-5dcb-b6fd-7d6c08bdb40e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which leukotrienes are involved in the late-phase reaction of type I hypersensitivity?', '["LTC4, LTD4, LTE4","TXA2","PGD2","PAF","LTB4"]'::jsonb, 'LTC4, LTD4, LTE4', 'Cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4) cause sustained bronchoconstriction and mucus secretion.'),
('dc6a7d54-9da7-595a-a9b8-36fc7e4a4810'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which disease is caused by type II hypersensitivity against the acetylcholine receptor?', '["Graves disease","Type 1 diabetes","Multiple sclerosis","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Myasthenia gravis"]'::jsonb, 'Myasthenia gravis', 'Autoantibodies against the acetylcholine receptor at the neuromuscular junction cause myasthenia gravis.'),
('73cb4b90-42aa-5876-bf8c-1c1e9bf78816'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which disease is caused by type II hypersensitivity with anti-glomerular basement membrane antibodies?', '["Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","Polyarteritis nodosa","SLE","Rheumatic fever","Goodpasture syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Goodpasture syndrome', 'Anti-GBM antibodies target the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen in kidneys and lungs.'),
('c82e455a-2c3f-53ec-be23-0a1f357a9923'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the Arthus reaction?', '["Systemic anaphylaxis mediated physiological mechanism","Localized type III hypersensitivity from intradermal antigen injection in immunized individuals","Delayed-type hypersensitivity mediated physiological mechanism","Contact dermatitis mediated physiological mechanism","Hemolytic transfusion reaction mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Localized type III hypersensitivity from intradermal antigen injection in immunized individuals', 'Arthus reaction involves local immune complex deposition causing inflammation, edema, and necrosis.'),
('fb0590a4-d6ca-5c29-937f-75ac800a9f60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is serum sickness?', '["Systemic type III hypersensitivity from foreign serum proteins","Type IV contact dermatitis mediated physiological mechanism","Autoimmune disease mediated physiological mechanism","Type II hemolysis mediated physiological mechanism","Type I anaphylaxis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic type III hypersensitivity from foreign serum proteins', 'Serum sickness results from immune complex deposition after exposure to foreign proteins (e.g., anti-venom).'),
('81febfeb-5552-5f51-86d0-ae34d7601dcf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which T cell subset mediates contact dermatitis (e.g., poison ivy)?', '["Th17 cells mediated physiological mechanism","CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and Th1 cells","B cells mediated physiological mechanism","Th2 cells mediated physiological mechanism","Tregs mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and Th1 cells', 'Contact dermatitis is a type IV reaction where haptens (e.g., urushiol) activate CD8+ and Th1 cells.'),
('acdeaa5f-010f-545e-80e6-93af1349f361'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the mechanism of hemolytic disease of the newborn (Rh disease)?', '["T cell-mediated attack mediated physiological mechanism","Complement deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Maternal IgM destroys fetal RBCs mediated physiological mechanism","Maternal anti-Rh IgG crosses placenta and destroys fetal RBCs","Immune complex deposition mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Maternal anti-Rh IgG crosses placenta and destroys fetal RBCs', 'Rh-negative mother produces anti-Rh IgG that crosses placenta and hemolyzes Rh-positive fetal RBCs.'),
('7d003fa5-5464-59eb-a3b1-349892bfb174'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which type I hypersensitivity mediator causes bronchoconstriction and increased vascular permeability?', '["Perforin","IL-2","IFN-gamma","TNF-alpha","Histamine"]'::jsonb, 'Histamine', 'Histamine causes smooth muscle contraction (bronchoconstriction) and increased vascular permeability via H1 receptors.'),
('58c3df70-dc9a-5978-a3f9-5f1469372b64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which condition is an example of type IV hypersensitivity in the skin?', '["Atopic dermatitis","Urticaria","Drug-induced urticaria","Allergic contact dermatitis","Angioedema"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic contact dermatitis', 'Nickel allergy is a classic T cell-mediated type IV hypersensitivity causing eczematous contact dermatitis.'),
('5d0369ad-0db8-513e-b209-670762715771'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which hypersensitivity type is responsible for transplant rejection?', '["Type V","Type II","Type I","Type IV","Type III"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV', 'Acute transplant rejection is primarily mediated by CD8+ CTLs and CD4+ T cells (type IV).'),
('42f21447-9a3e-50f8-b877-aa45597c2c6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of Th2 cells in type I hypersensitivity?', '["Recruit neutrophils mediated physiological mechanism","Suppress allergic responses mediated physiological mechanism","Produce IFN-gamma mediated physiological mechanism","Kill allergen-specific B cells mediated physiological mechanism","Produce IL-4, IL-5, IL-13 promoting IgE class switching and eosinophil activation"]'::jsonb, 'Produce IL-4, IL-5, IL-13 promoting IgE class switching and eosinophil activation', 'Th2 cytokines drive IgE production, mast cell sensitization, and eosinophil recruitment in allergy.'),
('85531ba9-9603-54b6-ba96-8d011b000d26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which hypersensitivity type is associated with celiac disease?', '["Type I mediated physiological mechanism","Type III mediated physiological mechanism","Type II mediated physiological mechanism","Type IV (T cell-mediated) to gluten","Type V mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV (T cell-mediated) to gluten', 'Celiac disease involves gluten-reactive CD4+ T cells in the intestinal mucosa causing inflammation.'),
('b467420f-6d7b-5628-9418-5f62f1c68ef5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which hypersensitivity type is seen in a positive tuberculin skin test?', '["Type I","Type IV","Type II","Type V","Type III"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV', 'PPD test is a classic delayed-type hypersensitivity (type IV) mediated by memory T cells.'),
('e5b5ac5c-178b-51be-965d-4be47b6fa0b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What differentiates type II from type III hypersensitivity?', '["Type II involves IgM only; type III involves IgG","Type II is T cell-mediated; type III is IgE-mediated","Type II is systemic; type III is localized","Type II is delayed; type III is immediate","Type II targets cell surface antigens; type III involves soluble immune complexes"]'::jsonb, 'Type II targets cell surface antigens; type III involves soluble immune complexes', 'In type II, antibodies bind to cell/tissue antigens; in type III, circulating immune complexes deposit in tissues.'),
('30b607cd-97e6-5cff-bf89-f84d77a8dd99'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which hypersensitivity reaction is triggered by allergen cross-linking of IgE on mast cells?', '["Type II cytotoxic","Type I immediate hypersensitivity","Type V stimulatory","Type IV delayed","Type III immune complex"]'::jsonb, 'Type I immediate hypersensitivity', 'Cross-linking of surface IgE on mast cells by multivalent allergen triggers immediate degranulation (type I).'),
('63b8a2cf-7fe0-5b9c-bdb4-139caa6e2383'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is atopy?', '["Complement deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Genetic predisposition to develop type I hypersensitivity to common allergens","Type IV hypersensitivity mediated physiological mechanism","Immunodeficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Autoimmune disease mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic predisposition to develop type I hypersensitivity to common allergens', 'Atopy refers to the inherited tendency to produce IgE antibodies against environmental allergens, leading to asthma, eczema, hay fever.'),
('e27f8c3d-e8b8-5f8d-b4fc-a782b2d5f0a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the mechanism of central tolerance?', '["Deletion of self-reactive T cells in thymus and B cells in bone marrow","Anergy induction mediated physiological mechanism","Suppression by Tregs mediated physiological mechanism","Clonal ignorance mediated physiological mechanism","Receptor editing mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Deletion of self-reactive T cells in thymus and B cells in bone marrow', 'Central tolerance eliminates developing lymphocytes that strongly recognize self-antigens during maturation.'),
('a6907678-744b-5f4c-a9e5-e4dd0679dea9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which autoimmune disease is characterized by antibodies against the TSH receptor causing hyperthyroidism?', '["Graves disease","Rheumatoid arthritis","Type 1 diabetes","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Addison disease"]'::jsonb, 'Graves disease', 'In Graves disease, thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) activate the TSH receptor, causing hyperthyroidism.'),
('4b6f7256-c5f1-5ca0-b2f8-31acc480310d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which autoimmune disease involves destruction of pancreatic beta cells by autoreactive T cells?', '["Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Cushing syndrome","Type 2 diabetes","Addison disease","Graves disease"]'::jsonb, 'Type 1 diabetes mellitus', 'Type 1 diabetes results from autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells by CD8+ T cells and autoantibodies.'),
('04f74197-08f5-5970-a936-f8ebdfb545ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which autoantibody is characteristic of systemic lupus erythematosus?', '["Anti-CCP antibodies","Anti-nuclear antibodies","Anti-Sm","Anti-dsDNA","Anti-Ro/SSA"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-nuclear antibodies', 'ANA is the hallmark autoantibody in SLE, present in >95% of patients.'),
('c57c55b6-5821-5b9b-bec7-ff4eb9dce9b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary immune defect in severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)?', '["Deficiency of both T and B cell function","Deficiency of B cells only","Deficiency of complement","Deficiency of phagocytes","Deficiency of T cells only"]'::jsonb, 'Deficiency of both T and B cell function', 'SCID involves severe defects in both T and B cell immunity, causing life-threatening infections early in life.'),
('5c7b0db1-e507-56c1-9ab5-f72dc6460773'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which enzyme deficiency causes about 50% of autosomal recessive SCID cases?', '["Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency","IL-7R deficiency","JAK3 deficiency","Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency","RAG1/2 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency', 'ADA deficiency leads to accumulation of toxic purine metabolites that kill developing lymphocytes, causing SCID.'),
('7bec3a34-4006-5015-a7e9-826b8edf8517'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which infection is characteristic of hyper-IgM syndrome?', '["Viral infections only","Opportunistic infections","Meningococcal sepsis","Staphylococcal abscesses","Fungal infections only"]'::jsonb, 'Opportunistic infections', 'Hyper-IgM syndrome patients have defective humoral and T cell immunity, leading to opportunistic infections.'),
('a8652e50-cbee-5f0a-89f5-8a4cededd667'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the molecular basis of autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (APS-1)?', '["Mutation in CTLA-4","Mutation in HLA-DR","Mutation in FOXP3","Mutation in IL-2","Mutation in AIRE gene"]'::jsonb, 'Mutation in AIRE gene', 'APS-1 is caused by AIRE mutations, disrupting central tolerance and causing multiple organ autoimmunity.'),
('0329ddc3-5ead-5bd2-b574-fd190ba21cdc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which autoimmune disease is associated with anti-citrullinated protein antibodies (ACPA)?', '["Sjogren syndrome","SLE","Systemic sclerosis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Polymyositis"]'::jsonb, 'Rheumatoid arthritis', 'Anti-CCP antibodies are highly specific for rheumatoid arthritis and target citrullinated proteins in joints.'),
('325c7f02-d05f-590a-b84b-38a5a2966903'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which immunodeficiency is characterized by recurrent sinopulmonary infections and very low B cells/immunoglobulins?', '["Transient hypogammaglobulinemia of infancy","Selective IgA deficiency","Hyper-IgM syndrome","Bruton agammaglobulinemia","Common variable immunodeficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Common variable immunodeficiency', 'CVID presents later in life with low immunoglobulins and impaired antibody responses, causing recurrent infections.'),
('b7864aaa-20f6-5c40-885d-f9489e8602b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is selective IgA deficiency associated with?', '["Lymphoproliferation","Anaphylactic reactions","Opportunistic infections","Severe bacterial infections","Recurrent mucosal infections and autoimmunity"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent mucosal infections and autoimmunity', 'Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency, predisposing to sinopulmonary infections and autoimmune diseases.'),
('d58b81e2-b9ca-51d1-be64-b0beafb1a671'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which immunodeficiency is linked to a defect in the phagocyte NADPH oxidase?', '["Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Chronic granulomatous disease","G6PD deficiency","Myeloperoxidase deficiency","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic granulomatous disease', 'CGD results from NADPH oxidase defects, impairing the respiratory burst and intracellular killing of catalase-positive bacteria.'),
('48885779-14f0-5338-9abe-8af7cc12ff22'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which type of autoimmunity is mediated by molecular mimicry between streptococcal M protein and cardiac myosin?', '["Rheumatoid arthritis","Multiple sclerosis","Acute rheumatic fever","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","SLE"]'::jsonb, 'Acute rheumatic fever', 'Streptococcal M protein cross-reacts with cardiac myosin, causing autoimmune myocarditis and valvulitis.'),
('b9623a3c-4a50-59eb-9ae4-b8601efbc335'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which cytokine therapy is used to treat chronic granulomatous disease?', '["IL-2","IFN-gamma","G-CSF","IL-12","GM-CSF"]'::jsonb, 'IFN-gamma', 'IFN-gamma replacement enhances residual NADPH oxidase activity and reduces infection risk in CGD.'),
('e78f1a2f-4b58-5d50-9e90-ceef9992b80b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the fundamental defect in autoimmune hemolytic anemia?', '["Defective RBC production mediated physiological mechanism","Iron deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Autoantibodies against RBC membrane antigens causing hemolysis","T cell deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Complement deficiency mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Autoantibodies against RBC membrane antigens causing hemolysis', 'Warm or cold autoantibodies bind RBCs, leading to complement-mediated or extravascular hemolysis.'),
('6d02df28-af48-5036-9a9b-2c65eb3981a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the first event in primary hemostasis after vascular injury?', '["Fibrin clot formation","Platelet adhesion","Fibrinolysis","Platelet aggregation","Vasoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction', 'Vascular injury triggers immediate reflex vasoconstriction to reduce blood flow, followed by platelet adhesion.'),
('3dc8a104-31f5-580c-9bc5-6f60fc093f11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which platelet surface receptor mediates adhesion to exposed collagen via von Willebrand factor?', '["GPIIb-IIIa","Integrin α2β1","GPIb-IX-V complex","GPVI","P-selectin"]'::jsonb, 'GPIb-IX-V complex', 'Platelet GPIb binds vWF, which bridges platelets to exposed collagen at high shear stress.'),
('ed7f377c-851d-5abb-af5a-7674011804f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which platelet integrin mediates platelet-to-platelet aggregation by binding fibrinogen?', '["GPIIb-IIIa","αvβ3","GPIb-IX-V","α2β1","GPVI"]'::jsonb, 'GPIIb-IIIa', 'Activated GPIIb-IIIa binds fibrinogen, cross-linking platelets into aggregates.'),
('b7af7cda-f5c4-578e-adb0-f29b3c81fdd7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which substance is the primary platelet agonist released from platelet dense granules?', '["ADP","Histamine","TXA2","Serotonin","PDGF"]'::jsonb, 'ADP', 'ADP released from dense granules amplifies platelet activation and recruits additional platelets.'),
('0832d1fe-0a1b-5e36-8f48-9e3fb9f0422f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which enzyme converts arachidonic acid to thromboxane A2?', '["Prostacyclin synthase","Phospholipase A2","Lipoxygenase","Cyclooxygenase","Thromboxane synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclooxygenase', 'COX converts arachidonic acid to PGH2, which is then converted to TXA2 by thromboxane synthase.'),
('db35f882-15a8-5c1a-803b-797d0a9728f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which prostaglandin produced by endothelial cells inhibits platelet aggregation?', '["PGF2α","PGD2","Prostacyclin","Thromboxane A2","PGE2"]'::jsonb, 'Prostacyclin', 'PGI2 from intact endothelium activates platelet adenylyl cyclase, increasing cAMP and inhibiting aggregation.'),
('92fffb28-cf68-5bc7-a109-fd28a8071104'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of von Willebrand factor in hemostasis?', '["Mediates platelet adhesion to subendothelium and carries factor VIII","Cross-links fibrin mediated physiological mechanism","Converts fibrinogen to fibrin mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibits thrombin mediated physiological mechanism","Activates factor X mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Mediates platelet adhesion to subendothelium and carries factor VIII', 'vWF bridges platelets to exposed collagen and stabilizes factor VIII in circulation.'),
('974fea5d-8060-5ec5-92fe-9ae00b8acc1e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which clotting factor is deficient in classic hemophilia (hemophilia A)?', '["Factor IX","Factor VIII","von Willebrand factor","Factor XI","Factor VII"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII', 'Hemophilia A is an X-linked deficiency of factor VIII, causing prolonged intrinsic pathway coagulation time.'),
('70eca27d-fbd4-53fe-8522-3fed0cba46e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which clotting factor is deficient in hemophilia B (Christmas disease)?', '["Factor IX","Factor XI","Factor VIII","Factor VII","Factor X"]'::jsonb, 'Factor IX', 'Hemophilia B (Christmas disease) is X-linked factor IX deficiency, clinically similar to hemophilia A.'),
('3390ec2d-12fc-542c-91b2-ad919623c5cd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which vitamin is required for the synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX, X?', '["Vitamin D","Vitamin K","Vitamin C","Vitamin E","Vitamin A"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin K', 'Vitamin K is a cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of glutamic acid residues on factors II, VII, IX, X.'),
('59ec9e14-be7d-5421-a78e-bc68fb544b95'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the initiator of the extrinsic coagulation pathway?', '["Factor IX","Factor XI","Tissue factor","Factor XII","Factor VIII"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue factor', 'Tissue factor expressed on damaged cells binds factor VIIa, initiating the extrinsic pathway.'),
('fd4d7729-32dd-5e27-a508-cfa61d95e72f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which factor is the final enzyme of the coagulation cascade that converts fibrinogen to fibrin?', '["Factor XIIIa","Thrombin","Factor Xa","Factor VIIa","Factor IXa"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombin', 'Thrombin cleaves fibrinopeptides from fibrinogen, forming fibrin monomers that polymerize.'),
('0b0c85d6-de87-5d3b-ad81-ef4d2cdf5c80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which factor cross-links fibrin polymers to stabilize the clot?', '["Factor XIIIa","Factor Xa","Fibrinogen","Factor IXa","Thrombin"]'::jsonb, 'Factor XIIIa', 'Factor XIIIa (fibrin-stabilizing factor) forms covalent cross-links between fibrin monomers, strengthening the clot.'),
('c6344b92-5368-51c2-a234-e885892e0dad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which clotting factor is the first enzyme of the intrinsic pathway activated by contact with negatively charged surfaces?', '["Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor XI","Prekallikrein","Factor XII"]'::jsonb, 'Factor XII', 'Factor XII undergoes autoactivation upon contact with subendothelial surfaces, initiating the intrinsic pathway.'),
('a536bb35-bc14-504f-be6d-1e978234bb3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which complex is the intrinsic tenase complex?', '["Factor Xa + Factor Va on phospholipid surface","Factor XIa + Factor IX","Factor IXa + Factor VIIIa on phospholipid surface","Factor VIIa + Tissue factor","Thrombin + thrombomodulin"]'::jsonb, 'Factor IXa + Factor VIIIa on phospholipid surface', 'The intrinsic tenase (IXa-VIIIa complex) activates factor X.'),
('4eaaccdf-c95f-513e-91a3-926900026f9c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which complex is the prothrombinase complex?', '["Factor IXa + Factor VIIIa","Factor VIIa + Tissue factor","Factor XIIIa + fibrin","Factor XIIa + HK + PK","Factor Xa + Factor Va on phospholipid surface"]'::jsonb, 'Factor Xa + Factor Va on phospholipid surface', 'Prothrombinase (Xa-Va) converts prothrombin (factor II) to thrombin (factor IIa).'),
('d14b22c1-8d29-54e4-b3ce-3898f99d4475'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the common pathway of coagulation?', '["Extrinsic pathway activation mediated physiological mechanism","Platelet aggregation mediated physiological mechanism","Fibrinolysis mediated physiological mechanism","Intrinsic pathway activation mediated physiological mechanism","Activation of factor X to Xa, conversion of prothrombin to thrombin, fibrin formation"]'::jsonb, 'Activation of factor X to Xa, conversion of prothrombin to thrombin, fibrin formation', 'The common pathway begins with factor X activation and culminates in fibrin clot formation.'),
('7e58e44a-858a-5eba-8fcf-79f5e7a6e06b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of thrombomodulin in hemostasis?', '["Binds thrombin, changing its specificity to activate protein C","Inhibits platelet aggregation mediated physiological mechanism","Converts plasminogen to plasmin mediated physiological mechanism","Stabilizes fibrin mediated physiological mechanism","Activates factor X mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Binds thrombin, changing its specificity to activate protein C', 'Thrombomodulin on endothelium binds thrombin, which then activates protein C (anticoagulant).'),
('1732e8a3-c151-52cd-943f-3f939e6ae235'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which natural anticoagulant inactivates factors Va and VIIIa?', '["Plasmin","Activated protein C","Antithrombin III","Tissue factor pathway inhibitor","Heparin"]'::jsonb, 'Activated protein C', 'APC proteolytically cleaves and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa, downregulating coagulation.'),
('ef8bcfb9-e6c9-5c69-b94a-c79193c11405'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'How does antithrombin III inhibit coagulation?', '["Binds calcium","Activates plasminogen","Blocks platelet adhesion","Directly inhibits thrombin and factor Xa","Degrades fibrin"]'::jsonb, 'Directly inhibits thrombin and factor Xa', 'Antithrombin III is a serine protease inhibitor that neutralizes thrombin and factor Xa; heparin accelerates this.'),
('c52395f1-798f-5453-8381-b6c6c1056699'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI)?', '["Activates protein C mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibits thrombin mediated physiological mechanism","Cross-links fibrin mediated physiological mechanism","Degrades platelet plug mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibits the extrinsic pathway by binding factor Xa and VIIa-TF"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits the extrinsic pathway by binding factor Xa and VIIa-TF', 'TFPI inhibits the VIIa-TF complex in a factor Xa-dependent manner.'),
('62916efb-f5d9-533d-b5f5-4b82d85c60ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which enzyme is primarily responsible for fibrin degradation (fibrinolysis)?', '["t-PA","Factor Xa","Thrombin","Urokinase","Plasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Plasmin', 'Plasmin degrades fibrin polymers into fibrin degradation products (FDPs) and D-dimers.'),
('5b62eae3-75fe-5173-b625-2fa1c2ad8ba9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the inactive precursor of plasmin?', '["Fibrinogen","Prothrombin","Plasminogen","t-PA","PAI-1"]'::jsonb, 'Plasminogen', 'Plasminogen circulates in plasma and is converted to active plasmin by t-PA or urokinase.'),
('a99ad619-43e8-5d62-bb85-1f26f145a4c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which factor binds to fibrin to enhance t-PA-mediated plasminogen activation?', '["Factor XIII","Thrombin","Protein C","Fibrin","Plasminogen activator inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrin', 'Fibrin binds both plasminogen and t-PA, localizing and enhancing plasmin generation on the clot surface.'),
('7da23bd5-19dc-52cf-976a-4e020e2d7a40'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which drug inhibits plasminogen activation and is used as an antifibrinolytic?', '["Tranexamic acid","Warfarin","Heparin","Aspirin","Clopidogrel"]'::jsonb, 'Tranexamic acid', 'Tranexamic acid inhibits plasminogen activation by blocking lysine binding sites, reducing fibrinolysis.'),
('66a1fcb8-9899-55d9-acc8-1a49054b80ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the principal inhibitor of plasmin in plasma?', '["Protein C","Antithrombin III","Alpha-2 antiplasmin","PAI-1","TFPI"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-2 antiplasmin', 'Alpha-2 antiplasmin rapidly inactivates free plasmin, protecting circulating fibrinogen from degradation.'),
('9b50d819-2926-5c18-b28c-782549416562'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which laboratory test measures the integrity of the intrinsic pathway?', '["Bleeding time","aPTT","Platelet count","Thrombin time","PT"]'::jsonb, 'aPTT', 'aPTT evaluates factors of the intrinsic and common pathways (XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, fibrinogen).'),
('2e9be42e-dd1e-54a6-a2f6-ab0261134704'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which laboratory test measures the integrity of the extrinsic pathway?', '["Factor XIII assay","aPTT","PT","Bleeding time","Thrombin time"]'::jsonb, 'PT', 'PT assesses factors VII (extrinsic) and common pathway factors (X, V, II, fibrinogen).'),
('bb5d97c9-a9f1-57bb-9be5-5c9042247468'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which test is prolonged in von Willebrand disease?', '["Bleeding time","Platelet count","Factor VIII level","PT only","Thrombin time only"]'::jsonb, 'Bleeding time', 'vWD prolongs bleeding time due to defective platelet adhesion; aPTT may be prolonged if factor VIII is low.'),
('959329c7-8d88-5ec0-afdc-8a7b7fefcae8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the principle of the International Normalized Ratio (INR)?', '["Measure of fibrinogen concentration mediated physiological mechanism","Measure of platelet function mediated physiological mechanism","Direct measure of thrombin activity mediated physiological mechanism","Standardized aPTT ratio mediated physiological mechanism","Standardized PT ratio to account for thromboplastin reagent variability"]'::jsonb, 'Standardized PT ratio to account for thromboplastin reagent variability', 'INR = (patient PT / mean normal PT)^ISI, allowing comparison of PT across different reagents.'),
('8b2d3f0e-cd54-529e-9ba2-d2f1366b3316'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following is a direct thrombin inhibitor?', '["Warfarin","Clopidogrel","Aspirin","Hirudin","Heparin"]'::jsonb, 'Hirudin', 'Direct thrombin inhibitors (e.g., hirudin, bivalirudin, dabigatran) directly bind and inhibit thrombin.'),
('0bd2266e-2d58-5678-9cff-5f9bb544be0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which anticoagulant is monitored by aPTT?', '["LMWH","Unfractionated heparin","Direct oral anticoagulants","Fondaparinux","Warfarin"]'::jsonb, 'Unfractionated heparin', 'Unfractionated heparin is monitored by aPTT due to its potentiating effect on antithrombin III.'),
('99fcafb6-81df-5963-8755-715fccac11ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which anticoagulant is monitored by PT/INR?', '["Warfarin","Heparin","Dabigatran","LMWH","Rivaroxaban"]'::jsonb, 'Warfarin', 'Warfarin inhibits vitamin K-dependent factors and is monitored by PT/INR.'),
('1fdbb60c-2f5f-598c-a337-b1d7a48fb006'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the effect of thrombin on platelet activation?', '["Induces platelet disaggregation","No effect on platelets","Inhibitor of platelet function","Potent activator via PAR receptors","Blocks fibrinogen binding"]'::jsonb, 'Potent activator via PAR receptors', 'Thrombin cleaves protease-activated receptors (PARs) on platelets, causing robust activation.'),
('8aa28439-9863-5d8b-80a8-9a07b86e478d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which platelet granule type contains ADP, ATP, calcium, and serotonin?', '["Glycogen granules","Dense granules","Specific granules","Lysosomes","Alpha granules"]'::jsonb, 'Dense granules', 'Dense granules contain small molecules that amplify platelet activation, including ADP and calcium.'),
('12eb3113-ac6f-5ed0-8752-e839557a6d0a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What do alpha granules of platelets contain?', '["Histamine mediated physiological mechanism","Defensins mediated physiological mechanism","vWF, fibrinogen, PDGF, PF4, factor V","ADP, ATP, serotonin mediated physiological mechanism","Acid hydrolases mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'vWF, fibrinogen, PDGF, PF4, factor V', 'Alpha granules contain proteins important for adhesion, aggregation, and wound healing.'),
('d56970b9-3112-5878-b6e5-e0b61f6ec396'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which platelet receptor is essential for shape change and granule secretion?', '["PAR","α2β1","GPIb-IX-V","GPVI","P2Y12"]'::jsonb, 'PAR', 'Thrombin activates platelets via PAR1 and PAR4, triggering shape change and granule secretion.'),
('eb1732e4-a600-5c3d-bc20-0fabf3bf7f85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which drug irreversibly inhibits COX and reduces TXA2 production?', '["Dipyridamole","Clopidogrel","Abciximab","Aspirin","Ticagrelor"]'::jsonb, 'Aspirin', 'Aspirin acetylates COX-1, irreversibly inhibiting TXA2 synthesis and platelet aggregation.'),
('6fe2dbb6-1339-54e7-af32-b1f2e67ed5ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which drug blocks the P2Y12 ADP receptor on platelets?', '["Clopidogrel","Aspirin","Tranexamic acid","Heparin","Abciximab"]'::jsonb, 'Clopidogrel', 'Clopidogrel and other thienopyridines irreversibly block P2Y12, inhibiting ADP-mediated platelet activation.'),
('086215a5-fa84-5eed-9fc4-c805bd9f4b06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the mechanism of action of abciximab?', '["Activates plasminogen mediated physiological mechanism","Blocks GPIIb-IIIa, inhibiting fibrinogen binding and platelet aggregation","Blocks COX mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibits thrombin mediated physiological mechanism","Blocks P2Y12 mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks GPIIb-IIIa, inhibiting fibrinogen binding and platelet aggregation', 'Abciximab is a monoclonal antibody Fab fragment against GPIIb-IIIa, the final common pathway of aggregation.'),
('e518835a-0b85-5169-84d6-b22314f75a45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the normal bleeding time?', '["30–60 seconds","<1 minute","15–30 minutes","2–7 minutes","10–15 minutes"]'::jsonb, '2–7 minutes', 'Normal bleeding time (Duke or Ivy method) is 2–7 minutes, reflecting platelet plug formation.'),
('e0bf0974-a5c6-5938-a31d-bc3724d18af7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which condition causes a prolonged bleeding time with normal platelet count?', '["von Willebrand disease","Factor XIII deficiency","Hemophilia B","Vitamin K deficiency","Hemophilia A"]'::jsonb, 'von Willebrand disease', 'vWD impairs platelet adhesion due to defective vWF, prolonging bleeding time despite normal platelet count.'),
('828b63fd-2147-517d-a743-7bd60d66ba50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the purpose of the D-dimer test?', '["Measure platelet count mediated physiological mechanism","Detect fibrin degradation products indicating recent clot formation and lysis","Measure fibrinogen level mediated physiological mechanism","Measure aPTT mediated physiological mechanism","Measure PT mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Detect fibrin degradation products indicating recent clot formation and lysis', 'D-dimer is specific for cross-linked fibrin degradation, used in DIC and VTE diagnosis.'),
('0bd9f0a7-45d0-594a-aa83-acc800235e73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What are the typical PT/aPTT findings in hemophilia A?', '["Both prolonged","Normal PT, prolonged aPTT","Prolonged PT, normal aPTT","Normal PT, increased bleeding time","Both normal"]'::jsonb, 'Normal PT, prolonged aPTT', 'Hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency) prolongs aPTT with normal PT, reflecting intrinsic pathway defect.'),
('b8f04802-9607-56d1-8d5f-029ae221ddf3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What are the typical PT/aPTT findings in vitamin K deficiency?', '["Normal PT, normal aPTT","Prolonged PT, normal or prolonged aPTT","Prolonged PT, normal aPTT","Both normal","Normal PT, prolonged aPTT"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged PT, normal or prolonged aPTT', 'Vitamin K deficiency affects factors II, VII, IX, X; PT (factor VII) prolongs first, aPTT later.'),
('8d12bd26-3a02-51a4-aeb3-7d0e79541792'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the coagulation abnormality in liver disease?', '["Elevated factor VIII mediated physiological mechanism","Normal PT/aPTT mediated physiological mechanism","Prolonged PT due to decreased synthesis of all coagulation factors except vWF","Prolonged aPTT only mediated physiological mechanism","Prolonged bleeding time only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged PT due to decreased synthesis of all coagulation factors except vWF', 'Liver synthesizes most clotting factors; liver disease impairs production, prolonging PT primarily.'),
('b00628ba-87b7-560f-801a-a1c2cae5da8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which factor is the only coagulation factor not produced by the liver?', '["Factor VIII","von Willebrand factor","Fibrinogen","Factor VII","Factor II"]'::jsonb, 'von Willebrand factor', 'vWF is produced by endothelial cells (Weibel-Palade bodies) and megakaryocytes, not hepatocytes.'),
('c878828f-480c-5d0c-9146-dc601cc5dd25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the role of calcium in coagulation?', '["Inhibitor of plasmin mediated physiological mechanism","Activator of protein C mediated physiological mechanism","Direct activator of thrombin mediated physiological mechanism","Promoter of platelet disaggregation mediated physiological mechanism","Required for assembly of vitamin K-dependent factors on phospholipid surfaces"]'::jsonb, 'Required for assembly of vitamin K-dependent factors on phospholipid surfaces', 'Calcium ions bridge the gamma-carboxyglutamic acid residues of factors II, VII, IX, X to phospholipid membranes.'),
('5e7a8efe-c0d3-575a-b62a-7b7ea46a214e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which natural anticoagulant is a vitamin K-dependent protein?', '["TFPI","Antithrombin III","Heparin","Protein C","Alpha-2 macroglobulin"]'::jsonb, 'Protein C', 'Protein C and its cofactor protein S are vitamin K-dependent anticoagulant factors.'),
('c4a3e54f-538a-5f25-a4cd-d16e1d4e8147'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the main activator of fibrinolysis in vivo?', '["Kallikrein","Factor XIIa","Plasmin","t-PA","Urokinase"]'::jsonb, 't-PA', 't-PA is released from endothelial cells and activates plasminogen on the fibrin surface, initiating fibrinolysis.'),
('0236a665-b4e1-5341-b3c7-d8bffa9ad6ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which drug is a recombinant t-PA used for thrombolysis in acute MI?', '["Tenecteplase","Reteplase","Urokinase","Alteplase","Streptokinase"]'::jsonb, 'Alteplase', 'Alteplase is a recombinant t-PA used to lyse clots in acute myocardial infarction and ischemic stroke.'),
('f9ab35f0-957e-51ad-b62c-d48e6f1d87bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which antigen is present on the surface of RBCs in blood group A?', '["Rh antigen","A antigen","Neither A nor B","B antigen","Both A and B"]'::jsonb, 'A antigen', 'Blood group A has A antigen on RBCs and anti-B antibodies in plasma.'),
('dc41b2b4-6ced-5ef9-bcab-6ac086594e7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which antibodies are present in the plasma of a person with blood group O?', '["Anti-Rh","Anti-A and Anti-B","No antibodies","Anti-A only","Anti-B only"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-A and Anti-B', 'Group O individuals have both anti-A and anti-B IgM antibodies in plasma.'),
('81965a2f-1b47-5ed0-ae51-1a0182afb207'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Why is blood group O considered the universal donor?', '["Has no antibodies mediated physiological mechanism","Is Rh-negative mediated physiological mechanism","Has both A and B antigens mediated physiological mechanism","Has low immunogenicity mediated physiological mechanism","Lacks A and B antigens on RBCs, minimizing transfusion reaction risk"]'::jsonb, 'Lacks A and B antigens on RBCs, minimizing transfusion reaction risk', 'Group O RBCs lack A and B antigens, so they are not agglutinated by recipient anti-A or anti-B.'),
('e7814cf4-5845-568f-b784-63065f95d681'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Why is blood group AB considered the universal recipient?', '["Has no anti-A or anti-B antibodies in plasma","Has anti-A and anti-B","Has both A and B antigens","Has high tolerance","Is Rh-positive"]'::jsonb, 'Has no anti-A or anti-B antibodies in plasma', 'AB individuals produce neither anti-A nor anti-B, so they can receive RBCs from any ABO group.'),
('6c285ff3-9012-58da-882e-c166bbe29bfa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the Rh (D) antigen?', '["A leukocyte antigen mediated physiological mechanism","A platelet antigen mediated physiological mechanism","A plasma protein mediated physiological mechanism","A major immunogenic RBC surface protein","A complement protein mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'A major immunogenic RBC surface protein', 'RhD is the most immunogenic of the Rh blood group antigens; D-negative individuals can produce anti-D.'),
('02ecef4c-ff91-59df-94f3-d0ea46a98c63'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the percentage of Rh-positive individuals in the general population?', '["~85%","~99%","~70%","~15%","~50%"]'::jsonb, '~85%', 'Approximately 85% of the population is Rh-positive (D antigen present); 15% are Rh-negative.'),
('87c1116c-09df-5dc1-acfc-5cdfc5392357'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the major complication of ABO-incompatible blood transfusion?', '["Intravascular hemolysis mediated by recipient anti-A/anti-B IgM","Graft-versus-host disease mediated physiological mechanism","Extravascular hemolysis only mediated physiological mechanism","Allergic reaction mediated physiological mechanism","No reaction mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Intravascular hemolysis mediated by recipient anti-A/anti-B IgM', 'IgM anti-A/anti-B fix complement, causing rapid intravascular hemolysis of donor RBCs.'),
('dc994d3f-7bf9-5e75-b7c7-a158379c023e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which blood group is the most common in the general population?', '["Rh-negative","AB","B","A","O"]'::jsonb, 'O', 'Blood group O is the most common, followed by A, B, and AB.'),
('aa31e2bc-54b4-5d8b-8754-79fa673ff4e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the Landsteiner rule?', '["ABO antigens are inherited mediated physiological mechanism","Cross-matching is required mediated physiological mechanism","Rh antigens are on RBCs mediated physiological mechanism","Universal donor is O mediated physiological mechanism","Anti-A and anti-B antibodies are always present in plasma against the missing RBC antigen"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-A and anti-B antibodies are always present in plasma against the missing RBC antigen', 'Landsteiner''s rule states that plasma contains antibodies against the opposite ABO antigen(s) absent on RBCs.'),
('12860807-82fe-5f34-bc41-a1c82ffa6b89'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the basis of ABO blood group inheritance?', '["X-linked inheritance mediated physiological mechanism","Two separate genes mediated physiological mechanism","Polygenic inheritance mediated physiological mechanism","Multiple alleles (A, B, O) at a single gene locus on chromosome 9","Mitochondrial inheritance mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple alleles (A, B, O) at a single gene locus on chromosome 9', 'ABO blood groups are determined by three alleles (A, B, O) of the ABO gene on chromosome 9.'),
('c93fff1b-1035-5836-9d90-d1ddd4741f4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary cause of hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn (HDFN)?', '["Kell incompatibility","Kidd incompatibility","Duffy incompatibility","Rh incompatibility","ABO incompatibility"]'::jsonb, 'Rh incompatibility', 'Rh-negative mothers exposed to Rh+ fetal RBCs produce anti-D IgG that crosses placenta and hemolyzes fetal RBCs.'),
('e28cddb3-6a72-55e1-9eb2-85d4c5ef3f3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'How is hemolytic disease of the newborn prevented?', '["Anti-D immunoglobulin (RhoGAM) given to Rh-negative mother at 28 weeks and within 72 hours of del","Exchange transfusion mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","IVIG mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Phototherapy mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Premature delivery mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-D immunoglobulin (RhoGAM) given to Rh-negative mother at 28 weeks and within 72 hours of del', 'Anti-D Ig prevents maternal sensitization by clearing fetal Rh+ RBCs before immune response develops.'),
('0650f646-5450-5d65-83e8-909cf4289ae7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the major blood group system in humans besides ABO?', '["Kell system","Rh system","Duffy system","Kidd system","MNS system"]'::jsonb, 'Rh system', 'The Rh system (D, C, c, E, e) is the second most clinically important blood group system after ABO.'),
('c2ccb508-3d84-57a3-b826-16ecf5e7d14b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the purpose of cross-matching before transfusion?', '["Check platelet count mediated physiological mechanism","Measure hemoglobin mediated physiological mechanism","Determine ABO group only mediated physiological mechanism","Detect clinically significant antibodies in recipient plasma against donor RBCs","Screen for infections mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Detect clinically significant antibodies in recipient plasma against donor RBCs', 'Cross-match mixes donor RBCs with recipient serum to detect unexpected antibodies.'),
('b9705c0c-ad77-5747-8a7f-3a15ad0f2c46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the major carbohydrate determinant of blood group A antigen?', '["Fucose","N-acetylgalactosamine","N-acetylglucosamine","Galactose","Glucose"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylgalactosamine', 'The A antigen has N-acetylgalactosamine as its terminal immunodominant sugar added by A glycosyltransferase.'),
('1b571b69-07a7-5879-ada4-400d0580c6ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the major carbohydrate determinant of blood group B antigen?', '["N-acetylgalactosamine","Galactose","Sialic acid","Glucose","Fucose"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose', 'The B antigen has galactose as its terminal immunodominant sugar added by B glycosyltransferase.'),
('c6e63b41-080c-5baa-bda1-62be1b7ba506'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the H antigen in the ABO system?', '["The Rh antigen mediated physiological mechanism","The O antigen mediated physiological mechanism","The precursor substance modified by ABO glycosyltransferases","The B antigen mediated physiological mechanism","The A antigen mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'The precursor substance modified by ABO glycosyltransferases', 'H antigen is a fucosylated glycan precursor; A/B transferases add sugars to H to form A/B antigens.'),
('2f998d76-3962-5daf-a349-c3c13932ff3e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the Bombay (Oh) phenotype?', '["Universal donor mediated physiological mechanism","RBCs lack Rh mediated physiological mechanism","RBCs have weak A mediated physiological mechanism","RBCs have both A and B mediated physiological mechanism","RBCs lack H, A, and B antigens due to FUT1 mutation; have anti-A, anti-B, and anti-H"]'::jsonb, 'RBCs lack H, A, and B antigens due to FUT1 mutation; have anti-A, anti-B, and anti-H', 'Bombay phenotype (h/h) lacks H transferase; individuals produce anti-H in addition to anti-A/anti-B.'),
('a009e7c4-8e12-50de-bfb2-a3b20b3f69d4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What blood type can a person with Bombay phenotype safely receive?', '["Type AB only","Type B only","Only Bombay (Oh) blood","Type A only","Type O only"]'::jsonb, 'Only Bombay (Oh) blood', 'Bombay individuals have anti-H, so they can only receive RBCs from other Bombay individuals.'),
('964e8700-5d1e-5931-b331-f318722d3d1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which blood group is associated with the highest risk of gastric cancer?', '["Blood group A","Blood group AB","Blood group B","Blood group O","Blood group Rh-negative"]'::jsonb, 'Blood group A', 'Blood group A is associated with a slightly higher risk of gastric cancer, possibly due to H. pylori interactions.'),
('82e31ec3-3c98-5c63-bb05-f371714c68ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'falciparum?', '["Blood group AB","Blood group B","Blood group O","Blood group Rh-positive","Blood group A"]'::jsonb, 'Blood group O', 'Blood group O is associated with a protective effect against severe P. falciparum malaria due to reduced rosetting.'),
('cf35dc47-77b8-5c46-882a-b9c6ab4f43e5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What component of blood is most commonly transfused?', '["Cryoprecipitate","Whole blood","Platelets","Packed red blood cells","Fresh frozen plasma"]'::jsonb, 'Packed red blood cells', 'PRBCs are the most commonly transfused blood component, used to increase oxygen-carrying capacity.'),
('7b0f30f2-d461-5186-ab50-4b12c88573d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the storage temperature for packed red blood cells?', '["-20°C","-80°C","37°C","22°C","1–6°C"]'::jsonb, '1–6°C', 'PRBCs are stored at 1–6°C to maintain viability and reduce bacterial growth.'),
('3ba5d5f8-19f1-5075-ad94-5aab6de14d64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the most common transfusion reaction?', '["TRALI","Bacterial contamination","Allergic reaction","Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction","Acute hemolytic reaction"]'::jsonb, 'Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction', 'FNHTR is the most common reaction, caused by cytokines or recipient antibodies against donor WBCs.'),
('f7b3fbb6-0657-5e1d-8b0d-864943d86424'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is TRALI (transfusion-related acute lung injury)?', '["Bacterial sepsis mediated physiological mechanism","Anaphylaxis mediated physiological mechanism","Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema due to anti-leukocyte antibodies in plasma products","Volume overload mediated physiological mechanism","Hemolysis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema due to anti-leukocyte antibodies in plasma products', 'TRALI is a serious reaction involving donor antibodies that activate recipient neutrophils, causing lung injury.'),
('d36e0757-505e-5ef5-8e0d-a4c7bb341299'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which test is used for antibody screening before transfusion?', '["Direct antiglobulin test","ELISA","Western blot","Agglutination on slide","Indirect antiglobulin test"]'::jsonb, 'Indirect antiglobulin test', 'IAT detects unexpected antibodies in recipient serum by incubating with screening RBCs and adding anti-human globulin.'),
('e71ea50a-d5cb-542d-8e4f-a5f77cf044df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which test is used to diagnose autoimmune hemolytic anemia?', '["Bleeding time","PT","aPTT","Direct antiglobulin test","Indirect antiglobulin test"]'::jsonb, 'Direct antiglobulin test', 'DAT detects antibodies or complement on patient RBCs, positive in autoimmune hemolytic anemia.'),
('c37fab08-54a4-5a3f-8b62-0cc51f893afb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the duration of viability of transfused platelets?', '["5–7 days at 20–24°C","2 weeks","1 day","1 month","Only 2 hours"]'::jsonb, '5–7 days at 20–24°C', 'Platelets are stored at room temperature (20–24°C) with agitation for up to 5–7 days.'),
('48a6b9d7-c2b7-5635-b4cb-45da605f2d56'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the indication for fresh frozen plasma (FFP) transfusion?', '["Increase hemoglobin mediated physiological mechanism","Increase platelet count mediated physiological mechanism","Albumin replacement mediated physiological mechanism","Correction of multiple coagulation factor deficiencies","Volume expansion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Correction of multiple coagulation factor deficiencies', 'FFP contains all coagulation factors and is used for bleeding with INR >1.5 or multiple factor deficiencies.'),
('f66aca39-f491-5336-b51f-18b409f429c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is cryoprecipitate rich in?', '["Factor VIII, vWF, fibrinogen, and factor XIII","All coagulation factors mediated physiological mechanism","Platelets mediated physiological mechanism","RBCs mediated physiological mechanism","Albumin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII, vWF, fibrinogen, and factor XIII', 'Cryoprecipitate is the cold-insoluble fraction of plasma, rich in high-molecular-weight clotting factors.'),
('8712565b-6eb5-5df0-94d7-901e148c45fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which bleeding disorder is characterized by defective platelet adhesion due to vWF deficiency or dysfunction?', '["Hemophilia A","Factor XIII deficiency","Hemophilia B","ITP","von Willebrand disease"]'::jsonb, 'von Willebrand disease', 'vWD is the most common inherited bleeding disorder, impairing platelet adhesion and/or factor VIII carriage.'),
('f3575ab5-e161-5790-8c00-3399343d50d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the inheritance pattern of von Willebrand disease?', '["Autosomal dominant","Polygenic","Mitochondrial","X-linked recessive","Autosomal recessive"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant', 'Type 1 vWD (mild quantitative deficiency) is autosomal dominant; type 3 (severe) is autosomal recessive.'),
('dac3e1d9-5fb2-5056-99ba-2b5bba1b6c53'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What laboratory finding distinguishes hemophilia A from von Willebrand disease?', '["Abnormal PT in hemophilia mediated physiological mechanism","Elevated fibrinogen in vWD mediated physiological mechanism","Abnormal D-dimer in hemophilia mediated physiological mechanism","Low platelet count in hemophilia mediated physiological mechanism","Normal bleeding time and normal platelet function in hemophilia A; prolonged bleeding time in vWD"]'::jsonb, 'Normal bleeding time and normal platelet function in hemophilia A; prolonged bleeding time in vWD', 'Hemophilia A has normal platelet function (normal bleeding time), while vWD impairs platelet adhesion (prolonged bleeding time).'),
('e3cf08e8-e12e-5d75-a366-58e090ac8d32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the characteristic clinical feature of hemophilia?', '["Epistaxis mediated physiological mechanism","Hemarthroses (bleeding into joints) and deep muscle hematomas","Mucocutaneous bleeding mediated physiological mechanism","Petechiae mediated physiological mechanism","Menorrhagia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hemarthroses (bleeding into joints) and deep muscle hematomas', 'Hemophilia A/B typically causes bleeding into joints (hemarthroses) and soft tissues, while mucocutaneous bleeding suggests platelet disorders.'),
('bb30395e-c25a-5611-9349-322e75c611a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which factor replacement is used to treat hemophilia A?', '["FFP","Cryoprecipitate","Factor IX concentrate","vWF concentrate","Recombinant factor VIII"]'::jsonb, 'Recombinant factor VIII', 'Factor VIII replacement (recombinant or plasma-derived) is the mainstay of hemophilia A treatment.'),
('a00887d9-3f00-5c7e-aa66-e8a85f56cf4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the treatment for hemophilia B?', '["Cryoprecipitate","DDAVP","Recombinant factor IX concentrate","Factor VIII concentrate","FFP"]'::jsonb, 'Recombinant factor IX concentrate', 'Factor IX concentrate (recombinant or plasma-derived) is used for hemophilia B (Christmas disease).'),
('060ca856-2b01-5e41-bd16-1ba7ab1e647b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which drug can be used in mild hemophilia A to raise factor VIII levels by releasing endothelial stores?', '["Desmopressin","Tranexamic acid","Vitamin K","Cryoprecipitate","Factor VIII concentrate"]'::jsonb, 'Desmopressin', 'DDAVP releases vWF and factor VIII from endothelial storage sites, useful in mild hemophilia A and some vWD.'),
('d3af470e-4c65-5431-a263-b3f971edba70'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)?', '["Congenital platelet deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Platelet dysfunction mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased platelet production mediated physiological mechanism","von Willebrand disease mediated physiological mechanism","Autoimmune destruction of platelets by antiplatelet antibodies"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune destruction of platelets by antiplatelet antibodies', 'ITP is an acquired disorder where autoantibodies (usually anti-GPIIb-IIIa) opsonize platelets for splenic clearance.'),
('34661531-27d7-590f-b1f1-9ab780e105ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the first-line treatment for ITP?', '["IVIG","Platelet transfusion","Splenectomy","Rituximab","Corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 'Corticosteroids', 'Corticosteroids reduce antibody production and phagocytosis, increasing platelet count in ITP.'),
('e5493216-aa9b-56c6-b7b0-432576a97212'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the characteristic rash of ITP?', '["Telangiectasias","Hemarthrosis","Ecchymoses","Hematomas","Petechiae and purpura"]'::jsonb, 'Petechiae and purpura', 'ITP presents with petechiae (pinpoint non-blanching spots) and purpura due to thrombocytopenia.'),
('88a5810c-05cb-5c29-9eac-9ae78d225805'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the mechanism of DIC?', '["Congenital factor deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Widespread activation of coagulation consuming factors and platelets, with secondary fibrinolysis","Primary hyperfibrinolysis mediated physiological mechanism","Platelet dysfunction mediated physiological mechanism","Vascular fragility mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Widespread activation of coagulation consuming factors and platelets, with secondary fibrinolysis', 'DIC involves systemic coagulation activation, microthrombi, consumption of factors/platelets, and bleeding.'),
('b87a5056-a533-5067-b314-684911990257'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which of the following is a common cause of DIC?', '["Hemophilia","von Willebrand disease","Factor XIII deficiency","Sepsis","ITP"]'::jsonb, 'Sepsis', 'Sepsis is the most common cause of DIC; endotoxin triggers tissue factor expression and widespread coagulation.'),
('38aeb41a-47aa-535a-9003-404da8e69299'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What are the typical laboratory findings in DIC?', '["Prolonged PT/aPTT, low platelets, low fibrinogen, elevated D-dimer","Normal PT/aPTT, low platelets mediated physiological mechanism","Prolonged PT only mediated physiological mechanism","Elevated platelets mediated physiological mechanism","Normal findings mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged PT/aPTT, low platelets, low fibrinogen, elevated D-dimer', 'DIC shows consumption of factors (prolonged PT/aPTT), platelets (thrombocytopenia), fibrinogen, and elevated D-dimer.'),
('d412df57-b353-5e60-98ae-d591396aaa8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the primary management approach for DIC?', '["Platelet transfusion only","Factor VIII concentrate","Treat the underlying cause","FFP only","Heparin therapy"]'::jsonb, 'Treat the underlying cause', 'The cornerstone of DIC management is addressing the triggering condition (e.g., antibiotics for sepsis).'),
('ef480b32-8008-5053-9a81-61cf352a3dc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which bleeding disorder is characterized by a deficiency of factor XI?', '["Dysfibrinogenemia","Factor XIII deficiency","von Willebrand disease","Factor VII deficiency","Hemophilia C"]'::jsonb, 'Hemophilia C', 'Factor XI deficiency (hemophilia C) is autosomal recessive, common in Ashkenazi Jews, causing mild bleeding.'),
('19970578-e5a8-506a-a4bb-4be1b89fb03c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which coagulation factor deficiency leads to poor wound healing and delayed bleeding?', '["Factor VIII deficiency","Factor VII deficiency","Factor IX deficiency","von Willebrand disease","Factor XIII deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Factor XIII deficiency', 'Factor XIII stabilizes the clot; deficiency causes delayed bleeding and impaired wound healing.'),
('212ff00c-ec4b-5f87-8b6f-6ccda14b2b00'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the most sensitive screening test for factor XIII deficiency?', '["Thrombin time","Bleeding time","PT","Clot solubility in 5M urea","aPTT"]'::jsonb, 'Clot solubility in 5M urea', 'Factor XIII-deficient clots are soluble in 5M urea, while normal cross-linked clots remain intact.'),
('9eb347f3-0ad8-5eb5-9230-0983dc8abe8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which acquired coagulation factor inhibitor causes a prolonged aPTT that does not correct on mixing?', '["Protein C deficiency","Lupus anticoagulant","Factor IX inhibitor","vWF inhibitor","Factor VIII inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII inhibitor', 'Acquired factor VIII inhibitors neutralize factor VIII, causing a prolonged aPTT that fails to correct with mixing.'),
('66c322d9-0c06-52fa-a47f-cd1e4b8d34c6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the difference between lupus anticoagulant and factor VIII inhibitor?', '["Both cause bleeding mediated physiological mechanism","Both cause thrombosis mediated physiological mechanism","LA prolongs aPTT but is associated with thrombosis; factor VIII inhibitor prolongs aPTT and cause","LA causes bleeding mediated physiological mechanism","Factor VIII inhibitor causes thrombosis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'LA prolongs aPTT but is associated with thrombosis; factor VIII inhibitor prolongs aPTT and cause', 'LA is an antiphospholipid antibody causing aPTT prolongation but thrombotic risk; factor VIII inhibitor neutralizes FVIII causing bleeding.'),
('fa98b664-0203-5e24-83b3-32938b47e4ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which endothelial function prevents platelet adhesion and aggregation under normal conditions?', '["Release of PGI2 (prostacyclin) and nitric oxide","Expression of P-selectin","Release of thromboxane A2","Release of vWF","Expression of tissue factor"]'::jsonb, 'Release of PGI2 (prostacyclin) and nitric oxide', 'Healthy endothelium releases PGI2 and NO, which inhibit platelet aggregation and promote vasodilation.'),
('21d8bf47-09a5-564b-aedd-0a739627c3cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'What is the treatment for acquired hemophilia A (factor VIII inhibitors)?', '["DDAVP mediated physiological mechanism","Immune suppression (steroids, rituximab) and bypass agents","Vitamin K mediated physiological mechanism","Factor VIII replacement alone mediated physiological mechanism","Platelet transfusion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Immune suppression (steroids, rituximab) and bypass agents', 'Bypass agents (rFVIIa or activated prothrombin complex concentrates) circumvent the inhibitor.'),
('e686867e-1743-5bc8-8904-28a563f107a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which test is used to assess platelet function (aggregation)?', '["aPTT mediated physiological mechanism","PT mediated physiological mechanism","Light transmission aggregometry","Thrombin time mediated physiological mechanism","Bleeding time mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Light transmission aggregometry', 'LTA measures platelet aggregation in response to agonists (ADP, collagen, arachidonic acid, ristocetin).'),
('43cb8ba0-8b70-54df-a011-90c9ddb9b558'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which condition causes impaired platelet aggregation with ristocetin that corrects with added normal plasma?', '["Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Glanzmann thrombasthenia","von Willebrand disease","Storage pool disease","Aspirin therapy"]'::jsonb, 'von Willebrand disease', 'Ristocetin-induced aggregation requires vWF; vWD shows impaired response corrected by normal plasma (vWF source).'),
('adee81c3-e01a-51b6-a132-f71d84c2420f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood and Immunity', 'Which condition shows impaired platelet aggregation with ristocetin that does NOT correct with normal plasma?', '["von Willebrand disease","Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Glanzmann thrombasthenia","Storage pool disease","Afibrinogenemia"]'::jsonb, 'Bernard-Soulier syndrome', 'Bernard-Soulier syndrome lacks the vWF receptor GPIb, so adding normal plasma (vWF) does not restore aggregation.'),
('ae47ee38-129b-56f7-a155-36452dbadd78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which ion is primarily responsible for the plateau phase of the cardiac action potential?', '["Potassium","Sodium","Calcium","Magnesium","Chloride"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium', 'The plateau phase (phase 2) of the cardiac action potential is maintained by L-type calcium channels. Inward calcium current balances outward potassium current, prolonging the action potential and preventing tetanus.'),
('f37e22ef-3311-5029-848a-3f2295de6424'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'What is the normal resting heart rate in an adult?', '["100-120 bpm","120-140 bpm","50-60 bpm","40-50 bpm","60-100 bpm"]'::jsonb, '60-100 bpm', 'Normal adult resting heart rate is 60-100 beats per minute. Athletes may have lower rates (40-60 bpm). Rates >100 bpm is tachycardia, <60 bpm is bradycardia.'),
('fad25338-bff6-503c-96ad-8c6a86cc7ab2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The first heart sound (S1) is produced by:', '["Ventricular filling","Closure of aortic valve","Atrial contraction","Opening of semilunar valves","Closure of mitral and tricuspid valves"]'::jsonb, 'Closure of mitral and tricuspid valves', 'S1 is the ''lub'' sound caused by closure of the atrioventricular (mitral and tricuspid) valves at the beginning of ventricular systole. It is best heard at the apex.'),
('96e71380-1ad1-596b-8cea-0088c3b14347'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The P wave on ECG represents:', '["Ventricular repolarization","Ventricular depolarization","Atrial depolarization","Atrial repolarization","AV nodal delay"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial depolarization', 'The P wave represents atrial depolarization. It is the first wave of the cardiac cycle, normally upright in leads I, II, aVF, and V4-V6. Duration <120 ms.'),
('bff82904-ecbd-5de5-96ce-2973b4998070'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following has the fastest inherent rate of spontaneous depolarization?', '["Bundle of His","Purkinje fibers","AV node","SA node","Ventricular muscle"]'::jsonb, 'SA node', 'The SA node has the fastest intrinsic rate (60-100/min), making it the primary pacemaker. AV node rate is 40-60/min. Purkinje fibers have the slowest rate (15-40/min).'),
('9902c104-eb5c-595b-859c-bbad5ce647c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The QRS complex on ECG represents:', '["Septal depolarization only","Ventricular repolarization","Atrial repolarization","Ventricular depolarization","Atrial depolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular depolarization', 'QRS complex (duration <120 ms) represents ventricular depolarization. It is the most prominent deflection. Wide QRS (>120 ms) indicates bundle branch block or ventricular origin.'),
('043ce7bc-b516-5804-a872-64239ec1d1ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is true about the cardiac action potential in the SA node?', '["Phase 0 is due to L-type Ca2+ channels","Fast upstroke due to Na+ channels","Plateau phase is prominent","Resting potential is -90 mV","No phase 4 depolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Phase 0 is due to L-type Ca2+ channels', 'SA node action potential has a slow upstroke (phase 0) mediated by L-type Ca2+ channels, not fast Na+ channels. It has spontaneous phase 4 depolarization (pacemaker potential).'),
('c4182fa9-99c5-51cc-b31a-cdfa60fe548a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The second heart sound (S2) is produced by:', '["Closure of semilunar valves","Ventricular contraction","Atrial contraction","Closure of AV valves","Opening of AV valves"]'::jsonb, 'Closure of semilunar valves', 'S2 is the ''dub'' sound caused by closure of the aortic and pulmonary semilunar valves at the beginning of ventricular diastole. It splits physiologically during inspiration.'),
('ef57121f-d117-506b-8afd-414b5acbbaab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the primary determinant of myocardial oxygen consumption?', '["Contractility","All of the above","Heart rate","Wall tension","Base metabolism only"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Myocardial O2 consumption is determined by heart rate, contractility (inotropic state), and wall tension (LaPlace''s law: tension = pressure × radius). These three are the major determinants.'),
('7df43f9f-8473-5439-8028-78af18e43723'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The PR interval on ECG normally measures:', '["<80 ms",">300 ms","80-120 ms","120-200 ms","200-300 ms"]'::jsonb, '120-200 ms', 'Normal PR interval is 120-200 ms (3-5 small squares). It represents AV conduction time. Prolonged PR (>200 ms) is first-degree AV block. Short PR (<120 ms) may indicate pre-excitation (WPW).'),
('554ed464-dbda-5232-bf9f-bcce83d68cff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following ECG leads are bipolar limb leads?', '["All leads","V1-V6","I, II, III","Precordial leads","aVR, aVL, aVF"]'::jsonb, 'I, II, III', 'Standard bipolar limb leads are I (LA-RA), II (LL-RA), and III (LL-LA). aVR, aVL, aVF are augmented unipolar limb leads. V1-V6 are precordial leads.'),
('21e99dd4-2758-51d4-b521-db907514e8b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Q-T interval represents:', '["Ventricular systole","AV conduction time","Ventricular repolarization only","Ventricular depolarization","Atrial depolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular systole', 'QT interval (corrected for heart rate, QTc) represents total ventricular electrical activity - from QRS onset to T wave end. Normal QTc <440 ms in males, <460 ms in females.'),
('6ca1c13c-966a-56d2-9a15-b0785b4c2cd0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'What is the effect of parasympathetic stimulation on the heart?', '["Increased contractility","Positive chronotropic","Negative chronotropic","Positive inotropic","Increased conduction velocity"]'::jsonb, 'Negative chronotropic', 'Parasympathetic (vagal) stimulation has a negative chronotropic effect (decreases heart rate) via M2 receptors. It has minimal direct effect on ventricular contractility.'),
('d03770c7-0143-5ca5-975a-e760c1546068'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a potent vasodilator produced by the endothelium?', '["Angiotensin II","Thromboxane A2","Nitric oxide","Catecholamines","Endothelin"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide', 'Nitric oxide (endothelium-derived relaxing factor) is produced by eNOS from L-arginine. It diffuses to smooth muscle, activates guanylyl cyclase, increasing cGMP and causing vasodilation.'),
('45ee6423-6f82-550b-a20f-a600bf800df0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Frank-Starling mechanism relates to:', '["Ventricular pressure and wall thickness","End-diastolic volume and stroke volume","Venous return and heart rate","Heart rate and cardiac output","Afterload and contractility"]'::jsonb, 'End-diastolic volume and stroke volume', 'Frank-Starling law: within limits, increased ventricular end-diastolic volume (preload) increases the force of contraction and stroke volume due to optimal actin-myosin overlap.'),
('ee9536ff-b47e-5df9-a87d-09073233cf14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following leads is most useful for detecting ischemia in the anterior wall of the left ventricle?', '["V3-V4","II, III, aVF","V5-V6","I, aVL","V1-V2"]'::jsonb, 'V3-V4', 'Precordial leads V3-V4 are optimal for detecting anterior wall ischemia. V1-V2 are for septal, V5-V6 for lateral, II/III/aVF for inferior, and I/aVL for high lateral.'),
('dc665857-608b-5008-b6cf-4911eb673390'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The dicrotic notch on the aortic pressure curve represents:', '["Opening of the aortic valve","Atrial contraction","Beginning of ventricular systole","Mitral valve closure","Closure of the aortic valve"]'::jsonb, 'Closure of the aortic valve', 'The dicrotic notch (incisura) is a brief rise in aortic pressure caused by aortic valve closure at the beginning of diastole. It marks the end of ventricular ejection.'),
('3e50dfad-f31c-5ad8-867d-0a86fcefaa25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the correct formula for cardiac output?', '["CO = (SBP-DBP)/HR","CO = BP × HR","CO = HR × SVR","CO = HR × SV","CO = SV/HR"]'::jsonb, 'CO = HR × SV', 'Cardiac output (CO) = Heart rate (HR) × Stroke volume (SV). Normal CO is 4-8 L/min. Cardiac index = CO/body surface area (normal 2.5-4 L/min/m²).'),
('da40ad16-f327-5de4-9059-b8dce3207273'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In the normal cardiac cycle, the highest pressure in the left ventricle during systole reaches approximately:', '["120 mmHg","60 mmHg","80 mmHg","160 mmHg","100 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '120 mmHg', 'Normal left ventricular systolic pressure reaches about 120 mmHg (equal to systolic BP). Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (LVEDP) is about 5-12 mmHg.'),
('f77527f2-53ca-5a45-8a4e-7bc31e44dcee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The T wave inversion in leads with upright QRS may indicate:', '["Normal variant","Myocardial ischemia or infarction","Digoxin effect","Hyperkalemia","Ventricular hypertrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial ischemia or infarction', 'T wave inversion (in leads with dominant R waves) can indicate myocardial ischemia, infarction, or strain. However, T wave inversion in leads III, aVL, and V1-V2 can be normal variants.'),
('a7cacf34-bba2-5dd3-b613-50e6ae357cbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following increases stroke volume the most during exercise?', '["Decreased preload","Increased venous return","Increased heart rate","Increased contractility","Decreased afterload"]'::jsonb, 'Increased contractility', 'During exercise, increased contractility (sympathetic stimulation and circulating catecholamines) is the primary mechanism increasing stroke volume, along with increased venous return.'),
('d6837563-f61c-5605-b599-4cc9403c5a18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) approximates:', '["Right ventricular pressure","Pulmonary artery pressure","Left atrial pressure","Right atrial pressure","Systemic arterial pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Left atrial pressure', 'PCWP (measured by Swan-Ganz catheter) approximates left atrial pressure and left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (LVEDP), provided no pulmonary venous obstruction exists.'),
('3975f63d-8279-5e5b-a715-63f4c1ea1a8e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of left bundle branch block?', '["Cardiomyopathy","Aortic stenosis","All of the above","Coronary artery disease","Hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'LBBB can result from CAD, hypertension, aortic stenosis, cardiomyopathy, and other conditions causing LV structural disease. It can also occur in apparently normal hearts.'),
('158d59ae-3b8a-53b1-b53c-aabcf5cd148e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The term ''preload'' refers to:', '["The degree of stretch of the ventricular muscle at end-diastole","The heart rate","The resistance against which the heart pumps","The pressure in the aorta","The thickness of the ventricular wall"]'::jsonb, 'The degree of stretch of the ventricular muscle at end-diastole', 'Preload is the end-diastolic volume that stretches the ventricular myocytes. It determines the initial sarcomere length and, via Frank-Starling, the force of contraction.'),
('ba9f4f5c-33d9-572e-a661-91db56fc4f82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which ECG finding is characteristic of acute pericarditis?', '["ST depression","ST elevation with upward concavity","Left axis deviation","T wave inversion","Pathological Q waves"]'::jsonb, 'ST elevation with upward concavity', 'Acute pericarditis shows diffuse ST elevation with upward concavity (saddle-shaped) and PR depression. Unlike MI, the ST elevation is not regional and Q waves are absent.'),
('ac4c8353-c226-53f2-8a10-7a7a3e7a34a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''a'' wave in the jugular venous pulse is caused by:', '["Aortic pulsation","Atrial contraction","Ventricular contraction","Tricuspid regurgitation","Ventricular filling"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial contraction', 'The ''a'' wave in JVP is produced by right atrial contraction. It is followed by the ''c'' wave (ventricular contraction pushing tricuspid into atrium) and ''v'' wave (atrial filling).'),
('611af952-4a44-52e5-8671-c2dfddeb30c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most sensitive indicator of myocardial infarction?', '["AST","Troponin I","CK-MB","Myoglobin","LDH"]'::jsonb, 'Troponin I', 'Troponin I (or T) is the most sensitive and specific cardiac biomarker. It rises within 3-6 hours, peaks at 12-24 hours, and remains elevated for 7-14 days. It can detect even micro-infarcts.'),
('980717f5-8ded-580d-bfb5-6e1cfb9c4f08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In which condition would you expect to see a wide and split S2 on expiration?', '["None of the above","Atrial septal defect","Right bundle branch block","Pulmonary stenosis","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Normally, S2 splits during inspiration and is single during expiration. Wide fixed splitting is characteristic of ASD. Wide splitting with delayed P2 occurs in RBBB and pulmonary stenosis.'),
('14a8b18f-1960-5bf2-b105-f00013c711fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The baroreceptor reflex in response to increased blood pressure results in:', '["Increased heart rate and increased contractility","Decreased heart rate and decreased peripheral resistance","Decreased heart rate and increased contractility","Increased heart rate and decreased peripheral resistance","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased heart rate and decreased peripheral resistance', 'Baroreceptors (carotid sinus, aortic arch) sense increased BP → increased afferent firing → increased vagal efferent and decreased sympathetic efferent → decreased HR and peripheral resistance → BP normalizes.'),
('d8e45d44-3ed4-53f2-b150-1140104eb151'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the primary cause of the increase in blood pressure during isometric exercise?', '["Decreased peripheral resistance","Increased stroke volume","Compression of blood vessels by contracting muscles","Increased venous return","Increased heart rate"]'::jsonb, 'Compression of blood vessels by contracting muscles', 'During isometric exercise, sustained muscle contraction compresses blood vessels, increasing peripheral resistance. Heart rate and cardiac output also increase,resulting in elevated BP.'),
('7ed76daa-47fa-52fd-a811-be7eefc45815'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'A right axis deviation on ECG is defined as the QRS axis being:', '["Between +90° and +180°","Between -30° and -90°","Between -30° and 0°","Between 0° and +90°","Between -90° and -180°"]'::jsonb, 'Between +90° and +180°', 'Right axis deviation is QRS axis between +90° and +180°. Causes include RVH, RBBB, COPD, and lateral wall MI. Left axis deviation is between -30° and -90°.'),
('96db398b-7569-579d-84b8-b7febae37076'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of the cardiac action potential in ventricular myocytes?', '["Slow response","Stable resting potential at -90 mV","Phase 0 due to Ca2+ influx","Spontaneous phase 4 depolarization","No plateau phase"]'::jsonb, 'Stable resting potential at -90 mV', 'Ventricular myocytes have a stable resting potential of -90 mV (determined by IK1). They exhibit fast response action potentials (phase 0: Na+ influx) with a prominent plateau (phase 2).'),
('51e62ac4-bceb-5ff7-9d9a-aa4ce4b2f6df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of digitalis on the heart includes:', '["Both positive inotropic and negative chronotropic","Negative chronotropic effect only","Positive inotropic effect","Negative inotropic effect","Decreased AV conduction"]'::jsonb, 'Both positive inotropic and negative chronotropic', 'Digitalis increases contractility (positive inotropy by inhibiting Na+/K+ ATPase, increasing intracellular Ca2+) and at higher doses slows AV conduction (negative dromotropic).'),
('68eddaf3-9a58-5e7e-83e9-b699bceeb8af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is consistent with hyperkalemia on ECG?', '["Wide QRS","Loss of P wave","All of the above","Sine wave pattern","Peaked T waves"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Hyperkalemia causes progressive ECG changes: tall peaked T waves (early), then widened QRS, loss of P wave, and eventually sine wave pattern leading to ventricular fibrillation/asystole.'),
('26284a81-2bb2-5c98-b551-87133261bf75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The central venous pressure (CVP) is normally:', '["2-8 mmHg","20-25 mmHg","15-20 mmHg","0-2 mmHg","10-15 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '2-8 mmHg', 'Normal CVP (right atrial pressure) is 2-8 mmHg (or 4-12 cm H2O). It reflects right ventricular preload and is measured via a central venous catheter.'),
('f6690f4d-ccf9-5f1e-b8b0-a1e21a6989f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The therapeutic effect of ACE inhibitors in heart failure is due to:', '["Reduced afterload and reduced preload","Increased contractility","Increased preload","Increased heart rate","Increased sodium retention"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced afterload and reduced preload', 'ACE inhibitors reduce afterload (vasodilation from decreased Angiotensin II) and reduce preload (decreased aldosterone → decreased fluid retention). This improves cardiac output in heart failure.'),
('12c6dd83-dc8d-51c5-8303-f277eb4ca07d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of constrictive pericarditis?', '["Prominent y descent","All of the above","Pericardial knock","Kussmaul sign","Low voltage QRS"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Constrictive pericarditis features: Kussmaul sign (↑ JVP on inspiration), prominent y descent, pericardial knock (early diastolic sound), low QRS voltage, and possible calcification on imaging.'),
('2b9087b8-feed-5507-8b39-efedcb16e1f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following ECG leads looks at the inferior wall of the heart?', '["V5-V6","I and aVL","II, III, aVF","V1-V3","V3-V4"]'::jsonb, 'II, III, aVF', 'Inferior wall (diaphragmatic surface) is best seen in leads II, III, and aVF. Lateral wall: I, aVL, V5-V6. Septal: V1-V2. Anterior: V3-V4. Posterior: V1-V2 (reciprocal).'),
('56b484ea-9b42-53ee-8db2-7b0f37791304'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'What is the approximate ejection fraction of a normal heart?', '["60%","40%","80%","50%","30%"]'::jsonb, '60%', 'Normal LV ejection fraction is approximately 55-65% (mean ~60%). EF = (EDV - ESV) / EDV × 100. EF <40% indicates systolic dysfunction.'),
('450f79c4-4857-5a02-aa8f-d8155ea95e55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following describes the mechanism of calcium-induced calcium release (CICR) in cardiac muscle?', '["IP3 activates SR Ca2+ release mediated physiological mechanism","NCX reverses and brings Ca2+ in mediated physiological mechanism","Mechanical stretch opens SR channels mediated physiological mechanism","Ca2+ influx through L-type channels triggers SR Ca2+ release via ryanodine receptors","Ca2+ from SR activates DHP receptors mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ influx through L-type channels triggers SR Ca2+ release via ryanodine receptors', 'In cardiac muscle, L-type Ca2+ channel current triggers a larger release of Ca2+ from the SR via ryanodine receptors (RyR2). This amplifies the Ca2+ signal for contraction.'),
('9c074249-cfbd-525f-88b9-85ba33921bbd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Purkinje fibers are characterized by:', '["Absence of gap junctions","Rapid conduction","Skeletal muscle type","No automaticity","Slow conduction"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid conduction', 'Purkinje fibers have the fastest conduction velocity (2-4 m/s) in the heart, enabling rapid spread of depolarization to ventricular muscle. They also have latent pacemaker activity.'),
('2fb9f9d9-6ab9-5aef-9d86-40c00da96ee0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of right ventricular failure?', '["Pulmonary embolism","Left ventricular failure","COPD","Pulmonary hypertension","Tricuspid regurgitation"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricular failure', 'Left ventricular failure is the most common cause of right ventricular failure (cor pulmonale refers specifically to RV failure from lung disease). Increased pulmonary pressures from LV failure overload the RV.'),
('1167d8ee-d0ed-5c94-ad4e-8d479300236b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The end-systolic volume (ESV) of the left ventricle is normally about:', '["70 mL","120 mL","45 mL","95 mL","20 mL"]'::jsonb, '45 mL', 'Normal LVESV is approximately 40-50 mL. With EDV of ~120 mL, stroke volume is ~70 mL and EF ~60%. Increased ESV indicates systolic dysfunction.'),
('8417411d-7a32-5922-9054-b03aee9aa708'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following ECG changes is seen in hypokalemia?', '["Peaked T waves","U waves","Wide QRS","ST elevation","P pulmonale"]'::jsonb, 'U waves', 'Hypokalemia causes ST depression, T wave flattening/inversion, and prominent U waves. U waves are best seen in the precordial leads (V3-V4). Severe hypokalemia can cause arrhythmias.'),
('17aa097c-4d8c-575f-aa83-93056a18b959'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Bainbridge reflex is:', '["A reflex from the aortic arch","An increase in HR due to increased venous return","A chemoreceptor reflex","A decrease in HR due to increased BP","A reflex from the carotid body"]'::jsonb, 'An increase in HR due to increased venous return', 'The Bainbridge reflex: increased venous return stretches the right atrium, activating atrial stretch receptors, which increase heart rate via increased sympathetic activity. It opposes the baroreflex.'),
('b5e40743-86c2-55c1-b2d2-db249fc74aaa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following factors is NOT a determinant of myocardial oxygen demand?', '["Wall tension","Heart rate","Contractility","Coronary artery patency","Preload"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary artery patency', 'Myocardial O2 demand is determined by HR, contractility, and wall tension (preload and afterload). Coronary artery patency determines O2 supply, not demand.'),
('421d97c6-3214-51c9-950c-61db49371b23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal pressure in the right atrium (CVP) is approximately:', '["15-20 mmHg","0-2 mmHg","-2 to 0 mmHg","2-8 mmHg","10-15 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '2-8 mmHg', 'Normal CVP is 2-8 mmHg. It is low in hypovolemia and elevated in right heart failure, fluid overload, pericardial tamponade, and constrictive pericarditis.'),
('cc39ae29-f08e-5341-b343-ba0d0f9480c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a late sign of cardiac tamponade?', '["Hypotension with JVD","Kussmaul sign","Pulsus paradoxus","Muffled heart sounds","Electrical alternans on ECG"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotension with JVD', 'Beck''s triad (hypotension, muffled heart sounds, JVD) is a late sign of cardiac tamponade. Pulsus paradoxus and electrical alternans are also characteristic but may appear earlier.'),
('4b3fd541-de9a-59f9-9460-4b113a418708'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Lead II of the ECG looks at the heart from which angle?', '["+60°","+120°","+90°","0°","-30°"]'::jsonb, '+60°', 'Lead II is oriented at +60° in the frontal plane (RA to LL). It is the best lead for seeing the P wave and is commonly used for rhythm monitoring.'),
('0ea7521f-8080-5b24-b785-4f6dab21e729'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of the fetal circulation?', '["Ductus arteriosus connects pulmonary artery to aorta","None of the above","All of the above","Blood flows from right atrium to left atrium via foramen ovale","Ductus venosus bypasses the liver"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All three shunts are characteristic of fetal circulation: foramen ovale (RA→LA), ductus arteriosus (pulmonary artery→descending aorta), and ductus venosus (umbilical vein→IVC).'),
('175ace4f-f585-5bec-9ca3-1925448bdbe6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''lub-dub'' heart sounds S1 and S2 correspond to:', '["Both are semilunar valve closure","S1: Aortic closure, S2: Mitral closure","S1: Semilunar closure, S2: AV closure","Both are AV valve closure","S1: AV closure, S2: Semilunar closure"]'::jsonb, 'S1: AV closure, S2: Semilunar closure', 'S1 (lub) is AV valve closure at start of systole. S2 (dub) is semilunar valve closure at start of diastole. S3 and S4 are additional sounds in diastole.'),
('efc76611-6a20-5387-a2bd-b1a47cb7dd8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In which of the following conditions would you expect to see a prominent S3 heart sound?', '["Mitral stenosis","Constrictive pericarditis","Atrial fibrillation","Heart failure with volume overload","Aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure with volume overload', 'S3 (ventricular gallop) occurs in conditions with rapid ventricular filling and high filling pressures, such as heart failure, volume overload, and dilated cardiomyopathy.'),
('b9bcfa73-0fcb-5ee8-b97c-bb1c1d2c59e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal blood pressure in the pulmonary artery is approximately:', '["80/40 mmHg","120/80 mmHg","25/10 mmHg","150/90 mmHg","50/20 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '25/10 mmHg', 'Normal pulmonary artery pressure is ~25/10 mmHg (mean ~15 mmHg). Pulmonary hypertension is defined as mean PAP >20-25 mmHg at rest.'),
('0a76552c-b640-5478-b333-2f2f03331ac4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following describes pulsus paradoxus?', '["Decrease in BP during expiration","Widening of pulse pressure","Narrowing of pulse pressure","Increase in BP during inspiration","Exaggerated decrease in BP during inspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Exaggerated decrease in BP during inspiration', 'Pulsus paradoxus is an exaggerated (>10 mmHg) decrease in systolic BP during inspiration. It occurs in cardiac tamponade, severe asthma/COPD, and constrictive pericarditis.'),
('cf18722e-31d1-5d69-8aea-fcdd5b90e1a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The maximum heart rate achievable during exercise is approximately:', '["180 - age","150 - age","250 - age","200 - age","220 - age"]'::jsonb, '220 - age', 'Maximum HR ≈ 220 - age (for adults). For a 40-year-old, max HR ≈ 180 bpm. Target HR for moderate exercise is 50-70% of maximum.'),
('f28376b5-3ab9-53ea-9ca6-4ce39c3e8457'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of pathological Q waves on ECG?', '["All of the above","Myocardial infarction","Normal variant","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Myocarditis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Pathological Q waves (>0.04 s wide or >1/3 of QRS height) occur in MI (transmural), HCM (septal), myocarditis, and can also be normal variants in certain leads.'),
('1f74a1b9-0328-5331-a38b-2e79eeff3a15'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'This means AV conduction time is:', '["Decreased","Increased","Variable","Normal","Absent"]'::jsonb, 'Normal', 'Normal PR interval is 120-200 ms. At 160 ms, AV conduction is normal. PR >200 ms = first-degree AV block. PR <120 ms may indicate pre-excitation (WPW pattern).'),
('34748591-feea-5e51-8110-656447d60d87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common arrhythmia in clinical practice?', '["Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus bradycardia","Atrial flutter","Premature ventricular contractions","Atrial fibrillation"]'::jsonb, 'Premature ventricular contractions', 'PVCs are the most common arrhythmia, occurring in most people at some point. Atrial fibrillation is the most common sustained arrhythmia requiring treatment.'),
('bf21d6c9-1559-5e6b-90d9-038866114ede'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Valsalva maneuver in a normal person causes:', '["Increase in BP during strain phase","Increase in HR during strain phase","No change in BP","Decrease in BP during strain phase","Both B and D"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and D', 'During Valsalva strain, venous return decreases → BP falls → HR increases (reflex). After release, BP overshoots → HR decreases. This tests autonomic function.'),
('0175f874-1f6d-5eb5-ae3b-5a010d36be10'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following characterizes the chronic phase of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?', '["Activation of RAAS","Fluid retention","All of the above","Sympathetic nervous system activation","Ventricular remodeling"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Chronic HFrEF involves compensatory mechanisms: RAAS activation (→ fluid retention, vasoconstriction), sympathetic activation (→ increased HR, contractility), and ventricular remodeling (→ dilation, hypertrophy).'),
('829720bc-658b-5ca7-94a8-75bc386d1a05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The coronary artery that supplies the SA node in most people is:', '["Posterior descending","Left main coronary","Left anterior descending","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery', 'In ~60% of people, the SA node is supplied by the right coronary artery (SA nodal artery). The AV node is supplied by the RCA in ~80% (right-dominant circulation).'),
('a8c6d436-8f62-5b7b-848b-3ba6912ca461'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following ECG changes is most specific for acute myocardial infarction?', '["T inversion","ST elevation","ST depression","Peaked T waves","Pathological Q waves"]'::jsonb, 'ST elevation', 'ST elevation (concave downward) is the most specific early sign of acute transmural MI. New pathological Q waves indicate completed infarction (necrosis).'),
('e4ec2ee0-ad25-5843-a269-6ee6e128fb23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal duration of the QRS complex is:', '["<120 ms","<80 ms","<60 ms","<140 ms","<100 ms"]'::jsonb, '<120 ms', 'Normal QRS duration is 80-120 ms (2-3 small squares). Wide QRS (>120 ms) indicates bundle branch block, ventricular rhythm, or pre-excitation.'),
('d5b49b24-1f17-5f5f-963a-d8a3a9adcb3e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a condition that decreases stroke volume?', '["Increased preload","Decreased heart rate","Increased venous return","Decreased contractility","Decreased afterload"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased contractility', 'Decreased contractility (negative inotropy) reduces stroke volume by impairing the heart''s ability to eject blood. Causes include myocardial ischemia, heart failure, and certain drugs.'),
('81325607-e6f9-5dc0-b789-14ff72efb639'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The formula for mean arterial pressure (MAP) is:', '["DBP + 1/3","HR × PP","SBP - DBP","SBP + DBP/2","SBP/DBP"]'::jsonb, 'DBP + 1/3', 'MAP = Diastolic BP + 1/3 of Pulse Pressure (SBP - DBP). Example: BP 120/80 → MAP = 80 + 13.3 = 93.3 mmHg. MAP is the average pressure driving blood flow.'),
('a13e5721-80b6-5667-80c7-4fa21df9dfaa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In which part of the cardiac cycle is coronary blood flow to the left ventricle maximal?', '["Late diastole","Atrial contraction","Early diastole","Systole","Mid-systole"]'::jsonb, 'Early diastole', 'Left coronary blood flow is maximal in early diastole. During systole, ventricular contraction compresses intramyocardial vessels, reducing flow. Right coronary flow is less affected by systole.'),
('561c6428-42cf-531e-9ac4-bb36a4d045e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most likely rhythm in a patient with no P waves and an irregularly irregular ventricular rate?', '["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation","Wandering pacemaker","Sinus arrhythmia"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial fibrillation', 'Atrial fibrillation is characterized by: absent P waves (fibrillatory f waves), irregularly irregular R-R intervals, and variable ventricular rate depending on AV conduction.'),
('ba8659e9-6624-543f-a509-f48ba278a5b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''c'' wave in the jugular venous pulse corresponds to:', '["Aortic pulsation transmitted to jugular","Right ventricular contraction pushing tricuspid valve into atrium","Atrial filling against closed tricuspid","Tricuspid regurgitation","Right atrial contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricular contraction pushing tricuspid valve into atrium', 'The ''c'' wave (or CV wave) in JVP is caused by the tricuspid valve bulging into the right atrium during right ventricular isovolumetric contraction.'),
('ea3b7e5d-0c45-514f-a449-8c49925692d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of left atrial enlargement?', '["Atrial fibrillation","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral stenosis', 'Mitral stenosis causes left atrial pressure overload, leading to left atrial enlargement (the most common cause). Other causes include mitral regurgitation and LV diastolic dysfunction.'),
('a587d031-62c8-5d5a-ae71-2f1e8e8904fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of norepinephrine on the heart is primarily:', '["Negative chronotropic and positive inotropic","Negative chronotropic and negative inotropic","No significant effect","Positive chronotropic and positive inotropic","Positive chronotropic and negative inotropic"]'::jsonb, 'Positive chronotropic and positive inotropic', 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic neurotransmitter) acts on β1-adrenergic receptors to increase HR (positive chronotropy) and contractility (positive inotropy), and also causes vasoconstriction (α1).'),
('4a19778e-e54c-557d-8cbf-3488af012003'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of third-degree (complete) AV block?', '["Variable PR interval","Atria and ventricles beat independently","PR interval progressively lengthens","PR interval prolongation","Dropped QRS complexes"]'::jsonb, 'Atria and ventricles beat independently', 'Complete AV block: no conduction between atria and ventricles. Atria are controlled by SA node, ventricles by an escape pacemaker (usually junctional or ventricular with rate 30-50/min).'),
('8b6f7cc7-4d5a-5bc1-a53b-879300be1402'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (LVEDP) is normally:', '["4-12 mmHg","15-20 mmHg","2-4 mmHg","35-40 mmHg","25-30 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '4-12 mmHg', 'Normal LVEDP is 4-12 mmHg. Elevated LVEDP indicates left ventricular diastolic dysfunction or volume overload. It is a major determinant of pulmonary capillary wedge pressure.'),
('b18ff436-790f-5b7f-8d5c-08a30e75394b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the mechanism of action of beta-blockers in heart failure?', '["Increase contractility mediated physiological mechanism","Block calcium channels mediated physiological mechanism","Increase renin release mediated physiological mechanism","Increase preload mediated physiological mechanism","Reduce heart rate and myocardial oxygen demand"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce heart rate and myocardial oxygen demand', 'Beta-blockers reduce HR, contractility, and BP, decreasing myocardial O2 demand. They also block the harmful effects of chronic sympathetic activation, improve LV remodeling, and reduce mortality in HF.'),
('3361c002-3370-5ea4-a0dc-273414754618'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The P wave in lead V1 is normally:', '["Inverted","Notched","Biphasic","Upright","Peaked"]'::jsonb, 'Biphasic', 'In V1, the P wave is often biphasic: the initial positive deflection (right atrial activation) followed by negative deflection (left atrial activation). A prominent negative terminal component suggests left atrial enlargement.'),
('15643eac-c93e-593c-b38a-848e13219df0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of supraventricular tachycardia?', '["Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","Atrial flutter","All of the above","Atrial fibrillation","Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All listed are SVTs (originating above the ventricles). AVNRT is the most common paroxysmal SVT. WPW involves an accessory pathway causing pre-excitation and reentrant tachycardia.'),
('96c451a2-df3c-575d-9c05-ccdc709f06e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The cardiac output in a healthy adult at rest is approximately:', '["12-16 L/min","2-3 L/min","16-20 L/min","8-12 L/min","4-8 L/min"]'::jsonb, '4-8 L/min', 'Normal cardiac output is 4-8 L/min at rest. Cardiac index (CO/BSA) is 2.5-4 L/min/m². CO increases 4-5 fold during maximal exercise.'),
('765d3d89-2e91-5d10-8ca3-47c9d6530cfa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of the normal cardiac action potential in a ventricular myocyte?', '["Resting potential -50 mV","No plateau phase","Rising phase due to Ca2+ influx","Duration 1-2 ms","Duration 200-400 ms"]'::jsonb, 'Duration 200-400 ms', 'Ventricular action potential duration is 200-400 ms (much longer than skeletal muscle), with phases 0-4. The long plateau (phase 2, ~200 ms) prevents tetanic contraction.'),
('4e31bfa8-2986-521b-99ab-41aae6c0fdb8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''Wenckebach phenomenon'' (Mobitz type I) is characterized by:', '["Fixed PR interval with dropped beats","No relationship between P and QRS","Variable PP interval","Constant PR interval","Progressive PR prolongation followed by a dropped QRS"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive PR prolongation followed by a dropped QRS', 'Wenckebach (Mobitz I) is progressive PR prolongation until a QRS is dropped. It usually occurs in the AV node and is often benign. Mobitz II has fixed PR with dropped beats and is more serious.'),
('809e4d5f-6a5a-56ee-92dd-f6ffa39fe5db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following contributes to the regulation of coronary blood flow?', '["Nitric oxide","Adenosine","Sympathetic nervous system","All of the above","Local myocardial O2 levels"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Coronary flow is regulated by metabolic factors (adenosine, O2, CO2), endothelial factors (NO, prostacyclin), autonomic nerves (sympathetic vasoconstriction, but metabolic factors override), and myogenic mechanisms.'),
('6965b9af-8214-54bf-a64c-603825050987'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal aortic valve area is approximately:', '["2.5-3.5 cm²","4.0-5.0 cm²","1.0-1.5 cm²","5.0-6.0 cm²","0.5-1.0 cm²"]'::jsonb, '2.5-3.5 cm²', 'Normal aortic valve area is 2.5-3.5 cm². Severe aortic stenosis is defined as area <1.0 cm² (or <0.6 cm²/m²). Aortic valve pressure gradient correlates with severity.'),
('f502d1c4-e8d3-5042-844a-c37b56dddf4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of mitral stenosis?', '["Atrial fibrillation","Opening snap","All of the above","Mid-diastolic murmur","Loud S1"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Mitral stenosis features: opening snap (OS) after S2, mid-diastolic rumble with presystolic accentuation, loud S1, and AF due to left atrial enlargement and pressure overload.'),
('93fda1d4-a0a0-5027-9c60-f4f9b8068fd2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The atrial kick contributes what percentage of ventricular filling?', '["5%","30-40%","10%","50%","15-25%"]'::jsonb, '15-25%', 'Atrial contraction (atrial kick) contributes about 15-25% of ventricular end-diastolic volume. This becomes more important in conditions with reduced ventricular compliance (LVH, HCM, aortic stenosis).'),
('cb45e6a1-6dd6-5cd7-8e62-2884b204ea6b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'A patient with an ECG showing left axis deviation, QS waves in V1-V3, and poor R wave progression likely has:', '["Left ventricular hypertrophy","Pulmonary embolism","Inferior wall MI","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Anterior wall MI"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior wall MI', 'Poor R wave progression with QS complexes in V1-V3 suggests anterior/septal MI. Left axis deviation also supports anterior MI involving the left anterior descending artery territory.'),
('4c99e770-6be2-56eb-8318-0a42717e333a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension?', '["COPD","Sleep apnea","Left heart disease","Pulmonary arterial hypertension","Pulmonary embolism"]'::jsonb, 'Left heart disease', 'Left heart disease (LV failure, mitral valve disease) is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension (Group 2 PH). It is due to passive backward transmission of elevated left-sided pressures.'),
('d765b690-010d-5b85-859c-e219855130d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The dromotropic effect refers to changes in:', '["Refractoriness","Heart rate","Conduction velocity","Automaticity","Contractility"]'::jsonb, 'Conduction velocity', 'Dromotropic refers to conduction velocity (especially through the AV node). Negative dromotropic agents (e.g., beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, digoxin) slow AV conduction.'),
('0086940f-6312-59cc-894e-58503081c4e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal cerebral blood flow is approximately:', '["500 mL/min","200 mL/min","1000 mL/min","1500 mL/min","750 mL/min"]'::jsonb, '750 mL/min', 'Cerebral blood flow is ~750 mL/min (15% of cardiac output, ~50 mL/100g/min). It is maintained constant by autoregulation between MAP of 50-150 mmHg.'),
('ca22fbbd-6d2f-5869-81e3-09750915a477'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following ECG leads is most likely to show the P wave most clearly?', '["V6","Lead II","Lead I","aVR","V1"]'::jsonb, 'Lead II', 'Lead II has the most prominent P wave because its orientation (+60°) is best aligned with the direction of atrial depolarization (SA node to AV node, which is right superior to left inferior).'),
('c9564f7c-ad2f-54e8-82e8-a4054c07a35c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The mechanism of action of adenosine in treating SVT is:', '["Blockade of AV nodal conduction via A1 receptors","Blockade of beta receptors","Blockade of sodium channels","Blockade of calcium channels","Enhancement of vagal tone"]'::jsonb, 'Blockade of AV nodal conduction via A1 receptors', 'Adenosine activates A1 receptors in the AV node, slowing conduction and terminating reentrant SVTs involving the AV node. It has a very short half-life (<10 seconds).'),
('3194653f-2923-59d1-a2fd-f3cbe443c6f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a major determinant of afterload for the left ventricle?', '["Aortic impedance and peripheral resistance","Atrial pressure mediated physiological mechanism","LV compliance mediated physiological mechanism","Heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Venous return mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic impedance and peripheral resistance', 'Afterload is the load against which the ventricle must eject blood. It is primarily determined by aortic impedance (MAP, arterial compliance) and systemic vascular resistance (SVR).'),
('6621cb84-f45a-5f07-b7b7-d58ff3607a99'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In cardiogenic shock, which of the following hemodynamic profiles is expected?', '["High CO, high PCWP, low SVR","Normal CO, high PCWP, high SVR","Low CO, high PCWP, high SVR","Low CO, low PCWP, low SVR","High CO, low PCWP, high SVR"]'::jsonb, 'Low CO, high PCWP, high SVR', 'Cardiogenic shock: low cardiac output, elevated PCWP (due to LV failure and increased filling pressures), and high SVR (compensatory vasoconstriction).'),
('f89fb4c4-274d-5263-8367-c1bac65e3318'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The presence of electrical alternans on ECG is most suggestive of:', '["Pulmonary embolism","LV hypertrophy","Atrial fibrillation","Acute MI","Large pericardial effusion"]'::jsonb, 'Large pericardial effusion', 'Electrical alternans (alternating QRS amplitude) on ECG is highly suggestive of large pericardial effusion with cardiac tamponade. It results from the heart swinging in the fluid-filled pericardial sac.'),
('9cc81e51-d460-55fd-b029-8cb6cf5ed59a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following occurs during the isovolumetric relaxation phase of the cardiac cycle?', '["Semilunar valves open","Ventricular filling occurs","AV valves open","All valves are closed","Atrial contraction occurs"]'::jsonb, 'All valves are closed', 'Isovolumetric relaxation (IVRT) begins at aortic valve closure and ends at mitral valve opening. All four valves are closed. Ventricular pressure falls while volume remains constant.'),
('a72b5b36-c757-5157-9724-7b32f547b41f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The QT interval is prolonged by:', '["Hypocalcemia","Hypokalemia","Congenital long QT syndrome","Class Ia and III antiarrhythmics","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'QT prolongation occurs with electrolyte disturbances (hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia), drugs (Class Ia/III antiarrhythmics, antipsychotics, some antibiotics), and congenital LQTS. Risk of torsades de pointes.'),
('1e0426a7-d833-5085-984f-12edef02f713'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following statements about the SA node is true?', '["It has no autonomic innervation","It is located in the right ventricle","Its action potential has a rapid upstroke from Na+ channels","It is supplied by the left circumflex artery in most people","It generates action potentials at 60-100/min"]'::jsonb, 'It generates action potentials at 60-100/min', 'SA node is in the right atrium near the SVC junction. It fires at 60-100/min intrinsically. Its action potential uses Ca2+ channels (slow response). It is richly innervated by both sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers.'),
('e70d8495-c922-50d5-a425-ce58f8bd85bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ejection phase of the cardiac cycle begins when:', '["Mitral valve opens mediated physiological mechanism","AV valves open mediated physiological mechanism","Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure","Aortic valve closes mediated physiological mechanism","Atrial contraction ends mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure', 'Ejection begins when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure, forcing the aortic valve open. It ends when LV pressure falls below aortic pressure, causing aortic valve closure.'),
('999cdd04-92ed-5329-afe0-636309246f96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a paradoxical split of S2?', '["Splitting widens on inspiration","Aortic valve closure occurs after pulmonary valve closure","Splitting widens on expiration","Split occurs only in expiration","S2 becomes single on inspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Splitting widens on expiration', 'Normally, S2 splits on inspiration (delayed P2). Paradoxical splitting = splitting widens on EXPIRATION (delayed A2 due to LBBB, aortic stenosis, or RV pacing).'),
('37de21cc-830b-56ac-8cf4-8c1e7f6d25b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'How does the fetal heart rate compare to the adult?', '["Slower","Varies by gender","Faster","Irrelevant","Same"]'::jsonb, 'Faster', 'Fetal heart rate is typically 120-160 bpm, faster than adult. It reaches maximum at ~9 weeks (170 bpm) and gradually decreases. Autonomic maturation alters baseline variability.'),
('00274126-e3d2-5e93-bfd6-f475b727b9bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which pressure gradient determines the driving force for blood flow across the systemic circulation?', '["Left ventricular - Aortic pressure","Atrial - Ventricular pressure","Mean arterial pressure - Right atrial pressure","Capillary - Venous pressure","Systolic - Diastolic"]'::jsonb, 'Mean arterial pressure - Right atrial pressure', 'Flow (cardiac output) = Pressure gradient (MAP - CVP) / Resistance (SVR). MAP ~93 mmHg, CVP ~3 mmHg, so the driving gradient is ~90 mmHg through the systemic circulation.'),
('95641f37-e50c-54f3-83c4-2a9c763c8e11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In a normal ECG, the J point is:', '["The end of the P wave mediated physiological mechanism","The end of the QRS and beginning of ST segment","The peak of the T wave mediated physiological mechanism","The end of the T wave mediated physiological mechanism","The beginning of the QRS mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'The end of the QRS and beginning of ST segment', 'The J point is the junction between the end of the QRS complex and the beginning of the ST segment. It is the baseline for measuring ST elevation or depression.'),
('5f479ca9-9788-5bb5-92d8-e914a79aeae4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a physiologic cause of ST elevation?', '["Pericarditis","Acute MI","Left ventricular aneurysm","Normal variant","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Normal variant', 'Early repolarization (benign early repolarization) is a normal variant with diffuse concave ST elevation, especially in young males and athletes. It does not indicate ischemia.'),
('a932467c-8927-5b7a-a7b6-2292fa379590'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In the normal cardiac cycle, isovolumetric contraction occurs:', '["During ventricular filling","Immediately after mitral valve closure","Immediately after aortic valve opening","After the T wave","During atrial contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Immediately after mitral valve closure', 'Isovolumetric contraction begins with mitral valve closure (start of systole) and ends with aortic valve opening. Ventricular pressure rises rapidly while volume remains constant.'),
('02527bd1-c6aa-5dbc-9495-cbb88cd4d544'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of wide QRS tachycardia?', '["Ventricular tachycardia","None of the above","Atrial fibrillation with pre-excitation","All of the above","SVT with aberrancy"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Wide QRS tachycardia (>120 ms) can be VT, SVT with bundle branch block/aberrancy, or AF with pre-excitation. VT is the most common and potentially life-threatening cause.'),
('b865f515-b9cd-5a87-87f1-c65f43096bd7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The heart''s contractility is enhanced by:', '["Increased intracellular Ca2+","Beta-1 adrenergic stimulation","Digitalis glycosides","All of the above","Increased heart rate"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Contractility is increased by β1 stimulation (via cAMP/PKA increasing Ca2+ current), the Bowditch treppe (increased HR increases Ca2+ entry), digitalis (Na+/K+ pump inhibition), and any factor increasing Ca2+ availability.'),
('a5769ce5-04f1-57b7-8c15-780e59225dfb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of the ''athlete''s heart''?', '["Increased LV wall thickness","Increased stroke volume","Increased LV cavity size","Resting bradycardia","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Athlete''s heart involves adaptive LV hypertrophy (eccentric or concentric), increased chamber size, resting bradycardia (high vagal tone), and increased stroke volume. ECG may show LVH pattern.'),
('3814b88b-1a63-5ca4-b70d-4dca38085ace'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The primary function of the coronary sinus is to:', '["Supply blood to the left ventricle","Supply the SA node","Drain blood from the lungs","Supply blood to the right coronary artery","Drain blood from most of the cardiac veins into the RA"]'::jsonb, 'Drain blood from most of the cardiac veins into the RA', 'The coronary sinus is a venous structure in the posterior AV groove that receives blood from the great, middle, and small cardiac veins and drains into the right atrium.'),
('b2384c83-3c43-5588-b94a-bb72bcd211bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following describes the Bowditch effect (treppe)?', '["No change in contractility with rate","Increasing contractility with increasing heart rate","Decreasing contractility with increasing heart rate","Decreased contractility with increased Ca2+","Increased contractility with decreased preload"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing contractility with increasing heart rate', 'The Bowditch effect (treppe/staircase phenomenon) is a progressive increase in contractility when heart rate increases, due to increased Ca2+ entry per unit time and accumulation of intracellular Ca2+.'),
('f620f691-a8ed-58b7-85dc-156453bf8d2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is most likely to cause a right ventricular strain pattern on ECG?', '["COPD","RV hypertrophy","Acute pulmonary embolism","All of the above","Pulmonary hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Acute RV strain (S1Q3T3 pattern, right axis, T inversion V1-V3) occurs in PE. Chronic RV strain appears as RA enlargement, right axis, and RVH pattern in COPD, PH, and pulmonic stenosis.'),
('d39e9331-c378-52d5-8831-c18b60922cbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The maximum exercise capacity of a person is limited primarily by:', '["Hemoglobin level","Chest wall compliance","Pulmonary function","Muscle strength","Cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output', 'Cardiac output is the primary determinant of VO2 max (maximal oxygen consumption). During exercise, CO increases 4-5 fold by increasing both HR and SV.'),
('1ecfcd27-4bbf-58a7-8248-8dfff1135065'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following leads is used to record a rhythm strip for arrhythmia monitoring?', '["V1","Lead I","V6","Lead II","aVR"]'::jsonb, 'Lead II', 'Lead II is most commonly used for rhythm monitoring because it provides a clear view of P waves and QRS complexes (at +60° orientation). Most telemetry monitors use a modified Lead II.'),
('ee40a7ba-1258-5e6a-810d-872f74638783'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The diagnosis of left ventricular hypertrophy on ECG is supported by:', '["All of the above","Tall R waves in V5-V6","LV strain pattern","Deep S waves in V1-V2","Left axis deviation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'LVH criteria include: S in V1 + R in V5/V6 >35 mm (Sokolow-Lyon), left axis deviation, prolonged QRS, and ST-T changes in lateral leads (LV strain pattern).'),
('121426fc-dea6-5845-a580-d22493730242'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a source of error in cardiac output measurement by thermodilution?', '["Improper catheter placement","All of the above","Intracardiac shunt","Tricuspid regurgitation","Incorrect injectate temperature"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Thermodilution CO measurement is affected by tricuspid regurgitation (indicator recirculation), shunts, incorrect injectate volume/temperature, and catheter malposition.'),
('3dab8b85-f741-5599-8193-f9b2e3ed0683'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal response to the Valsalva maneuver''s release phase is:', '["BP decreases then slowly returns","BP drops and stays low","BP increases only","BP decrease then overshoot","No BP change"]'::jsonb, 'BP decrease then overshoot', 'After Valsalva release, venous return surges, cardiac output increases, and BP overshoots above baseline, triggering baroreflex-mediated bradycardia. This tests autonomic integrity.'),
('53534403-a85a-53dd-9dd5-18b3f71da0e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The site of highest resistance in the coronary circulation is:', '["Venules","Capillaries","Large epicardial arteries","Small intramyocardial arterioles","Coronary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Small intramyocardial arterioles', 'Intramyocardial arterioles are the primary resistance vessels in the coronary circulation. They regulate flow in response to metabolic demands and are the site of autoregulation.'),
('53a29f30-e5df-5072-ae95-edd05a93da1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of cardiogenic pulmonary edema?', '["Frothy pink sputum","Dyspnea and orthopnea","High PCWP","All of the above","Bilateral infiltrates on chest X-ray"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Cardiogenic pulmonary edema features: elevated PCWP (>18-20 mmHg), pink frothy sputum, bilateral perihilar infiltrates (bat wing pattern), and symptoms of dyspnea/orthopnea.'),
('5ea2dc0a-0353-53c5-b5cc-face028c18a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'This is most likely:', '["Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Atrial tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial flutter', 'Typical atrial flutter shows a sawtooth (F wave) pattern at ~300/min in leads II, III, aVF, with 2:1 AV block giving a ventricular rate of ~150/min.'),
('a4464413-f36d-5bb9-90dd-a3187359e840'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a major source of vascular resistance in the systemic circulation?', '["Capillaries","Large veins","Arterioles","Aorta","Venules"]'::jsonb, 'Arterioles', 'Arterioles are the primary resistance vessels, contributing ~50-60% of total peripheral resistance. Their narrow diameter and thick smooth muscle layer allow precise regulation of blood flow.'),
('9326bd20-0cd2-52a6-919e-557fc4f2228e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The phenomenon of ''autoregulation'' in the coronary circulation is primarily mediated by:', '["Endothelial factors alone","Vagal nerves","Local metabolic factors","Myogenic response","Sympathetic nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Local metabolic factors', 'Coronary autoregulation maintains flow over a range of perfusion pressures, primarily via local metabolic factors. Adenosine (vasodilator) is a key mediator linking flow to metabolic demand.'),
('9fa86c6a-a5b1-5bba-ae60-48af21404d45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The direction of blood flow through the heart in a left-to-right shunt is from:', '["Right ventricle to left ventricle","Aorta to pulmonary artery","Right atrium to left atrium","Systemic to pulmonary circulation","Pulmonary to systemic circulation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic to pulmonary circulation', 'Left-to-right shunt: oxygenated blood flows from the left heart (higher pressure) to the right heart or pulmonary circulation (lower pressure). Examples: ASD, VSD, PDA.'),
('fde4d772-1458-58c8-9d60-aaa9f2f79f8c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The J point is elevated in leads V2-V4 in a 25-year-old asymptomatic male. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["LV aneurysm","Early repolarization","Pericarditis","Brugada syndrome","Acute anterior MI"]'::jsonb, 'Early repolarization', 'Early repolarization (benign) is common in young males and athletes. It shows concave ST elevation with a notch at the J point, mainly in V2-V4. No symptoms, no reciprocal changes.'),
('db46ab3c-c865-5778-80be-5b7edcd42d27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The QT interval corrects for heart rate using which formula?', '["QTc = QT / RR","QTc = QT / √RR","QTc = QT + 0.1","QTc = RR/√QT","QTc = QT × √RR"]'::jsonb, 'QTc = QT / √RR', 'Bazett''s formula: QTc = QT / √RR (where RR is the RR interval in seconds). Normal QTc is <440 ms in males and <460 ms in females. Prolonged QTc increases risk of torsades de pointes.'),
('86a77c62-248d-5ac7-9627-91ca2e4398df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The primary mechanism of the positive inotropic effect of digitalis is:', '["Increased beta mediated physiological mechanism","Increased heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibition of Na+/K+ ATPase leading to increased intracellular Ca2+ through NCX","Increased Ca2+ channel opening mediated physiological mechanism","Increased cAMP mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of Na+/K+ ATPase leading to increased intracellular Ca2+ through NCX', 'Digitalis inhibits the Na+/K+ pump, increasing intracellular Na+. This reduces Ca2+ efflux via Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (NCX), increasing intracellular Ca2+ and contractility.'),
('a84d9f69-1353-5f71-8d7e-c5f26a87db4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following components of the ECG is the first to appear after the onset of ventricular systole?', '["T wave","P wave","QRS complex","U wave","ST segment"]'::jsonb, 'QRS complex', 'The QRS complex appears at the beginning of ventricular systole, immediately after P wave. It represents ventricular depolarization, which precedes ventricular contraction.'),
('e88c6aa0-3352-589e-9985-562135767a50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The increase in heart rate during inspiration (sinus arrhythmia) is due to:', '["Baroreceptor activation","Decreased sympathetic tone","Increased sympathetic tone","Increased vagal tone","Decreased vagal tone"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased vagal tone', 'Sinus arrhythmia (HR increase during inspiration) is caused by decreased vagal tone due to inhibition of cardiac vagal efferents during inspiration. It is a normal phenomenon, especially in young people.'),
('ab020233-48c6-5774-9799-59ea0cf78480'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a criterion for the diagnosis of diastolic heart failure (HFpEF)?', '["EF <30%","LVEF <40%","LVEF 50% with dilated LV","LVEF ≥50% with evidence of diastolic dysfunction","LVEF 60% with normal filling pressures"]'::jsonb, 'LVEF ≥50% with evidence of diastolic dysfunction', 'HFpEF (heart failure with preserved ejection fraction) is diagnosed when LVEF is ≥50% with evidence of diastolic dysfunction (impaired relaxation, increased filling pressures) and HF symptoms.'),
('8fb305cc-9b99-5683-b1ab-6902e8c95083'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The term ''lusitropic'' refers to:', '["Heart rate","Relaxation of the heart","Electrical conduction","Refractoriness","Contraction of the heart"]'::jsonb, 'Relaxation of the heart', 'Lusitropic refers to myocardial relaxation. Positive lusitropy (e.g., β-adrenergic stimulation) enhances relaxation and diastolic filling. Impaired lusitropy is a feature of diastolic dysfunction.'),
('9733d8a0-a9d5-58dc-b6c8-a008dab5b399'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a major ECG criteria for the diagnosis of right ventricular hypertrophy?', '["All of the above","R in V1 + S in V5/V6 >10.5 mm","ST depression and T inversion in right precordial leads","R > S in V1","Right axis deviation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'RVH criteria: right axis deviation >+100°, R/S ratio >1 in V1, R in V1 + S in V5/V6 >10.5 mm, and ST-T changes (strain pattern) in right precordial leads.'),
('1aeeeb5a-ddf8-59ad-83fc-6cf74e039e19'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following interventions increases the force of contraction of the heart?', '["All of the above","Increasing the rate of stimulation","Adding calcium to the perfusing solution","Beta-adrenergic stimulation","Digitalis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All listed interventions increase contractility: increased extracellular Ca2+ increases Ca2+ influx, β-stimulation increases Ca2+ current via PKA, digitalis increases Ca2+ via NCX, and increased rate increases Ca2+ accumulation (Bowditch).'),
('6e71bf90-b44b-5fcf-9a62-3a4a37208608'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The phenomenon of ''post-extrasystolic potentiation'' is due to:', '["Increased Ca2+ loading of the SR during compensatory pause","Increased HR mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased Ca2+ in SR mediated physiological mechanism","Increased vagal tone mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased contractility mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased Ca2+ loading of the SR during compensatory pause', 'After a premature beat, the compensatory pause allows more time for Ca2+ loading of the SR, resulting in a stronger subsequent contraction (post-extrasystolic potentiation).'),
('b7a84afe-6827-5477-971e-129eb4c50b86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In a surgical context, This is most likely:', '["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Wandering pacemaker","Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial fibrillation"]'::jsonb, 'Multifocal atrial tachycardia', 'MAT shows ≥3 different P wave morphologies with varying PP intervals, usually with a ventricular rate >100/min. It is associated with COPD, pulmonary disease, and electrolyte imbalances.'),
('d4bc44f9-d4ad-5b8f-b006-904959f8ced1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of the Bainbridge reflex?', '["Decreased SVR in response to increased CO","Increased vagal tone with increased preload","Venoconstriction and increased HR in response to increased atrial pressure","Decreased HR in response to increased BP","Increased contractility in response to increased afterload"]'::jsonb, 'Venoconstriction and increased HR in response to increased atrial pressure', 'The Bainbridge reflex: increased right atrial pressure (from increased venous return) stimulates atrial stretch receptors, increasing HR via sympathetic activation. This helps match CO to venous return.'),
('11909dc8-64fb-5016-be22-7bbbe0226e35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most immediate compensatory mechanism for a decrease in blood pressure?', '["Renal fluid retention mediated physiological mechanism","Activation of RAAS mediated physiological mechanism","Ventricular hypertrophy mediated physiological mechanism","Increase in ADH secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Baroreceptor-mediated increase in sympathetic outflow"]'::jsonb, 'Baroreceptor-mediated increase in sympathetic outflow', 'The baroreceptor reflex is the most immediate response (seconds) to BP changes. It decreases baroreceptor firing → increased sympathetic and decreased vagal outflow → increased HR, contractility, and vasoconstriction.'),
('3e948768-9918-5914-8089-2080a66a1739'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following murmurs is best heard at the left lower sternal border?', '["Tricuspid regurgitation","Pulmonic stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Tricuspid regurgitation', 'Tricuspid regurgitation is best heard at the left lower sternal border (4th-5th ICS). It increases with inspiration (Carvallo sign). Aortic stenosis is at the right upper sternal border.'),
('fc5e8bca-0e72-5d32-8dc9-dfa558b52a38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal left atrial pressure is approximately:', '["25-30 mmHg","5-12 mmHg","15-20 mmHg","2-4 mmHg","35-40 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '5-12 mmHg', 'Normal left atrial pressure is 5-12 mmHg. It is reflected by the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP). Elevated LAP occurs in mitral stenosis, LV failure, and diastolic dysfunction.'),
('243b187a-0617-5869-bee3-ba43006410ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In a patient with atrial fibrillation, the ventricular rate is controlled primarily by:', '["AV node conduction properties","Ventricular muscle refractoriness","Purkinje fiber automaticity","Atrial refractoriness","SA node automaticity"]'::jsonb, 'AV node conduction properties', 'In AF, the ventricular rate is determined by AV node conduction (its refractory period). AV node acts as a filter, preventing all atrial impulses from reaching the ventricles.'),
('e79ecb3c-b3cd-5e2c-a7e1-f4d2adfaed3c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of new-onset atrial fibrillation?', '["Coronary artery disease","Hypertension","Alcohol binge","Thyrotoxicosis","Pulmonary embolism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension', 'Hypertension is the most common underlying cause of AF. Other causes include CAD, valvular heart disease (especially mitral), thyrotoxicosis, obesity, and alcohol (holiday heart syndrome).'),
('54c2ed17-84dd-5aee-afb3-c56f14713b28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The S4 heart sound is caused by:', '["Semilunar valve closure","Rapid ventricular filling","Ventricular ejection","AV valve closure","Atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle', 'S4 (atrial gallop) occurs just before S1 and is caused by atrial contraction forcing blood into a non-compliant, stiff ventricle. It is heard in LVH, HCM, aortic stenosis, and post-MI.'),
('d9e0e88b-8a68-57cf-b7a3-61be1a54dabd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most important factor in determining cardiac output during exercise?', '["Heart rate response","Frank-Starling mechanism","Sympathetic stimulation","Venous return","Peripheral vascular resistance"]'::jsonb, 'Venous return', 'Venous return (determined by the pressure gradient between peripheral veins and right atrium) is the primary determinant of cardiac output. The heart pumps whatever blood it receives (Frank-Starling).'),
('6f01ae30-e6bc-5814-923e-c9027361ad87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'A patient with ECG showing ST depression in leads V3-V6 during a stress test likely has:', '["Posterior wall ischemia","Anterior wall ischemia","Septal ischemia","Inferior wall ischemia","Lateral wall ischemia"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior wall ischemia', 'ST depression during stress test in leads V3-V6 suggests anterior/lateral ischemia (LAD territory). Inferior ischemia would show changes in II, III, aVF.'),
('6b895b75-911e-5037-8c17-4cb1b1f39601'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following statements about the carotid body chemoreceptors is true?', '["They respond to PO2 primarily","They respond to blood pressure","They are more sensitive to CO2 than O2","They respond to O2 content primarily","They are located only in the carotid sinus"]'::jsonb, 'They respond to PO2 primarily', 'Carotid body chemoreceptors sense PO2 (not O2 content). They are stimulated by low PO2 (<60 mmHg), high PCO2, and low pH. They increase ventilation. Anemia does not stimulate them (normal PO2).'),
('c0dbb25b-54b4-5d76-83e5-53ae70438715'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of acetylcholine on the heart includes:', '["All of the above","Decreased HR","Shortened atrial refractory period","Decreased AV conduction velocity","Decreased atrial contractility"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'ACh (released by vagus) acts on M2 receptors in the heart: decreases HR (negative chronotropy), slows AV conduction (negative dromotropy), decreases atrial contractility, and shortens atrial refractory period.'),
('fae3e592-7006-5d0c-aa28-3947f036d554'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of the His-Purkinje system?', '["No automaticity","Origin in the AV node","Fast conduction velocity","Absence of gap junctions","Slow conduction velocity"]'::jsonb, 'Fast conduction velocity', 'The His-Purkinje system has the fastest conduction velocity (2-4 m/s) in the heart, enabling rapid, synchronized ventricular depolarization. It also has latent pacemaker activity (15-40/min).'),
('cd34d07e-9ad3-5ccc-a80f-f702a9af7d58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Frank-Starling curve is shifted upward and to the left with:', '["Increased afterload","Decreased contractility","Increased contractility","Decreased preload","Beta-blocker therapy"]'::jsonb, 'Increased contractility', 'Increased contractility (e.g., catecholamines, digitalis) shifts the Frank-Starling curve up and left, producing greater stroke volume for any given end-diastolic volume.'),
('72b3caeb-96d0-5caa-ac1d-d766c21e9441'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most specific ECG finding for acute pericarditis?', '["Diffuse ST elevation with upward concavity","T wave inversion in one lead","Regional ST elevation","LVH","Pathological Q waves"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse ST elevation with upward concavity', 'Diffuse, concave ST elevation (often with PR depression) is characteristic of acute pericarditis. Unlike MI, it is not regional and does not develop Q waves or reciprocal changes.'),
('e72204f1-4f8f-50f3-ab9b-46e931b0f5af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal wall thickness of the left ventricle is approximately:', '["1.2-1.8 cm","2.0-2.5 cm","0.3-0.5 cm","0.6-1.1 cm","3.0 cm"]'::jsonb, '0.6-1.1 cm', 'Normal LV wall thickness is 0.6-1.1 cm at end-diastole. Concentric hypertrophy (increased thickness) occurs in hypertension and aortic stenosis. Eccentric hypertrophy (increased cavity size) in volume overload.'),
('8acea600-8beb-5425-9d72-c098e6d9e914'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of low voltage QRS on ECG?', '["All of the above","Obesity","Hypothyroidism","Pericardial effusion","COPD"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Low voltage QRS can be due to pericardial effusion (fluid insulates), COPD (hyperinflation, air trapping), obesity (increased distance), hypothyroidism (myxedema), and infiltrative heart diseases.'),
('187b25c4-8b13-5acb-856a-c46cc96c929a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The positive inotropic effect of beta-adrenergic stimulation is mediated by:', '["Increased cAMP and PKA-dependent phosphorylation of Ca2+ channels","Increased cGMP mediated physiological mechanism","Increased DAG mediated physiological mechanism","Increased IP3 mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased cAMP mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased cAMP and PKA-dependent phosphorylation of Ca2+ channels', 'β1-adrenergic stimulation activates Gs → adenylyl cyclase → increased cAMP → PKA phosphorylates L-type Ca2+ channels → increased Ca2+ influx → increased contractility.'),
('ba447e93-4dc7-5130-b0a4-9395fb87096c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of pseudoinfarction pattern on ECG?', '["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","LVH","WPW pattern","LBBB","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Pseudoinfarction (Q waves without MI) occurs in LVH (septal Q), LBBB (QS in right leads), WPW (delta wave mimics Q), HCM (septal Q), and others like cardiomyopathies and pneumothorax.'),
('24d5fb6d-ac11-54a5-a73b-a1567e8be4db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Purkinje fibers are located in the:', '["Epicardium","Myocardium only","Atria","SA node","Subendocardium of ventricles"]'::jsonb, 'Subendocardium of ventricles', 'Purkinje fibers are located in the subendocardial layer of the ventricles, forming the terminal portion of the specialized conduction system. They penetrate the ventricular wall from endocardium to epicardium.'),
('0163a918-e119-5522-a923-531e4ccc43f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following conditions is associated with a wide and fixed split S2?', '["Fallot tetralogy","Pulmonary stenosis","Ventricular septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus","Atrial septal defect"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial septal defect', 'Wide fixed splitting of S2 is characteristic of ASD. The increased right ventricular volume delays P2, and the split does not vary with respiration because the shunt maintains constant RV filling.'),
('8d729ff9-cb7e-5b7c-a888-fece6cd9f80a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ECG paper speed is normally:', '["100 mm/s","10 mm/s","5 mm/s","25 mm/s","50 mm/s"]'::jsonb, '25 mm/s', 'Standard ECG paper speed is 25 mm/s. At this speed: 1 mm = 0.04 s (40 ms), 5 mm = 0.2 s, and large box (25 mm) = 1 s. Speed can be changed to 50 mm/s for detailed analysis.'),
('31b20dd2-0242-579f-8fc1-1b522f733600'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The pressure in the right ventricle during systole is normally about:', '["40-50 mmHg","25-30 mmHg","60-70 mmHg","15-20 mmHg","120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '25-30 mmHg', 'Normal RV systolic pressure is 25-30 mmHg (pulmonary artery pressure). RV systolic pressure >35-40 mmHg indicates pulmonary hypertension.'),
('92ddbece-e2d2-5192-b2ac-d735274bd854'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is an important determinant of the rate of ventricular relaxation (lusitropy)?', '["Titin compliance","SERCA activity","All of the above","Phospholamban phosphorylation","Cytosolic Ca2+ removal"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Ventricular relaxation depends on SERCA Ca2+ reuptake (regulated by phospholamban), NCX Ca2+ extrusion, titin (passive stiffness), and the rate of cross-bridge detachment.'),
('e6871eb9-23ae-5fd5-8b24-09e65b19c872'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Wenckebach phenomenon (Mobitz I) is most commonly seen in which condition?', '["All of the above except A","Exercise-induced AV block","Digoxin toxicity","Anterior MI","Inferior MI"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above except A', 'Wenckebach is commonly associated with inferior MI (AV nodal ischemia), digoxin toxicity (increased vagal tone), and is often exercise-induced. Anterior MI more commonly causes Mobitz II.'),
('c65b6030-f61f-56d3-96b4-6f7b4fa54164'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is characteristic of second-degree AV block Mobitz type II?', '["Constant PR interval, no dropped beats","All P waves conducted","PR interval shortens before dropped beat","Progressive PR prolongation","Fixed PR interval with dropped QRS"]'::jsonb, 'Fixed PR interval with dropped QRS', 'Mobitz II: fixed (normal or prolonged) PR interval with intermittent non-conducted P waves. Unlike Mobitz I, it is usually due to disease below the AV node (His-Purkinje) and carries a worse prognosis.'),
('905035d1-62ca-5497-9a90-d4956f8435cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal response to an increase in afterload is:', '["No change in stroke volume mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased stroke volume initially, then recovery","Increased venous return mediated physiological mechanism","Increased stroke volume mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased stroke volume initially, then recovery', 'Sudden increase in afterload (e.g., aortic clamping) causes an immediate decrease in stroke volume (as LV must generate more pressure). Over time, the Frank-Starling mechanism and hypertrophy can compensate.'),
('6a5c200d-1b0e-5d53-a69a-c4c05072ced1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of ST depression on ECG?', '["Digoxin effect","All of the above","Subendocardial ischemia","LVH with strain","Hypokalemia"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'ST depression (horizontal or downsloping) can be due to subendocardial ischemia (stable angina), digitalis effect (scooped), LVH strain pattern, hypokalemia, and reciprocal changes in MI.'),
('df4a2291-1e3f-5a8e-b0ee-943cc43a56da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Bainbridge reflex opposes the baroreceptor reflex by:', '["Increasing vagal tone during exercise","Decreasing HR when BP is high","Decreasing SVR when CO is low","Decreasing contractility when afterload increases","Increasing HR when venous return increases"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing HR when venous return increases', 'The Bainbridge reflex increases HR in response to increased venous return (atrial stretch), counteracting the baroreflex-mediated bradycardia that would otherwise occur from increased BP.'),
('c0df4c1f-00a8-5694-a30e-5df8b0783726'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common form of congenital heart disease?', '["Fallot tetralogy","Pulmonary stenosis","PDA","VSD","ASD"]'::jsonb, 'VSD', 'Ventricular septal defect is the most common congenital heart disease (~30% of all CHD). Small VSDs may close spontaneously. Large VSDs cause left-to-right shunt and pulmonary hypertension.'),
('085ae453-41e6-54d6-a02c-ef5dc96b41a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The flow of blood through the coronary arteries is greatest during:', '["Atrial contraction","Late systole","Early systole","Early diastole","Late diastole"]'::jsonb, 'Early diastole', 'Coronary blood flow is maximal in early diastole when ventricular relaxation relieves compression of intramyocardial vessels. During systole, myocardial compression impedes flow, especially in the left ventricle.'),
('edc305dd-0d10-5547-9e3c-838d2ba8d085'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following ECG changes is characteristic of digoxin toxicity?', '["Scooped ST depression","Both B and D","Short QT interval","Prolonged PR interval and PVCs","Peaked T waves"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and D', 'Digoxin toxicity causes: scooped ST depression (digitalis effect), prolonged PR (AV block), PVCs (most common), atrial tachycardia with block, and various other arrhythmias. T wave inversion/flattening occurs.'),
('d7796ab6-7f1c-5ce9-9e52-0c137afe8547'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The velocity of blood flow is slowest in which part of the circulation?', '["Arteries","Vena cava","Veins","Capillaries","Aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Capillaries', 'Blood flow velocity is inversely related to total cross-sectional area. Capillaries have the largest total cross-sectional area, so velocity is slowest (~0.3 mm/s), allowing efficient exchange.'),
('b7813aa5-9908-56c6-b473-147fdb64d806'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of an elevated jugular venous pressure?', '["Bilateral jugular vein thrombosis","Left heart failure","Right heart failure","Constrictive pericarditis","SVC syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Right heart failure', 'Right heart failure is the most common cause of elevated JVP. It impairs right ventricular emptying, increasing right atrial pressure, which is transmitted back to the jugular veins.'),
('0a02c87c-28b0-5fc9-a8c4-c7d7bb9ae86b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Purkinje fibers action potential differs from ventricular muscle by having:', '["Spontaneous phase 4 depolarization","All of the above","Faster phase 0","More negative resting potential","Shorter action potential duration"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Purkinje fibers have a more negative resting potential (~-95 mV), faster phase 0 (Na+ dependent), spontaneous phase 4 depolarization (pacemaker activity), and longer action potential duration than ventricular muscle.'),
('55f2bbdd-e6d7-5f62-bb8d-a291f411b41b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of prolonged QRS duration?', '["All of the above","WPW pattern","Ventricular paced rhythm","LBBB","RBBB"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'QRS prolongation (>120 ms) occurs in LBBB, RBBB, non-specific IVCD, ventricular paced beats, ventricular arrhythmias, and WPW syndrome (delta wave widens QRS).'),
('29a91a4c-2130-528b-9bd5-d07b62e2e6ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The most common complication of acute myocardial infarction is:', '["Arrhythmia","Papillary muscle rupture","Cardiac rupture","Heart failure","Cardiogenic shock"]'::jsonb, 'Arrhythmia', 'Arrhythmias are the most common complication of acute MI. Ventricular arrhythmias (VT/VF) are most serious. Other complications: heart failure, cardiogenic shock, mechanical complications (rupture, MR, VSD).'),
('36c21b60-d643-540f-9599-5aa6d57935d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The rate-pressure product (double product) is a measure of:', '["LV compliance mediated physiological mechanism","Cardiac work and myocardial oxygen consumption","Peripheral resistance mediated physiological mechanism","Stroke volume mediated physiological mechanism","Venous return mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac work and myocardial oxygen consumption', 'Rate-pressure product = HR × SBP. It is a reliable non-invasive index of myocardial O2 consumption. RPP >20,000 during exercise indicates high cardiac workload.'),
('4fa60784-fcb1-5a9e-8fe6-6b6e9f62f6b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is consistent with hyperacute T waves on ECG?', '["LVH","Pericarditis","Hypokalemia","Normal variant","Early acute MI"]'::jsonb, 'Early acute MI', 'Hyperacute T waves (tall, broad, asymmetric) are an early sign of acute MI (minutes to hours), preceding ST elevation. They indicate severe myocardial ischemia. Also seen in hyperkalemia.'),
('8c61cfa7-beb4-5c32-8f8a-24cefcc87032'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal P wave duration is:', '["<60 ms","<160 ms","<200 ms","<80 ms","<120 ms"]'::jsonb, '<120 ms', 'Normal P wave duration is <120 ms (3 small squares). P mitrale (>120 ms, bifid) indicates left atrial enlargement. P pulmonale (>2.5 mm tall, peaked) indicates right atrial enlargement.'),
('4817a55c-b889-5af6-8591-1625db35061f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is an indication for permanent pacemaker implantation?', '["Mobitz II AV block","All of the above","Complete AV block","Bifascicular block with intermittent complete block","Symptomatic sinus bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Permanent pacing is indicated for symptomatic bradycardia, complete AV block, Mobitz II, and certain cases of bifascicular/trifascicular block. The decision depends on symptoms and block location.'),
('a0f7f361-284c-5c74-bf3f-086d84386ad2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of beta-blockers on the Frank-Starling curve is:', '["Increased plateau","No change","Steeper slope","Shift upward and left","Shift downward and right"]'::jsonb, 'Shift downward and right', 'Beta-blockers (negative inotropes) shift the Frank-Starling curve downward and right, producing less stroke volume for any given preload. They reduce myocardial O2 demand.'),
('96a6abe8-98ac-5a98-9801-98cdb7688238'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes?', '["Anomalous coronary artery origin","Long QT syndrome","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Coronary artery disease","Myocarditis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy', 'HCM is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes (~36%). It causes LV outflow tract obstruction, myocardial ischemia, and ventricular arrhythmias.'),
('3fe89f7e-33e8-579e-852a-971c5857d749'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In the normal heart, the sequence of electrical activation is:', '["AV node → SA node → His → Purkinje","SA node → AV node → His → bundle branches → Purkinje","AV node → His → Purkinje → SA node","Purkinje → His → AV node → SA node","SA node → His → AV node → Purkinje"]'::jsonb, 'SA node → AV node → His → bundle branches → Purkinje', 'The normal sequence: SA node (pacemaker) → atrial myocardium → AV node (delay) → His bundle → bundle branches → Purkinje fibers → ventricular myocardium.'),
('da6bafd5-8d0b-5943-88db-b43963458707'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following murmurs is holosystolic?', '["Ventricular septal defect","Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Holosystolic (pansystolic) murmurs include MR, TR, and VSD. They occupy the entire systole because the pressure gradient between chambers exists throughout systole.'),
('30c09240-dc56-5128-a807-1f81aaf575a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ECG finding of ''poor R wave progression'' in precordial leads is defined as:', '["R wave amplitude <3 mm in V3","None of the above","R/S ratio does not increase","R wave does not increase in amplitude from V1 to V3","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Poor R wave progression: R wave amplitude <3 mm in V3, R does not exceed S by V3-V4, or R in V3 ≤3 mm. Causes: anterior MI, LVH, LBBB, COPD, and technical factors.'),
('1b4e50a5-b991-5b2c-8ee8-1004bbfb5d0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the mechanism of the Bezold-Jarisch reflex?', '["Activation of RV chemoreceptors mediated physiological mechanism","Peripheral chemoreceptor activation mediated physiological mechanism","Activation of atrial stretch receptors mediated physiological mechanism","Activation of vagal afferents from LV chemoreceptors causing bradycardia and hypotension","Activation of carotid sinus baroreceptors mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Activation of vagal afferents from LV chemoreceptors causing bradycardia and hypotension', 'The Bezold-Jarisch reflex is triggered by LV chemoreceptors (during ischemia, certain drugs). Vagal afferents → increased vagal efferent → bradycardia, hypotension, and vasodilation.'),
('9a90eb04-8f56-53b1-ad13-07ca72e3e2f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The absolute value of the left ventricular end-diastolic volume is approximately:', '["160 mL","80 mL","50 mL","200 mL","120 mL"]'::jsonb, '120 mL', 'Normal LVEDV is approximately 120 mL (range 100-150 mL). EDV varies with body size (indexed: 50-80 mL/m²). LV end-systolic volume is ~45 mL.'),
('cb341b5f-d7af-5088-8d38-8af3d72557a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a condition that causes pulsus alternans?', '["Severe LV systolic dysfunction","Aortic stenosis","Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"]'::jsonb, 'Severe LV systolic dysfunction', 'Pulsus alternans (alternating strong and weak pulses) indicates severe LV systolic dysfunction. It is due to alternating contractile force from impaired Ca2+ handling. Seen in end-stage heart failure.'),
('8bda71d3-37b7-564f-b860-0b60d7c41038'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal rise in right atrial pressure during the ''a'' wave is about:', '["15-20 mmHg","7-10 mmHg","3-6 mmHg","10-15 mmHg","1-3 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '3-6 mmHg', 'The ''a'' wave in JVP normally rises 3-6 mmHg above baseline. Elevated ''a'' wave occurs in tricuspid stenosis, RVH, and pulmonary hypertension. Cannon ''a'' waves occur in AV dissociation.'),
('27ae078c-7996-5c84-9566-d663b412825e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of the electrocardiogram in Brugada syndrome?', '["ST depression in inferior leads mediated physiological mechanism","Right bundle branch block pattern with ST elevation in V1-V3","T wave inversion in all leads mediated physiological mechanism","Pathological Q waves mediated physiological mechanism","Left axis deviation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Right bundle branch block pattern with ST elevation in V1-V3', 'Brugada syndrome shows a coved-type ST elevation in V1-V3 (Type 1) with RBBB-like pattern. It predisposes to ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death, especially in young Southeast Asian males.'),
('1d122910-c968-56c9-b5e1-bf69c4960dbd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The greatest increase in cardiac output during exercise occurs due to:', '["Decreased peripheral resistance","Increased heart rate","Increased contractility","Increased venous return","Increased stroke volume"]'::jsonb, 'Increased heart rate', 'During exercise, heart rate can increase 2-3 fold (from 70 to 180-200 bpm). Stroke volume increases only 30-50%. Thus, HR increase contributes most to the increase in cardiac output.'),
('ee9e9012-ef9a-5846-979a-8cf53a11bafb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of right atrial enlargement on ECG?', '["Short PR","P mitrale","P pulmonale","Normal P wave","Biphasic P in V1"]'::jsonb, 'P pulmonale', 'Right atrial enlargement causes P pulmonale: tall (>2.5 mm), peaked P waves in leads II, III, aVF. Left atrial enlargement causes P mitrale (wide, bifid P in II, negative P in V1).'),
('7e163af5-96de-52c0-a18d-acb78cc504ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The primary determinant of the resistance to flow in a blood vessel is:', '["Vessel radius","Pressure gradient","Vessel length","Endothelial factors","Blood viscosity"]'::jsonb, 'Vessel radius', 'Poiseuille''s law: Resistance = (8ηL)/(πr⁴). Resistance is inversely proportional to the FOURTH power of the radius. A small change in radius dramatically affects resistance.'),
('e96dfde3-876d-5fca-bccf-5bac8bda2cb5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is an indication for implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) implantation?', '["All of the above","High risk for sudden death","Survived cardiac arrest from VT/VF","LVEF <35% on optimal medical therapy","Sustained VT with structural heart disease"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'ICD is indicated for secondary prevention (survived VT/VF arrest) and primary prevention in patients at high risk: LVEF <35%, HCM, ARVC, LQTS, and certain other conditions.'),
('415f4356-40b8-5597-aa29-a226676e3e1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The difference between the aVR and other limb leads is that aVR:', '["All of the above","Is a bipolar lead","Usually shows inverted P, QRS, T","Has the positive electrode on the right arm","Is oriented at -150°"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'aVR (augmented Vector Right) has its positive electrode on the right arm, oriented at -150°. It normally shows predominantly negative P, QRS, and T waves (everything moving away from aVR).'),
('b320e22e-2052-532a-be97-14d5ed713c99'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of restrictive cardiomyopathy?', '["Amyloidosis","All of the above","Hemochromatosis","Sarcoidosis","Endomyocardial fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Restrictive cardiomyopathy is characterized by impaired diastolic filling with preserved systolic function. Causes include infiltrative diseases (amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis) and endomyocardial fibrosis.'),
('ee5a2400-944c-5064-9e7f-e333259a18ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal right ventricular end-diastolic pressure is approximately:', '["12-18 mmHg","0-2 mmHg","2-8 mmHg","8-12 mmHg","18-25 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '2-8 mmHg', 'Normal RVEDP is 2-8 mmHg (similar to CVP/RAP). It is elevated in RV failure, pulmonary hypertension, and pericardial constriction/restriction.'),
('f4a57fcf-5672-5c62-839e-556e9fbcc63e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following medications is most likely to cause torsades de pointes?', '["Sotalol","Procainamide","Amiodarone","Mexiletine","Lidocaine"]'::jsonb, 'Sotalol', 'Sotalol (Class III antiarrhythmic with beta-blocker activity) prolongs QT and has a significant risk of torsades de pointes. Amiodarone also prolongs QT but rarely causes torsades.'),
('f67867aa-059f-5bca-a9bf-3f56f10f3cd0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The concept of ''anrep effect'' refers to:', '["Decrease in HR with increased BP","Decrease in contractility with increased HR","Increase in HR with increased preload","Gradual increase in contractility following an increase in afterload","No change in contractility with stretch"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual increase in contractility following an increase in afterload', 'The Anrep effect: when afterload is suddenly increased, there is an initial decrease, followed by a gradual increase in contractility over several minutes, due to increased Ca2+ entry and myofilament sensitivity.'),
('cfa152d4-97c6-597f-b74e-cbea11af926a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following ECG leads are unipolar?', '["I and II","Both B and C","V1-V6","Both A and B","aVR, aVL, aVF"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and C', 'Augmented unipolar limb leads (aVR, aVL, aVF) and precordial leads (V1-V6) are unipolar. Bipolar limb leads (I, II, III) measure between two active electrodes.'),
('b6872a4d-52ef-5331-98d8-af2a9ed8e7ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of Prinzmetal (variant) angina?', '["Coronary artery spasm","All of the above","Occurs at rest","Responds to calcium channel blockers","ST elevation during pain"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Prinzmetal angina is caused by coronary artery spasm, occurs at rest (often at night), shows ST elevation (not depression) during pain, and responds well to Ca channel blockers and nitrates.'),
('7e8ccd90-158e-5407-971b-d6baeca95ee9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of isometric handgrip exercise on cardiac output is:', '["Unpredictable","Decrease","No change","Large increase","Moderate increase"]'::jsonb, 'Moderate increase', 'Isometric exercise (handgrip) causes a moderate increase in cardiac output (HR and contractility increase) along with a significant increase in BP from compression of blood vessels.'),
('9f6f919c-f06e-52d9-9343-22ec6393bcb4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common valvular abnormality in older adults?', '["Pulmonic stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Aortic regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic stenosis', 'Aortic stenosis is the most common valvular abnormality in older adults (>65), most commonly due to calcific degeneration of trileaflet valves or bicuspid aortic valve stenosis.'),
('58303564-0c9d-507b-bdde-6fce5b65d5e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following heart sounds is present in normal children and young adults but disappears with age?', '["S2","Opening snap","S4","S3","S1"]'::jsonb, 'S3', 'A physiologic S3 (ventricular gallop) is normal in children and young adults (<40 years). In older adults, S3 indicates LV dysfunction or volume overload. S4 is always pathologic.'),
('5faed546-899e-52ae-a67d-cff1f0654770'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of the Valsalva maneuver on the murmur of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is:', '["No change","Increased intensity","Murmur disappears","Murmur becomes continuous","Decreased intensity"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intensity', 'The Valsalva maneuver decreases preload, which increases LV outflow tract obstruction in HCM, making the systolic murmur louder. Squatting increases preload, decreasing the murmur.'),
('650ac156-b8af-50f5-bd2a-5fdd15896ad7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Lown-Ganong-Levine (LGL) syndrome is characterized by:', '["Short PR, wide QRS, delta wave","Long PR, wide QRS, T inversion","Normal PR, wide QRS, delta wave","Short PR, normal QRS, no delta wave","Short PR, normal QRS, and WPW pattern"]'::jsonb, 'Short PR, normal QRS, no delta wave', 'LGL syndrome involves a James fiber (atrio-Hisian bypass), causing a short PR interval but normal QRS duration (no delta wave). It may predispose to SVT.'),
('12f6a7b5-c333-5a6b-91bf-4a1529fd9503'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The coronary arteries are perfused during diastole because:', '["Sympathetic tone is higher in diastole","Aortic pressure is lower in diastole","Myocardial compression during systole impedes flow","Coronary flow is autoregulated","Diastole is longer than systole"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial compression during systole impedes flow', 'Left coronary flow is impeded during systole because the contracting myocardium compresses intramyocardial vessels. During diastole, compression is relieved and flow is maximal.'),
('e28f21f7-c96d-56f2-80bf-8594b10d52ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The endocardial viability ratio (EVR) combines which two factors?', '["Diastolic time and systolic pressure","Systolic BP and systolic time","Heart rate and systolic function","Cardiac output and resistance","Diastolic BP and diastolic time"]'::jsonb, 'Diastolic BP and diastolic time', 'EVR = (Diastolic BP × Diastolic time) / (Systolic BP × Systolic time). It estimates the supply/demand ratio for myocardial perfusion. EVR <1 suggests subendocardial ischemia.'),
('7e897b3a-9bd8-58d8-bfd7-27f6755912b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of a continuous murmur?', '["Patent ductus arteriosus","All of the above","AV fistula","Ruptured sinus of Valsalva","Aortopulmonary window"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Continuous murmurs occur when there is a pressure gradient throughout the cardiac cycle. Causes: PDA, AV fistula, ruptured sinus of Valsalva, aortopulmonary window, and venous hum.'),
('72c7077f-14fd-5709-8638-72d13be8ca43'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ECG pattern of ''de Winter'' T waves (upsloping ST depression with tall T waves in precordial leads) indicates:', '["Anterior STEMI equivalent","Posterior MI","Pericarditis","LVH","Normal variant"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior STEMI equivalent', 'DeWinter T waves (tall, symmetric T waves with upsloping ST depression in V2-V5) are a high-risk STEMI equivalent indicating proximal LAD occlusion requiring emergent reperfusion.'),
('2a768fa4-ddce-5064-a294-04f3694d7541'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''myogenic response'' in vascular smooth muscle refers to:', '["Relaxation in response to hypoxia","Vasoconstriction in response to increased intraluminal pressure","Contraction in response to neural stimuli","Vasodilation in response to increased metabolic demand","Relaxation in response to shear stress"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction in response to increased intraluminal pressure', 'The myogenic response is the intrinsic tendency of vascular smooth muscle to contract when stretched (increased intraluminal pressure) and relax when pressure decreases, contributing to autoregulation.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('8b00d6ce-1176-502a-bc0c-a2a35411e07d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most sensitive non-invasive test for detecting coronary artery disease?', '["Resting ECG","Exercise stress ECG","CT coronary angiography","Stress echocardiography","Myocardial perfusion imaging"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial perfusion imaging', 'Myocardial perfusion imaging (SPECT or PET) is highly sensitive (~85-90%) for detecting CAD. Exercise ECG is less sensitive (~65%). Coronary angiography remains the gold standard.'),
('67ccfb6f-7c39-5b58-891f-e0df95229034'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The phenomenon of ''concordance'' in the QRS-T complex refers to:', '["QRS and T in same direction","Axis deviation","QRS and T in opposite direction","Prolonged QT","Wide QRS"]'::jsonb, 'QRS and T in same direction', 'Normal QRS-T concordance: the T wave is in the same direction (upright) as the QRS complex in most leads (except aVR and V1). Discordance (e.g., T inversion with upright QRS) suggests pathology.'),
('3e5f58e4-a1d6-57a9-a728-51272d3dbbfa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of acute pericarditis?', '["Uremia","Bacterial infection","Autoimmune disease","Myocardial infarction","Viral infection"]'::jsonb, 'Viral infection', 'Viral infections (Coxsackie, Echo, influenza, EBV) are the most common cause of acute pericarditis. Other causes: idiopathic, uremia, post-MI (Dressler), TB, and connective tissue diseases.'),
('68cf577c-800d-5b2b-b6af-db3cdbcad1a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''reverse splitting'' of S2 is caused by:', '["Early aortic valve closure","Atrial septal defect","Wide splitting of S1","Delayed aortic valve closure","Delayed pulmonary valve closure"]'::jsonb, 'Delayed aortic valve closure', 'Reverse (paradoxical) splitting of S2 occurs when A2 is delayed (LBBB, aortic stenosis, RV pacing), so P2 precedes A2. The split widens during expiration (opposite of normal).'),
('8379e1ec-910b-530e-99c8-40f00539588b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of high-output cardiac failure?', '["Anemia","AV fistula","Thyrotoxicosis","All of the above","Paget disease of bone"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'High-output HF: increased cardiac output due to high metabolic demand or shunting, but still inadequate for tissue needs. Causes: thyrotoxicosis (↑metabolism), anemia (↓O2 carrying), Paget (↑vascularity), AV fistula (shunt).'),
('487afc83-ebcf-59ef-9052-fd22a31cedc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The S1 heart sound is loudest in which condition?', '["Mitral stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Heart failure","Aortic stenosis","Pericarditis"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral stenosis', 'Loud S1 occurs in mitral stenosis because the mitral valve leaflets are still wide open at the onset of systole (due to high LA pressure) and close forcefully. S1 is soft in MR and low LV contractility.'),
('4155adcf-4194-54cc-95c8-46f80d4d9a71'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is true of the ''delta wave'' on ECG?', '["Is always pathologic mediated physiological mechanism","Leads to prolonged PR mediated physiological mechanism","Indicates early ventricular activation via an accessory pathway","Indicates slow AV conduction mediated physiological mechanism","Is caused by LVH mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Indicates early ventricular activation via an accessory pathway', 'Delta wave is the slurred upstroke of the QRS in WPW syndrome, caused by early ventricular activation via an accessory pathway (Kent bundle). It widens QRS and shortens PR.'),
('6c6b64ed-71af-55a8-8b49-1d9606972d07'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The carotid sinus is located at the:', '["Bifurcation of the common carotid artery","Origin of the external carotid","Subclavian artery","Origin of the internal carotid","Aortic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Bifurcation of the common carotid artery', 'The carotid sinus is a dilation at the bifurcation of the common carotid artery. It contains baroreceptors that sense blood pressure changes and regulate HR and BP via the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX).'),
('84340455-ee32-5897-9fa8-6f9986919040'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following electrolyte abnormalities is most arrhythmogenic?', '["Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hypokalemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hyperphosphatemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypokalemia', 'Hypokalemia is highly arrhythmogenic, causing prolonged QT, prominent U waves, and predisposing to ventricular arrhythmias, especially in patients on digoxin or with myocardial ischemia.'),
('d3b655b5-9a52-52e3-adff-424ff920e2c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal cardiac index is:', '["8.5-10.0 L/min/m²","4.5-6.0 L/min/m²","1.5-2.0 L/min/m²","2.5-4.0 L/min/m²","6.5-8.0 L/min/m²"]'::jsonb, '2.5-4.0 L/min/m²', 'Cardiac Index (CI) = Cardiac Output / Body Surface Area. Normal CI = 2.5-4.0 L/min/m². CI <2.2 suggests low cardiac output states. CI >4.0 may indicate high-output states.'),
('4cd66abb-187b-5448-ad80-57ff37753e66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The diagnosis of left anterior fascicular block (LAFB) on ECG requires:', '["qR in I, aVL; rS in II, III, aVF","None of the above","QRS <120 ms","All of the above","Left axis deviation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Left anterior fascicular block criteria: left axis deviation (-45° to -90°), QRS <120 ms, qR in I/aVL, rS in II/III/aVF. It is the most common fascicular block.'),
('b3fd758c-86f7-5ee4-8d75-f0871cc985f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following conditions does NOT increase the risk of atrial fibrillation?', '["Hypothyroidism","Obesity","Endurance exercise","Hypertension","Moderate alcohol consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothyroidism', 'Hypothyroidism decreases metabolic rate and is associated with bradycardia, NOT AF. Risk factors for AF: obesity, alcohol, hypertension, sleep apnea, diabetes, heart disease, and endurance sports (atrial remodeling).'),
('cc94710b-6de8-53d6-9ea3-fdb4b552f5d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''baroreceptor resetting'' in chronic hypertension means:', '["Baroreceptors cause hypotension mediated physiological mechanism","Baroreceptors become more sensitive mediated physiological mechanism","Baroreceptors are destroyed mediated physiological mechanism","Baroreceptors adapt to higher BP and respond around the new set point","Baroreceptors no longer function mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Baroreceptors adapt to higher BP and respond around the new set point', 'In chronic hypertension, baroreceptors reset to operate at the higher BP level. They still buffer acute changes, but around the elevated baseline. This is why treated hypertensives may feel dizzy at ''normal'' BP.'),
('f1b48f3d-ba6b-50bf-b847-d2190787cd5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which type of heart block is most commonly associated with structural damage to the conduction system below the AV node?', '["Third-degree AV block","Mobitz type I","Sinoatrial block","First-degree AV block","Mobitz type II"]'::jsonb, 'Mobitz type II', 'Mobitz II is usually due to disease in the His-Purkinje system (infranodal), often with structural damage (fibrosis, infarction). It is more likely to progress to complete AV block than Mobitz I.'),
('226b4edd-a90a-5068-a71a-3ddb360b50ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The Tp-e interval (T peak to T end) on ECG is a measure of:', '["QT interval variability","Transmural dispersion of repolarization","Atrial repolarization","Ventricular action potential duration","PR interval"]'::jsonb, 'Transmural dispersion of repolarization', 'Tp-e interval reflects transmural dispersion of repolarization (difference between epicardial and M-cell repolarization). Increased Tp-e indicates arrhythmia risk, especially for torsades de pointes.'),
('06991f91-4038-5327-aea5-3a5847108b0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is NOT a component of the cardiac conduction system?', '["Papillary muscles","Bundle of His","AV node","SA node","Purkinje fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary muscles', 'Papillary muscles are muscular projections in the ventricles that attach to chordae tendineae, preventing AV valve prolapse. They are not part of the electrical conduction system.'),
('f7a12d30-3ccc-5164-ba3e-b463902cc72d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The increase in cardiac output during the first trimester of pregnancy is primarily due to:', '["Increased blood volume","Increased HR","All of the above","Increased stroke volume","Decreased afterload"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'During pregnancy, CO increases 30-50% due to increased HR (15-20%), increased stroke volume (from increased blood volume and decreased afterload), and decreased SVR.'),
('9fe26b81-34bf-53cf-8bd4-33e1e0acc71c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a typical finding in a patient with cardiac tamponade?', '["Muffled heart sounds","Kussmaul sign","Pulsus paradoxus","All of the above","Electrical alternans"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Cardiac tamponade features Beck''s triad (hypotension, muffled sounds, JVD), pulsus paradoxus, Kussmaul sign (rarely), and electrical alternans on ECG from cardiac oscillation.'),
('f90e38eb-b5ad-592e-9487-89e03803ca31'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''a'' wave in the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure tracing disappears in:', '["Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus rhythm"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial fibrillation', 'In atrial fibrillation, the ''a'' wave (due to atrial contraction) is absent in both JVP and PCWP tracings. The ''v'' waves become more prominent.'),
('c447f359-49e0-5479-85a2-6eac7556d91c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common ECG manifestation of acute pulmonary embolism?', '["Sinus tachycardia","Incomplete RBBB","Right axis deviation","T inversion in V1-V3","S1Q3T3 pattern"]'::jsonb, 'Sinus tachycardia', 'Sinus tachycardia is the most common ECG finding in PE (~70%). The classic S1Q3T3 (McGinn-White) is specific but seen in only ~15% of cases. Others: RV strain, RBBB, right axis.'),
('8c3bbe6c-58a6-5a00-9970-531b9952682f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal depolarization sequence of the interventricular septum occurs from:', '["Inferior to superior","Superior to inferior","Left to right","Simultaneous","Right to left"]'::jsonb, 'Left to right', 'Septal depolarization begins on the left side (first part of QRS), traveling to the right. This produces a small q wave in left-sided leads (I, aVL, V5-V6) and a small r wave in right precordial leads.'),
('b8800fc1-8d6b-5d28-bedb-7d9f09369d1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is NOT mediated by the baroreceptor reflex?', '["Increase in contractility with hypotension","Immediate vasoconstriction with hypotension","Immediate increase in HR with hypotension","Long-term regulation of mean arterial pressure","Bradycardia in response to hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Long-term regulation of mean arterial pressure', 'Baroreceptors are responsible for short-term (seconds-minutes) BP regulation. Long-term BP regulation involves renal mechanisms (RAAS, fluid balance). Baroreceptors adapt to chronic changes.'),
('04b94db8-f277-54ca-b94b-7aa390f5f32f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ECG finding of ''R-on-T phenomenon'' is significant because it can:', '["Indicate LVH","Cause heart block","Indicate acute MI","Precipitate torsades de pointes or VF","Cause atrial fibrillation"]'::jsonb, 'Precipitate torsades de pointes or VF', 'R-on-T occurs when a PVC lands on the T wave (vulnerable period of repolarization), potentially initiating torsades de pointes (with prolonged QT) or ventricular fibrillation.'),
('aa2af5f0-424c-56c4-88b3-86ddf2425cb5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal PR segment depression in acute pericarditis is best explained by:', '["Atrial ischemia","Myocardial infarction","Pericardial friction","Atrial inflammation","Ventricular injury"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial inflammation', 'PR depression in pericarditis is due to atrial inflammation/injury (atrial current of injury). It is most commonly seen in leads II, III, aVF, and V5-V6. It often precedes ST elevation.'),
('a1b449ab-3d57-5a1a-8b7b-a138a44db606'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of diastolic dysfunction?', '["LV hypertrophy","Myocardial fibrosis","Diabetes","All of the above","Aging"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Diastolic dysfunction (impaired relaxation) is caused by LVH, fibrosis, aging (increased stiffness), diabetes (glycation), ischemia, and infiltrative diseases. It leads to HFpEF.'),
('b49bc2cb-88d4-5b14-bb2e-e72eae84ce41'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The zigzag ECG pattern in lead V1 showing rSR'' with QRS <120 ms is called:', '["Incomplete RBBB","Brugada pattern","LVH","Normal variant","RBBB"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete RBBB', 'Incomplete RBBB (rSR'' in V1, QRS 100-119 ms) can be a normal variant, especially in young people, but can also indicate RV volume overload (ASD).'),
('618f8035-9012-5e3e-baeb-dac2f543829a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is characteristic of the carotid upstroke in aortic stenosis?', '["Pulsus alternans","Dicrotic pulse","Water-hammer (Corrigan) pulse","Pulsus parvus et tardus","Bisferiens pulse"]'::jsonb, 'Pulsus parvus et tardus', 'Aortic stenosis produces a weak, delayed carotid upstroke (parvus et tardus). A bisferiens pulse (bifid) is seen in HCM and AR. Water-hammer pulse is in AR.'),
('249cab1b-b368-5c6f-ba8e-0c93419d3b1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of squatting on the murmur of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is:', '["Murmur becomes diastolic","Murmur becomes continuous","Increased intensity","Decreased intensity","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased intensity', 'Squatting increases preload and afterload, decreasing LV outflow tract obstruction in HCM, making the murmur softer. Standing/Valsalva decrease preload, increasing the murmur.'),
('d767ca58-0d42-564b-8076-8fad1b4503ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of death in patients with aortic stenosis?', '["Infective endocarditis","Sudden cardiac death","Heart failure","Myocardial infarction","Cerebral embolism"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden cardiac death', 'Sudden cardiac death is the most common cause of death in symptomatic aortic stenosis. Once symptoms develop (angina, syncope, HF), survival without surgery is poor (<2-3 years).'),
('597baab1-852d-50a8-8dd4-a44f2af11d95'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''cannon a wave'' in JVP is most commonly seen in:', '["Constrictive pericarditis","Complete AV block","Atrial fibrillation","Tricuspid stenosis","Pulmonary hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Complete AV block', 'Cannon ''a'' waves occur when the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, most commonly in complete AV block (atria and ventricles dissociated, atrium contracts during ventricular systole).'),
('7093b6f9-44a5-533a-a5c6-77d4701a5a7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'In which of the following conditions would you expect an increased PCWP with a normal CVP?', '["Pulmonary embolism","Cardiac tamponade","LV failure","Constrictive pericarditis","RV infarction"]'::jsonb, 'LV failure', 'LV failure increases PCWP (backward failure) while CVP is initially normal. As LV failure progresses and causes RV failure, CVP also rises. RV infarction increases CVP with normal PCWP.'),
('5236c402-ae99-5940-8dc4-300b1eb683c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of Type I second-degree AV block?', '["Occurs in AV node","Often due to increased vagal tone","All of the above","Usually benign","Responds to atropine"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Mobitz I (Wenckebach) is usually benign, often AV nodal, can be due to increased vagal tone (e.g., athletes), and responds to atropine. Mobitz II is more serious and infranodal.'),
('d28543ab-6a4c-57d2-ac16-59679f113491'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The concept of ''accentuated antagonism'' in the heart refers to:', '["Parasympathetic withdrawal only","Enhanced vagal effect during sympathetic stimulation","Constant vagal tone","Enhanced sympathetic effect","No interaction between ANS divisions"]'::jsonb, 'Enhanced vagal effect during sympathetic stimulation', 'Accentuated antagonism: the cardiac response to vagal stimulation is greater when background sympathetic activity is higher. This is due to M2 receptor inhibition of adenylyl cyclase (opposing β1 stimulation).'),
('f7a4b0f2-7003-5d58-86a5-66fc4219cdc4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of Eisenmenger syndrome?', '["Clubbing","All of the above","Pulmonary hypertension","Reversed (right-to-left) shunt through a previously left-to-right shunt","Cyanosis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Eisenmenger syndrome: long-standing left-to-right shunt causes pulmonary hypertension, which eventually reverses the shunt (right-to-left), causing cyanosis and clubbing. It is irreversible.'),
('2138be66-1f65-5e32-a600-bcf2e8ebcae3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The T wave represents which phase of the ventricular action potential?', '["Phase 2","Phase 1","Phase 3","Phase 4","Phase 0"]'::jsonb, 'Phase 3', 'The T wave corresponds to phase 3 (rapid repolarization) of the ventricular action potential. The QRS corresponds to phase 0 (depolarization). The ST segment corresponds to phase 2 (plateau).'),
('03a3e0b8-c3cc-5d1f-80b4-a524ff4c739d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common complication of myocardial infarction in the first 24 hours?', '["Arrhythmia","Pericarditis","Cardiac rupture","Heart failure","Ventricular aneurysm"]'::jsonb, 'Arrhythmia', 'Ventricular arrhythmias (VT/VF) are most common in the first 24 hours after MI. Reperfusion arrhythmias occur with thrombolysis/PCI. Late complications: aneurysm, rupture, pericarditis, MR.'),
('1a6f3dd6-6b31-5cd6-8ff7-78c1362c3dcd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Lidocaine''s antiarrhythmic mechanism is primarily:', '["Calcium channel blockade","Sodium channel blockade","Adenosine agonism","Potassium channel blockade","Beta-blockade"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium channel blockade', 'Lidocaine is a Class IB antiarrhythmic that blocks Na+ channels with rapid kinetics. It preferentially affects ischemic tissue and is used for ventricular arrhythmias (especially post-MI).'),
('51ce503f-3b41-5ed5-ab35-6070d59bb40e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal QRS axis in the frontal plane in adults is:', '["0° to +90°","-30° to +90°","+180° to -90°","+90° to +180°","-30° to -90°"]'::jsonb, '0° to +90°', 'Normal QRS axis is 0° to +90° (some sources say -30° to +90°). Left axis: -30° to -90°. Right axis: +90° to +180°. Extreme axis: -90° to +180°.'),
('d5e8e746-c6f8-51f8-853a-843fd42ac824'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of increased pulse pressure?', '["Severe anemia","All of the above","Hyperthyroidism","Aortic regurgitation","PDA"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Increased pulse pressure (SBP - DBP) occurs when stroke volume increases or diastolic runoff is rapid: AR (regurgitant runoff), PDA (aortic-to-pulmonary shunt), anemia (high CO, low SVR), hyperthyroidism (high CO).'),
('7a242acf-cc51-568b-adf7-a80d095f246c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The hemodynamic hallmark of constrictive pericarditis is:', '["Kussmaul sign","Dip-and-plateau (square root) sign in ventricular pressure","Pulsus paradoxus","Equalization of diastolic pressures in all cardiac chambers","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Constrictive pericarditis: diastolic pressures equalize (LVEDP = RVEDP = RAP = PCWP ~15-25 mmHg), dip-and-plateau pattern, Kussmaul sign, and pulsus paradoxus.'),
('2f7e6838-35cc-526a-8bd3-040f8591611d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following murmurs increases with inspiration?', '["Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Tricuspid regurgitation', 'Tricuspid regurgitation increases with inspiration (Carvallo sign) because increased venous return augments RV volume and regurgitant flow. Left-sided murmurs decrease with inspiration.'),
('3e2aa122-925d-5d11-a61f-fac727d6a4f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The specialized conduction cells in the heart differ from working myocardial cells by:', '["All of the above","Having fewer myofibrils","Having automaticity","Having gap junctions","Having ability to conduct faster"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Conduction cells (nodal, Purkinje) have fewer myofibrils (poorly contractile), exhibit automaticity (pacemaker activity), have gap junctions for conduction, and can conduct impulses rapidly.'),
('b0ff6bf5-159a-532f-9de2-188e565a4428'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ECG in a patient with hypercalcemia shows:', '["Shortened QT","Peaked T waves","Prolonged QT","U waves","Wide QRS"]'::jsonb, 'Shortened QT', 'Hypercalcemia shortens the QT interval (mainly ST segment). Hypocalcemia prolongs the QT interval. ECG shows: shortened ST segment, normal T wave, and possible Osborn (J) wave in hypothermia.'),
('0f49fc77-3302-5fd3-8b8d-700d6f02bae8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''fail-safe'' mechanism of the heart refers to:', '["Frank-Starling mechanism","Baroreflex","Anrep effect","Bowditch effect","Coronary autoregulation"]'::jsonb, 'Frank-Starling mechanism', 'The Frank-Starling mechanism acts as a fail-safe: if venous return increases, the heart automatically pumps more blood without requiring external regulation. This balances left and right ventricular output.'),
('2a40d29a-1a17-5602-9016-20f494a3d912'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of acute aortic regurgitation?', '["Normal LV size","Pulsus bisferiens","Wide pulse pressure","Early diastolic murmur","Soft S1"]'::jsonb, 'Normal LV size', 'Acute AR (e.g., endocarditis, aortic dissection) presents with a soft/absent S1 (premature mitral valve closure), normal LV size, short early diastolic murmur, and pulmonary edema.'),
('42944568-2178-51df-8313-d6eb8f1a7947'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The role of ATP-sensitive potassium channels (K-ATP) in cardiac ischemia is:', '["They are activated by increased ATP","They open during ischemia, shortening action potential duration","They prolong the QT interval","They cause vasoconstriction","They have no role in ischemia"]'::jsonb, 'They open during ischemia, shortening action potential duration', 'K-ATP channels open when ATP decreases (ischemia). K+ efflux shortens the action potential duration, reducing Ca2+ entry and contractility, protecting the heart by reducing energy consumption.'),
('c4ccc249-6d9e-581e-958d-4168137bd52f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is NOT a cause of right axis deviation?', '["Left posterior fascicular block","COPD","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Pulmonary hypertension","Lateral wall MI"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral wall MI', 'Lateral wall MI (loss of lateral forces) causes RIGHT axis deviation. Wait, actually lateral wall MI shifts the axis RIGHT because the lateral forces (I, aVL) are lost. But the most classic causes of RAD are RVH, RBBB, COPD, LPFB, and PE.'),
('6ea0453c-e122-5baf-af73-875238b6a6b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The triple product (HR × SBP × PCWP) is a measure of:', '["Coronary blood flow","Myocardial O2 consumption","Vascular resistance","LV contractility","Cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial O2 consumption', 'The triple product is a more accurate index of myocardial O2 consumption than rate-pressure product. It incorporates PCWP as a measure of preload/wall tension.'),
('0b56f730-1cea-55ae-97a5-e8176b7f4c4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of the ECG in hypothermia?', '["Shivering artifact","Bradycardia","J (Osborn) waves","Prolonged PR, QRS, QT","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Hypothermia ECG: J (Osborn) waves at the QRS-ST junction, bradycardia, prolonged PR/QRS/QT, and muscle tremor artifact. Severe hypothermia can lead to VF.'),
('77d708f2-4eb9-565a-ba1d-f247aa79353b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The phenomenon of ''pseudonormalization'' of T waves refers to:', '["Upright T waves becoming inverted","Inverted T waves becoming upright","Biphasic T waves","Flat T waves","Peaked T waves"]'::jsonb, 'Inverted T waves becoming upright', 'Pseudonormalization: previously inverted T waves (from chronic ischemia or LVH) transiently become upright during an acute ischemic episode, mimicking normalization but actually indicating acute ischemia.'),
('d57a3a85-ddf6-5f5c-91e2-3ac7f8196e7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following statements about the cardiac cycle is correct?', '["Atrial systole and ventricular systole coincide","Atrial systole occurs during QRS","Atrial systole occurs during ventricular systole","Atrial systole occurs during the latter part of ventricular diastole","Ventricular systole ends during the P wave"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial systole occurs during the latter part of ventricular diastole', 'Atrial systole occurs during late ventricular diastole (starts after P wave). Ventricular systole begins after QRS and ends after T wave. The atria contract before the ventricles.'),
('68cfa395-f03a-5891-b761-cf061c76c436'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of a ventricular aneurysm on ECG?', '["Poor R wave progression","Pathological Q waves","Persistent ST elevation after MI","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'LV aneurysm (post-MI) shows persistent ST elevation (weeks-months after MI) with Q waves in the same leads. It does not necessarily cause poor R wave progression.'),
('30ce8201-fa7d-581d-98dd-fccdb90a422b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The most important factor that determines the blood pressure in the aorta is:', '["Blood volume","Heart rate","Peripheral resistance","Arterial compliance","Cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'Arterial compliance', 'Arterial compliance (elasticity of the aorta and large arteries) is the primary determinant of systolic BP and pulse pressure. Stiff arteries (decreased compliance) increase systolic BP and widen pulse pressure.'),
('a9e47446-5976-54cd-9c34-fb0010e18c21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of ''low T wave'' on ECG?', '["Hypokalemia","Pericarditis","All of the above","LVH","Myocardial ischemia"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Low amplitude or flattened T waves can be due to ischemia, electrolyte imbalance (hypokalemia), pericarditis, LVH, and normal variants. T <1 mm in limb leads or <2 mm in precordial leads is abnormal.'),
('8c04d103-8035-585c-8b23-a3c980d8b1bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The brain-heart connection in stress cardiomyopathy (Takotsubo) involves:', '["Coronary spasm","Microvascular dysfunction","Catecholamine excess causing myocardial stunning","All of the above","Apical ballooning"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Takotsubo (stress cardiomyopathy) is triggered by severe emotional/physical stress, causing catecholamine surge → microvascular spasm, myocardial stunning, and apical ballooning, mimicking STEMI but without coronary obstruction.'),
('ff017709-c30d-5a1e-8214-28f878e2697f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''U wave'' on ECG is thought to represent:', '["Atrial depolarization","Late repolarization of Purkinje fibers or papillary muscles","Atrial repolarization","AV nodal conduction","Ventricular septal depolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Late repolarization of Purkinje fibers or papillary muscles', 'The U wave (small deflection after T wave) likely represents late repolarization of the Purkinje fibers or papillary muscles. Prominent U waves occur in hypokalemia, bradycardia, and LVH.'),
('075315a9-8915-5c74-8afa-906e80ed71d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a feature of the Brugada syndrome Type 1 ECG pattern?', '["Negative T wave in V1-V3","RBBB pattern","None of the above","All of the above","Coved ST elevation ≥2 mm in V1-V3"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Brugada Type 1: coved ST elevation ≥2 mm followed by negative T wave in V1-V3, with RBBB-like morphology. This pattern is diagnostic and indicates high risk for VF/sudden death.'),
('287c5053-9e79-547d-8a5b-5557b380689b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The mechanism of cardioprotection from ischemic preconditioning involves:', '["PKC activation","All of the above","K-ATP channel opening","Adenosine receptor activation","Reduced mitochondrial injury"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Ischemic preconditioning (brief ischemia before prolonged ischemia) protects the heart via adenosine receptors, K-ATP channels, PKC, and other signaling pathways that reduce mitochondrial damage and infarction.'),
('6b9111b6-7f24-5340-b725-92edc06907d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of deep T wave inversion?', '["Apical HCM","Wellens syndrome","Cerebrovascular accident","LVH strain pattern","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Deep T wave inversion can occur in Wellens syndrome (proximal LAD stenosis, T inversion V2-V3), LVH strain, CVA (neurogenic T waves), apical HCM, and PE.'),
('2ba8e5a2-2561-549d-be2a-355f4cb59dc8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ECG pattern of ''P pulmonale'' is best seen in which leads?', '["I and aVL","V1-V2","II, III, aVF","aVR","V5-V6"]'::jsonb, 'II, III, aVF', 'P pulmonale (right atrial enlargement) shows tall, peaked P waves >2.5 mm in leads II, III, aVF. P mitrale (left atrial enlargement) shows wide, bifid P in II and negative P in V1.'),
('f11786b7-f99e-5acc-83b9-36568da9fbf1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following murmurs is mid-systolic?', '["Aortic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation","Ventricular septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic stenosis', 'Aortic stenosis produces a mid-systolic (ejection) murmur, starting after S1, rising and falling (crescendo-decrescendo), and ending before S2. MR, TR, and VSD are holosystolic.'),
('13755a74-f1b4-582d-aec3-8e3fcd3aac46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The physiologic basis of the third heart sound (S3) is:', '["Rapid ventricular filling abruptly halted","Semilunar valve closure","Turbulent flow in great vessels","AV valve closure","Atrial contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid ventricular filling abruptly halted', 'S3 is caused by the sudden deceleration of blood flow during rapid ventricular filling in early diastole. When the ventricle is non-compliant or volume-overloaded, the vibration is audible.'),
('b44df8a3-54d5-5008-8fbf-2bb0c400edc7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common cause of syncope in older adults?', '["Seizure","Cardiac arrhythmia","Vasovagal syncope","Psychogenic","Orthostatic hypotension"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac arrhythmia', 'Cardiac syncope (arrhythmias, structural heart disease) is the most common cause of syncope in older adults. Vasovagal is more common in young people. Orthostatic causes increase with age.'),
('901e54a9-e99e-5151-9b26-662002ffd899'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The combination of ST elevation in aVR with diffuse ST depression is most suggestive of:', '["Pericarditis mediated physiological mechanism","Inferior MI mediated physiological mechanism","Left main coronary artery occlusion","Takotsubo mediated physiological mechanism","Anterior MI mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Left main coronary artery occlusion', 'ST elevation in aVR with diffuse ST depression (especially V4-V6) is a pattern of left main coronary artery occlusion or severe three-vessel disease, indicating global subendocardial ischemia.'),
('8707db26-4e5c-5ff9-bafd-6690e3a05c92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''water-hammer pulse'' (Corrigan pulse) is characteristic of:', '["Pulmonary stenosis","HCM","Aortic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic regurgitation', 'Water-hammer pulse is a rapidly rising and collapsing pulse in aortic regurgitation due to increased LV stroke volume and rapid diastolic runoff into the LV. Also seen in PDA and hyperdynamic states.'),
('fb5144ba-1748-5b2c-bed5-e21ab2920cc3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of ''pulsus bisferiens''?', '["All of the above","Aortic stenosis + AR","None of the above","HCM","Mixed aortic valve disease"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Pulsus bisferiens (bifid carotid upstroke) is seen in HCM (with dynamic LVOT obstruction) and can also occur in aortic stenosis with AR or significant aortic regurgitation.'),
('353ab40a-e0a0-5cd9-8702-22b2ba0c62d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of pacing rate on cardiac output is:', '["Only decreases","Linear","Only affects stroke volume","No effect","Increases then decreases at very high rates"]'::jsonb, 'Increases then decreases at very high rates', 'Cardiac output increases with HR up to an optimal rate (~150-180/min), then decreases at very high rates because diastolic filling time is reduced, decreasing stroke volume.'),
('a1502b71-d686-50ea-9bec-801752ae2567'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of the ''chronic phase'' of Chagas cardiomyopathy?', '["Apical aneurysm","All of the above","Conduction abnormalities","Heart failure","Thromboembolism"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Chagas cardiomyopathy (Trypanosoma cruzi) features: apical aneurysm, biventricular failure, RBBB + LAFB (most common), complete AV block, ventricular arrhythmias, and thromboembolism.'),
('99640462-5690-5252-985d-ee383a6c9a78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The phenomenon of ''ventricular interdependence'' is most important in:', '["Pulmonary hypertension","Constrictive pericarditis","Aortic stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Dilated cardiomyopathy"]'::jsonb, 'Constrictive pericarditis', 'Ventricular interdependence: the filling of one ventricle affects the other because they share the interventricular septum and pericardium. It is exaggerated in constrictive pericarditis and tamponade.'),
('98ce7874-e340-5672-97a1-9d67be2c0750'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the best predictor of cardiovascular risk?', '["Total cholesterol","VLDL cholesterol","HDL cholesterol","LDL cholesterol","Triglycerides"]'::jsonb, 'LDL cholesterol', 'LDL cholesterol is the primary atherogenic lipoprotein and the best predictor of cardiovascular risk. Each 1 mmol/L (39 mg/dL) reduction in LDL reduces cardiovascular events by ~20-25%.'),
('7920b03a-4217-5668-b406-ddd95f7bc0e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''Gallowav rhythm'' refers to the combination of:', '["Opening snap","Soft S1 and S2","Loud S1","S3 or S4 with S1 and S2","Wide split S2"]'::jsonb, 'S3 or S4 with S1 and S2', 'Gallop rhythm is S1, S2 plus S3 (ventricular gallop) or S4 (atrial gallop). A summation gallop occurs when S3 and S4 fuse during tachycardia. It indicates heart failure.'),
('76f292d5-f72c-55d0-821e-369be1f5772f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the primary determinant of the T wave morphology?', '["Atrial depolarization","AV conduction time","Ventricular repolarization sequence","Ventricular depolarization sequence","Coronary perfusion"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular repolarization sequence', 'T wave morphology reflects the sequence of ventricular repolarization. Normally, epicardium repolarizes before endocardium, producing an upright T wave in most leads.'),
('540583fb-76ba-5fc0-a45b-43368364c6f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The condition of ''supravalvular aortic stenosis'' is associated with:', '["Turner syndrome","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Williams syndrome","Noonan syndrome","Marfan syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Williams syndrome', 'Supravalvular aortic stenosis is associated with Williams syndrome (7q11.23 deletion), which also features elfin facies, intellectual disability, and hypercalcemia.'),
('a313c639-9787-53b8-9adf-c7f8baa31f8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy?', '["All of the above","Atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter","PVCs with high burden","Persistent atrial fibrillation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy: chronic tachycardia (any supraventricular or ventricular arrhythmia with high rate/burden) can cause LV systolic dysfunction that is reversible with rate/rhythm control.'),
('68a5c560-b11b-514f-9571-6306a7301c90'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal P wave axis in the frontal plane is:', '["-30° to 0°","+90° to +120°","0° to +30°","+60° to +90°","+15° to +75°"]'::jsonb, '+15° to +75°', 'Normal P wave axis is +15° to +75° (mean ~+60°). Abnormal P axis suggests ectopic atrial rhythm (negative P in II, III, aVF = low atrial or junctional rhythm).'),
('75adddec-9b74-560a-8587-cb713f6a272b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following ECG patterns is associated with arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC)?', '["All of the above","Late potentials on SAECG","Wide QRS","Epsilon wave in V1-V3","T inversion in V1-V3"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'ARVC features: epsilon waves (small deflections after QRS in V1-V3), T wave inversion in right precordial leads, wide QRS, and late potentials on signal-averaged ECG. Risk of VT/sudden death.'),
('6a147ca1-d3aa-57e4-9cc8-1805ac46735a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of the ''Mueller maneuver'' (forced inspiration against closed airway) on the heart is:', '["Decreased preload and decreased afterload","Decreased afterload only","Increased preload and increased afterload","No change","Decreased preload and increased afterload"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased preload and increased afterload', 'Mueller maneuver (negative intrathoracic pressure) increases venous return (preload) but decreases LV afterload (negative pressure around the heart). It mimics many Valsalva effects.'),
('78569190-4bd0-56d8-9ab3-d09afc8cbb25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common mechanism of supraventricular tachycardia?', '["Vagally mediated","Triggered activity","Accessory pathway","Reentry","Enhanced automaticity"]'::jsonb, 'Reentry', 'Reentry is the most common mechanism of SVT (AVNRT, AVRT, atrial flutter, atrial tachycardia). Reentry requires a circuit with two conducting pathways with different refractory periods.'),
('59a87cf0-c642-5ac5-abae-25f6930e29b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''N / a'' wave in the JVP is commonly associated with:', '["Atrial fibrillation","Sinus rhythm","Tricuspid regurgitation","Constrictive pericarditis","Complete heart block"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial fibrillation', 'In atrial fibrillation, the ''a'' wave is absent because there is no organized atrial contraction. The JVP shows only ''v'' waves and a prominent ''c'' wave.'),
('b39163a7-e404-5edf-b82e-01fd925f854a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most reliable physical sign of increased jugular venous pressure?', '["Visible external jugular vein","Hepatojugular reflux positive","Absent pulsations","Distended neck veins at 45°","Kussmaul sign"]'::jsonb, 'Distended neck veins at 45°', 'Elevated JVD (>3-4 cm above sternal angle at 45°) is the most reliable sign of increased right atrial pressure. Hepatojugular reflux and Kussmaul sign are supporting findings.'),
('9fa14787-8b5d-521d-a11e-a6a32e0bf5d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following heart sounds is associated with the opening of a myxomatous mitral valve?', '["S4","Opening snap","S2","S1","Tumor plop"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor plop', 'Atrial myxoma produces a ''tumor plop'' sound when the pedunculated tumor passes through the mitral valve during diastole. It is often mistaken for an opening snap or S3.'),
('9c43892d-e207-55b2-bad4-885054ef35f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The normal intracardiac pressure in the left ventricle at end-diastole is:', '["4-12 mmHg","30-40 mmHg","25-30 mmHg","2-4 mmHg","15-20 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '4-12 mmHg', 'Normal LVEDP is 4-12 mmHg (mean ~8 mmHg). LVEDP >15 indicates LV diastolic dysfunction or volume overload. It correlates with PCWP and is elevated in HF.'),
('ee0c7f34-dbcf-510a-aa74-94b3a388133b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a cause of early diastolic murmur?', '["All of the above","None of the above","Aortic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Pulmonary regurgitation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Early diastolic murmurs begin immediately after S2 and include aortic regurgitation (decrescendo) and pulmonary regurgitation. Mitral stenosis produces a mid-diastolic murmur.'),
('00ab8306-8009-5ac2-90b4-aebe183b336e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''myocardial oxygen supply-demand ratio'' is best estimated by:', '["PCWP × HR","HR × BP","CO × SVR","SV × HR","DPTI/SPTI"]'::jsonb, 'DPTI/SPTI', 'The DPTI/SPTI ratio (Buckberg index) estimates the balance between myocardial oxygen supply (diastolic perfusion) and demand (systolic work). A ratio <0.7-0.8 suggests subendocardial ischemia.'),
('07cae12d-093a-5941-82da-89bdc862428e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following would be LEAST likely to cause acute pulmonary edema?', '["Hypertensive emergency","Acute mitral regurgitation","Acute aortic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Acute MI"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral stenosis', 'Mitral stenosis causes chronic pulmonary congestion. Acute pulmonary edema is more common in acute MR, acute AR (sudden LV volume overload), acute MI, and hypertensive crisis.'),
('4a0baa23-598d-5a71-9d3e-ffe1788dea01'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ECG findings of ST segment elevation in V1-V2 with tall R waves, downsloping ST depression in V5-V6 suggests:', '["Lateral STEMI","Posterior STEMI","Inferior STEMI","Anterior STEMI","Pericarditis"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior STEMI', 'Isolated posterior STEMI shows ST elevation in V1-V3 (tall R, ST↓) as a mirror image of the posterior wall injury. The ST segment in V1-V3 is actually elevated, but appears as ST depression in anterior leads.'),
('ad87c012-1084-5da2-8d9b-96bc4278f11a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a contraindication to exercise stress testing?', '["Acute MI","Uncontrolled arrhythmia","Severe aortic stenosis","Unstable angina","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Contraindications to stress testing: acute MI (<48h), unstable angina, severe symptomatic AS, uncontrolled arrhythmias, acute PE, acute myocarditis/pericarditis, and decompensated HF.'),
('362dc103-436e-5bd0-92f2-e83cab664b9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The physiologic splitting of S2 is due to:', '["Increased venous return during inspiration","Increased pulmonary vascular resistance during inspiration","Decreased pulmonary vascular resistance during inspiration","Increased HR during inspiration","Decreased venous return during inspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Increased venous return during inspiration', 'Inspiration increases venous return to the RV, increasing RV stroke volume and prolonging RV ejection, which delays P2. This causes the normal inspiratory splitting of S2.'),
('d3138a71-a0e6-5280-b9f2-608005d67086'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is associated with short PR interval and delta wave?', '["LGL syndrome","AV nodal reentry","Sinus tachycardia","Junctional rhythm","WPW syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'WPW syndrome', 'WPW syndrome: short PR + delta wave (slurred QRS upstroke) + wide QRS, due to accessory pathway (Kent bundle). Type A: delta wave positive in V1 (left-sided pathway). Type B: negative in V1.'),
('e5142620-ce95-5263-a813-cbae6771aa7e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''dicrotic pulse'' (two systolic peaks) is characteristic of:', '["Aortic regurgitation","HCM","Constrictive pericarditis","Sepsis","Aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Sepsis', 'A dicrotic pulse has two systolic peaks separated by a mid-systolic dip. It is classically associated with sepsis and low SVR states. Less commonly with HCM and aortic stenosis + AR.'),
('46b0e4d8-f67d-56f4-b76b-1fc9b5a911f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ECG lead that best visualizes the P wave for rhythm analysis is:', '["V1","V6","Lead I","aVR","Lead II"]'::jsonb, 'Lead II', 'Lead II best shows the P wave (upright, prominent) because its axis (+60°) aligns with the direction of atrial depolarization. It is the standard rhythm lead in most monitoring systems.'),
('aaa2bfcd-5a27-5604-99b8-2b4ebc555657'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following arrhythmias is characterized by a normal QRS duration and a ventricular rate of 150-250 bpm with abrupt onset and termination?', '["Ventricular tachycardia mediated physiological mechanism","Sinus tachycardia mediated physiological mechanism","Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia","Atrial flutter mediated physiological mechanism","Atrial fibrillation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia', 'AVNRT presents with narrow QRS tachycardia (150-250 bpm), abrupt onset/termination, and often no visible P waves (buried in QRS). It is the most common paroxysmal SVT.'),
('a59cf199-2c8d-5831-a0ab-bbc1798c53f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The treatment of choice for acute pulmonary edema with hypotension is:', '["IV morphine","Non-invasive ventilation","IV furosemide","IV dobutamine","IV nitroglycerin"]'::jsonb, 'IV dobutamine', 'Acute pulmonary edema with hypotension (low output state) requires inotropic support (dobutamine) along with diuretics. Nitroglycerin and diuretics are used when BP is adequate.'),
('dcadc8d2-2219-5114-b39a-da1f63ef7c18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following best describes the function of the intercalated disc in cardiac muscle?', '["Calcium storage mediated physiological mechanism","Protein synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Energy production mediated physiological mechanism","Action potential propagation and mechanical coupling between myocytes","Myofilament formation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Action potential propagation and mechanical coupling between myocytes', 'Intercalated discs contain gap junctions (electrical coupling) and desmosomes (mechanical coupling). They enable rapid spread of depolarization and coordinated contraction of cardiac muscle.'),
('960cf9ec-b946-52ce-ba10-c0fcc917455b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The QT dispersion (QT max - QT min) on a 12-lead ECG is increased in:', '["Long QT syndrome","Diabetes","Myocardial ischemia","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Increased QT dispersion (>40-50 ms) indicates heterogeneous repolarization and increased risk of ventricular arrhythmias. It is increased in LQTS, MI, diabetes, and cardiomyopathies.'),
('6b922d6d-d3d1-532c-8719-9b3f4b226e02'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is the most common valvular lesion in intravenous drug users?', '["Mitral regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Pulmonary stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Tricuspid regurgitation', 'IV drug users have a high risk of right-sided infective endocarditis (tricuspid valve), typically from S. aureus. This causes tricuspid regurgitation with septic pulmonary emboli.'),
('53115c38-93c9-5484-9d9b-75059ac560db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The effect of cardiac glycosides on the AV node is:', '["No effect","Shortened refractory period","Increased automaticity","Increased conduction","Decreased conduction"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased conduction', 'Digitalis decreases AV conduction velocity and prolongs the AV node refractory period (negative dromotropy) via increased vagal tone and direct effects. This is used to control ventricular rate in AF.'),
('210dd908-ec6a-594c-83d4-820889083482'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is characteristic of the ECG in hypermagnesemia?', '["Peaked T waves","Short QT","Prolonged PR and QRS","U waves","ST elevation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged PR and QRS', 'Hypermagnesemia (Mg >5 mg/dL) prolongs PR interval and QRS duration, similar to hyperkalemia. Severe hypermagnesemia can cause complete AV block and cardiac arrest.'),
('d3776788-f817-578a-8013-5a6dc6a2b594'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'The ''atrial kick'' contribution to ventricular filling is most important in:', '["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Normal heart","Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Atrial fibrillation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic stenosis', 'In conditions with reduced LV compliance (LVH, aortic stenosis, HCM), the atrial kick contributes 30-40% of LV filling. Loss of atrial contraction (AF) can cause significant hemodynamic compromise.'),
('c2b96067-d2c9-5727-95c1-057a5eb40498'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System', 'Which of the following is a key difference between the left and right ventricular pressure-volume loops?', '["None of the above","RV has smaller area","RV has lower systolic pressure","The RV loop is more triangular than rectangular","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The RV pressure-volume loop is more triangular (with minimal isovolumetric relaxation if pulmonary impedance is low). RV systolic pressure is lower (~25 vs 120 mmHg), and RV stroke work is less (~1/4 of LV).'),
('f6a3dd4d-2b80-5173-8a93-b35a5794447c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The cardiovascular centers in the medulla include:', '["Cardioaccelerator and cardioinhibitory centers","Only cardioaccelerator mediated physiological mechanism","Only cardioinhibitory mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebellar centers mediated physiological mechanism","Spinal centers mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Cardioaccelerator and cardioinhibitory centers', 'The medullary cardiovascular centers: cardioaccelerator (sympathetic: increases HR/contractility) and cardioinhibitory (parasympathetic: decreases HR). They integrate baroreceptor/chemoreceptor input.'),
('dc238fa5-b972-5af3-be19-60facfb2dc60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most important factor determining mean arterial pressure is:', '["Blood volume","Cardiac output and SVR","Venous return","Heart rate alone","Stroke volume alone"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output and SVR', 'MAP = CO x SVR. CO (HR x SV) and SVR are the two determinants of MAP. The body regulates both to maintain adequate tissue perfusion pressure.'),
('6a04afa1-282e-5b1b-aa5d-005b8a548451'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The arterial baroreceptor reflex is an example of:', '["Negative feedback","Open-loop control","Positive feedback","Feed-forward control","Unregulated response"]'::jsonb, 'Negative feedback', 'Baroreceptor reflex is a classic negative feedback loop: increased BP → baroreceptor firing → vagal activation + sympathetic inhibition → decreased HR/SVR → BP decreases toward normal.'),
('300e5d29-a6ec-5dbe-8e87-59b7d9a98392'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The Valsalva maneuver involves:', '["Coughing mediated physiological mechanism","Forced inspiration mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperventilation mediated physiological mechanism","Forced expiration against a closed glottis","Sneezing mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Forced expiration against a closed glottis', 'Valsalva: forced expiration against closed glottis → increased intrathoracic pressure → decreased venous return → decreased CO → baroreflex-mediated tachycardia/vasoconstriction.'),
('102d916d-989f-5468-80d9-dea1b55e9952'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'During the Valsalva maneuver''s release phase:', '["BP remains low","No change occurs","Venous return surges causing BP overshoot","Blood flow reverses","HR decreases during strain"]'::jsonb, 'Venous return surges causing BP overshoot', 'After Valsalva release, intrathoracic pressure normalizes, venous return and CO increase suddenly, causing BP overshoot above baseline, triggering baroreflex bradycardia.'),
('f8f33f01-1acf-570f-aeca-ee2eb4e77ff2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common cause of secondary hypertension is:', '["Chronic kidney disease","Pheochromocytoma","Renal artery stenosis","Primary aldosteronism","Coarctation of aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic kidney disease', 'Chronic kidney disease is the most common cause of secondary hypertension. Renal artery stenosis is the most common curable cause. Others include primary aldosteronism and pheochromocytoma.'),
('ce04561c-dba3-513c-a9e1-58872ff262dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ''pressure-natriuresis curve'' in hypertension is:', '["Flattened","Not changed","Vertical","Shifted to the left","Shifted to the right"]'::jsonb, 'Shifted to the right', 'In hypertension, the pressure-natriuresis curve is reset to the right: kidneys require higher BP to excrete the same amount of Na+. This maintains the elevated BP.'),
('e6f2df4f-0083-5371-86c5-f2e4d7502748'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The therapeutic effect of diuretics in hypertension is primarily due to:', '["Reduced HR","Increased SVR","Vasodilation","Renin inhibition","Reduced blood volume and CO"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced blood volume and CO', 'Diuretics initially decrease plasma volume and CO. Over time, SVR also decreases (due to reduced vascular wall Na+ and sensitivity to vasoconstrictors). This lowers BP.'),
('467ab6af-7361-5375-9466-fdfe82183337'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The mechanism of action of ACE inhibitors includes:', '["Decreased angiotensin II and increased bradykinin","Increased SVR mediated physiological mechanism","Increased angiotensin II mediated physiological mechanism","Increased aldosterone mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased bradykinin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased angiotensin II and increased bradykinin', 'ACE inhibitors reduce Angiotensin II (vasoconstriction, aldosterone) and increase bradykinin (vasodilation). The combination lowers BP effectively. Cough is a side effect from bradykinin.'),
('a48349a1-58f8-5d84-9493-62fed7b3189b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Orthostatic hypotension is defined as:', '["Drop in SBP ≥20 mmHg or DBP ≥10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing","Decrease in BP only after 10 minutes mediated physiological mechanism","Drop in SBP ≥10 mmHg mediated physiological mechanism","Increase in HR ≥30 bpm mediated physiological mechanism","Drop in DBP ≥20 mmHg mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Drop in SBP ≥20 mmHg or DBP ≥10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing', 'Orthostatic (postural) hypotension: SBP drop ≥20 or DBP drop ≥10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing. Causes: hypovolemia, autonomic failure, medications, prolonged bed rest.'),
('8945621e-8ca5-5aef-8c13-677a1522f0cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ''splanchnic circulation'' receives approximately what percentage of cardiac output?', '["15%","25%","35%","50%","10%"]'::jsonb, '25%', 'The splanchnic (GI, liver, spleen, pancreas) circulation receives about 25-30% of cardiac output. It serves as a large blood reservoir that can be mobilized during exercise or hemorrhage.'),
('0b99b63b-342e-5fcc-863f-8d4540d54cb1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ''muscle pump'' of the venous system:', '["Increases venous return during exercise","Causes venous stasis","Decreases venous return","Decreases cardiac output","Increases capillary filtration"]'::jsonb, 'Increases venous return during exercise', 'Contraction of skeletal muscles compresses deep veins, propelling blood toward the heart (one-way valves prevent backflow). This muscle pump is essential for venous return, especially during standing.'),
('e0beb772-02f8-5524-bc69-3adea9d58bd9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ''respiratory pump'' assists venous return because:', '["Inspiration decreases intrathoracic pressure, increasing the pressure gradient for venous return","Coughing increases return mediated physiological mechanism","Expiration increases venous return mediated physiological mechanism","Lung inflation compresses veins mediated physiological mechanism","The diaphragm blocks venous flow mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Inspiration decreases intrathoracic pressure, increasing the pressure gradient for venous return', 'During inspiration, negative intrathoracic pressure expands the right atrium, decreasing RAP and increasing the pressure gradient for venous return from peripheral veins.'),
('e5c7419f-0f62-5241-80c0-6996002b2440'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'What happens to total peripheral resistance during acute severe hemorrhage?', '["Increases","Decreases","Becomes zero","Fluctuates unpredictably","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Increases', 'Sympathetic activation during hemorrhage causes widespread arteriolar vasoconstriction, increasing SVR. This maintains BP despite decreased CO, but at the cost of reduced tissue perfusion.'),
('bff26deb-6262-5984-8193-3d272f425fd1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In late (decompensated) hemorrhagic shock:', '["CO increases mediated physiological mechanism","HR decreases mediated physiological mechanism","SVR continues to increase mediated physiological mechanism","SVR decreases due to failure of compensatory mechanisms","SVR remains normal mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'SVR decreases due to failure of compensatory mechanisms', 'In decompensated shock, severe acidosis, vasodilator metabolites, and loss of vascular tone cause SVR to fall, leading to refractory hypotension despite ongoing vasoconstrictor signals.'),
('73c04861-a27a-547e-8b39-9265011fb8e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ''central command'' during exercise refers to:', '["Venous return increase mediated physiological mechanism","Parallel activation of motor and cardiovascular centers from higher brain centers","Reflex from working muscles mediated physiological mechanism","Baroreceptor resetting mediated physiological mechanism","Chemoreceptor activation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Parallel activation of motor and cardiovascular centers from higher brain centers', 'Central command: higher brain centers (cortical, hypothalamic) simultaneously activate motor pathways and cardiovascular centers, causing immediate HR increase and vasodilation at exercise onset.'),
('078cbab7-f609-5e14-bdbf-c9de128eee60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The exercise pressor reflex originates from:', '["Carotid sinus mediated physiological mechanism","Lung stretch receptors mediated physiological mechanism","Skeletal muscle mechanoreceptors and metaboreceptors","Joint receptors mediated physiological mechanism","Aortic arch mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal muscle mechanoreceptors and metaboreceptors', 'Group III (mechanoreceptors) and Group IV (metaboreceptors) afferents from working muscles respond to contraction and metabolic byproducts, increasing sympathetic outflow and BP during exercise.'),
('06b4de88-b848-5bcb-be4a-e83b307a9e69'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following describes ''flow-mediated vasodilation''?', '["Increased shear stress stimulates endothelial NO production, causing vasodilation","Neural vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Myogenic relaxation mediated physiological mechanism","Increased pressure causes vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Metabolic vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased shear stress stimulates endothelial NO production, causing vasodilation', 'Flow-mediated vasodilation: increased blood flow increases shear stress on endothelial cells, stimulating eNOS to produce NO. NO diffuses to smooth muscle, causing relaxation and vasodilation.'),
('8d2accad-9d27-5d95-9113-25967bba2d2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary function of the arteriovenous anastomosis is:', '["Reabsorption","Gas exchange","Thermoregulation","Filtration","Nutrient exchange"]'::jsonb, 'Thermoregulation', 'AV anastomoses (shunts) directly connect arterioles to venules, bypassing capillaries. They are abundant in skin and regulate heat exchange by controlling skin blood flow.'),
('c46709c3-e8d9-52c3-a881-d9c476e380ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Capillary density is highest in:', '["Skin","Cardiac and skeletal muscle","Bone","Cartilage","Tendons"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac and skeletal muscle', 'Capillary density is proportional to metabolic rate. Cardiac muscle has the highest O2 consumption and capillary density. Skeletal muscle also has high density, varying by fiber type (higher in type I).'),
('ffc69050-d192-5611-ad1c-a6ac0062a6fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most important factor determining filtration across the glomerular capillary is:', '["Glomerular hydrostatic pressure","Bowman''s capsule pressure","GFR","Plasma oncotic pressure","Renal blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerular hydrostatic pressure', 'Glomerular filtration is driven by glomerular hydrostatic pressure (~55 mmHg). The filtration barrier is highly permeable. GFR autoregulation maintains constant filtration across BP changes.'),
('a3977007-27ed-55d3-9e30-2d19d4e85bbb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The efferent arteriole of the glomerulus supplies:', '["The bladder","The renal pelvis","The ureter","The renal artery","Peritubular capillaries"]'::jsonb, 'Peritubular capillaries', 'The efferent arteriole carries blood from the glomerulus to the peritubular capillaries (and vasa recta in juxtamedullary nephrons), which surround the tubules for reabsorption/secretion.'),
('e5814785-0a96-59ac-94fb-fba8827fbd93'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is a vasodilator?', '["Bradykinin","Vasopressin","Endothelin","Norepinephrine","Angiotensin II"]'::jsonb, 'Bradykinin', 'Bradykinin is a potent vasodilator (acts on B2 receptors, releasing NO and PGI2). It is degraded by ACE (which is why ACE inhibitors increase bradykinin, contributing to their BP-lowering effect).'),
('4cff9cb3-bc70-5fa2-94dc-2515f41953be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The T-tubule system in cardiac muscle:', '["Synthesizes proteins mediated physiological mechanism","Forms gap junctions mediated physiological mechanism","Produces ATP mediated physiological mechanism","Transmits the action potential into the cell interior","Stores calcium mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Transmits the action potential into the cell interior', 'T-tubules are invaginations of the sarcolemma that conduct the action potential to the cell interior, triggering calcium release from the SR. In cardiac muscle, T-tubules are larger than in skeletal muscle.'),
('134a791b-fa2e-511e-af2f-7681b7738c59'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The Frank-Starling mechanism ensures:', '["CO is always maximal","CO equals venous return","CO is independent of preload","LV and RV output are unequal","HR determines CO"]'::jsonb, 'CO equals venous return', 'Frank-Starling mechanism: increased venous return increases ventricular filling (preload), which increases stroke volume, ensuring that the heart pumps whatever blood it receives, matching CO to venous return.'),
('23bf0ccf-fb7b-5b5a-b9d2-ff563fcf94ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary compensation for hemorrhagic shock in the first hour is:', '["Hemodilution","Transcapillary refill","ADH release","Increased lymph flow","Transfusion"]'::jsonb, 'Transcapillary refill', 'Transcapillary refill: decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure (from vasoconstriction) allows fluid to move from interstitium into capillaries, auto-transfusing up to 1 L in the first hours.'),
('ab724009-65f4-5ddf-9d6f-d74f1113cee7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which organ has the highest O2 extraction ratio at rest?', '["Brain","Kidney","Liver","Skin","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart extracts ~60-70% of delivered O2 at rest (highest O2ER), leaving little reserve. This is why increased myocardial O2 demand requires increased coronary blood flow.'),
('f593ab44-25a0-58d9-bd9c-83f0e931cbe7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary determinant of capillary exchange in the brain is:', '["Blood-brain barrier","Hydrostatic pressure","High filtration rate","High capillary permeability","Oncotic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Blood-brain barrier', 'The blood-brain barrier (continuous tight junctions, pericyte support) restricts paracellular diffusion. Transport occurs via specific carriers (Glut1) and transcytosis, regulating the brain''s chemical environment.'),
('ff0949e5-2bfb-50c2-bde4-d19217907129'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Korotkoff sounds are produced by:', '["Capillary flow mediated physiological mechanism","Valve closure mediated physiological mechanism","Venous collapse mediated physiological mechanism","Cardiac contraction mediated physiological mechanism","Turbulent blood flow in a partially compressed artery"]'::jsonb, 'Turbulent blood flow in a partially compressed artery', 'Korotkoff sounds are heard during BP measurement as the cuff pressure falls between systolic and diastolic. Turbulent flow through the compressed artery creates the tapping sounds.'),
('f4fec94f-7c31-5034-8ae1-4f6a65b5a01d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The first Korotkoff sound corresponds to:', '["Systolic BP","Diastolic BP","Pulse pressure","Mean BP","CVP"]'::jsonb, 'Systolic BP', 'The first sound (phase I) heard as the cuff deflates corresponds to systolic BP. The sounds disappear (phase V) at diastolic BP. In children, phase IV (muffling) may be used.'),
('0ba3c911-a92d-5570-b64a-5c0328265381'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The pulsatile nature of blood flow is greatest in:', '["Aorta","Venules","Arterioles","Vena cava","Capillaries"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta', 'Pulsatility is maximal in the aorta (systolic-diastolic variation of ~40-50 mmHg). It decreases progressively through the arterial tree; capillaries have nearly continuous (non-pulsatile) flow.'),
('8e039a33-b230-5695-82db-f793ffc63a18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary site of blood flow regulation during exercise is:', '["Heart","Kidneys","Skin","Brain","Skeletal muscle arterioles"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal muscle arterioles', 'During exercise, skeletal muscle arterioles dilate dramatically (metabolic vasodilation), increasing muscle blood flow up to 20-fold. Sympathetic vasoconstriction is overridden by local metabolites.'),
('ce2882d5-5467-54d4-b1a6-9393073361f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ''bohr integration'' in the capillary describes:', '["O2 unloading facilitated by CO2 uptake","Temperature regulation","pH regulation","O2 loading","CO2 unloading"]'::jsonb, 'O2 unloading facilitated by CO2 uptake', 'The Bohr effect (and Haldane effect) are integrated in capillaries: O2 unloading facilitates CO2 uptake (Haldane), and CO2/H+ loading facilitates O2 unloading (Bohr), optimizing gas exchange.'),
('f6ea4f99-81b4-5450-a90b-84fdaf4abc19'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is the primary defense against edema?', '["Lymphatic drainage","All of the above","Low interstitial proteins","High interstitial pressure","Low capillary permeability"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Multiple safety factors prevent edema: low capillary permeability, lymphatic drainage clears excess fluid/proteins, interstitial hydrostatic pressure rises (buffering), and interstitial proteins are diluted.'),
('95d2a24b-0600-5561-96fa-ca0a315475a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ''edema safety factor'' in the lungs is approximately:', '["25 mmHg","5 mmHg","15 mmHg","10 mmHg","20 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '5 mmHg', 'The lung has an edema safety factor of about 5 mmHg, meaning lymphatics can handle increased filtration up to this pressure. Beyond this, pulmonary edema develops rapidly.'),
('1ee6a791-b866-54f3-979e-7f35e3336544'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common cause of pulmonary edema is:', '["Increased hydrostatic pressure","Increased permeability","Decreased oncotic pressure","High altitude","Lymphatic obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Increased hydrostatic pressure', 'Cardiogenic pulmonary edema (from increased PCWP due to LV failure, mitral stenosis, fluid overload) is most common. Non-cardiogenic (ARDS) involves increased permeability.'),
('69025948-190e-5c8d-9a7f-a12d540e450e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following occurs during compensated shock?', '["Normal BP, increased lactate","Hypotension, decreased lactate","Normal BP, normal lactate","Hypotension, increased lactate","Normal BP, decreased lactate"]'::jsonb, 'Normal BP, increased lactate', 'Compensated shock: BP is maintained by compensatory mechanisms, but tissue hypoperfusion still causes lactic acidosis. Normal BP does not exclude shock. Lactate is a key screening tool.'),
('69507098-7d1a-549d-99ce-070ba5cb038c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The Do2/VO2 relationship in shock shows that:', '["VO2 always depends on DO2","VO2 increases as DO2 decreases","VO2 is independent of DO2 until a critical threshold","VO2 is never related to DO2","DO2 always equals VO2"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 is independent of DO2 until a critical threshold', 'O2 consumption (VO2) is normally independent of O2 delivery (DO2) because O2 extraction can increase. Below a critical DO2 (~8 mL/kg/min), VO2 becomes supply-dependent (delivery-dependent shock).'),
('309122f7-5c0f-513f-b53a-4c256adc9584'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary advantage of balanced crystalloids (LR, Plasmalyte) over normal saline is:', '["Lower cost mediated physiological mechanism","Better O2 transport mediated physiological mechanism","Lower chloride content reduces risk of hyperchloremic acidosis","Less contamination mediated physiological mechanism","Higher oncotic pressure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Lower chloride content reduces risk of hyperchloremic acidosis', 'High-chloride solutions (NS, 0.9% saline) cause hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis and can impair renal function. Balanced solutions (LR) more closely mimic plasma electrolyte composition.'),
('2fe2fb86-f518-55e8-9a05-33e6b791bbaf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is a cause of high central venous pressure?', '["Cardiac tamponade","Tension pneumothorax","Hypovolemia","Right heart failure","All of the above except A"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above except A', 'Increased CVP occurs in RV failure, fluid overload, cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, and constrictive pericarditis. Hypovolemia DECREASES CVP.'),
('18644176-6192-5cf0-8b5c-09cada4660a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary determinant of venous return is:', '["Lymphatic pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Capillary pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Mean circulatory filling pressure - right atrial pressure","Heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Arterial pressure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Mean circulatory filling pressure - right atrial pressure', 'Venous return = (MCFP - RAP) / Venous resistance. The gradient between MCFP (~7 mmHg) and RAP (~2-4 mmHg) is the driving force. MCFP increases with volume or venoconstriction.'),
('d81a5fea-bc75-5ca1-8881-fa8762c67a8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is NOT a feature of septic shock?', '["Low SVR","High SVR","Low PCWP","Warm extremities","High cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'High SVR', 'Septic shock: early hyperdynamic phase has high CO, low SVR (vasodilation), warm skin, and low/normal PCWP. Late hypodynamic phase has low CO, high SVR, and cold skin.'),
('f16b936a-601d-5301-9f79-d9c2be529b1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most appropriate vasopressor for septic shock is:', '["Phenylephrine","Vasopressin","Epinephrine","Norepinephrine","Dobutamine"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine', 'Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor for septic shock (SSC guidelines). It provides potent α1 vasoconstriction with moderate β1 inotropy. Vasopressin and epinephrine are second-line.'),
('3762a9b5-376c-5d1d-a577-8efd6733a0e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Dobutamine is preferred over dopamine for cardiogenic shock because:', '["It increases HR more mediated physiological mechanism","It has better vasoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism","It is less expensive mediated physiological mechanism","It has better renal effects mediated physiological mechanism","It is a more potent inotrope with less chronotropy and fewer arrhythmias"]'::jsonb, 'It is a more potent inotrope with less chronotropy and fewer arrhythmias', 'Dobutamine (β1 > β2 agonist) increases contractility with minimal HR increase and some vasodilation (reduces afterload). Dopamine causes more tachycardia and arrhythmias at inotropic doses.'),
('d8fa4d44-c13b-5f96-a4f2-d9c5e7889d1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ''renal-dose dopamine'' concept has been debunked because:', '["Low-dose dopamine does not prevent or improve renal outcomes","It causes renal vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","It improves GFR mediated physiological mechanism","All of the above mediated physiological mechanism","It increases urine output mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Low-dose dopamine does not prevent or improve renal outcomes', 'Low-dose (renal-dose) dopamine was thought to protect kidneys, but evidence shows no benefit for renal outcomes and potential harm (arrhythmias). Fenoldopam is a selective DA1 agonist with better profile.'),
('3042ee4d-97a1-52f0-b326-ec375aa35da8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary danger of excessive fluid resuscitation in shock is:', '["Pulmonary edema","Hepatic congestion","Cerebral edema","Arrhythmias","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary edema', 'Excessive fluid can cause pulmonary edema (especially in cardiogenic shock or ARDS), worsening oxygenation. Balanced resuscitation guided by dynamic measures (SVV, PPV) is recommended.'),
('a6c79437-27b8-56a5-bb15-0425eccc4fd1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Dynamic measures of fluid responsiveness include:', '["PA pressure","Pulse pressure variation","CVP","LVEDP","PCWP"]'::jsonb, 'Pulse pressure variation', 'PPV and stroke volume variation (SVV) during mechanical ventilation predict fluid responsiveness. A high PPV (>13%) indicates likely benefit from fluids. Static measures (CVP, PCWP) are less reliable.'),
('2e60f341-71e7-5b00-9fa7-297d964fd691'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The passive leg raise (PLR) test:', '["Has no clinical value mediated physiological mechanism","Is invasive mediated physiological mechanism","Requires special equipment mediated physiological mechanism","Transfers ~300 mL of venous blood from legs to central circulation, simulating fluid bolus","Directly measures CO mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Transfers ~300 mL of venous blood from legs to central circulation, simulating fluid bolus', 'PLR: raising legs to 45° in a semi-recumbent patient auto-transfuses ~300 mL. If CO increases (measured non-invasively), the patient is fluid-responsive. It is reversible.'),
('c9e19d02-3128-5dfb-a2ea-f97c16774604'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which shock type requires the LEAST amount of fluid resuscitation?', '["Septic shock","Obstructive shock","Hypovolemic shock","Distributive shock","Cardiogenic shock"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiogenic shock', 'Cardiogenic shock: fluid overload is dangerous (pulmonary edema). Give judicious fluids (250-500 mL) only if no congestion. Hypovolemic and distributive shock require more.'),
('6c4fe0b5-a2a9-5a81-b65b-98e0e0da57c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The difference between ''warm'' and ''cold'' septic shock is:', '["Warm: low CO. Cold: high CO.","Warm: dry skin. Cold: moist skin.","Warm: high CO, low SVR. Cold: low CO, high SVR.","Warm: bradycardia. Cold: tachycardia.","Warm: vasoconstriction. Cold: vasodilation."]'::jsonb, 'Warm: high CO, low SVR. Cold: low CO, high SVR.', 'Early warm septic shock: vasodilation, high CO, bounding pulses, warm extremities. Late cold shock: vasoconstriction, low CO, cold mottled extremities, indicating decompensation.'),
('954f20d6-7fba-53ce-8e30-f73d94c4e9b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following best describes the relationship between pulse pressure and stroke volume?', '["Inverse relationship","Positive correlation","No correlation","Negative correlation","Exponential"]'::jsonb, 'Positive correlation', 'Pulse pressure (SBP - DBP) = SV / Arterial compliance. For a given compliance, higher stroke volume increases pulse pressure. Pulse pressure also increases with arterial stiffness.'),
('d4f89d81-ff6a-5872-981d-280082746f1f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common cause of monotonous (non-variable) wide-complex tachycardia is:', '["Sinus tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation","SVT with aberrancy","Ventricular tachycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular tachycardia', 'VT is the most common cause of wide-complex tachycardia in older adults or those with structural heart disease. Brugada criteria and Vereckei algorithm help differentiate VT from SVT.'),
('85364d95-9b29-59de-845d-cb92ab331139'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is an independent predictor of outcome in cardiogenic shock?', '["Rhythm","Heart rate","Age","BP","Lactate clearance"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate clearance', 'Lactate clearance (decrease over first 6-8 hours of treatment) is a strong independent predictor of survival in cardiogenic and septic shock. Failure to clear lactate indicates ongoing hypoperfusion.'),
('74de6652-3f0a-5738-9db4-4e6f02407291'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary cause of metabolic acidosis in shock is:', '["Lactic acidosis from anaerobic metabolism","Drugs mediated physiological mechanism","Ketoacidosis mediated physiological mechanism","Renal failure mediated physiological mechanism","Diarrhea mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Lactic acidosis from anaerobic metabolism', 'In shock, tissue hypoxia shifts metabolism to anaerobic glycolysis, producing lactate. Lactic acidosis (lactate >2 mmol/L) is a hallmark of inadequate O2 delivery and correlates with mortality.'),
('ae35a85b-a66e-5f55-9bc6-b89a38da8f09'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Base excess (BE) is defined as:', '["The HCO3- level mediated physiological mechanism","The pH of blood mediated physiological mechanism","The amount of strong acid needed to titrate blood to pH 7.4 at 37°C and PCO2 40 mmHg","The PCO2 level mediated physiological mechanism","The lactic acid level mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'The amount of strong acid needed to titrate blood to pH 7.4 at 37°C and PCO2 40 mmHg', 'BE (reference -2 to +2) quantifies the metabolic component of acid-base status. Negative BE (base deficit) indicates metabolic acidosis. A BE of -6 mEq/L suggests ~6 mEq/L bicarbonate deficit.'),
('fd52fc54-2b3c-5e37-a228-57e1844bb486'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In a patient with lactic acidosis and septic shock, the first priority is:', '["Treat the underlying infection","Correct the acidosis with bicarbonate","Start vasopressors","Give more fluids","Dialyze"]'::jsonb, 'Treat the underlying infection', 'The treatment of lactic acidosis is to treat the underlying cause. In septic shock, source control and antibiotics are paramount. Bicarbonate therapy is not recommended (no outcome benefit).'),
('a7ac0a67-dddb-504d-8b54-9dda827e0ac1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is the strongest vasopressor?', '["Dopamine","Norepinephrine","Epinephrine","Phenylephrine","Dobutamine"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine', 'Norepinephrine is the most potent α1 agonist (vasoconstrictor) with moderate β1 activity. Phenylephrine is a pure α1 agonist. Epinephrine has both α and β effects and is more potent but with more side effects.'),
('fb1f4c18-33e8-5ac9-a896-8b3cd381da4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary goal of early goal-directed therapy (EGDT) in septic shock was:', '["None","Achieve SBP >90 mmHg","All of the above","Achieve CVP 8-12 mmHg","Achieve ScvO2 >70% within 6 hours"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'EGDT (Rivers 2001) targeted CVP 8-12, MAP >65, and ScvO2 >70%. Recent trials (ProCESS, ARISE, ProMISe) found EGDT no better than usual care, but early recognition and treatment remain critical.'),
('8a8909f2-6b3c-552e-bf63-962f6536fc8d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which laboratory value is most useful for risk stratification in shock?', '["Bilirubin","Hemoglobin","Serum lactate","Creatinine","White cell count"]'::jsonb, 'Serum lactate', 'Lactate is the most important prognostic marker. Admission lactate >4 mmol/L in sepsis indicates high mortality (severe sepsis). Lactate clearance predicts response to resuscitation.'),
('7d8360bb-c11c-536e-a02b-04f0085983b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The normal lactate level is:', '["4.0-5.0 mmol/L","0.5-1.0 mmol/L","1.0-2.0 mmol/L","3.0-4.0 mmol/L","2.0-3.0 mmol/L"]'::jsonb, '1.0-2.0 mmol/L', 'Normal lactate is <2 mmol/L. Hyperlactatemia (2-4 mmol/L) may be due to increased production (shock, exercise) or decreased clearance (liver failure). Lactate >4 suggests severe tissue hypoxia.'),
('0b91946b-b99e-524f-9009-2121c092f68f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Type A lactic acidosis is caused by:', '["Liver disease","Malignancy","Metformin","Alcohol","Tissue hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue hypoxia', 'Type A: due to tissue hypoxia (decreased O2 delivery or increased O2 demand). Type B: without hypoxia (metformin, liver failure, thiamine deficiency, malignancy, inborn errors).'),
('53d9faa6-8f0e-5915-8ccb-17761e9a42d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is a cause of high ScvO2 (>80%) in shock?', '["Anemia","All","Hypoxia","Low CO","Cytopathic hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Cytopathic hypoxia', 'High ScvO2 (>80%) indicates decreased O2 extraction, either from impaired mitochondrial function (cytopathic hypoxia in sepsis) or arteriovenous shunting. It carries a poor prognosis.'),
('ee0d72a7-bae1-553d-b443-4faf60e5a7e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Venous oxygen saturation (SvO2) normally is:', '["95%","75%","55%","85%","65%"]'::jsonb, '75%', 'Normal mixed venous O2 saturation (SvO2) from PA catheter is ~75%. ScvO2 (from central line) is ~70% (slightly lower). Values <60% indicate inadequate O2 delivery or increased demand.'),
('2ae5c872-9cb7-548a-bc0f-ec0a0130b0bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The systemic vascular resistance (SVR) formula is:', '["MAP / CO","CO x SVR","HR x SVR","(MAP - PCWP) / CO","(MAP - CVP) / CO × 80"]'::jsonb, '(MAP - CVP) / CO × 80', 'SVR = (MAP - CVP) x 80 / CO. Normal SVR is 800-1200 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵. High in hypovolemic/cardiogenic shock. Low in distributive shock.'),
('e628f2c5-3df1-5765-a10e-ae0e77690617'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Normal pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) is:', '["600-800 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵","100-250 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵","50-100 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵","250-400 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵","400-600 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵"]'::jsonb, '100-250 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵', 'PVR = (MPAP - PCWP) x 80 / CO. Normal PVR is 100-250 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵. PVR >250 is pulmonary hypertension. PVR is lower than SVR because pulmonary circulation is a low-pressure system.'),
('36d51b4f-5988-5186-80d7-1c1b263a2e46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is a cause of pulmonary hypertension?', '["All of the above","Left heart disease","PE","Parenchymal lung disease","Idiopathic PAH"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Pulmonary hypertension (WHO groups): 1. PAH (idiopathic, connective tissue), 2. Left heart disease (most common), 3. Lung disease/hypoxia, 4. Chronic PE, 5. Miscellaneous.'),
('98c0238d-4c5f-5565-b457-e0101efbbe97'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The treatment of choice for PAH (pulmonary arterial hypertension) includes:', '["All of the above","PDE5 inhibitors","None","Prostacyclin analogs","Endothelin receptor antagonists"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'PAH therapy: endothelial receptor antagonists (bosentan, ambrisentan), PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil), prostacyclin analogs (epoprostenol, treprostinil), and calcium channel blockers (responders only).'),
('99c4210f-bfdb-5a38-b95e-0cd83a33ca49'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The initial management of hypertensive emergency with end-organ damage includes:', '["IV nitroprusside, labetalol, or nicardipine","Oral nifedipine mediated physiological mechanism","IV fluids mediated physiological mechanism","Beta-blockers alone mediated physiological mechanism","Diuretics alone mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'IV nitroprusside, labetalol, or nicardipine', 'Hypertensive emergency (BP >180/120 with end-organ damage) requires controlled BP reduction with IV agents: nitroprusside, labetalol, nicardipine, or clevidipine. Target: 25% reduction in first hour.'),
('82800f23-9fc1-56c5-9835-6134db4b6002'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is a contraindication to nitroprusside infusion?', '["Leber optic atrophy","Liver failure","All of the above","Renal failure","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Nitroprusside is contraindicated in renal failure (thiocyanate accumulation), liver failure (cyanide accumulation), and Leber optic atrophy (cyanide toxicity). It should be used for <48-72 hours.'),
('84791e04-fc41-5334-af76-9793c121e926'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The mechanism of action of hydralazine includes:', '["ACE inhibition","Direct arteriolar vasodilation","Alpha blockade","Calcium channel blockade","Beta-blockade"]'::jsonb, 'Direct arteriolar vasodilation', 'Hydralazine directly relaxes arteriolar smooth muscle (mechanism involves NO release and hyperpolarization). It causes reflex tachycardia, so it is often combined with beta-blockers.'),
('c1f16191-63a9-5b58-8c02-a99b1ec287d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The mechanism of action of calcium channel blockers includes:', '["Alpha blockade mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Diuresis mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Beta blockade mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Blockade of L-type Ca2+ channels, causing vasodilation and negative inotropy/chronotropy","ACE inhibition mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Blockade of L-type Ca2+ channels, causing vasodilation and negative inotropy/chronotropy', 'CCBs (verapamil, diltiazem, nifedipine) block L-type Ca2+ channels. Dihydropyridines (nifedipine) are predominantly vasodilators. Non-dihydropyridines (verapamil/diltiazem) also have cardiac effects.'),
('965769b0-128e-5ceb-b6f6-01a49a23a8de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following is the most common adverse effect of ACE inhibitors?', '["All of the above","Angioedema","Cough","Renal failure","Hyperkalemia"]'::jsonb, 'Cough', 'Dry cough is the most common side effect (~10-20%), due to bradykinin accumulation. Angioedema is rare but serious (<1%). Renal impairment and hyperkalemia are more common in CKD.'),
('f127b20d-9889-50d9-a195-1e40b59a3a85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The mechanism of action of ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) is:', '["Vasodilation only mediated physiological mechanism","Aldosterone blockade mediated physiological mechanism","Block AT1 receptors, preventing angiotensin II effects","ACE inhibition mediated physiological mechanism","Renin inhibition mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Block AT1 receptors, preventing angiotensin II effects', 'ARBs selectively block AT1 receptors (vasoconstriction, aldosterone, inflammation), leaving AT2 receptors unopposed (potentially beneficial vasodilation). They do not affect bradykinin (no cough).'),
('9bee3423-619f-5286-a45a-e64e56c858a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Direct renin inhibitors (e.g., aliskiren) work by:', '["Vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking aldosterone mediated physiological mechanism","Directly binding to renin, preventing angiotensinogen cleavage","Inhibiting ACE mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking AT1 receptors mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Directly binding to renin, preventing angiotensinogen cleavage', 'Aliskiren directly inhibits renin''s catalytic activity, reducing angiotensin I and II formation. It has a long half-life. Avoid combining with ACE inhibitors or ARBs in CKD/diabetes.'),
('0dc90bcc-8976-5482-9edb-085a2d8dead2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Spironolactone''s antihypertensive mechanism includes:', '["Vasodilation","Calcium channel blockade","Aldosterone receptor blockade","Beta blockade","ACE inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone receptor blockade', 'Spironolactone (K+-sparing diuretic) blocks mineralocorticoid receptors, reducing ENaC activity in collecting duct: increased Na+ and water excretion, K+ retention. Also has antifibrotic effects.'),
('d57c003f-af91-53e3-b32f-caeeb331c7bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Starling forces in capillary exchange include all EXCEPT:', '["Hydrostatic pressure","Interstitial colloid osmotic pressure","Blood viscosity","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure","Colloid osmotic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Blood viscosity', 'Starling forces: capillary hydrostatic pressure, interstitial hydrostatic pressure, plasma colloid osmotic pressure, interstitial colloid osmotic pressure. Blood viscosity affects flow resistance, not filtration.'),
('9cde9c0c-37da-563d-a926-7bcad5736166'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which pressure favors filtration at the arterial end of a capillary?', '["Capillary hydrostatic pressure","Capillary oncotic pressure","Interstitial colloid osmotic pressure","Plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Capillary hydrostatic pressure', 'Capillary hydrostatic pressure (~35 mmHg at arterial end) pushes fluid OUT of the capillary. It declines to ~15 mmHg at the venous end.'),
('95364f80-5fd3-527b-a341-409ea2473137'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Net filtration pressure at the venous end of a capillary is typically:', '["Equal to capillary hydrostatic pressure","Zero","Positive","Equal to plasma oncotic pressure","Negative"]'::jsonb, 'Negative', 'At the venous end, oncotic pressure (28 mmHg) exceeds hydrostatic pressure (15 mmHg), producing net inward (reabsorptive) force.'),
('f170624b-e521-5e1c-9437-57db2768401c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary force opposing filtration at the capillary level is:', '["Lymphatic drainage","Capillary hydrostatic pressure","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure","Plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Tissue tension"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma colloid osmotic pressure', 'Plasma colloid osmotic pressure (oncotic pressure) ~28 mmHg, mainly due to albumin, pulls water into capillaries.'),
('99122bc8-1c8c-5713-93a7-04e16bd2f3e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which protein contributes most to plasma colloid osmotic pressure?', '["Hemoglobin","Fibrinogen","Immunoglobulin G","Globulin","Albumin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin contributes ~70-75% of plasma oncotic pressure due to its high concentration (3.5-5 g/dL) and relatively low molecular weight.'),
('febd8e2f-3f0f-5c7e-b1f0-2122c7d8b823'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Kf in the Starling equation represents:', '["Filtration rate","Filtration coefficient","Reflection coefficient","Hydrostatic pressure gradient","Oncotic pressure gradient"]'::jsonb, 'Filtration coefficient', 'Kf = filtration coefficient, determined by capillary surface area and permeability. It is a measure of the capillary''s ability to filter fluid.'),
('a6ed8d3a-e8f1-512b-80ed-729a92c65c59'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The reflection coefficient (σ) in Starling''s equation ranges from:', '["1 to 10","0 to 100","0 to 1","0 to ∞","-1 to 1"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'σ = 1 means the membrane is impermeable to that solute; σ = 0 means freely permeable. For plasma proteins, σ ≈ 0.95 in most capillaries.'),
('c09c9ba4-0c41-5ab4-83d2-0e1aa4699518'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following increases capillary filtration?', '["Decreased capillary permeability","Increased plasma oncotic pressure","Decreased venous pressure","Arteriolar vasoconstriction","Arteriolar vasodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Arteriolar vasodilation', 'Arteriolar dilation increases capillary hydrostatic pressure, favoring filtration. This is why arteriolar dilators can cause edema.'),
('10d14d59-7a5d-5780-a432-315186c1f60b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Lymphatic system''s role in fluid balance is to:', '["Produce red blood cells","Return interstitial fluid to circulation","Filter blood plasma","Regulate body temperature","Secrete hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Return interstitial fluid to circulation', 'Lymphatics drain excess interstitial fluid (~2-4 L/day) and return it to the venous system via the thoracic duct.'),
('d31788b6-21a7-573e-8d0a-e9d41db99f2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Edema results primarily from:', '["Arteriolar vasoconstriction","Increased plasma oncotic pressure","Increased lymphatic drainage","Decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Increased net filtration pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Increased net filtration pressure', 'Edema occurs when filtration exceeds reabsorption, overwhelming lymphatic capacity. Causes: ↑ hydrostatic pressure, ↓ oncotic pressure, ↑ permeability, lymphatic obstruction.'),
('adff57e4-d638-53d7-a789-8512e73b0abc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common cause of generalized edema worldwide is:', '["Cirrhosis","Lymphatic obstruction","Heart failure","Renal failure","Hypoalbuminemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoalbuminemia', 'Hypoalbuminemia (from malnutrition, nephrotic syndrome, or liver disease) reduces plasma oncotic pressure → increased filtration → generalized edema.'),
('d26a0baf-eddb-5d03-a063-17de5b9a7a7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Pulmonary edema most commonly results from:', '["Decreased alveolar pressure","Increased pulmonary capillary pressure","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","Lymphatic obstruction in lungs","Increased capillary permeability only"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pulmonary capillary pressure', 'Pulmonary edema is most commonly cardiogenic—elevated left atrial pressure from LV failure increases pulmonary capillary pressure.'),
('5c5d2c6f-513c-5926-ae84-a33fa21a0477'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Capillary exchange of lipid-soluble substances occurs primarily via:', '["Active transport","Pinocytosis","Bulk flow","Filtration through intercellular clefts","Diffusion through endothelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Diffusion through endothelial cells', 'Lipid-soluble substances (O2, CO2) diffuse directly through endothelial cell membranes. Water-soluble substances use intercellular clefts or pores.'),
('d7b1fe09-8b31-563b-af23-0d26388108c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which capillary type has the highest permeability?', '["Tight junction","Continuous","Continuous","Sinusoidal","Fenestrated"]'::jsonb, 'Sinusoidal', 'Sinusoidal capillaries (liver, bone marrow, spleen) have large gaps allowing passage of cells and large proteins. Fenestrated capillaries (kidney, intestine) have small pores.'),
('c4a4a59e-a7b2-505c-99ae-1529b27e1abc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The capillary type found in the blood-brain barrier is:', '["Discontinuous","Continuous non-fenestrated","Channeled","Sinusoidal","Fenestrated"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous non-fenestrated', 'BBB capillaries are continuous with tight junctions, minimal pinocytosis, and lack fenestrations—restricting passage of many substances.'),
('2f18971f-1d36-53ef-9ab2-b97aac619208'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'At the capillary level, bulk flow is primarily responsible for:', '["Nutrient exchange","Hormone transport","Waste removal","Distribution of fluid volumes","Gas exchange"]'::jsonb, 'Distribution of fluid volumes', 'Bulk flow governs volume distribution between plasma and interstitium. Gases and small solutes exchange by diffusion, not bulk flow.'),
('09bcc9ce-3b36-53dd-bd04-4b8477eed90f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary determinant of net fluid movement across capillaries is:', '["Central venous pressure","Plasma sodium concentration","Cardiac output","The balance of Starling forces","Arterial blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 'The balance of Starling forces', 'Net filtration = Kf × [(Pc - Pi) - σ(πc - πi)]. The balance of hydrostatic and oncotic pressures determines direction and rate of fluid movement.'),
('9b76f8c0-2c26-5cb4-815f-632086a22945'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which would INCREASE capillary reabsorption?', '["Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Increased lymphatic obstruction","Arteriolar dilation","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","Increased interstitial oncotic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Increased interstitial oncotic pressure', 'Increased interstitial colloid osmotic pressure opposes reabsorption by reducing the net oncotic gradient favoring inward movement.'),
('07b87b22-e20d-59a1-a7ee-d9d1d686d35a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The lymphatic system returns approximately what volume of fluid to circulation daily?', '["0.5 L","20 L","8-10 L","10-15 L","2-4 L"]'::jsonb, '2-4 L', 'Lymphatic drainage returns 2-4 L of interstitial fluid per day plus filtered proteins. Blockage causes lymphedema.'),
('4172f1f7-9237-5484-81c2-d3121f01e844'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In heart failure, edema formation is primarily due to:', '["Increased capillary permeability","Increased venous hydrostatic pressure","Lymphatic obstruction","Decreased arterial pressure","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Increased venous hydrostatic pressure', 'Heart failure → elevated venous pressure → increased capillary hydrostatic pressure → increased filtration → edema.'),
('de5d578a-2123-572f-86b6-cace689c6445'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which Starling force is most altered in hypoproteinemia?', '["Interstitial colloid osmotic pressure","Filtration coefficient","Capillary hydrostatic pressure","Plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma colloid osmotic pressure', 'Hypoproteinemia (↓ albumin) reduces plasma oncotic pressure → net filtration increases → edema. Common in nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition.'),
('7ee00c91-c9c2-55d6-8b59-936d00d513d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The filtration coefficient Kf is determined by:', '["Capillary hydrostatic pressure","Capillary surface area and permeability","Lymphatic flow rate","Interstitial pressure","Plasma protein concentration"]'::jsonb, 'Capillary surface area and permeability', 'Kf = product of hydraulic conductivity and surface area. Increased in inflammation (↑ permeability), varies between tissues.'),
('d128abf7-94b5-5cbe-a305-58e402ee20d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following best describes the washdown phenomenon?', '["Dilution of interstitial proteins when filtration increases","Decreased capillary permeability reducing filtration","Increased lymph flow removing interstitial proteins","Decreased interstitial hydrostatic pressure","Increased plasma oncotic pressure reducing edema"]'::jsonb, 'Dilution of interstitial proteins when filtration increases', 'Washdown: increased filtration dilutes interstitial proteins, reducing interstitial oncotic pressure, which helps limit further edema formation.'),
('80734987-39e4-58d4-bc10-920609c91402'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The safety factor against edema includes all EXCEPT:', '["Washdown of interstitial proteins","Decreased interstitial oncotic pressure","Increased interstitial hydrostatic pressure","Increased lymph flow","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased plasma oncotic pressure', 'Edema safety factors: ↑ lymph flow, washdown, ↑ interstitial pressure, ↓ interstitial oncotic pressure. ↓ plasma oncotic pressure PROMOTES edema.'),
('5edfb6bc-3f75-51a4-9caa-e52d9cd7d2af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which is NOT a component of the Starling-Landis equation?', '["Plasma colloid osmotic pressure","Reflection coefficient","Capillary hydrostatic pressure","Cardiac output","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output', 'Starling-Landis: Jv = Kf[(Pc - Pi) - σ(πc - πi)]. Cardiac output does not appear directly—it affects Pc via hemodynamics.'),
('7955b4d3-01cc-587b-b659-34a7d5d3ad13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In the microcirculation, precapillary sphincters regulate:', '["Blood flow into individual capillaries","Venous return","Heart rate","Systemic blood pressure","Central venous pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Blood flow into individual capillaries', 'Precapillary sphincters (smooth muscle at capillary origins) control flow into individual capillaries based on local metabolic needs.'),
('bb127733-2ba8-5d7c-ad0a-86cbdb8f5c1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Shock is best defined as:', '["Loss of consciousness","Inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygenation","Tachycardia with hypotension","Decreased cardiac output","Low blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygenation', 'Shock = circulatory failure causing inadequate tissue O2 delivery. Hypotension is common but not defining—compensated shock can have normal BP.'),
('f7d78db0-f186-5ff6-8196-638b31442df1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The earliest stage of shock is called:', '["Decompensated shock","Refractory shock","Progressive shock","Compensated shock","Irreversible shock"]'::jsonb, 'Compensated shock', 'Compensated shock: homeostatic mechanisms (tachycardia, vasoconstriction, ↑ contractility) maintain BP despite reduced perfusion. BP may be normal.'),
('c93c5260-9fee-5ac3-90c5-06e3c4e84bdf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The transition from compensated to decompensated shock occurs when:', '["Lactate exceeds 2 mmol/L","Heart rate exceeds 100 bpm","Urine output drops below 30 mL/hr","Compensatory mechanisms fail","Systolic BP drops below 90 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 'Compensatory mechanisms fail', 'Decompensated shock: compensatory mechanisms can no longer maintain adequate perfusion → hypotension, organ dysfunction, metabolic acidosis.'),
('301447cf-e103-5d0b-a522-14e07b9fc5b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common type of shock is:', '["Hypovolemic shock","Cardiogenic shock","Distributive shock","Neurogenic shock","Obstructive shock"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemic shock', 'Hypovolemic shock (from hemorrhage, dehydration, burns) is the most common type worldwide, especially in trauma and surgical settings.'),
('14778f49-60a2-54c9-a183-ae91e9b74b8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Distributive shock includes all EXCEPT:', '["Adrenal crisis-related shock","Neurogenic shock","Hypovolemic shock","Septic shock","Anaphylactic shock"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemic shock', 'Distributive shock: septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic, adrenal crisis. Hypovolemic shock is a separate category due to actual volume loss.'),
('2c5236ca-d29c-506c-b6ac-f112c64f4ead'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which type of shock is characterized by low SVR and high cardiac output?', '["Cardiogenic shock","Distributive shock","Obstructive shock","Hemorrhagic shock","Hypovolemic shock"]'::jsonb, 'Distributive shock', 'Distributive shock: ↓ SVR (vasodilation), initially ↑ or normal CO (compensatory). Septic shock is the classic example.'),
('48f5cd03-8845-50c2-922d-25953ffaefe3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In cardiogenic shock, the primary problem is:', '["Bleeding","Vasodilation","Obstruction to flow","Pump failure","Volume loss"]'::jsonb, 'Pump failure', 'Cardiogenic shock: myocardial failure (MI, myocarditis, arrhythmia) → ↓ CO → ↓ perfusion → compensatory vasoconstriction.'),
('a205fa5e-085c-56ba-9a17-60a765770408'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Obstructive shock results from:', '["Myocardial pump failure","Loss of intravascular volume","Widespread vasodilation","Adrenal insufficiency","Physical obstruction to blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'Physical obstruction to blood flow', 'Obstructive shock: cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive PE, constrictive pericarditis obstruct venous return or outflow.'),
('ba3bbeb2-fc59-57f1-a95d-b82dd8d9533a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The earliest compensatory response to hypovolemic shock is:', '["Activation of renin-angiotensin system","Increased ADH secretion","Increased capillary reabsorption","Baroreceptor-mediated tachycardia","Release of endogenous catecholamines"]'::jsonb, 'Baroreceptor-mediated tachycardia', 'Baroreceptors sense ↓ BP → ↑ sympathetic outflow → tachycardia (fastest response). RAAS, ADH, and capillary reabsorption follow.'),
('d2648709-afb8-5623-ab95-276e77978e65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which receptor mediates vasoconstriction in shock?', '["Beta-1 adrenergic","Alpha-1 adrenergic","Beta-2 adrenergic","Dopaminergic D1","Muscarinic M2"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 adrenergic', 'α1-receptors on vascular smooth muscle mediate vasoconstriction (↑ SVR) in shock, shunting blood to vital organs.'),
('e29fdbc6-db38-5cab-b5cb-740823e2325d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In hemorrhagic shock, the initial heart rate response is:', '["No change","Bradycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Irregular rhythm","Tachycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Tachycardia', 'Tachycardia is an early compensatory response to ↓ stroke volume (due to ↓ preload) in hemorrhagic shock, mediated by ↑ sympathetic tone.'),
('e7df32ce-91e4-53f7-9ed6-34727702213e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The hallmark of decompensated shock is:', '["Tachycardia mediated physiological mechanism","Cool extremities mediated physiological mechanism","Anxiety mediated physiological mechanism","Hypotension despite compensatory mechanisms","Oliguria mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotension despite compensatory mechanisms', 'Decompensated shock: BP falls despite maximal compensation. Preceded by compensated shock (normal BP with signs of hypoperfusion).'),
('a120542e-c36e-5c1a-909d-4d5feff08d92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Lactic acidosis in shock indicates:', '["Liver dysfunction","Anaerobic metabolism from tissue hypoperfusion","Increased lactate clearance","Renal failure","Respiratory alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Anaerobic metabolism from tissue hypoperfusion', 'Inadequate O2 delivery → anaerobic glycolysis → pyruvate → lactate → lactic acidosis. Lactate > 2 mmol/L indicates hypoperfusion. > 4 mmol/L is severe.'),
('84225294-4287-5389-a2f7-6e46c4c3f47a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most reliable early sign of shock is:', '["Tachycardia","Hypotension","Altered mental status","Oliguria","Cool extremities"]'::jsonb, 'Tachycardia', 'Tachycardia is often the earliest sign. BP may be normal in compensated shock. Oliguria, AMS, and cool skin are later findings.'),
('1907d68a-0bf8-5572-af61-90f01772cbae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In septic shock, vasodilation is primarily mediated by:', '["Norepinephrine","Nitric oxide","Vasopressin","Angiotensin II","Endothelin"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide', 'Endotoxins/lipopolysaccharides → iNOS → ↑ NO → vascular smooth muscle relaxation → profound vasodilation → distributive shock.'),
('8a398753-8db0-5590-a26f-f359e315ad61'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The therapeutic goal in shock resuscitation is primarily to:', '["Increase blood pressure","Increase heart rate","Improve urine output","Restore tissue perfusion","Decrease lactate"]'::jsonb, 'Restore tissue perfusion', 'Goal: restore adequate O2 delivery to tissues. BP is a surrogate; endpoints include lactate clearance, ScvO2, urine output, and mental status.'),
('57b71d63-cb33-50c0-b894-2a746ca4d06b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following indicates inadequate tissue perfusion?', '["Lactate > 4 mmol/L","Urine output > 30 mL/hr","Normal mental status","Heart rate 70 bpm","Systolic BP > 100 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate > 4 mmol/L', 'Lactate > 4 mmol/L indicates severe hypoperfusion. Urine output < 0.5 mL/kg/hr is also a marker. Normal vitals don''t rule out compensated shock.'),
('c8ecc960-7d0e-54a7-acfe-1c0ad149d7e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Fluid resuscitation in hemorrhagic shock should initially be:', '["Isotonic crystalloid","Hypertonic saline","Blood products","D5W","Colloid"]'::jsonb, 'Isotonic crystalloid', 'Initial resuscitation: 1-2 L isotonic crystalloid. LR is preferred; NS may cause hyperchloremic acidosis. Blood products if hemorrhagic shock.'),
('ace68c19-9944-5a6c-8bd1-09c0a1ac0f6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In cardiogenic shock, the FIRST-line vasopressor is:', '["Dobutamine","Epinephrine","Norepinephrine","Vasopressin","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine', 'NE is first-line vasopressor in cardiogenic shock (↑ BP with less tachycardia than dopamine). Dobutamine is an inotrope, not a vasopressor.'),
('90ceab78-f6b7-5579-b33a-a7912225c81a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which vasopressor is preferred in distributive (septic) shock?', '["Vasopressin","Phenylephrine","Norepinephrine","Epinephrine","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine', 'NE is first-line in septic shock (α-agonist vasoconstriction, β-1 inotropy). Vasopressin may be added as second-line.'),
('e59beddc-02ba-5636-b0aa-b6cad51263ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The goal mean arterial pressure in shock resuscitation is typically:', '["> 65 mmHg","> 80 mmHg","> 100 mmHg","> 50 mmHg","> 90 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '> 65 mmHg', 'MAP ≥ 65 mmHg is the general target. Higher targets (≥ 75 mmHg) may benefit patients with known hypertension, but with risk of vasopressor complications.'),
('efedcc47-1618-5b42-a8e5-8ea2efad8e01'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In obstructive shock due to tension pneumothorax, the immediate treatment is:', '["Pericardiocentesis","Vasopressors","Needle decompression","Fluid bolus","Thrombolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Needle decompression', 'Tension pneumothorax → ↑ intrapleural pressure → ↓ venous return → obstructive shock. Immediate needle decompression (2nd ICS, MCL) then chest tube.'),
('880c27aa-8bcb-511d-9b13-186ba6e1b3ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In obstructive shock due to cardiac tamponade, the immediate treatment is:', '["Thrombolysis","Fluid bolus","Vasopressors","Needle decompression","Pericardiocentesis"]'::jsonb, 'Pericardiocentesis', 'Cardiac tamponade → fluid in pericardium → ↓ ventricular filling → ↓ CO. Pericardiocentesis relieves pressure and restores hemodynamics.'),
('9c2e8cb4-8312-5957-9462-4b6bd4183257'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Massive PE causing obstructive shock is best treated with:', '["Embolectomy","Thrombolysis","Heparin alone","Vasopressors only","IVC filter"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombolysis', 'Massive PE with shock → thrombolysis (alteplase) is indicated. Reduces clot burden and restores pulmonary blood flow. Surgical or catheter embolectomy if contraindicated.'),
('99f2642d-5364-5595-a9ba-2b4276f399ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The term irreversible shock refers to:', '["Ongoing tissue damage despite restored perfusion","Shock resistant to vasopressors","Stage where death is inevitable despite treatment","Refractory hypotension","Shock with multiorgan failure"]'::jsonb, 'Ongoing tissue damage despite restored perfusion', 'Irreversible shock: cellular damage progresses even after hemodynamic stabilization due to reperfusion injury, mitochondrial dysfunction, and widespread inflammation.'),
('1320ff17-ee85-571c-aa8e-033173d6ad6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Class I hemorrhagic shock involves blood loss of:', '["> 40%","> 50%","15-30%","30-40%","< 15%"]'::jsonb, '< 15%', 'Class I: minimal tachycardia, normal BP, normal RR. Mild anxiety. No fluid resuscitation typically needed; crystalloid if symptomatic.'),
('5543377d-893d-5fd1-9cc5-305752ede4d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Class II hemorrhagic shock is characterized by:', '["Profound hypotension and anuria","Altered mental status and hypotension","No symptoms","15-30% blood loss with tachycardia and narrowed pulse pressure","Mild anxiety only"]'::jsonb, '15-30% blood loss with tachycardia and narrowed pulse pressure', 'Class II: HR > 100, normal BP but narrowed PP, RR 20-30. Pallor, cool extremities, anxiety. Crystalloid resuscitation.'),
('9982b1c8-beab-5173-846f-f2341e0f4726'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In class III hemorrhagic shock, the typical heart rate is:', '["80-100 bpm","Normal","> 120 bpm","60-80 bpm","100-110 bpm"]'::jsonb, '> 120 bpm', 'Class III (30-40% loss): HR > 120, hypotension, tachypnea, AMS, oliguria. Requires blood transfusion + crystalloid.'),
('5fd8ed88-e56c-51bd-ab71-d26e1cef38fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Class IV hemorrhagic shock requires immediate:', '["Massive transfusion protocol","Vasopressors","Oral fluids","Observation","Crystalloid only"]'::jsonb, 'Massive transfusion protocol', 'Class IV (> 40% loss): profound hypotension, anuria, AMS. Requires MTP (PRBC:FFP:platelets) and emergency surgical hemostasis.'),
('6045f12e-e579-5689-bc9e-40bc25c7f8bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'During shock, blood flow is preferentially maintained to:', '["Brain and heart","Kidneys and liver","Gut and pancreas","Skin and muscle","Lungs and spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Brain and heart', 'Sympathetic vasoconstriction shunts blood away from skin, muscle, gut, and kidneys toward heart and brain (vital organ preservation).'),
('f51bfd06-f018-53ac-9840-5a00c8fee3c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system in shock causes all EXCEPT:', '["Vasodilation","Increased thirst","Water retention","Vasoconstriction","Sodium retention"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation', 'RAAS: renin → Ang I → Ang II (vasoconstrictor, ↑ aldosterone → Na/H2O retention, ↑ thirst). All effects aim to restore BP and volume.'),
('8ca54347-4f03-5fb3-8a04-95613b3d73eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'ADH (vasopressin) secretion in shock leads to:', '["Increased urine output","Water retention and vasoconstriction","Sodium excretion","Vasodilation only","Decreased thirst"]'::jsonb, 'Water retention and vasoconstriction', 'ADH ↑ water reabsorption in collecting ducts and causes vasoconstriction (V1 receptors). Released in response to ↓ volume and ↑ osmolality.'),
('c829ba46-8780-5af6-88ea-183f805c2a3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Cytokine storm in septic shock is characterized by:', '["Excessive pro-inflammatory cytokine release","Decreased vascular permeability","Immunosuppression","Metabolic alkalosis","Bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive pro-inflammatory cytokine release', 'TNF-α, IL-1, IL-6, IL-8 released in response to pathogens → vasodilation, capillary leak, myocardial depression, DIC.'),
('23b69bdd-3665-5186-b2c9-7759e47d76e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Capillary leak syndrome in sepsis leads to:', '["Pulmonary edema only","Hypovolemia from fluid shift to interstitium","Increased plasma oncotic pressure","Bradycardia","Hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemia from fluid shift to interstitium', 'Capillary leak (↑ permeability): plasma proteins and fluid escape to interstitium → ↓ intravascular volume → ↓ preload → shock.'),
('44473918-0913-57e9-9ef1-2df1a88f5be1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Myocardial depression in septic shock is mediated by:', '["Increased afterload","Endotoxin directly","Coronary vasospasm","Bacterial invasion of myocardium","TNF-α and IL-1β"]'::jsonb, 'TNF-α and IL-1β', 'TNF-α and IL-1β ↓ myocardial contractility, ↓ LVEF, and cause ventricular dilation. Reversible with recovery.'),
('5f9e5a0c-bb77-51fb-a56b-acf455e8b037'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The oxygen extraction ratio in shock typically:', '["Decreases","Is not measurable","Becomes zero","Increases","Stays the same"]'::jsonb, 'Increases', 'In shock ↓ O2 delivery → tissues extract more O2 (↑ O2ER). When O2ER max is reached, anaerobic metabolism begins → lactate rises.'),
('83ee939d-4f05-5639-a038-ac84c7e33bef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'ScvO2 < 70% indicates:', '["Excess oxygen delivery mediated physiological mechanism","Peripheral shunting mediated physiological mechanism","High cardiac output mediated physiological mechanism","Normal perfusion mediated physiological mechanism","Inadequate oxygen delivery relative to demand"]'::jsonb, 'Inadequate oxygen delivery relative to demand', 'ScvO2 < 70%: O2 consumption > O2 delivery. Goal ScvO2 ≥ 70% in EGDT for septic shock. Low values suggest hypoperfusion.'),
('5ab9367f-c0a2-5cba-b6a5-71bc07cc000f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Base deficit in shock reflects:', '["Mixed acid-base disorder mediated physiological mechanism","Respiratory acidosis mediated physiological mechanism","Metabolic acidosis from anaerobic metabolism","Metabolic alkalosis mediated physiological mechanism","Respiratory alkalosis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic acidosis from anaerobic metabolism', 'Base deficit quantifies metabolic acidosis. In shock, lactic acidosis consumes bicarbonate → negative base excess. > -6 mEq/L suggests severe shock.'),
('8f8884cf-d183-5e02-9829-4e519a59f4b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Central venous pressure (CVP) in hypovolemic shock is:', '["Normal","Unmeasurable","Low","Variable","High"]'::jsonb, 'Low', 'Low CVP reflects ↓ preload in hypovolemic shock. High CVP suggests cardiogenic or obstructive shock. Used to guide fluid resuscitation.'),
('4d336531-64ea-5112-98a9-8cfb8b0b607c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Pulmonary artery occlusion pressure (PAOP) is low in:', '["Hypovolemic shock","Septic shock with LV dysfunction","Mitral stenosis","Obstructive shock","Cardiogenic shock"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemic shock', 'Low PAOP in hypovolemic shock. High PAOP in cardiogenic shock (pulmonary congestion). Normal or low in distributive shock.'),
('f560d762-3dee-5622-9fce-8b3d05f7a4cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common cause of death in shock is:', '["Cardiac arrest","ARDS","Multiorgan dysfunction syndrome","Renal failure","Refractory hypotension"]'::jsonb, 'Multiorgan dysfunction syndrome', 'MODS is the leading cause of death in shock. Progressive organ failure (lungs, kidneys, liver, coagulopathy, CNS) from prolonged hypoperfusion.'),
('16458385-7b2d-56e9-94a2-99bd9bc350ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'ARDS in shock patients is characterized by:', '["Pneumothorax","Atelectasis","Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema","Pleural effusion","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema"]'::jsonb, 'Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema', 'ARDS: inflammatory lung injury → increased alveolar-capillary permeability → protein-rich pulmonary edema (non-cardiogenic). PaO2/FiO2 < 300.'),
('21112296-7615-5046-9425-60435f9600d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Acute kidney injury in shock is primarily due to:', '["Glomerulonephritis","Urinary obstruction","Renal artery thrombosis","Renal hypoperfusion","Direct nephrotoxin exposure"]'::jsonb, 'Renal hypoperfusion', 'Prerenal AKI from ↓ renal blood flow → ↓ GFR. If prolonged → acute tubular necrosis (intrinsic renal). Early volume resuscitation protects kidneys.'),
('4085266d-f3f0-5b96-8bce-2d99400bbb51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Stress ulcers in shock patients are caused by:', '["Bile reflux","Increased gastric acid secretion","NSAID use","H. pylori infection","Splanchnic hypoperfusion and ischemia"]'::jsonb, 'Splanchnic hypoperfusion and ischemia', 'Splanchnic vasoconstriction → gastric mucosal ischemia → breakdown of mucosal barrier → stress ulcers. Prophylaxis with PPI or H2 blocker.'),
('c1a2cd1a-6101-5260-9660-31f394c7f67d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) in shock results from:', '["Hemophilia A mediated physiological mechanism","Platelet excess mediated physiological mechanism","Vitamin K deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Widespread activation of coagulation cascade","von Willebrand disease mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Widespread activation of coagulation cascade', 'Shock → endothelial injury + inflammatory cytokines → tissue factor expression → widespread coagulation → microthrombi → consumption of clotting factors → bleeding.'),
('7e620c5c-2d61-5486-8b58-2473e0b8ca3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary goal of vasopressor therapy in shock is:', '["Increase urine output mediated physiological mechanism","Decrease heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Correct acidosis mediated physiological mechanism","Increase cardiac output mediated physiological mechanism","Increase MAP to adequate perfusion pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Increase MAP to adequate perfusion pressure', 'Vasopressors ↑ MAP ensuring adequate perfusion pressure for vital organs. Target MAP ≥ 65 mmHg. They do not directly ↑ CO or correct acidosis.'),
('133b8b8c-78c7-5390-a1ad-16c2230f6c2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Dobutamine is preferred over dopamine for inotropic support because:', '["Longer half-life","Less expensive","Better renal perfusion","Less tachycardia and arrhythmogenic potential","Stronger vasoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Less tachycardia and arrhythmogenic potential', 'Dobutamine (β1 agonist) ↑ contractility with less tachycardia and fewer arrhythmias than dopamine. Also causes mild vasodilation (β2).'),
('a7eaeaf7-8707-5374-8b38-6ab4aec1bd97'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In anaphylactic shock, the first-line drug is:', '["Norepinephrine","Epinephrine","Corticosteroids","Diphenhydramine","Albuterol"]'::jsonb, 'Epinephrine', 'Epinephrine (0.3-0.5 mg IM) is first-line: α-agonist vasoconstriction, β-agonist bronchodilation, ↑ cAMP → stabilizes mast cells.'),
('ab7f8c54-bb29-5736-a0cb-888c26600f71'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The pathognomonic feature of neurogenic shock is:', '["Hypotension with tachycardia","Hypertension with bradycardia","Hypertension with tachycardia","Normal vitals","Hypotension with bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotension with bradycardia', 'Neurogenic shock (spinal cord injury): loss of sympathetic tone → vasodilation (↓ SVR) + unopposed vagal → hypotension + bradycardia.'),
('fa6ff3b7-5bcc-5749-9a3a-4e0f5a5d17f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Fluid resuscitation alone is often insufficient in neurogenic shock because:', '["IV fluids worsen bradycardia","Fluids increase intracranial pressure","The blood-brain barrier blocks fluid","The patient is already hypervolemic","The primary problem is vasodilation, not hypovolemia"]'::jsonb, 'The primary problem is vasodilation, not hypovolemia', 'Neurogenic shock: ↓ SVR from loss of sympathetic tone. Vasopressors (phenylephrine or NE) are key. Fluids alone cannot correct vasodilation.'),
('9d57541c-bbea-501b-8dd4-784af1f4e5d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In obstructive shock from massive PE, the classic ECG finding is:', '["Left bundle branch block","ST elevation","Atrial fibrillation","S1Q3T3 pattern","T wave inversion"]'::jsonb, 'S1Q3T3 pattern', 'S1Q3T3: S wave in lead I, Q wave in III, inverted T in III. Indicates right heart strain from acute PE. May also show RBBB.'),
('bd72d1da-99a0-5949-97de-a265687e057b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The pathophysiology of shock in adrenal crisis involves:', '["Cortisol deficiency → ↓ vascular tone + ↓ cardiac contractility","Aldosterone excess → hypokalemia mediated physiological mechanism","ADH deficiency → dehydration mediated physiological mechanism","Excess cortisol → hypertension mediated physiological mechanism","Epinephrine excess → tachycardia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Cortisol deficiency → ↓ vascular tone + ↓ cardiac contractility', 'Adrenal crisis: cortisol deficiency → permissive vasodilation + ↓ catecholamine responsiveness + ↓ myocardial contractility → shock.'),
('a5c59e1f-f59a-58b4-8555-48faf3868c9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In burn shock, the primary cause of hypovolemia is:', '["Capillary leak and fluid shift","Sweating","Hemorrhage","Decreased intake","Vomiting"]'::jsonb, 'Capillary leak and fluid shift', 'Burn shock: ↑ capillary permeability → massive fluid shift from intravascular to interstitial space. Also evaporative losses from burned surface.'),
('eadc9358-6a45-5735-802c-ca0fd60ded00'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The Parkland formula for burn resuscitation calculates fluid needs in:', '["2 mL × %TBSA × weight","10 mL × %TBSA × weight","6 mL × %TBSA × weight","1 mL × %TBSA × weight","4 mL × %TBSA × weight"]'::jsonb, '4 mL × %TBSA × weight', 'Parkland: 4 mL LR/kg/%TBSA for first 24 hours. Half in first 8 hours, half in next 16 hours. Modified for children and electrical burns.'),
('92b66e8d-3288-5688-ab11-d6af530a89cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which hormone has both vasoconstrictor and antidiuretic effects?', '["Renin","Vasopressin","Atrial natriuretic peptide","Aldosterone","Angiotensin II"]'::jsonb, 'Vasopressin', 'ADH: V1 receptors → vasoconstriction; V2 receptors → water reabsorption in collecting ducts. Released by ↑ osmolality or ↓ volume.'),
('e242dd80-e356-5595-8fc8-2c14c989d034'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Angiotensin II causes all EXCEPT:', '["Increased sympathetic outflow","Vasodilation","Vasoconstriction","Aldosterone release","Increased thirst"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation', 'Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor (↑ SVR). Also ↑ aldosterone, ↑ thirst (hypothalamus), ↑ sympathetic activity, ↑ ADH release.'),
('f5b33755-eb78-5948-ba5a-985abb6329f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In compensated shock, blood pressure is maintained by:', '["Peripheral vasodilation","Decreased contractility","Decreased heart rate","Vagal activation","Increased systemic vascular resistance"]'::jsonb, 'Increased systemic vascular resistance', 'Compensated shock: ↑ SVR (vasoconstriction) maintains BP despite ↓ CO. If ↓ CO worsens, compensation fails → hypotension.'),
('7d31bfe9-70ba-57c3-b4f7-8cff30a96380'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The pulse pressure in hypovolemic shock is typically:', '["Narrowed","Normal","Widened","Increased","Unchanged"]'::jsonb, 'Narrowed', 'Narrowed pulse pressure (↓ systolic, ↑ or normal diastolic) due to ↑ SVR from compensatory vasoconstriction. A late sign.'),
('59c332b0-e3e2-5203-a4ed-83312d3573be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which of the following suggests inadequate fluid resuscitation?', '["Persistent tachycardia and oliguria","Improved mental status","Lactate clearance","Increased urine output > 30 mL/hr","Normalized blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent tachycardia and oliguria', 'Persistent tachycardia, oliguria, and elevated lactate indicate ongoing hypoperfusion despite fluid resuscitation. Further volume or vasopressors needed.'),
('4774f175-1aba-5c77-95dc-3517ba7b5d70'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Passive leg raise (PLR) as a fluid responsiveness test:', '["Predicts response to fluid bolus","Assesses lung water","Measures cardiac output directly","Evaluates valve function","Detects arrhythmias"]'::jsonb, 'Predicts response to fluid bolus', 'PLR: elevating legs → autotransfusion of ~300 mL → if ↑ CO by > 10%, patient is fluid-responsive. Reversible and non-invasive.'),
('15260a73-bcb8-5877-89ce-4fc466706e8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'A patient is fluid-responsive if stroke volume increases by ___ after a fluid challenge:', '["> 25%","> 50%","> 75%","> 10-15%","< 5%"]'::jsonb, '> 10-15%', 'Fluid responsiveness: ↑ SV or CO by > 10-15% after 250-500 mL crystalloid or PLR. Non-responders may vasopressors/inotropes.'),
('b4dfca08-6e99-5dac-a759-c378832f556b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary driver of the microcirculation in shock is:', '["Hormonal regulation","Local metabolic autoregulation","Myogenic response","Endothelial shear stress","Central sympathetic drive"]'::jsonb, 'Local metabolic autoregulation', 'In shock, local metabolic factors (adenosine, H+, K+, PO2) override central sympathetic tone to regulate microvascular flow. This can cause flow heterogeneity.'),
('5843386b-c858-5c5e-9e9b-b9d16d85e85f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Heterogeneity of microvascular blood flow in shock leads to:', '["Decreased lactate production","Improved tissue oxygenation","Increased O2 extraction","Uniform hypoperfusion","Regions of hypoxia adjacent to well-perfused areas"]'::jsonb, 'Regions of hypoxia adjacent to well-perfused areas', 'Microvascular flow heterogeneity: some capillaries fail to open despite high global O2 demand → tissue hypoxia despite normal global DO2.'),
('c5fe2ed6-ece0-5a45-9f7d-600c855718cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The glycocalyx layer on capillary endothelium:', '["Promotes platelet aggregation","Reduces plasma oncotic pressure","Regulates permeability and mechanotransduction","Increases vascular resistance","Increases capillary filtration"]'::jsonb, 'Regulates permeability and mechanotransduction', 'Glycocalyx: endothelial surface layer that regulates permeability, senses shear stress, and prevents leukocyte/platelet adhesion. Damaged in shock → ↑ leak.'),
('1b5ed4b8-5df7-505d-959f-0da5d3c9d170'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Shedding of the glycocalyx in shock leads to:', '["Improved oxygen delivery","Reduced inflammation","Enhanced coagulation","Increased vascular permeability","Decreased capillary leak"]'::jsonb, 'Increased vascular permeability', 'Glycocalyx shedding (from TNF-α, ROS, proteases) exposes endothelial adhesion molecules → ↑ permeability, ↑ leukocyte adhesion, capillary leak.'),
('a4de32e0-75b3-528a-a4a6-de84997bbb1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The role of pericytes in the microcirculation includes:', '["Filtering lymph","Producing red blood cells","Generating action potentials","Secreting hormones","Regulating capillary diameter"]'::jsonb, 'Regulating capillary diameter', 'Pericytes are contractile cells around capillaries that regulate diameter and blood flow, particularly in the CNS and retina. Dysfunctional in shock.'),
('92eb83c8-aead-56a0-9494-0d7519674022'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Nitric oxide (NO) in the microcirculation primarily causes:', '["Platelet aggregation","Leukocyte adhesion","Vasodilation","Vasoconstriction","Smooth muscle proliferation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation', 'NO produced by eNOS in endothelium diffuses to vascular smooth muscle → cGMP → relaxation → vasodilation. Key local regulator of microvascular tone.'),
('d63ec106-c258-566c-92f9-ea64867a171a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Endothelin-1 in shock contributes to:', '["Bronchodilation","Vasodilation","Decreased platelet aggregation","Excessive vasoconstriction and organ ischemia","Increased heart rate"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive vasoconstriction and organ ischemia', 'Endothelin-1 (from damaged endothelium) is a potent vasoconstrictor. Elevated in shock → excessive vasoconstriction → organ ischemia, especially renal.'),
('8e2760ac-acc8-5a03-8901-b36b5e131748'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The no-reflow phenomenon after resuscitation refers to:', '["Refractory vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Continued hypotension mediated physiological mechanism","Persistent microvascular obstruction despite restored macrovascular flow","Increased cardiac output mediated physiological mechanism","Improved tissue perfusion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent microvascular obstruction despite restored macrovascular flow', 'No-reflow: despite adequate MAP, microvessels remain obstructed by edema, leukocyte plugging, and microthrombi → continued tissue hypoperfusion.'),
('14d9a484-1ed1-5105-b2e0-e4575d6894e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Leukocyte adhesion to endothelium in shock is mediated by:', '["Albumin","Selectins and integrins","Complement factor C5a","Immunoglobulin E","Histamine"]'::jsonb, 'Selectins and integrins', 'Selectins (P-selectin, E-selectin) mediate rolling; integrins (LFA-1, Mac-1) mediate firm adhesion → transmigration → tissue inflammation and injury.'),
('428d92db-072d-51e2-8e1f-582991bccced'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The role of VEGF in capillary permeability is:', '["Promotes lymph flow mediated physiological mechanism","Causes vasoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism","Increases permeability by opening intercellular junctions","Inhibits angiogenesis mediated physiological mechanism","Decreases permeability mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increases permeability by opening intercellular junctions', 'VEGF (vascular endothelial growth factor) increases microvascular permeability by phosphorylation of VE-cadherin → opening of intercellular junctions.'),
('a111faaf-83ff-514a-b9cc-0611ede8a69b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Thrombomodulin and protein C system in shock:', '["Complement activation mediated physiological mechanism","Platelet activation mediated physiological mechanism","Vasoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism","Procoagulant effects mediated physiological mechanism","Anticoagulant and anti-inflammatory effects"]'::jsonb, 'Anticoagulant and anti-inflammatory effects', 'Thrombomodulin binds thrombin → activates protein C → inactivates factors Va and VIIIa (anticoagulant) + anti-inflammatory effects. Impaired in sepsis → DIC.'),
('10e4140f-1916-50be-aede-a95beca24ad8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Histamine released during anaphylaxis causes:', '["Vasoconstriction","Increased platelet aggregation","Bronchoconstriction","Vasodilation and increased capillary permeability","Decreased heart rate"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation and increased capillary permeability', 'Histamine H1 receptors: vasodilation, ↑ capillary permeability, bronchoconstriction, pruritus. H2: ↑ gastric acid, also vasodilation.'),
('cb88f93e-f6e1-56b3-bdfe-55e07e8432af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Bradykinin in shock contributes to:', '["Analgesia mediated physiological mechanism","Vasoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism","Bronchodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Platelet aggregation mediated physiological mechanism","Vasodilation and increased permeability"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation and increased permeability', 'Bradykinin: potent vasodilator (via B2 receptors → NO), increases capillary permeability, causes pain. Activated by contact system in sepsis/trauma.'),
('12ac6be9-5fbd-53bd-b4ab-3c4dbf5653f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The complement system in septic shock contributes to:', '["Tissue injury and further inflammation","Vasoconstriction","Reduced vascular permeability","Inhibition of cytokine release","Antibacterial defense only"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue injury and further inflammation', 'Complement activation (C5a, C3a) in sepsis → neutrophil activation, degranulation, ROS release, further endothelial injury, and MOF.'),
('19b36ade-c25e-5085-8fc8-bdc551654dcc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Mitochondrial dysfunction in shock leads to:', '["Reduced ROS generation","Decreased lactate","Cytopathic hypoxia","Improved cellular function","Increased ATP production"]'::jsonb, 'Cytopathic hypoxia', 'Mitochondrial dysfunction ↓ O2 utilization → continued anaerobic metabolism despite adequate DO2 → ↑ lactate. A key feature of septic shock.'),
('e436e948-bc51-5748-8a15-d90481b82322'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Reperfusion injury after shock resuscitation is mediated by:', '["Hypoxia","Hypothermia","Hyperglycemia","Alkalosis","Reactive oxygen species"]'::jsonb, 'Reactive oxygen species', 'Reperfusion: reintroduction of O2 → ↑ ROS (superoxide, H2O2, hydroxyl radical) from xanthine oxidase, mitochondria, and neutrophils → tissue injury.'),
('156a0bec-ac42-549e-9f6f-a1496f706e05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Ischemic preconditioning protects tissues by:', '["Inhibiting angiogenesis","Inducing tolerance to subsequent ischemia","Promoting apoptosis","Increasing metabolic demand","Reducing blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'Inducing tolerance to subsequent ischemia', 'Brief ischemia-reperfusion cycles before prolonged ischemia trigger protective pathways (↓ ROS, ↑ adenosine, ↑ HSP, ↑ NO) reducing infarct size.'),
('35714e8d-0fc7-5d94-8857-9ed33541d4a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Temperature management in shock resuscitation targets:', '["No temperature control","Normothermia","Hyperthermia","Hypothermia","Core temperature > 40°C"]'::jsonb, 'Normothermia', 'Maintain normothermia. Hypothermia worsens coagulopathy, acidosis, and metabolism. Hyperthermia increases O2 demand and inflammation.'),
('d1dac036-ccd0-5763-a078-8798d2f40b4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The role of albumin in shock resuscitation is:', '["Only in burn shock mediated physiological mechanism","Controversial; may be considered when crystalloids insufficient","Always preferred over crystalloids mediated physiological mechanism","First-line fluid mediated physiological mechanism","Contraindicated mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Controversial; may be considered when crystalloids insufficient', 'Albumins (4-5% or 25%) role debated. SAFE trial: equivalent to NS overall; subset with septic shock may benefit. More expensive, not first-line.'),
('0f1850b3-218c-535b-b0c7-b7047c707720'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Hypertonic saline in shock resuscitation works by:', '["Reducing sodium levels","Expanding interstitial volume","Causing vasodilation","Drawing fluid from interstitium into vasculature","Inducing diuresis"]'::jsonb, 'Drawing fluid from interstitium into vasculature', 'Hypertonic saline (3-7.5%) creates osmotic gradient → shifts water from interstitium to plasma → rapidly expands intravascular volume. Also ↓ intracranial pressure.'),
('f4246693-b644-5f21-ab90-a9ba42072047'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Balanced crystalloids (LR, Plasmalyte) are preferred over NS because:', '["Longer shelf life mediated physiological mechanism","Lower cost mediated physiological mechanism","Less risk of hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","Less allergic reactions mediated physiological mechanism","Better glucose control mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Less risk of hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis', 'NS (0.9% NaCl): high Cl- → hyperchloremic non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ↓ renal perfusion. LR/Plasmalyte have more physiologic Cl- levels.'),
('48bff0ff-cbbb-58b7-8761-a827b9d2bac3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The SMART trial demonstrated that balanced crystalloids:', '["Caused more hyperkalemia mediated physiological mechanism","Had no difference compared to NS mediated physiological mechanism","Reduced mortality and renal complications in critically ill","Increased mortality mediated physiological mechanism","Were only useful in sepsis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced mortality and renal complications in critically ill', 'SMART trial (NEJM 2018): balanced crystalloids ↓ major adverse kidney events and mortality in critically ill patients vs NS. Practice-changing.'),
('46ad183e-653a-5d04-9432-48468046e05f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Early goal-directed therapy (EGDT) for septic shock includes all EXCEPT:', '["Maintaining CVP 8-12 mmHg","Hemoglobin > 10 g/dL","MAP ≥ 65 mmHg","ScvO2 ≥ 70%","Urine output > 0.5 mL/kg/hr"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoglobin > 10 g/dL', 'EGDT: CVP 8-12, MAP ≥ 65, ScvO2 ≥ 70%, urine output ≥ 0.5 mL/kg/hr. Hb target ≥ 7 (later relaxed) if ScvO2 < 70% after CVP/MAP goals met.'),
('f736ef4f-a459-55f1-8cbb-308fcec00560'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The Rivers trial (2001) on EGDT demonstrated:', '["Reduced mortality in severe sepsis/septic shock","No benefit mediated physiological mechanism","Worsened renal function mediated physiological mechanism","Increased length of stay mediated physiological mechanism","Increased mortality mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced mortality in severe sepsis/septic shock', 'Rivers (NEJM 2001): EGDT ↓ mortality from 46.5% to 30.5% in septic shock. Later PROWESS-SHOCK, ARISE, ProCESS trials found modern usual care similarly effective.'),
('1516821f-05d2-5204-bdf9-4755b55a42a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The current Surviving Sepsis Campaign 1-hour bundle includes:', '["CT scan, MRI, echocardiogram mediated physiological mechanism","Corticosteroids for all patients mediated physiological mechanism","Urine output > 50 mL/hr mediated physiological mechanism","Central line insertion only mediated physiological mechanism","Measure lactate, blood cultures, broad-spectrum abx, fluids, vasopressors"]'::jsonb, 'Measure lactate, blood cultures, broad-spectrum abx, fluids, vasopressors', '1-hour bundle: ↓ lactate, blood cultures, broad-spectrum antibiotics, 30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension/lactate > 4, vasopressors if MAP < 65.'),
('2ce7ce1e-43f2-5864-964b-c63a8a4bf8aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In septic shock, empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics should be given within:', '["6 hours","3 hours","1 hour of recognition","12 hours","24 hours"]'::jsonb, '1 hour of recognition', 'SSC guidelines: antibiotics within 1 hour of shock recognition. Each hour delay increases mortality (Kumar et al., 2006). Cultures before abx if no delay.'),
('7ea6b423-624b-5d20-b9c3-b1a3edaf949a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Source control in septic shock refers to:', '["Fluid resuscitation","Antibiotic administration","Nutritional support","Surgical drainage/removal of infection source","Ventilator support"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical drainage/removal of infection source', 'Source control: drainage of abscess, debridement of necrotic tissue, removal of infected device. Essential, often within 6-12 hours.'),
('53d51d20-47af-56ba-af96-284ac9be45c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Corticosteroids in septic shock are indicated when:', '["All septic shock patients mediated physiological mechanism","When lactate > 2 mmol/L mediated physiological mechanism","Hemodynamic instability despite adequate fluids and vasopressors","Only with positive blood cultures mediated physiological mechanism","Only in pediatric sepsis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hemodynamic instability despite adequate fluids and vasopressors', 'HC 200 mg/day IV in refracory septic shock (CORTICUS, ADRENAL trials). HPA axis sometimes insufficient, but routine use not recommended.'),
('3db15eec-8434-5d62-9db7-9d967ff0238b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Activated protein C (drotrecogin alfa) for septic shock:', '["Only in children","Used in all septic shock","Equivalent to placebo","First-line therapy","Withdrawn from market; no survival benefit"]'::jsonb, 'Withdrawn from market; no survival benefit', 'PROWESS-SHOCK trial (NEJM 2012): no benefit. Drug withdrawn. Previously approved for severe sepsis with high APACHE scores.'),
('cab1778e-550c-5b46-b7f7-9e23c64a8fd6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Vasopressin in septic shock is used as:', '["For bradycardia","Second-line vasopressor when NE insufficient","Only in hypovolemic shock","Not recommended","First-line vasopressor"]'::jsonb, 'Second-line vasopressor when NE insufficient', 'VASST trial: adding vasopressin to NE did not reduce mortality overall but may benefit less severe shock. NE remains first-line. Vasopressin dose: 0.03 U/min.'),
('440c0311-eb02-5f8c-a694-a1929b3351a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Angiotensin II as a vasopressor (Giapreza) is indicated for:', '["Cardiogenic shock only mediated physiological mechanism","Pediatric shock mediated physiological mechanism","Hypovolemic shock mediated physiological mechanism","First-line in all shock types mediated physiological mechanism","Refractory distributive shock despite high-dose vasopressors"]'::jsonb, 'Refractory distributive shock despite high-dose vasopressors', 'ATHOS-3 trial: Ang II ↑ BP in vasodilatory shock refractory to high-dose vasopressors. Especially useful in ACE inhibitor overdose and hepatorenal syndrome.'),
('70819e18-8f07-53fc-acb3-537f9de08ba5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Methylene blue in refractory septic shock:', '["Improves renal function mediated physiological mechanism","Causes vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Reduces heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibits NO/guanylate cyclase pathway → ↑ vasoconstriction","Increases lactate clearance mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits NO/guanylate cyclase pathway → ↑ vasoconstriction', 'Methylene blue inhibits NO synthase and guanylate cyclase → reverses NO-mediated vasodilation. Used as rescue in refractory septic shock.'),
('0d4c4ff9-540a-5305-a817-28e0a1cb3d1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The role of ECMO in refractory shock is:', '["Contraindicated in shock mediated physiological mechanism","Only in pediatric patients mediated physiological mechanism","Permanent cardiac replacement mediated physiological mechanism","Temporary circulatory support for potentially reversible causes","First-line therapy mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Temporary circulatory support for potentially reversible causes', 'VA-ECMO provides biventricular + respiratory support in refractory shock (myocarditis, PE, post-cardiotomy). Bridge to recovery or longer-term device.'),
('848de2f6-4e1f-515b-aef3-86cc0eaf274b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ideal fluid for shock resuscitation should:', '["Contain glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Be hypertonic mediated physiological mechanism","Remain intravascular, be inert, and have physiologic composition","Be stored at 4°C mediated physiological mechanism","Contain steroids mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Remain intravascular, be inert, and have physiologic composition', 'Ideal: stays in vasculature, chemically inert, physiologic electrolyte composition, no histamine release, no effect on coagulation. No perfect fluid exists.'),
('b6d5d0ba-1d8e-513e-80a6-7d1ceb9f3e89'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Hydroxyethyl starch (HES) is no longer recommended due to:', '["Superior volume expansion","Increased mortality and renal injury","Improved coagulation","Lower cost","Fewer allergic reactions"]'::jsonb, 'Increased mortality and renal injury', 'CHEST, 6S trials: HES ↑ mortality, ↑ AKI, ↑ bleeding. Contraindicated in sepsis/septic shock. NOT recommended by SSC or FDA.'),
('22f844dd-9930-5c88-b8dd-9cc902ffae86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Gelatin-based colloids for shock resuscitation:', '["Superior to albumin mediated physiological mechanism","First-line fluids mediated physiological mechanism","Equivalent to crystalloids mediated physiological mechanism","Renal protective mediated physiological mechanism","Less evidence; potential for allergic reactions"]'::jsonb, 'Less evidence; potential for allergic reactions', 'Gelatins widely used in Europe but ↓ evidence. Risk of allergic reactions, coagulation impairment, and potential for prion transmission. Not FDA-approved.'),
('3eec0a5e-65a9-516d-8062-bcf319d51d5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The Frank-Starling curve in hypovolemic shock shows:', '["↑ contractility → ↑ SV","↓ afterload → ↑ SV","No change in SV","↓ preload → ↓ SV","↑ preload → ↓ SV"]'::jsonb, '↓ preload → ↓ SV', 'Hypovolemia → ↓ LVEDV (preload) → ↓ SV. On the steep portion of the Starling curve, small preload changes significantly affect SV → fluid responsiveness.'),
('66f83997-fa93-5793-9444-1b67e33e4c5a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Ventricular interdependence in shock refers to:', '["Direct communication between atria","Right ventricular overload affecting left ventricular filling","Independent function of both ventricles","Equalization of diastolic pressures","LV failure causing RV failure"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricular overload affecting left ventricular filling', 'In PE, RV hypertension → IVS shifts left → ↓ LV filling → ↓ CO. Also in tamponade, RV compression limits LV filling.'),
('36cb2063-4bb2-586e-a51b-dbe18439929d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Dynamic arterial elastance (Eadyn) predicts:', '["Ventilator weaning success","Need for inotropes","Fluid responsiveness","Response to vasopressor therapy","Risk of arrhythmia"]'::jsonb, 'Response to vasopressor therapy', 'Eadyn = pulse pressure variation / stroke volume variation. High Eadyn → vasopressors will ↑ MAP. Low Eadyn → fluid or inotropes may be needed.'),
('2ca77490-6b14-5f20-9746-ff573307964e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The ratio of heart rate to systolic BP (shock index) > 1.0 suggests:', '["Hypovolemia and need for resuscitation","Vasodilation","Hypertensive emergency","Normal blood volume","Hyperdynamic circulation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemia and need for resuscitation', 'Shock index = HR/SBP. Normal ~0.5-0.7. > 1.0 suggests significant hypovolemia and need for urgent resuscitation, even if SBP is normal.'),
('f2e0a60a-535f-5484-a92a-7fde9231a6e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Persistent lactate > 4 mmol/L after initial resuscitation indicates:', '["Successful resuscitation","Thiamine deficiency","Ongoing tissue hypoperfusion with high mortality","Liver disease","Hyperlactatemia from seizures"]'::jsonb, 'Ongoing tissue hypoperfusion with high mortality', 'Lactate clearance (↓ by > 20% in 2 hours) is associated with improved outcomes. Persistent elevation despite resuscitation indicates ongoing shock.'),
('9595ae7b-cf53-5178-9a40-761455c0996b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The optimal ScvO2 target in septic shock is:', '["> 70%","> 50%","> 60%","> 80%","> 90%"]'::jsonb, '> 70%', 'ScvO2 (central venous O2 saturation) > 70% suggested by Rivers EGDT. > 70% suggests adequate O2 delivery. Low ScvO2 < 70% triggers interventions (fluids, transfusion, inotropes).'),
('b2387c70-18cd-56c1-885e-bf9cfe318768'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Arterial-venous CO2 difference (Pv-aCO2 > 6 mmHg) indicates:', '["Respiratory alkalosis","Adequate perfusion","Inadequate cardiac output despite ScvO2","Effective resuscitation","Metabolic alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Inadequate cardiac output despite ScvO2', 'Wide Pv-aCO2 gap (> 6 mmHg) suggests low flow/CO even if ScvO2 > 70%. Useful when ScvO2 is misleading (e.g., septic shock with mitochondrial dysfunction).'),
('9754b3c2-93d0-5e4f-a701-d34bd4bd5995'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Volume responsiveness can be assessed by all EXCEPT:', '["Pulse pressure variation","Passive leg raise","Stroke volume variation","Central venous pressure","IVC collapsibility on ultrasound"]'::jsonb, 'Central venous pressure', 'CVP is a poor predictor of fluid responsiveness (meta-analyses show AUC ~0.56). PLR, PPV (> 13%), SVV (> 10-12%), IVC collapsibility are better dynamic measures.'),
('321f46d0-e00f-500c-b063-5faedb894422'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'PPV > 13% predicts fluid responsiveness when:', '["Patient has arrhythmias mediated physiological mechanism","Patient is on controlled mechanical ventilation with regular rhythm","Patient has low lung compliance mediated physiological mechanism","Patient is breathing spontaneously mediated physiological mechanism","Patient has right heart failure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Patient is on controlled mechanical ventilation with regular rhythm', 'PPV requires controlled ventilation (6-8 mL/kg TV), regular rhythm, and no RV failure or open chest. Not reliable in spontaneous breathing or arrhythmias.'),
('c2f1a685-45f5-5df4-954e-fe2764f1cf35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) in shock helps assess all EXCEPT:', '["IVC diameter","LV function","Detailed coronary anatomy","Pericardial effusion","RV size and function"]'::jsonb, 'Detailed coronary anatomy', 'POCUS (RUSH protocol): evaluates LV function, RV size, IVC (volume status), pericardial effusion, pneumothorax, aortic pathology. Does NOT assess coronary anatomy in detail.'),
('a18617d2-13e5-5454-a00f-3ad8e4073565'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The RUSH protocol for shock includes evaluation of:', '["Only cardiac valves","Only IVC diameter","Only heart function","Pump, tank, and pipes","Only lung sliding"]'::jsonb, 'Pump, tank, and pipes', 'RUSH (Rapid Ultrasound in Shock): Pump (heart), Tank (IVC, lung), Pipes (aorta, AAA, DVT). Comprehensive assessment of shock etiology.'),
('7d603823-6575-5e4e-b811-cef2846181d4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In undifferentiated shock, the first step is:', '["Order CT scan mediated physiological mechanism","Start vasopressors mediated physiological mechanism","Obtain central access mediated physiological mechanism","Clinical assessment and POCUS to identify shock type","Give empiric antibiotics mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical assessment and POCUS to identify shock type', 'History, exam + POCUS (heart, IVC, lungs, aorta) to distinguish hypovolemic, cardiogenic, obstructive, distributive shock → targeted therapy.'),
('984c1a3c-dcdd-55ed-bc58-383a07e62af1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common etiology of cardiogenic shock is:', '["Acute myocardial infarction","Cardiomyopathy","Arrhythmia","Valvular disease","Myocarditis"]'::jsonb, 'Acute myocardial infarction', 'AMI with extensive LV damage is the most common cause. RV infarction, mechanical complications (VSD, papillary rupture) can also cause shock.'),
('6e9e5d68-e163-5c1f-b910-f7be3394c95c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In cardiogenic shock, the hemodynamic profile is:', '["High CO, high PCWP, normal SVR","Low CO, low PCWP, low SVR","Low CO, high PCWP, high SVR","High CO, low PCWP, low SVR","Normal CO, low PCWP, high SVR"]'::jsonb, 'Low CO, high PCWP, high SVR', 'Cardiogenic: ↓ CO (CI < 2.2 L/min/m²), ↑ PCWP (> 15 mmHg), ↑ SVR (compensatory vasoconstriction). This distinguishes from hypovolemic (low PCWP) and distributive (low SVR).'),
('48afc0a4-0c57-52ed-837d-57bdc67a2645'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) in cardiogenic shock:', '["Decreases cardiac output","Augments diastolic coronary perfusion and ↓ afterload","Increases afterload","Replaces LV function","Acts as a ventricular assist device"]'::jsonb, 'Augments diastolic coronary perfusion and ↓ afterload', 'IABP: inflates in diastole ↑ coronary perfusion, deflates in systole ↓ afterload (LV unloading). No mortality benefit in SHOCK II trial (2012).'),
('a5b3fe9b-5f3f-5f10-bb38-5534d4bf8808'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'VA-ECMO in cardiogenic shock provides:', '["No hemodynamic support","Left ventricular support only","Respiratory support only","Full biventricular and respiratory support","Right ventricular support only"]'::jsonb, 'Full biventricular and respiratory support', 'VA-ECMO: drains blood from RA, oxygenates, returns to arterial system → supports both ventricles and gas exchange. Used in refractory cardiogenic shock.'),
('9937e409-7df5-5875-900f-4c8b3101bedf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Impella device in cardiogenic shock:', '["Direct LV unloading by pumping blood from LV to aorta","Replaces the aortic valve mediated physiological mechanism","Supports RV only mediated physiological mechanism","Acts as a pacemaker mediated physiological mechanism","Provides respiratory support mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Direct LV unloading by pumping blood from LV to aorta', 'Impella is a microaxial pump placed across aortic valve → ↓ LV preload and afterload, ↑ CO. Less evidence than VA-ECMO.'),
('184c19d8-5f1b-5690-a3b7-26b8127122e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The SHOCK trial (1999) showed that for cardiogenic shock:', '["Early revascularization improved survival","Thrombolysis was superior to angioplasty","Medical therapy alone was sufficient","IABP reduced mortality","ECMO was first-line"]'::jsonb, 'Early revascularization improved survival', 'SHOCK trial (NEJM 1999): early revasc (PCI/CABG) ↓ 6-month mortality vs initial medical stabilization in AMI-cardiogenic shock. Landmark trial.'),
('de960339-e688-5af5-b6e6-27e7992b42d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In traumatic hemorrhagic shock, damage control resuscitation includes:', '["Warming only mediated physiological mechanism","High-dose vasopressors mediated physiological mechanism","Delayed transfusion mediated physiological mechanism","Aggressive crystalloid resuscitation mediated physiological mechanism","Permissive hypotension, limited crystalloid, early blood products"]'::jsonb, 'Permissive hypotension, limited crystalloid, early blood products', 'DCR: SBP 80-90 until surgical hemostasis, minimize crystalloid, early 1:1:1 PRBC:FFP:platelet, tranexamic acid. Avoids dilutional coagulopathy.'),
('346cfa7c-85cd-57c6-9a2d-49484052dfbb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Permissive hypotension in trauma is contraindicated in:', '["Penetrating trauma","Extremity injury","Hemorrhagic shock in young patients","Blunt trauma","Traumatic brain injury"]'::jsonb, 'Traumatic brain injury', 'Permissive hypotension: SBP 80-90 until hemostasis. NOT in TBI (↓ CPP worsens secondary brain injury). TBI: maintain SBP > 110-120, MAP > 80.'),
('322839cc-2b5b-56c2-936d-aec876373e1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Tranexamic acid (TXA) in traumatic hemorrhage:', '["Inhibits fibrinolysis → ↓ hemorrhage mortality","Promotes bleeding","Has no effect on mortality","Causes hypertension","Increases clot breakdown"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits fibrinolysis → ↓ hemorrhage mortality', 'CRASH-2 trial (Lancet 2010): TXA within 3 hours ↓ mortality in traumatic hemorrhage. Mechanism: inhibits plasminogen activation → ↓ fibrinolysis.'),
('d20da62b-c058-5b45-9eea-a156aac3b624'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The CRASH-2 trial demonstrated that TXA:', '["Was equivalent to placebo","Reduced all-cause mortality if given within 3 hours of injury","Only effective in penetrating trauma","Caused more seizures","Increased mortality from thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced all-cause mortality if given within 3 hours of injury', 'CRASH-2: TXA 1g then 1g over 8 hours ↓ all-cause mortality (14.5% vs 16.0%). Greatest benefit if given within 1 hour. After 3 hours, no benefit.'),
('65816f4d-6842-5c0c-abc2-4509f3fd80d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The interstitial gel phase consists of:', '["Red blood cells mediated physiological mechanism","Glycosaminoglycans (GAGs) and proteoglycans","Lipids mediated physiological mechanism","Fibrin strands mediated physiological mechanism","Collagen fibers only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Glycosaminoglycans (GAGs) and proteoglycans', 'Interstitial gel: GAGs (hyaluronan) and proteoglycans form a hydrated gel that resists fluid flow and buffers interstitial volume changes.'),
('0bda459b-5192-5768-b783-f6b5fc793a9e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Interstitial compliance is defined as:', '["Interstitial protein concentration","Change in interstitial volume per change in pressure","Change in pressure per change in volume","Lymph flow rate","Total interstitial volume"]'::jsonb, 'Change in interstitial volume per change in pressure', 'Interstitial compliance = ΔV/ΔP. High compliance (e.g., subcutaneous tissue) allows large edema before pressure rises. Low compliance (e.g., capsule-bound organs) limits swelling.'),
('96f3be87-1003-5b71-9a9d-0a33c1cfa625'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In low-compliance tissues (e.g., kidney, enclosed organs):', '["Small edema causes large pressure increases","Pressure inhibits filtration","Edema does not occur","Large edema causes minimal pressure change","Lymph flow is absent"]'::jsonb, 'Small edema causes large pressure increases', 'In encapsulated organs (kidney, liver, spleen), low compliance means small increases in interstitial volume significantly raise interstitial pressure, opposing further filtration.'),
('1e7ec8f3-4857-5e69-8e1f-9da00c0c68f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Prefunctional lymphatics (initial lymphatics) are characterized by:', '["Smooth muscle walls","Tight endothelial junctions","High hydrostatic pressure","Continuous basement membrane","One-way valves and anchoring filaments"]'::jsonb, 'One-way valves and anchoring filaments', 'Initial lymphatics: blind-ended, one-way valves, anchoring filaments attach to interstitium. When interstitial pressure ↑, filaments pull vessels open → lymph entry.'),
('0d2cf2a7-1eba-5781-906d-59bce62e1217'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Lymph propulsion relies primarily on:', '["Respiratory movements mediated physiological mechanism","Gravity mediated physiological mechanism","Skeletal muscle pump mediated physiological mechanism","Cardiac pulsations mediated physiological mechanism","Intrinsic lymphatic smooth muscle contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic lymphatic smooth muscle contraction', 'Lymphatic vessels contract intrinsically (pacemaker activity) ~6-10/min. Extrinsic compression (muscle, respiration) also contributes. Valves ensure unidirectional flow.'),
('f5a67791-130b-5459-b5b0-a02c828bc390'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'On diagnostic evaluation, The thoracic duct drains into the:', '["Right atrium","Superior vena cava","Right subclavian vein","Left subclavian vein","Azygos vein"]'::jsonb, 'Left subclavian vein', 'Thoracic duct drains most of body''s lymph into the left venous angle (L subclavian + L IJ). Right lymphatic duct drains R arm, R thorax into R venous angle.'),
('c296120b-c847-5089-8a19-0a58342d7e1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Lymphatic obstruction leads to:', '["Hypoproteinemia","Accelerated lymphangiogenesis","Decreased interstitial pressure","Lymphedema","Increased plasma oncotic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphedema', 'Lymphatic obstruction → impaired protein clearance from interstitium → ↑ interstitial oncotic pressure → fluid accumulation → protein-rich edema. Non-pitting.'),
('7e769750-f5da-5b93-b61f-7082892176b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In burn shock, fluid resuscitation formulas target urine output of:', '["< 0.5 mL/kg/hr","0.5-1.0 mL/kg/hr","> 2 mL/kg/hr","1.5-2.5 mL/kg/hr","3-4 mL/kg/hr"]'::jsonb, '0.5-1.0 mL/kg/hr', 'End-point of burn resuscitation: UOP 0.5-1.0 mL/kg/hr in adults. Higher in children. Over-resuscitation → compartment syndrome, pulmonary edema (fluid creep).'),
('a6500ea2-8bb6-5fe5-bea3-124fecbcded8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Abdominal compartment syndrome in shock patients is caused by:', '["Intra-abdominal bleeding mediated physiological mechanism","Pneumoperitoneum mediated physiological mechanism","Peritonitis mediated physiological mechanism","Interstitial edema and ascites from fluid resuscitation","Bowel obstruction mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Interstitial edema and ascites from fluid resuscitation', 'Massive fluid resuscitation → bowel edema, ascites → ↑ intra-abdominal pressure > 20 mmHg → ↓ renal perfusion, ↓ venous return, ↓ CO. IAP monitoring in shock.'),
('72d8c7b9-6897-5c62-a525-eda511925796'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The intestinal microcirculation is particularly vulnerable in shock because:', '["Absence of precapillary sphincters","Thick capillary walls","High baseline flow","Excess collateral circulation","Countercurrent exchange in villi creates O2 gradient"]'::jsonb, 'Countercurrent exchange in villi creates O2 gradient', 'Villus countercurrent exchanger: O2 shunts from arteriole to venule at base → tip of villus is hypoxic → especially vulnerable to ischemia in shock → mucosal barrier breakdown.'),
('adfcd172-a3bc-5796-89c0-14c3cbc7d560'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Gut barrier failure in shock leads to:', '["Bacterial translocation and systemic inflammation","Reduced inflammation mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased permeability mediated physiological mechanism","Improved absorption mediated physiological mechanism","Enhanced immunity mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial translocation and systemic inflammation', 'Splanchnic ischemia → gut mucosal injury → loss of barrier function → bacteria/endotoxins enter portal circulation → amplifies systemic inflammation (gut hypothesis of MODS).'),
('fdaac68c-2ccf-5268-a6e4-02f6f68cf0ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Enteral nutrition in shock should be:', '["High","Started immediately","Glucose-free","Withheld until hemodynamically stable","Given only parenterally"]'::jsonb, 'Withheld until hemodynamically stable', 'Enteral nutrition in unstable shock → non-occlusive bowel necrosis (gut is ischemic, cannot absorb). Start trophic feeds once hemodynamically stable.'),
('ff33197d-c1b7-5986-8291-39a6c5f5c98b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Hepatic dysfunction in shock is signaled by:', '["Hypoglycemia only","Decreased bilirubin","Elevated transaminases and INR","Increased albumin synthesis","Improved coagulation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated transaminases and INR', 'Shock liver (ischemic hepatitis): ↑↑ ALT/AST (centrilobular necrosis), ↑ INR (↓ clotting factor synthesis), ↑ lactate. Usually reversible if perfusion restored.'),
('1cdbcb90-74c2-5704-b3a4-e8680b0c2f44'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary determinant of cerebral perfusion in shock is:', '["Heart rate","Hemoglobin concentration","MAP minus ICP","Blood glucose level","PaCO2"]'::jsonb, 'MAP minus ICP', 'CPP = MAP - ICP. Brain autoregulates flow between MAP 50-150 mmHg. In shock, when MAP falls below autoregulatory range → cerebral ischemia. TBI requires higher MAP.'),
('aa8c995c-8742-53aa-af91-5fa48b9016fe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Cerebral autoregulation is impaired in:', '["Chronic hypertension","Mild hypovolemia","Sleep deprivation","Healthy young adults","Traumatic brain injury and sepsis"]'::jsonb, 'Traumatic brain injury and sepsis', 'Endothelial dysfunction and inflammation in TBI and sepsis impair cerebral autoregulation → flow becomes pressure-dependent → vulnerable to hypotension/ hypertension.'),
('7cd0fd43-2540-5a52-b01b-92a2bfdc7e80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary treatment for anaphylactic shock is:', '["Methylprednisolone IV","Diphenhydramine IV","Epinephrine IM","Normal saline bolus","Norepinephrine drip"]'::jsonb, 'Epinephrine IM', 'Epinephrine 0.3-0.5 mg IM anterolateral thigh is first-line. Alpha effects reverse vasodilation, beta effects bronchodilate and stabilize mast cells. Repeat q5-15min.'),
('81e721d8-ba0f-5a08-82e7-bb9f20fb6150'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In hypovolemic shock, the most appropriate initial fluid is:', '["Hypertonic saline","Packed RBCs","Hetastarch","Isotonic crystalloid","Albumin 5%"]'::jsonb, 'Isotonic crystalloid', 'Initial 1-2 L crystalloid bolus. Balanced solutions (LR) preferred to avoid hyperchloremic acidosis from NS. Blood products if hemorrhagic shock.'),
('3f73c38e-68a3-5a0a-94c2-1fccedc52522'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The target hemoglobin in critically ill patients without active bleeding is:', '["Any level","10-12 g/dL","> 15 g/dL","7-9 g/dL","> 13 g/dL"]'::jsonb, '7-9 g/dL', 'TRICC trial: Hb 7-9 g/dL is safe in most critically ill patients (not ACS). Transfusion threshold 7 g/dL. Higher threshold (9 g/dL) in ACS.'),
('88f52608-3a93-5f72-9096-fe1e2f49c952'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common arrhythmia in septic shock is:', '["Atrial fibrillation","Bradycardia","SVT","Ventricular tachycardia","Torsades de pointes"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial fibrillation', 'AF is common in septic shock due to inflammation, electrolyte disturbances, catecholamines, and volume shifts. Rate control with beta-blockers or amiodarone.'),
('a74b279f-10b2-590a-9e75-52b2b3da6c86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Which vasopressor causes both alpha and beta-1 stimulation?', '["Phenylephrine","Norepinephrine","Vasopressin","Methylene blue","Angiotensin II"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine', 'NE: potent α1 (vasoconstriction) + moderate β1 (inotropy). First-line vasopressor in most shock types. Phenylephrine is pure α1; vasopressin is non-catecholamine vasoconstrictor.'),
('9c7997b1-031b-5e4a-b924-d8f7a8e68146'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The pulmonary artery catheter (Swan-Ganz) measures all EXCEPT:', '["Pulmonary artery occlusion pressure","Left ventricular end-diastolic volume","Cardiac output","Mixed venous oxygen saturation","Pulmonary artery pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricular end-diastolic volume', 'PA catheter measures: PAP, PAOP (surrogate for LVEDP), CO (thermodilution), SvO2. Does NOT directly measure LVEDV. PAOP affected by LV compliance, MR, PEEP.'),
('275b7a05-4392-593f-a4b3-b1f7f50ef7d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'A low PAOP with low CO suggests:', '["Obstructive shock","Cardiogenic shock","Septic shock","Distributive shock","Hypovolemic shock"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemic shock', 'Low PAOP = ↓ preload. With ↓ CO → hypovolemic shock. Cardiogenic: high PAOP, ↓ CO. Distributive: low/normal PAOP, normal/high CO initially.'),
('0521513f-f60b-5350-a83f-2e4978c6d195'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In cardiogenic shock, PAOP is typically:', '["< 8 mmHg","8-12 mmHg","Normal","Unmeasurable","> 18 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '> 18 mmHg', 'Cardiogenic shock: LVEDP ↑ → PAOP > 18 mmHg. Indicates pulmonary congestion/edema. Differentiates from hypovolemic (low PAOP) and distributive (normal-low PAOP).'),
('56a8a413-26f1-5435-9622-e705813df672'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The oxygen delivery equation (DO2) is:', '["CO × SvO2","CO × CaO2 × 10","PaO2 × CO","MAP × CO × 10","CaO2 × Hb × SaO2"]'::jsonb, 'CO × CaO2 × 10', 'DO2 = CO × CaO2 × 10 (converting dL to L). CaO2 = (Hb × 1.34 × SaO2) + (PaO2 × 0.003). Normal DO2 ~900-1100 mL/min.'),
('b3e68684-0875-53ed-bed2-8ec2cc279e6d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Oxygen consumption (VO2) is calculated as:', '["SvO2 × Hb × 1.34","CaO2 × Hb","PaO2 × FiO2","CO × CaO2 × 10","CO × (CaO2 - CvO2) × 10"]'::jsonb, 'CO × (CaO2 - CvO2) × 10', 'VO2 = CO × (CaO2 - CvO2). Normal ~200-250 mL/min. Widened A-V O2 difference suggests ↓ CO; narrowed suggests ↓ O2 extraction or shunt.'),
('1cd541ce-4bc3-5027-be65-55c5dd58f1e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The oxygen extraction ratio (O2ER) is normally:', '["0.50","0.10","0.25","0.90","0.75"]'::jsonb, '0.25', 'O2ER = VO2/DO2. Normally ~0.25. Increases when DO2 ↓ (to maintain VO2). > 0.5 suggests severe O2 debt. Cannot increase > 0.6-0.7.'),
('58e18c9c-b935-551b-a0fb-bdd7195e66c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Critical DO2 is the point at which:', '["VO2 becomes dependent on DO2","Lactate normalizes","VO2 is independent of DO2","ScvO2 > 70%","O2ER is maximal"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 becomes dependent on DO2', 'Normally VO2 is supply-independent. When DO2 falls below critical level (~7-8 mL O2/kg/min in anesthesia), VO2 falls → anaerobic metabolism → lactate rises.'),
('3f4617e4-dfbf-57ac-a393-8391eec65c6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The Fick principle for cardiac output calculation uses:', '["CO = MAP / SVR","(VO2) /","CO = HR × SV","CaO2 / CvO2","VO2 × CaO2"]'::jsonb, '(VO2) /', 'Fick: CO = VO2 / (CaO2 - CvO2). Requires measured VO2, arterial and mixed venous O2 content. Gold standard but invasive. Thermodilution is more practical.'),
('f7f1f617-b4b2-5347-8d39-f2af53de75a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) is calculated as:', '["MAP - CVP / CO","(MAP - CVP) × CO","MAP / CO","(MAP - CVP) / CO × 80","CO / MAP"]'::jsonb, '(MAP - CVP) / CO × 80', 'SVR = (MAP - CVP) / CO × 80 (dyne·s/cm5). Normal 800-1200. High SVR = vasoconstriction (cardiogenic, hypovolemic). Low SVR = vasodilation (distributive).'),
('dcda6408-952d-5372-a3df-95116ffe13c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In distributive shock, SVR is typically:', '["Zero","High","Unmeasurable","Low","Normal"]'::jsonb, 'Low', 'Distributive shock: vasodilation → ↓ SVR < 600 dyne·s/cm5. Initial CO may be normal/high. High SVR suggests cardiogenic or hypovolemic shock.'),
('859d1318-7dca-518d-9ebe-2059b9e05797'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Pulse pressure variation (PPV) during mechanical ventilation reflects:', '["Blood pressure variability mediated physiological mechanism","Cyclic changes in LV stroke volume with respiration","Heart rate variability mediated physiological mechanism","Respiratory rate mediated physiological mechanism","Tidal volume mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic changes in LV stroke volume with respiration', 'PPV: positive pressure → ↓ venous return → ↓ LV preload → ↓ SV → ↓ pulse pressure. PPV > 13% predicts fluid responsiveness in ventilated patients.'),
('77ecf91c-d85a-54ae-976d-10bf882b57c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Stroke volume variation (SVV) > 10% indicates:', '["Fluid non-responsiveness","Hypovolemia","Fluid responsiveness","Arrhythmia","Cardiogenic shock"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid responsiveness', 'SVV > 10-12% (on controlled ventilation, regular rhythm) predicts fluid responsiveness. Reflects position on Frank-Starling curve (steep portion = preload dependent).'),
('fe8609ea-7ec4-52fa-98fd-45c73a0f2033'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'IVC collapsibility index > 50% on ultrasound suggests:', '["Pulmonary hypertension","Tricuspid regurgitation","Right heart failure","Volume depletion and fluid responsiveness","Volume overload"]'::jsonb, 'Volume depletion and fluid responsiveness', 'IVC collapsibility index = (expiratory diam - inspiratory diam) / expiratory diam × 100%. > 50% suggests low CVP (< 5 mm Hg) and likely fluid responsiveness.'),
('6962fc06-8496-56bd-bb2e-6688d6941c3e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The RUSH exam in shock evaluates:', '["Only the aorta mediated physiological mechanism","Only the IVC mediated physiological mechanism","Heart (pump), IVC (tank), aorta/vessels","Only the heart mediated physiological mechanism","Only the lungs mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Heart (pump), IVC (tank), aorta/vessels', 'RUSH protocol: Pump (LV, RV, pericardium), Tank (IVC, lung sliding, pleural effusion), Pipes (aorta, AAA, DVT). Rapid assessment of shock etiology.'),
('ee400c28-3a62-55d3-bc01-ce36a08322a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In obstructive shock due to massive PE, echo typically shows:', '["Aortic dissection flap","Normal RV size","LV dilation","RV dilation and D-shaped LV","Pericardial effusion"]'::jsonb, 'RV dilation and D-shaped LV', 'Acute PE → ↑ RV pressure → RV dilation → IVS shifts left → D-shaped LV → ↓ LV filling → ↓ CO. Also: McConnell sign (RV free wall hypokinesis, apical sparing).'),
('13a86795-8c3e-521a-98be-237d5328ca0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'McConnell sign on echo is suggestive of:', '["Acute pulmonary embolism","Aortic stenosis","RV infarction","LV failure","Cardiac tamponade"]'::jsonb, 'Acute pulmonary embolism', 'McConnell sign: RV free wall hypokinesis with apical sparing. Specific for acute PE. Differentiates from chronic PH or RV infarction.'),
('a5a36ae2-bb80-56cb-ab1e-718ce1002375'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In cardiac tamponade, echocardiography shows:', '["Pericardial effusion with RA/RV diastolic collapse","RV dilation mediated physiological mechanism","Aortic valve vegetation mediated physiological mechanism","LV hypertrophy mediated physiological mechanism","Mitral stenosis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Pericardial effusion with RA/RV diastolic collapse', 'Tamponade: RA systolic collapse and RV diastolic collapse. IVC dilated with ↓ respiratory variation. Respiratory variation in mitral inflow velocity.'),
('fdb75fe3-2bb3-5d03-8639-4baacaacfc90'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Pulsus paradoxus in cardiac tamponade is defined as:', '["Fall in SBP > 10 mmHg during inspiration","Loss of pulse","Bradycardia","Rise in SBP during inspiration","Fall in SBP during expiration"]'::jsonb, 'Fall in SBP > 10 mmHg during inspiration', 'Pulsus paradoxus: exaggerated inspiratory ↓ in SBP > 10 mmHg. Due to ↑ RV filling with inspiration → IVS shifts left → ↓ LV filling → ↓ SBP. Also in asthma, PE.'),
('8d603157-35d9-58b3-9a13-fb8adcf5e531'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Kussmaul sign (↑ JVP with inspiration) is seen in:', '["Right heart failure","Tricuspid regurgitation","Constrictive pericarditis","Cardiac tamponade","Superior vena cava syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Constrictive pericarditis', 'Kussmaul sign: JVP rises (not falls) with inspiration. Due to fixed pericardial restraint limiting RV filling. Absent in tamponade (unless effusive-constrictive).'),
('9f27582e-355b-5996-a00c-78da90ca8317'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Beck triad for cardiac tamponade includes:', '["Chest pain, dyspnea, hemoptysis","Hypotension, muffled heart sounds, JVD","Syncope, JVD, hepatomegaly","Hypotension, tachycardia, fever","Hypotension, bradycardia, JVD"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotension, muffled heart sounds, JVD', 'Beck triad: ↓ BP, muffled HS, JVD. Due to ↓ CO (hypotension), fluid in pericardium (muffled), ↑ CVP (JVD). Classic but not always present.'),
('2f8bdee6-dfa0-5b81-8d20-7eaa62f7f945'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The FAST exam in trauma evaluates for:', '["Spinal injury mediated physiological mechanism","Skull fracture mediated physiological mechanism","Vascular injury mediated physiological mechanism","Hemoperitoneum, hemopericardium, hemothorax, pneumothorax","Bowel injury mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoperitoneum, hemopericardium, hemothorax, pneumothorax', 'FAST: Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma. Views: RUQ (Morison pouch), LUQ (splenorenal), pelvis, subxiphoid (pericardium). Extended: lungs for pneumothorax.'),
('c8970e2b-ca9b-542b-be24-add31f5a4aec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In trauma, the primary goal of the primary survey (ABCDE) is:', '["Definitive surgical repair","Obtain detailed history","Identify and treat life-threatening injuries","Consult all specialties","CT scan all body parts"]'::jsonb, 'Identify and treat life-threatening injuries', 'ATLS primary survey: A (airway + C-spine), B (breathing), C (circulation + hemorrhage control), D (disability), E (exposure/environment). Treat threats immediately.'),
('cf02a22a-41e8-5b8f-aa50-0efc379d1ecf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The lethal triad in trauma refers to:', '["Hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hypokalemia","Hyperthermia, alkalosis, bleeding","Infection, inflammation, organ failure","Hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy","Hypotension, bradycardia, hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy', 'Lethal triad: ↓ temp → platelet dysfunction + ↓ clotting factor activity. Acidosis → ↓ clotting factor function. Coagulopathy → continued bleeding. Vicious cycle.'),
('6f90ec3f-a5af-5b86-b877-876423eb4921'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Damage control surgery (DCS) is indicated when:', '["Only blunt trauma","Patient is in extremis with physiologic exhaustion","All trauma patients","Stable patient with isolated injury","Only penetrating abdominal trauma"]'::jsonb, 'Patient is in extremis with physiologic exhaustion', 'DCS: abbreviated laparotomy → hemorrhage control + contamination control → ICU resuscitation → planned reoperation. Indications: hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy, massive transfusion.'),
('a406af54-26f4-5ee3-9033-a0f0500f0565'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The primary endpoint of damage control resuscitation is:', '["Normalization of all lab values","Definitive surgical repair","Complete organ system imaging","Patient awake and alert","Physiologic restoration"]'::jsonb, 'Physiologic restoration', 'DCR endpoints: temp > 35°C, pH > 7.2, INR < 1.5, lactate < 4 mmol/L, then return to OR for definitive surgery.'),
('5edc0527-ffb1-5299-a593-517d7bf63173'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'REBOA (Resuscitative Endovascular Balloon Occlusion of the Aorta) is used for:', '["Temporary hemorrhage control in non-compressible torso hemorrhage","Cerebral perfusion mediated physiological mechanism","Cardiac resuscitation mediated physiological mechanism","Renal protection mediated physiological mechanism","Definitive vascular repair mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Temporary hemorrhage control in non-compressible torso hemorrhage', 'REBOA: balloon inflated in aorta to ↓ distal blood flow and ↑ proximal pressure. Zone 1 (supraceliac) for abdominal/pelvic bleeding. Zone 3 (infrarenal) for pelvic bleeding.'),
('1ed9caf6-8a04-5766-bb60-9c2a39641952'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Massive transfusion is defined as:', '["> 10 units PRBC in 24 hours","> 20 units PRBC in 24 hours","> 4 units FFP","> 5 units PRBC in 1 hour","Any blood product given"]'::jsonb, '> 10 units PRBC in 24 hours', 'Massive transfusion: ≥ 10 units PRBC in 24h or ≥ 4 units in 1 hour. Triggers MTP. Ratio-based resuscitation (1:1:1 PRBC:FFP:platelet per PROPPR trial).'),
('8f8b30e7-bdaf-5288-9d11-902555dd4ef9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The PROPPR trial demonstrated that in massive transfusion:', '["1:1:1 ratio is as good as 1:1:2 with no difference in mortality","Colloids are superior to blood products","Crystalloid alone is better","1:1:2 ratio is superior","Delayed transfusion improves outcomes"]'::jsonb, '1:1:1 ratio is as good as 1:1:2 with no difference in mortality', 'PROPPR (NEJM 2015): 1:1:1 vs 1:1:2 PRBC:FFP:platelet. No difference in 24h or 30d mortality. Trend toward fewer exsanguination deaths in 1:1:1 group.'),
('34c01f71-e0b2-5549-9b43-273a1030e374'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Calcium administration during massive transfusion is important to:', '["Counteract citrate toxicity and prevent hypocalcemia","Increase cardiac contractility","Reduce transfusion reactions","Prevent hyperkalemia","Improve oxygen delivery"]'::jsonb, 'Counteract citrate toxicity and prevent hypocalcemia', 'Citrate in blood products chelates Ca2+ → hypocalcemia → ↓ myocardial contractility, hypotension. Monitor ionized Ca, replace with Ca gluconate/chloride.'),
('4e7f3cb7-261f-5e6e-a8f1-94a0ab780e67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common electrolyte abnormality in shock resuscitation is:', '["Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia","Hypomagnesemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypokalemia', 'Hypokalemia from dilution (resuscitation fluids), diuretics, insulin therapy, and catecholamines (β2 → intracellular K+ shift). Monitor and replete carefully.'),
('448e95ff-dbb7-5366-b85a-7893de8c3f4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Hyperkalemia in shock patients can result from:', '["Hyperventilation mediated physiological mechanism","Hypothermia mediated physiological mechanism","Acidosis shifting K+ extracellularly","Alkalosis mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoglycemia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Acidosis shifting K+ extracellularly', 'Metabolic acidosis → H+ enters cells → K+ exits cells → hyperkalemia. Each 0.1 ↓ pH ↑ serum K+ by ~0.5-0.6 mEq/L. Also from AKI, massive transfusion, cell lysis.'),
('a7bbeca6-8a7b-5411-80b5-9cb7525bfcfe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Thiamine deficiency in shock patients can cause:', '["Hypocalcemia mediated physiological mechanism","Respiratory alkalosis mediated physiological mechanism","Lactic acidosis refractory to resuscitation","Hyperkalemia mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoglycemia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Lactic acidosis refractory to resuscitation', 'Thiamine (B1) is cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase. Deficiency → pyruvate cannot enter TCA cycle → shunted to lactate → type B lactic acidosis. Replenish thiamine in malnutrition, alcoholism.'),
('877ac29f-79a7-56b7-b55b-3427b630bbf7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The optimal method for lactate clearance monitoring is:', '["Venous lactate only","Capillary lactate","Single lactate measurement","Lactate measured only at 24 hours","Serial lactate measurements"]'::jsonb, 'Serial lactate measurements', 'Lactate clearance (↓ by ≥ 20% in 2 hours) predicts survival. Failure to clear suggests ongoing hypoperfusion and need for further resuscitation.'),
('3322c64e-7b13-50bc-b8d7-a1b8cc5e27dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The role of vasodilators in cardiogenic shock is:', '["Cause vasoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism","Increase heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Increase preload mediated physiological mechanism","Reduce afterload and improve cardiac output","Improve renal function mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce afterload and improve cardiac output', 'Vasodilators (NTG, nitroprusside) ↓ SVR (afterload) → ↑ SV and CO. Only if SBP > 90 mmHg without hypotension. Combined with inotropes if needed.'),
('9943530c-e6fe-5a19-b7c9-08a916cd965c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Dobutamine in cardiogenic shock improves:', '["Blood pressure by vasoconstriction","Preload by venodilation","Oxygen delivery by bronchodilation","Heart rate by chronotropy","Cardiac output by increasing contractility"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output by increasing contractility', 'Dobutamine (β1 > β2) ↑ contractility → ↑ SV → ↑ CO. Also mild β2 vasodilation → ↓ afterload. May ↓ BP if patient is hypovolemic.'),
('e186e32f-503d-51be-bf85-83586b247d4b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Milrinone differs from dobutamine by:', '["Greater chronotropic effect","Mechanism: phosphodiesterase III inhibitor","Shorter half-life","Beta","Stronger vasoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Mechanism: phosphodiesterase III inhibitor', 'Milrinone: PDE-III inhibitor → ↑ cAMP → ↑ inotropy + vasodilation (inodilator). Half-life 2-4 hours (longer in renal failure). Effective in beta-blocker overdose.'),
('c5ca50fd-6623-5b33-ae0d-6299b87e84fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Levosimendan (calcium sensitizer) in cardiogenic shock:', '["Enhances contractility without increasing O2 demand","Only for hypertension mediated physiological mechanism","Strong vasoconstrictor mediated physiological mechanism","Phosphodiesterase inhibitor mediated physiological mechanism","Beta mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Enhances contractility without increasing O2 demand', 'Levosimendan binds troponin C → sensitizes myofilaments to Ca2+ → ↑ contractility without ↑ ATP use or O2 demand. Also opens KATP channels → vasodilation.'),
('3d87a0ec-5fc3-523b-bbe9-a8a3e6cd605c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common cause of cardiogenic shock post-MI is:', '["RV infarction","Papillary muscle rupture","LV failure","Free wall rupture","Ventricular septal rupture"]'::jsonb, 'LV failure', 'Extensive LV infarction (> 40% of LV mass) → pump failure. Mechanical complications (VSR, papillary rupture, free wall rupture) are less common but catastrophic.'),
('998e9e94-d3b4-50c9-99bd-acd5ffa878e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In RV infarction causing shock, the key management step is:', '["Rapid pacing","Inhaled nitric oxide","Volume resuscitation to maintain RV preload","Diuresis to ↓ lung edema","Vasodilation to ↓ afterload"]'::jsonb, 'Volume resuscitation to maintain RV preload', 'RV infarction → ↓ RV compliance → ↓ RV filling → ↓ CO. Maintain preload with fluids (↓ RV filling = ↓ CO). Avoid vasodilators and diuretics. Inotropes if needed.'),
('2696ba43-0030-5f33-8789-f9bfd7a94f2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'A new holosystolic murmur post-MI with shock suggests:', '["Aortic regurgitation mediated physiological mechanism","Aortic stenosis mediated physiological mechanism","Ventricular septal rupture or mitral regurgitation","Pericarditis mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary embolism mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular septal rupture or mitral regurgitation', 'New murmur + shock post-MI: VSR (loud holosystolic, LUSB + thrill) or papillary muscle rupture (MR, apex, pulmonary edema). Echo confirms. Surgical or percutaneous repair.'),
('778bda8f-7d98-5b9d-983e-fbba2e5c657d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Papillary muscle rupture post-MI most commonly involves:', '["Chordae tendineae","Anterolateral papillary muscle","Mitral annulus","Both equally","Posteromedial papillary muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedial papillary muscle', 'Posteromedial has single blood supply (PDA from RCA). Anterolateral has dual supply (LAD + LCx). Therefore posteromedial is more susceptible to rupture in inferior MI.'),
('ee1dea39-bcd8-5b7a-8c16-ace7bf3f2a5f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Ventricular free wall rupture post-MI presents with:', '["Chest pain mediated physiological mechanism","New murmur mediated physiological mechanism","Sudden electromechanical dissociation","Dyspnea only mediated physiological mechanism","Fever mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden electromechanical dissociation', 'Free wall rupture → hemopericardium → tamponade → PEA cardiac arrest. Rarely contained by pericardium (pseudoaneurysm). Lethal without immediate pericardiocentesis and surgery.'),
('147ec3a2-56d4-57c0-9b04-dc29a94553b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most important modifiable risk factor for hemorrhagic shock mortality is:', '["Season of year","BMI","Time to hemorrhage control","Patient age","Gender"]'::jsonb, 'Time to hemorrhage control', 'Time to hemorrhage control is the MOST important modifiable factor. Each hour delay ↑ mortality. Damage control surgery should be expedited.'),
('983e3cad-deb1-5360-851f-660c16e8e2f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In resource-limited settings, the WHO shock protocol for children recommends:', '["No fluid resuscitation mediated physiological mechanism","Fluid bolus 20 mL/kg, reassess, repeat if no improvement","Vasopressors first mediated physiological mechanism","Colloids only mediated physiological mechanism","Immediate blood transfusion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid bolus 20 mL/kg, reassess, repeat if no improvement', 'WHO: 20 mL/kg isotonic crystalloid over 15-30 min. Reassess. ± 20 mL/kg if persistent signs. Avoid excessive fluids (FEAST trial showed harm from boluses in febrile illness).'),
('29f5baff-99ea-5207-8c1d-6d02ce0cccd1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The FEAST trial (2011) found that in African children with shock:', '["Blood transfusion was best","Colloids were superior to crystalloids","Fluid boluses increased mortality compared to no bolus","Vasopressors improved outcomes","Fluid boluses decreased mortality"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid boluses increased mortality compared to no bolus', 'FEAST trial (NEJM 2011): boluses (saline or albumin) ↑ 48h mortality vs maintenance fluids only in febrile children with shock. Practice-changing: caution with boluses in resource-limited settings.'),
('a47ef65e-28af-5c41-bfa5-8da24fb44906'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Endothelial glycocalyx thickness is normally:', '["~5-10 μm","~50-100 μm","~1 mm","~0.5-1 μm","~1 nm"]'::jsonb, '~0.5-1 μm', 'Glycocalyx: 0.5-1 μm thick layer of proteoglycans, glycoproteins, GAGs lining endothelium. Regulates permeability, mechanotransduction, leukocyte adhesion. Degraded by inflammatory mediators in shock.'),
('8534c949-85cf-5527-939f-8349476eb45f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Heparan sulfate in the glycocalyx contributes to:', '["Neutral charge","Negative charge barrier restricting albumin passage","Increased permeability","Leukocyte adhesion","Positive charge facilitating albumin passage"]'::jsonb, 'Negative charge barrier restricting albumin passage', 'Heparan sulfate is negatively charged → repels negatively charged albumin → contributes to charge-selective permeability barrier. Degradation → albuminuria, capillary leak.'),
('f2dc55d3-4a77-5d83-917b-457b59fbea2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Syndecan-1 levels in blood are a marker of:', '["Liver function","Glycocalyx shedding","Renal function","Cardiac function","Nutritional status"]'::jsonb, 'Glycocalyx shedding', 'Syndecan-1 (proteoglycan shed from glycocalyx) measured in plasma. Elevated levels correlate with glycocalyx degradation, capillary leak, and mortality in shock.'),
('14307cd7-2fdc-5da0-b95f-da3d5b53acd2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Hydrocortisone in refractory septic shock:', '["Has no effect mediated physiological mechanism","Causes hyperglycemia only mediated physiological mechanism","Increases mortality mediated physiological mechanism","May improve hemodynamics and reduce vasopressor duration","Only effective in pediatrics mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'May improve hemodynamics and reduce vasopressor duration', 'Hydrocortisone 200 mg/day IV may ↓ vasopressor duration in refractory shock (ADRENAL, APROCCHSS trials). No survival benefit in ADRENAL, modest benefit in APROCCHSS.'),
('af88cc6d-d625-540e-a098-ce13578c6a73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The preferred vasopressor in pregnancy with shock is:', '["Norepinephrine","Phenylephrine","Ephedrine","Vasopressin","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Ephedrine', 'Ephedrine (β-agonist) historically preferred as it best preserves uterine blood flow. NE also acceptable. Phenylephrine may ↓ uteroplacental flow (pure α-agonist).'),
('6e2e2c01-1e65-55de-9b69-da8f9394e1b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In pediatric septic shock, the first-line vasopressor is:', '["Dobutamine","Dopamine","Epinephrine or norepinephrine","Phenylephrine","Vasopressin"]'::jsonb, 'Epinephrine or norepinephrine', 'SSC pediatric guidelines: epinephrine or NE as first-line. Dopamine no longer recommended (more arrhythmias, less efficacy). Fluid resuscitation 40-60 mL/kg initially.'),
('3e62a8c0-0589-5357-9d60-ad71c3adf71e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common cause of shock in children worldwide is:', '["Septic shock","Anaphylactic shock","Neurogenic shock","Cardiogenic shock","Hypovolemic shock"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemic shock', 'Hypovolemic shock from acute gastroenteritis/dehydration is the leading cause of shock in children globally. WHO plan C: rapid IV/IO fluids, reassess frequently.'),
('636289b4-ea9e-555f-8efb-788a19071b22'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'In neurogenic shock, the hemodynamic profile shows:', '["Hypotension with low-normal HR","Hypertension with tachycardia","Hypotension with tachycardia","Hypertension with bradycardia","Normal BP with tachycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotension with low-normal HR', 'Neurogenic shock (spinal cord injury): loss of sympathetic outflow → unopposed vagal tone → peripheral vasodilation (↓ SVR) + bradycardia. Warm, dry skin.'),
('12d87897-601f-5182-99fe-e1492684f3df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Spinal shock (areflexia) differs from neurogenic shock by:', '["Neither term is clinically relevant","Spinal shock is loss of reflexes (neurologic); neurogenic shock is cardiovascular","Both are synonymous with hypovolemic shock","Spinal shock is cardiovascular; neurogenic shock is neurologic","Both terms describe the same phenomenon"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal shock is loss of reflexes (neurologic); neurogenic shock is cardiovascular', 'Spinal shock: temporary loss of all spinal reflexes below level of SCI (flaccid paralysis, areflexia). Neurogenic shock: cardiovascular collapse from loss of sympathetic tone.'),
('26bc16a7-68e6-598b-a7eb-e13cc0cb7575'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The first step in managing undifferentiated shock should always be:', '["Obtain CT scan","Start broad-spectrum antibiotics","Check lactate","Assessment of airway, breathing, and circulation","Give 2 L crystalloid"]'::jsonb, 'Assessment of airway, breathing, and circulation', 'Always start with ABC assessment. Identify and treat immediate life threats first (airway obstruction, tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, external hemorrhage).'),
('cf4156f1-f4c0-5ecd-84a3-4d1dea3b0d13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'End-tidal CO2 (ETCO2) monitoring in shock reflects:', '["Cardiac output and pulmonary blood flow","Ventilator settings only","Oxygen saturation","Bicarbonate level","Arterial CO2 directly"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output and pulmonary blood flow', 'ETCO2 correlates with CO if ventilation constant. ↓ ETCO2 suggests ↓ CO (hypovolemia, PE, cardiac arrest). ↑ ETCO2 with ROSC is earliest sign of return of circulation.'),
('175ae1ad-8ba5-5135-884e-1e60217c26f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Norepinephrine extravasation should be treated with:', '["Phentolamine","Heparin","Morphine","Nitroglycerin paste","Warm compresses"]'::jsonb, 'Phentolamine', 'Phentolamine 5-10 mg in 10 mL NS infiltrated locally reverses NE extravasation (α-blocker → vasodilation). Also: topical NTG. Prevent with central line placement.'),
('d1e68234-279e-5912-a5e0-86d3bc643ab7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'The most common site for central venous access in shock is:', '["Intraosseous","Subclavian vein","Femoral vein","Internal jugular vein","Peripheral IV"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular vein', 'IJ preferred due to highest success rate, lowest mechanical complications (pneumothorax with subclavian). Use ultrasound guidance. Subclavian: lowest infection risk. Femoral: highest infection/DVT risk.'),
('99de3e04-1f53-5850-807f-11299bea531e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Intraosseous (IO) access is preferred over peripheral IV when:', '["Elderly patients mediated physiological mechanism","Patient is normotensive mediated physiological mechanism","Peripheral IV access fails or is delayed > 90 seconds","Only in cardiac arrest mediated physiological mechanism","All pediatric patients mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral IV access fails or is delayed > 90 seconds', 'IO: rapid, reliable access in shock when PIV fails. Can give all resuscitation drugs/fluids. Preferred sites: proximal tibia, distal femur, humerus. Effective in all ages.'),
('01a293ce-1121-59f2-8994-d05be6f1587a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Circulation', 'Ultrasound-guided central line insertion reduces:', '["Thrombosis rates","Cost","Infection rates","Mechanical complications","Procedure time initially"]'::jsonb, 'Mechanical complications', 'US guidance reduces mechanical complications (NNT ~30 for arterial puncture, pneumothorax). Recommended by all guidelines for central line insertion.'),
('83aa10cd-d7c4-5100-95d9-891fc1609d39'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'At rest, the intrapleural pressure is normally:', '["+5 cm H2O","-10 cm H2O","-5 cm H2O","+10 cm H2O","0 cm H2O"]'::jsonb, '-5 cm H2O', 'Intrapleural pressure is subatmospheric at rest, approximately -5 cm H2O, due to chest wall outward recoil and lung inward recoil.'),
('cd32432a-a480-515b-845e-ba06b450caf7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'During normal quiet inspiration, intrapleural pressure becomes:', '["More negative","Zero","Less negative","Unchanged","Positive"]'::jsonb, 'More negative', 'During inspiration, diaphragmatic contraction expands thoracic cavity, making intrapleural pressure more negative (e.g., -8 cm H2O).'),
('b683f1a5-95db-5f1e-91a8-60bed66723a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Transpulmonary pressure is defined as:', '["Intrapleural pressure minus alveolar pressure","Alveolar pressure minus atmospheric pressure","Atmospheric pressure minus alveolar pressure","Intrapleural pressure minus atmospheric pressure","Alveolar pressure minus intrapleural pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar pressure minus intrapleural pressure', 'Transpulmonary pressure (Ptp = PA - Pip) is the pressure difference across the lung wall, determining lung expansion.'),
('7c3b628a-5d8f-5916-999a-6bf2a5c453fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Law of Laplace applied to alveoli states that wall tension is proportional to:', '["Pressure / radius^2","Pressure / radius","Pressure x radius^2","Radius / pressure","Pressure x radius"]'::jsonb, 'Pressure x radius', 'Laplace''s law: T = P x r / 2 (for sphere). Smaller alveoli have higher wall tension for same pressure, making them prone to collapse.'),
('fdcab3c8-ba76-5827-9cac-f81c613409e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Surfactant is produced by which lung cells?', '["Goblet cells","Type II pneumocytes","Clara cells","Type I pneumocytes","Alveolar macrophages"]'::jsonb, 'Type II pneumocytes', 'Type II pneumocytes produce dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC) surfactant, reducing surface tension and preventing alveolar collapse.'),
('0a21a97d-0317-55fa-806d-70929df307d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Surfactant primarily reduces:', '["Surface tension at air-liquid interface","Transpulmonary pressure","Lung compliance","Airway resistance","Alveolar ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface tension at air-liquid interface', 'Surfactant decreases surface tension, especially during expiration, preventing alveolar collapse and reducing work of breathing.'),
('69f2dca8-cb05-5dbb-9d7d-0dbc1ec16299'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Lack of surfactant in neonates causes:', '["Pulmonary hypoplasia","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","Meconium aspiration","Infant respiratory distress syndrome","Bronchopulmonary dysplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Infant respiratory distress syndrome', 'Surfactant deficiency in preterm infants leads to alveolar collapse, hyaline membrane formation, and severe hypoxemia.'),
('508cd2f7-3b24-54b3-93d4-b1398c9b82c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The surfactant composition includes the following major lipid:', '["Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine","Phosphatidylethanolamine","Sphingomyelin","Cholesterol ester","Triacylglycerol"]'::jsonb, 'Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine', 'DPPC is the most abundant and functionally critical component of pulmonary surfactant responsible for lowering surface tension.'),
('b2a32c2a-924e-54ce-a707-b3411a9f91a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Compliance of lungs is defined as:', '["Change in volume per unit change in pressure","Pressure at a given volume","Product of volume and pressure","Change in pressure per unit change in volume","Volume at a given pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Change in volume per unit change in pressure', 'Compliance (C = dV/dP) indicates lung distensibility. Normal lung compliance is ~200 mL/cm H2O.'),
('ec33da50-195e-5d38-b99a-232ae87825e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In emphysema, lung compliance is:', '["Increased","Decreased","Unchanged","Normal","Initially increased then decreased"]'::jsonb, 'Increased', 'Destruction of alveolar walls in emphysema increases compliance (more distensible) due to loss of elastic tissue.'),
('e920553d-c970-58bb-974c-f5c128aa881a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In pulmonary fibrosis, lung compliance is:', '["Decreased","Increased","Normal","Variable","Unchanged"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased', 'Fibrotic lungs are stiffer; compliance is markedly reduced, requiring greater pressure to achieve same volume change.'),
('053fe6c0-3d89-5524-822a-c71e7f05caf5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Elastance is the reciprocal of:', '["Compliance","Conductance","Surface tension","Resistance","Work of breathing"]'::jsonb, 'Compliance', 'Elastance = 1/compliance. It measures the tendency of lungs to recoil toward resting volume after expansion.'),
('39f3d2e3-cb24-576c-81e6-7b3ee1a5cb73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The main determinant of lung elastic recoil is:', '["Elastic fibers in lung parenchyma","Alveolar surface tension","Diaphragmatic contractility","Chest wall elasticity","Bronchial smooth muscle tone"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar surface tension', 'Surface tension at the air-liquid interface accounts for ~2/3 of elastic recoil; tissue elastic fibers account for ~1/3.'),
('c3f9db6a-4897-5293-b534-e2105837d9f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The pressure-volume curve of the lung shows hysteresis due to:', '["Muscle contraction","Surface tension effects","Chest wall interaction","Airway closure","Gravity"]'::jsonb, 'Surface tension effects', 'Hysteresis occurs because surface tension differs during inflation vs deflation due to surfactant behavior.'),
('38ce61a0-8e9e-541c-a587-4838848907b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'At FRC, the outward recoil of chest wall and inward recoil of lung are:', '["Chest wall recoil is greater","Lung recoil is greater","Both are zero","Equal and opposite","Both are maximal"]'::jsonb, 'Equal and opposite', 'At FRC, opposing recoils balance, making alveolar pressure equal to atmospheric pressure with no air flow.'),
('6e657237-9ecc-5fc5-ad74-9ce954533b38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Airway resistance is inversely proportional to:', '["Radius^5","Radius^2","Radius^3","Radius","Radius^4"]'::jsonb, 'Radius^4', 'Poiseuille''s law: Resistance = 8nl/(pi r^4). Small decreases in airway radius drastically increase resistance.'),
('4f63efe2-16d1-57e1-9666-d01ab1ef6040'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The major site of airway resistance in normal lungs is:', '["Terminal bronchioles","Alveolar ducts","Trachea","Medium-sized bronchi","Nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Medium-sized bronchi', 'Medium-sized bronchi (~2-5 mm diameter) contribute most to airflow resistance; small airways contribute little normally.'),
('cd4aca31-44ea-5d81-bffe-240cc8c77529'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'During forced expiration, airway resistance increases due to:', '["Dynamic airway compression","Decreased elastic recoil","Increased lung volume","Bronchodilation","Surfactant deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Dynamic airway compression', 'High pleural pressure during forced expiration compresses airways, increasing resistance — the equal pressure point concept.'),
('eff5fd88-846f-5206-aaaa-7935898730c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Equal pressure point (EPP) in the airways moves toward alveoli in:', '["Pulmonary fibrosis","Asthma","Bronchiectasis","Normal lungs","Emphysema"]'::jsonb, 'Emphysema', 'In emphysema, loss of elastic recoil moves EPP more peripherally, causing dynamic airway compression earlier during expiration.'),
('ca049e86-4110-55f0-af36-9539f7ddd2ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Work of breathing is least at:', '["FRC","VC midpoint","RV","TLC","IC"]'::jsonb, 'FRC', 'At FRC, lung and chest wall compliances are optimal, minimizing total work of breathing against elastic and resistive forces.'),
('3023fdd2-883e-5aa5-b75f-23ea4d11924d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The majority of work of breathing during normal quiet breathing is against:', '["Gravitational forces","Resistive forces","Surface tension forces","Inertial forces","Elastic forces"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic forces', '~65% of work is against elastic recoil (lung + chest wall), ~35% against airway resistance and tissue viscosity.'),
('dc79511a-bdbb-585a-a93c-233b0a638c0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which pressure gradient drives airflow into the lungs during inspiration?', '["Alveolar pressure < atmospheric","Atmospheric pressure < alveolar","Intrapleural pressure < atmospheric","Alveolar pressure > atmospheric","Transpulmonary pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar pressure < atmospheric', 'During inspiration, alveolar pressure becomes subatmospheric (negative relative to mouth), creating a pressure gradient for air inflow.'),
('eaa33a3c-84ff-5e47-88c7-3577f02b93fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Boyle''s law applied to ventilation states that:', '["Pressure / volume = constant","Volume / pressure = constant","Pressure x temperature = constant","Pressure x volume = constant","Volume x temperature = constant"]'::jsonb, 'Pressure x volume = constant', 'Boyle''s Law (P1V1 = P2V2) explains how increasing thoracic volume decreases alveolar pressure, causing inspiration.'),
('92098177-7f6e-5360-a3bc-7993f02bb0d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The diaphragm receives innervation from:', '["Intercostal nerves","Accessory nerve","Sympathetic chain","Phrenic nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Phrenic nerve', 'Phrenic nerve originates from cervical roots C3-C5. Diaphragmatic paralysis occurs with cervical spine injury above C5.'),
('158465a2-620d-5934-ae2d-3ffbacaaf540'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Accessory muscles of inspiration include all EXCEPT:', '["Sternocleidomastoid","Scalenes","Pectoralis minor","Internal intercostals","External intercostals"]'::jsonb, 'Internal intercostals', 'Internal intercostals are expiratory muscles. Sternocleidomastoid, scalenes and external intercostals are inspiratory.'),
('a1805cc4-9340-5f45-b124-3a7d4d2c4cd9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Intrapleural pressure becomes positive during:', '["Quiet inspiration","Forced expiration","Quiet expiration","At FRC","During breath-holding"]'::jsonb, 'Forced expiration', 'During forced expiration, contraction of abdominal muscles and internal intercostals raises intrapleural pressure above atmospheric.'),
('ddfb23de-5fcd-501e-80a1-c51efc8afa21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Pneumothorax causes:', '["Increased intrapleural negativity","Equalization of intrapleural and atmospheric pressure","Increased lung compliance","Hyperinflation of affected lung","Decreased alveolar pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Equalization of intrapleural and atmospheric pressure', 'Air in pleural space eliminates negative pressure, causing lung collapse due to its intrinsic elastic recoil.'),
('0d4b3542-3ca6-5ebe-8c64-b77e3959dd87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The alveolar pressure at the end of expiration (before next inspiration) is:', '["-1 cm H2O","-5 cm H2O","+5 cm H2O","+1 cm H2O","0 cm H2O"]'::jsonb, '0 cm H2O', 'At end-expiration (FRC), no airflow occurs because alveolar pressure equals atmospheric pressure.'),
('a6ac4ef7-e29a-5254-b34a-1cdab4140b71'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'During normal tidal breathing, alveolar pressure during inspiration is approximately:', '["0 cm H2O","-10 cm H2O","-5 cm H2O","-1 cm H2O","+1 cm H2O"]'::jsonb, '-1 cm H2O', 'Alveolar pressure during quiet inspiration is only slightly negative (~ -1 cm H2O) due to low flow resistance.'),
('4140ea35-1725-5f93-97c0-2b243ead8858'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Surface tension in alveoli is lowest at:', '["Mid-lung volumes","Low lung volumes","Peak inspiration","At TLC","High lung volumes"]'::jsonb, 'Low lung volumes', 'Surfactant molecules are more concentrated when alveolar surface area decreases, maximally reducing surface tension at low volumes.'),
('cd0c3d01-d24a-5516-8ceb-7653682b90af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The most important factor preventing alveolar collapse at end-expiration is:', '["Airway smooth muscle","Pulmonary blood volume","Surfactant","Collagen fibers","Elastic fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant', 'Surfactant reduces surface tension to near zero at low volumes, preventing alveolar collapse (atelectasis).'),
('3695a5d1-8591-5a7e-88ba-5489bc055650'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A decrease in compliance means the lungs are:', '["More compliant","More distensible","Less elastic","Stiffer","Hyperinflated"]'::jsonb, 'Stiffer', 'Decreased compliance indicates increased stiffness; more pressure is required to achieve the same volume change.'),
('8439f3f0-b5e7-5080-83c9-174a33e7d11d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Specific compliance normalizes compliance for:', '["Body weight","Age","Gender","Height","Lung volume"]'::jsonb, 'Lung volume', 'Specific compliance = compliance / FRC, allowing comparison between lungs of different sizes.'),
('de6e86a4-9ef5-5fb0-8161-f49b49c161df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'At high lung volumes, compliance decreases because:', '["Elastic fibers are fully stretched","Alveoli are overdistended","Surfactant is depleted","Collagen fibers limit further expansion","Chest wall restricts expansion"]'::jsonb, 'Collagen fibers limit further expansion', 'At high volumes, collagen fibers (stiffer than elastin) become taut, sharply limiting further distension (safety mechanism).'),
('eb6eaa7f-52e8-58c0-bc84-63f4afa2ed64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The time constant (tau) of a lung unit equals:', '["1 /","Resistance + Compliance","Compliance / Resistance","Resistance / Compliance","Resistance x Compliance"]'::jsonb, 'Resistance x Compliance', 'Tau = R x C. It determines the rate of filling/emptying of alveolar units. ~63% filling occurs in one time constant.'),
('9e35a2da-ea64-5330-9fa1-9b482257cd00'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In obstructive lung disease, the time constant is:', '["Variable","Unchanged","Normal","Decreased","Increased"]'::jsonb, 'Increased', 'Increased airway resistance (and often increased compliance in emphysema) prolongs the time constant, slowing lung emptying.'),
('653c5671-eaf9-5038-9cd8-cc77815987f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The pleural pressure gradient from apex to base in upright position is due to:', '["Thoracic shape","Lung weight and gravity","Diaphragmatic contraction","Regional compliance differences","Airway branching"]'::jsonb, 'Lung weight and gravity', 'Lung weight causes apical regions to have more negative intrapleural pressure than basal regions in upright posture.'),
('06a17cca-9db9-595d-a1c0-27afa497da46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Regional ventilation in upright lungs is greatest at:', '["Apex","Posterior segments","Bases","Anterior segments","Hilum"]'::jsonb, 'Bases', 'Basal regions have less negative intrapleural pressure and are on a steeper portion of the compliance curve, receiving more ventilation.'),
('86b1b1b0-8856-56b7-b3a7-1ec0a3bcb5b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which of the following increases lung compliance?', '["Pulmonary fibrosis","Pneumothorax","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Emphysema","Pleural effusion"]'::jsonb, 'Emphysema', 'Emphysema destroys alveolar walls, reducing elastic recoil and increasing compliance (i.e., making lungs more distensible).'),
('41973313-2943-51d9-9087-6c065b0b44e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex is mediated by:', '["Chemoreceptors","Stretch receptors in airways","J-receptors","Irritant receptors","Baroreceptors"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch receptors in airways', 'Slowly adapting stretch receptors in airway smooth muscle send signals via vagus nerve to inhibit inspiration, preventing overinflation.'),
('c4ac71d8-39d8-51e6-840e-074d3c4c9858'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Dynamic compliance differs from static compliance because it is measured:', '["At zero flow","At breath-hold","After maximal expiration","During active breathing","At TLC"]'::jsonb, 'During active breathing', 'Dynamic compliance is measured during normal breathing; it is lower than static compliance if airway resistance is increased.'),
('4f1c8cf9-1dad-500b-91f2-d414a980b9b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Chest wall compliance is reduced in:', '["Diabetes","Emphysema","Pulmonary embolism","Kyphoscoliosis","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Kyphoscoliosis', 'Chest wall deformities like kyphoscoliosis stiffen the chest wall, reducing its compliance and increasing work of breathing.'),
('841d15f3-ee39-50b8-b9e2-90f8b9e602f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The neutral position of the chest wall (no recoil) occurs at:', '["FRC","TLC","~75% of TLC","RV","~50% of TLC"]'::jsonb, '~75% of TLC', 'The chest wall''s neutral volume is about 75% of TLC. Below this, chest wall recoils outward; above, it recoils inward.'),
('a8590525-9fc8-5325-99b8-89aab18011af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which determinate of airway resistance is most important in small airways?', '["Flow pattern","Length","Viscosity","Gas density","Radius"]'::jsonb, 'Radius', 'Poiseuille''s law shows resistance is inversely proportional to radius^4, making radius the dominant factor.'),
('c4a2178c-7a68-54ea-9847-b1e65f9ae6ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The effect of sympathetic stimulation on bronchiolar smooth muscle is:', '["No effect","Relaxation","Enhanced cough reflex","Increased mucus secretion","Constriction"]'::jsonb, 'Relaxation', 'Beta-2 adrenergic receptors mediate bronchial smooth muscle relaxation, used therapeutically in asthma.'),
('4dc8fb46-306d-560b-87a1-4f4cfc062b2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Parasympathetic stimulation causes bronchoconstriction via:', '["Norepinephrine on beta receptors","Acetylcholine on nicotinic receptors","Serotonin on 5-HT receptors","Acetylcholine on M3 receptors","Epinephrine on alpha receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine on M3 receptors', 'Vagal release of ACh acts on M3 muscarinic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle, causing contraction.'),
('7fdc384e-0127-5513-b09b-28f5679a2723'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Tidal volume (TV) in a normal adult at rest is approximately:', '["100 mL","500 mL","1000 mL","250 mL","1500 mL"]'::jsonb, '500 mL', 'Normal tidal volume is ~6-8 mL/kg, approximately 500 mL in a 70 kg adult.'),
('a0fefed9-b2b0-5830-9bb5-92de2ad61b67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) is the:', '["Maximum volume that can be exhaled","Volume in lungs at end-inspiration","Extra volume inhaled above tidal volume","Volume remaining after maximal expiration","Volume exhaled after normal expiration"]'::jsonb, 'Extra volume inhaled above tidal volume', 'IRV is ~3000 mL, the additional air that can be inhaled after a normal tidal inspiration.'),
('807e4cde-6ae6-5dd6-ae1e-c9bf9a993bb3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Expiratory reserve volume (ERV) is the:', '["Volume in dead space","Volume remaining after maximal expiration","Maximum expiratory flow rate","Volume inhaled during normal breath","Extra volume exhaled after normal expiration"]'::jsonb, 'Extra volume exhaled after normal expiration', 'ERV is ~1200 mL, the additional air that can be forcibly exhaled after a normal tidal expiration.'),
('e9cbd55e-9567-5e92-9800-43e990e17654'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Residual volume (RV) is the volume of air remaining in lungs after:', '["Forced expiration","Normal expiration","Maximal inspiration","Maximal expiration","Normal inspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Maximal expiration', 'RV (~1200 mL) prevents lung collapse and maintains continuous gas exchange between breaths.'),
('3ce051f6-28cb-55e9-9124-10a91d246c85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which lung volume cannot be measured by simple spirometry?', '["Expiratory reserve volume","Vital capacity","Residual volume","Tidal volume","Inspiratory reserve volume"]'::jsonb, 'Residual volume', 'RV cannot be measured by spirometry because it remains in lungs after maximal exhalation; requires helium dilution or plethysmography.'),
('bbba6605-20de-5a0b-ac0b-675cc3a2ae97'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Vital capacity (VC) is the sum of:', '["TV + IRV","TV + IRV + ERV + RV","TV + IRV + ERV","IRV + ERV","ERV + RV"]'::jsonb, 'TV + IRV + ERV', 'VC = TV + IRV + ERV (~4600 mL), the maximum volume exhaled after maximal inspiration.'),
('a284fbb8-bb69-5441-8d8c-26e52fbc38c6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Total lung capacity (TLC) is the sum of:', '["VC + RV","TV + IRV","FRC + TV","TV + IRV + ERV","IC + ERV"]'::jsonb, 'VC + RV', 'TLC = VC + RV (~5800 mL). It is the total volume of air in lungs at maximal inflation.'),
('31b47ebc-229d-519b-b06c-a9e77d6f0c78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Functional residual capacity (FRC) is the sum of:', '["RV + TV","IRV + TV","TV + ERV","ERV + TV","ERV + RV"]'::jsonb, 'ERV + RV', 'FRC = ERV + RV (~2400 mL). It represents the volume at end-normal expiration when respiratory muscles are relaxed.'),
('f101c100-1566-56bf-ad6a-d9bcffee0dbc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Inspiratory capacity (IC) is the sum of:', '["TV + IRV + ERV","TV + IRV","RV + TV","IRV + ERV","ERV + TV"]'::jsonb, 'TV + IRV', 'IC = TV + IRV (~3500 mL), the maximum volume inhaled from FRC.'),
('cf596e53-4a0c-5f0e-9e9b-354b86027e0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'FRC is important because it:', '["Prevents large changes in PaO2 between breaths","Increases oxygen delivery","Reduces dead space","Enhances surfactant production","Maximizes vital capacity"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents large changes in PaO2 between breaths', 'FRC acts as a buffer reservoir, preventing wide swings in alveolar gas tensions during the respiratory cycle.'),
('140ca0e8-89be-5b59-8860-f37d41c1d4cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal VC is approximately what percentage of TLC?', '["~50%","~70%","~80%","~90%","~60%"]'::jsonb, '~80%', 'VC/TLC ratio is normally ~80%. A reduced ratio suggests restrictive disease (low TLC) or obstructive disease with air trapping.'),
('e38365c0-1fdd-5213-a825-81dd6c94fa8c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Spirometry cannot directly measure which of the following?', '["FRC","TV","ERV","IRV","VC"]'::jsonb, 'FRC', 'FRC contains RV which cannot be exhaled, so FRC requires indirect methods (helium dilution, nitrogen washout, body plethysmography).'),
('62890306-1a09-5af5-8dfb-05ac261b1e8f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In restrictive lung disease, TLC is:', '["Normal","Variable","Increased","Decreased","Initially increased then decreased"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased', 'Restrictive diseases (fibrosis, kyphoscoliosis) reduce TLC due to decreased compliance or chest wall expansion limitation.'),
('279b4ac8-e7f1-55a7-a18a-ad5339d23649'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In obstructive lung disease, which lung volume is characteristically increased?', '["TV only","RV and FRC","IRV only","IC only","VC only"]'::jsonb, 'RV and FRC', 'Air trapping in obstructive disease increases RV and FRC; TLC may also be increased in emphysema.'),
('758ce311-31da-51fc-888d-421ae1932682'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The RV/TLC ratio is increased in:', '["Acute asthma attack","COPD","Pulmonary fibrosis","Normal young adults","Athletes"]'::jsonb, 'COPD', 'Air trapping increases RV, and TLC is also increased, but RV/TLC ratio rises (often >40% in severe COPD).'),
('264de486-55cf-54f5-977d-8a294a489971'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Closing capacity is the lung volume at which:', '["Large airways collapse","Maximum expiratory flow occurs","Pulmonary capillaries close","Small airways in dependent lung zones begin to close","Surfactant is maximally effective"]'::jsonb, 'Small airways in dependent lung zones begin to close', 'Closing capacity increases with age and may exceed FRC, causing airway closure during normal breathing and V/Q mismatch.'),
('4b3aab9d-92d5-53b2-8d64-b2957b022986'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'If closing capacity exceeds FRC, the result is:', '["Improved gas exchange","Airway closure during tidal breathing","Increased compliance","Decreased dead space","Increased vital capacity"]'::jsonb, 'Airway closure during tidal breathing', 'When CC > FRC, dependent airways close during parts of the tidal cycle, causing hypoxemia (common in elderly).'),
('233aac8a-273b-5a86-9188-a3eec92f5da0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Minute ventilation (VE) equals:', '["TV x respiratory rate","(TV - DS) x respiratory rate","TV + respiratory rate","VC x respiratory rate","TV / respiratory rate"]'::jsonb, 'TV x respiratory rate', 'Minute ventilation = tidal volume x breaths/min (normally ~500 x 12 = 6 L/min).'),
('468ff56d-7e3c-5484-9cc4-6d4ee952bb02'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Alveolar ventilation (VA) equals:', '["TV x respiratory rate","VC x respiratory rate","(TV + DS) x respiratory rate","FRC x respiratory rate","(TV - DS) x respiratory rate"]'::jsonb, '(TV - DS) x respiratory rate', 'Alveolar ventilation accounts for gas-exchanging portion of each breath (TV minus dead space). Normal ~4.2 L/min.'),
('7f9de378-fd15-51ae-8de0-c419c994c1fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal ratio of dead space to tidal volume (VD/VT) is:', '["0.7-0.8","0.8-0.9","0.1-0.2","0.2-0.4","0.5-0.6"]'::jsonb, '0.2-0.4', 'Normal VD/VT is ~0.3 (150 mL dead space / 500 mL TV). It increases in conditions with wasted ventilation.'),
('caa52fa5-78ee-5a84-8071-fd25ad0132c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'By the helium dilution method, if initial He concentration = 10% and final = 5% in a 5 L spirometer, FRC is:', '["10 L","7.5 L","1 L","5 L","2.5 L"]'::jsonb, '5 L', 'C1V1 = C2V2; 10% x (5) = 5% x (5 + FRC); FRC = 5 L.'),
('a02f575a-cc95-5ec0-8952-eff3b8eecb99'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Body plethysmography measures FRC based on:', '["Henry''s law","Dalton''s law","Boyle''s law","Poiseuille''s law","Fick''s law"]'::jsonb, 'Boyle''s law', 'Plethysmography uses Boyle''s law (P1V1 = P2V2) as the subject pants against a closed shutter, measuring thoracic gas volume.'),
('7c48a802-ec65-5af8-9a4d-02e9a0035a08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The volume of air in the conducting airways that does not participate in gas exchange is called:', '["Residual volume","Physiological dead space","Minute volume","Anatomical dead space","Alveolar dead space"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical dead space', 'Anatomical dead space (~150 mL) is the volume of conducting airways (nose to terminal bronchioles).'),
('8543b727-3871-546a-8720-e997adb3bcc7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Alveolar dead space increases in:', '["Asthma","Bronchitis","Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary fibrosis","Pneumonia"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary embolism', 'In PE, ventilated but unperfused alveoli create alveolar dead space, increasing physiological dead space.'),
('f5501c4f-abe5-5bbf-a369-007c1b537da2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Physiological dead space equals:', '["Anatomical - alveolar dead space","Alveolar dead space - anatomical","Anatomical + alveolar dead space","Anatomical dead space x 2","Total lung capacity - FRC"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical + alveolar dead space', 'Physiological dead space is the sum of anatomical and alveolar dead spaces; measured by Bohr equation.'),
('e2884fca-ab7d-5e0b-896b-33a51e887e93'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Bohr equation quantifies:', '["Alveolar ventilation mediated physiological mechanism","Dead space (VD = VT x (PaCO2 - PeCO2)/PaCO2)","Shunt fraction mediated physiological mechanism","Oxygen consumption mediated physiological mechanism","Diffusion capacity mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Dead space (VD = VT x (PaCO2 - PeCO2)/PaCO2)', 'Bohr equation: VD/VT = (PaCO2 - PeCO2) / PaCO2. Measures physiological dead space fraction.'),
('93f8e0c2-cf9c-5b2b-8bef-a61b4dd22e48'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Maximum voluntary ventilation (MVV) is:', '["Maximal volume breathed in 12-15 seconds extrapolated to 1 min","Maximal volume exhaled forcefully mediated physiological mechanism","Maximal volume exhaled in 1 second mediated physiological mechanism","Resting minute ventilation mediated physiological mechanism","Ventilation at maximal exercise mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Maximal volume breathed in 12-15 seconds extrapolated to 1 min', 'MVV tests overall respiratory function including airway resistance, muscle strength, and compliance. Normal ~170 L/min in males.'),
('c81812b8-dabf-5548-a47c-879c72468962'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which capacity represents the maximum amount of air a person can exhale after a maximal inspiration?', '["Total lung capacity","Functional residual capacity","Inspiratory capacity","Vital capacity","Expiratory reserve volume"]'::jsonb, 'Vital capacity', 'VC is the maximum exhaled volume after maximum inspiration. It can be measured by spirometry.'),
('368b5833-8ba2-5d97-a9ed-62648feb8604'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'FRC is increased in which condition?', '["Emphysema","ARDS","Pneumothorax","Kyphoscoliosis","Pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Emphysema', 'Loss of elastic recoil in emphysema increases lung volume at relaxation point — FRC is increased.'),
('6ce43139-b770-5d09-a722-e5b3fb889417'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The volume of air inhaled or exhaled during normal quiet breathing is:', '["Alveolar ventilation","Tidal volume","Vital capacity","Dead space volume","Minute volume"]'::jsonb, 'Tidal volume', 'Tidal volume is ~500 mL for normal quiet breathing.'),
('cd738e10-8b7a-5334-82c2-7e43f544e615'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'FEV1 is the volume of air exhaled in the first second of:', '["A forced vital capacity (FVC) maneuver","Peak flow measurement mediated physiological mechanism","Slow vital capacity mediated physiological mechanism","A maximal inspiration mediated physiological mechanism","A normal tidal breath mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'A forced vital capacity (FVC) maneuver', 'FEV1 is measured during a maximal forced expiration after full inspiration.'),
('2414b219-0e0c-5427-848e-126fb050973e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Normal FEV1/FVC ratio is approximately:', '["0.90-0.95","0.40-0.50","0.75-0.85","0.60-0.70","0.50-0.60"]'::jsonb, '0.75-0.85', 'Normal FEV1/FVC is ~0.75-0.85. Values <0.70 suggest obstructive ventilatory defect.'),
('877d1bbb-fdb3-5187-ab36-ad6f7c9c56f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In obstructive lung disease, FEV1/FVC is:', '["Unchanged","Variable","Decreased","Increased","Normal"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased', 'Airway obstruction reduces FEV1 more than FVC, lowering the FEV1/FVC ratio (<0.7).'),
('db139e55-0ddf-5209-8c3c-6f19111719e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In restrictive lung disease, FEV1/FVC is:', '["Markedly decreased","Decreased","Zero","Normal or increased","Unmeasurable"]'::jsonb, 'Normal or increased', 'In restriction, both FEV1 and FVC are reduced proportionally, so ratio is normal or even increased (>0.85).'),
('499e4005-7a51-5357-80b2-63b8bc28a34a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) is primarily dependent on:', '["Diaphragmatic strength","Small airway resistance","Chest wall compliance","Effort and large airway caliber","Lung compliance"]'::jsonb, 'Effort and large airway caliber', 'PEFR reflects large airway function and patient effort, commonly used in asthma monitoring.'),
('d382a836-f584-59d6-a07c-f48d8ffa10c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which test best differentiates obstructive from restrictive lung disease?', '["DLCO","FEV1","MVV","FEV1/FVC ratio","PEFR"]'::jsonb, 'FEV1/FVC ratio', 'FEV1/FVC ratio is the key discriminator: low in obstruction, normal/high in restriction.'),
('25f22fab-d710-5a87-b249-8b70c88f73f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Flow-volume loop in obstructive lung disease shows:', '["Concave expiratory limb with reduced peak flow","Normal shape","Square-shaped loop","Symmetrical reduction in all flows","Convex expiratory limb"]'::jsonb, 'Concave expiratory limb with reduced peak flow', 'Obstruction shows characteristic scooped-out or concave expiratory curve due to flow limitation during forced exhalation.'),
('820f0151-3e71-57d7-bbad-d409c8bf5c67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Flow-volume loop in restrictive lung disease shows:', '["Increased flows throughout mediated physiological mechanism","Reduced volumes with relatively preserved flows","Concave expiratory limb mediated physiological mechanism","Markedly reduced peak flow mediated physiological mechanism","Elongated expiratory limb mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced volumes with relatively preserved flows', 'Restriction produces a smaller loop with reduced TLC and VC, but flow rates are relatively preserved or even increased for lung size.'),
('4ad0eb4f-a540-5ee4-aec4-5bbc61bff41a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The most sensitive test for detecting early small airway disease is:', '["FEF25-75%","PEFR","FEV1/FVC","FVC","FEV1"]'::jsonb, 'FEF25-75%', 'FEF25-75% reflects flow through small airways and can be reduced early in obstructive disease before FEV1 declines.'),
('90be0900-f4f3-5fec-b7d1-87d47f2667a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Bronchodilator reversibility is defined as an increase in FEV1 of:', '[">20% and >500 mL",">15% and >300 mL",">5% and >100 mL",">12% and >200 mL from baseline",">10% and >50 mL"]'::jsonb, '>12% and >200 mL from baseline', 'ATS/ERS criteria for significant bronchodilator response: ≥12% and ≥200 mL increase in FEV1 or FVC.'),
('cfb60596-0f10-59e2-b7a4-756f21e0b7b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The 6-minute walk test primarily assesses:', '["Pulmonary mechanics","Gas exchange efficiency","Ventilatory reserve","Functional exercise capacity","Peak aerobic capacity"]'::jsonb, 'Functional exercise capacity', 'The 6MWT measures the distance a patient can walk in 6 minutes, reflecting submaximal functional capacity.'),
('8f6c083a-9741-5a3a-8ddd-ffccae6b81c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In severe COPD, FEV1 is typically:', '["<70% predicted","<80% predicted","<50% predicted","<30% predicted","<60% predicted"]'::jsonb, '<30% predicted', 'GOLD stage 4 (very severe COPD): FEV1 < 30% predicted or < 50% with chronic respiratory failure.'),
('f75b6246-c96b-55a6-bec4-36c3eba564e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Lung volume reduction surgery benefits patients with:', '["Homogeneous emphysema mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary fibrosis mediated physiological mechanism","Heterogeneous emphysema with upper lobe predominance","Bronchiectasis mediated physiological mechanism","Asthma mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Heterogeneous emphysema with upper lobe predominance', 'LVRS removes most damaged lung regions, improving elastic recoil and diaphragm mechanics in selected emphysema patients.'),
('bbbdcf89-53c9-53c7-b445-d52611aa4053'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The single-breath nitrogen washout test evaluates:', '["Airway resistance mediated physiological mechanism","Closing volume and distribution of ventilation","Thoracic gas volume mediated physiological mechanism","Diffusion capacity mediated physiological mechanism","Maximal expiratory flow mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Closing volume and distribution of ventilation', 'The nitrogen washout test measures closing volume and reveals uneven ventilation distribution (slope of phase III).'),
('ace37a26-3254-5f36-87c6-ec67c6a970e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A positive methacholine challenge test indicates:', '["Fixed airway obstruction","Pulmonary fibrosis","Airway hyperresponsiveness","Emphysema","Restrictive lung disease"]'::jsonb, 'Airway hyperresponsiveness', 'Methacholine causes bronchoconstriction; a PC20 < 4 mg/mL indicates airway hyperresponsiveness typical of asthma.'),
('26d12409-6169-5d4a-8671-24951f818b49'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which pulmonary function parameter is most reduced in pulmonary fibrosis?', '["FEV1/FVC","FEF25-75%","MVV","FVC","PEFR"]'::jsonb, 'FVC', 'In restrictive disease, FVC is markedly reduced. FEV1/FVC ratio is normal or increased.'),
('cc7c9de6-cac8-5688-913a-e45ec201ad75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO) is reduced in:', '["Anxiety hyperventilation","Obesity hypoventilation","Chronic bronchitis","Pulmonary fibrosis","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary fibrosis', 'DLCO is reduced in conditions that thicken or destroy the alveolar-capillary membrane (fibrosis, emphysema, ILD).'),
('f7971ccb-caef-5867-9f60-3d8d5be4c443'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'DLCO is increased in:', '["Pulmonary embolism","Polycythemia","Anemia","Emphysema","Pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Polycythemia', 'Increased hemoglobin concentration in polycythemia increases CO binding sites, raising DLCO.'),
('c334992b-9185-5d47-85ff-ef4741b5e5f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A normal FEV1/FVC with reduced FVC suggests:', '["Normal variant","Upper airway obstruction","Obstructive lung disease","Mixed disorder","Restrictive lung disease"]'::jsonb, 'Restrictive lung disease', 'FEV1/FVC ≥0.7 with reduced FVC is the classic pattern of restriction (low TLC needed for confirmation).'),
('aa50319c-3282-53ee-9273-f4aaba1d10b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A reduced FEV1/FVC with normal FEV1 may be seen in:', '["Normal individuals","Severe restriction","Mixed disease","Restrictive disease","Early obstructive disease"]'::jsonb, 'Early obstructive disease', 'In early COPD, FEV1/FVC may drop before FEV1 falls below 80% predicted (GOLD 1).'),
('dbecf48c-97f3-5342-bbd7-d110bf530995'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'PEFR monitoring at home is most useful for managing:', '["COPD","Bronchiectasis","Asthma","Pulmonary fibrosis","Pneumonia"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma', 'Home PEFR monitoring helps detect early deterioration and guide asthma management based on personal best values.'),
('94368cf1-08ae-53a4-be5c-8fbf8bfc153c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Lung function tests that require patient cooperation include all EXCEPT:', '["MVV","DLCO","All require cooperation","PEFR","FVC maneuver"]'::jsonb, 'DLCO', 'DLCO requires a 10-second breath-hold but is less effort-dependent than FVC, PEFR, or MVV maneuvers.'),
('533573b3-ced1-5415-83e6-dc03a54c7ee9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which condition shows a flow-volume loop with a flattened inspiratory limb?', '["Restrictive lung disease","Obstructive lung disease","Muscle weakness","Fixed upper airway obstruction","Normal variant"]'::jsonb, 'Fixed upper airway obstruction', 'Fixed upper airway obstruction (e.g., tracheal stenosis) limits flow equally in inspiration and expiration — both limbs flattened.'),
('66bf121b-2669-5bfd-a9f3-b1507fd7b5ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Variable extrathoracic obstruction (e.g., vocal cord paralysis) shows:', '["Flattened expiratory limb mediated physiological mechanism","Normal loop mediated physiological mechanism","Flattened inspiratory limb with preserved expiratory flow","Both limbs flattened mediated physiological mechanism","Increased peak flow mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Flattened inspiratory limb with preserved expiratory flow', 'Extrathoracic obstruction narrows during inspiration due to negative intraluminal pressure, limiting inspiratory flow.'),
('32e298b6-4516-5a7a-954c-381046149dc1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The single most important pulmonary function test for diagnosing COPD is:', '["Post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC < 0.70","TLC < 80%","DLCO < 80%","PEFR < 80%","Pre-bronchodilator FEV1 < 80%"]'::jsonb, 'Post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC < 0.70', 'GOLD guidelines define COPD by post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC < 0.70 confirming persistent airflow limitation.'),
('aa84c370-b248-5f08-83da-20edeeb046ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Maximum inspiratory pressure (MIP) at the mouth measures:', '["Lung compliance","Airway resistance","Expiratory muscle strength","Chest wall compliance","Inspiratory muscle strength"]'::jsonb, 'Inspiratory muscle strength', 'MIP (or NIF) < -30 cm H2O suggests adequate inspiratory muscle strength; low values predict weaning failure.'),
('751569e1-d4b6-5255-b242-a7800cd44333'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The sniff nasal inspiratory pressure (SNIP) test assesses:', '["Nasal patency","Mucociliary clearance","Diaphragmatic strength","Upper airway resistance","Olfactory function"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragmatic strength', 'SNIP is a non-invasive test of inspiratory muscle (especially diaphragm) strength using a nasal catheter during a sniff.'),
('f6addb69-651b-5931-832b-731dbcdaa95d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Pre-bronchodilator FEV1 of 65% predicted with FEV1/FVC 0.65 and 15% reversibility indicates:', '["Normal lungs","Mixed disorder","COPD","Restrictive lung disease","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'COPD', 'Airflow limitation with partial reversibility is characteristic of COPD; asthma typically shows >12% and >200 mL reversibility.'),
('52681cbf-89d7-5442-b3f1-7c82a7724308'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A TLC of 60% predicted with FEV1/FVC of 0.85 indicates:', '["Poor effort","Normal variant","Restrictive lung disease","Obstructive lung disease","Mixed disorder"]'::jsonb, 'Restrictive lung disease', 'Low TLC with normal FEV1/FVC confirms restrictive defect (e.g., fibrosis, chest wall disease, neuromuscular).'),
('36ca0aa2-72ab-5ecf-9780-c07a602a4d93'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which gas is most commonly used for single-breath diffusion capacity measurement?', '["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Helium","Nitrogen","Carbon monoxide"]'::jsonb, 'Carbon monoxide', 'CO binds tightly to hemoglobin with high affinity, making it ideal for measuring diffusion capacity across alveolar-capillary membrane.'),
('95097146-ce6f-5f3a-bb4b-a01962e684da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Fick''s law of diffusion states that gas transfer is proportional to:', '["Molecular weight / surface area","Partial pressure gradient x thickness","Surface area x partial pressure gradient / thickness","Thickness / surface area","Surface area / thickness"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x partial pressure gradient / thickness', 'Fick''s law: Vgas = A/T x D x (P1-P2). Gas transfer rate depends on area, gradient, and membrane properties.'),
('bb781f10-50c6-54ab-b451-b5649cba995b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Diffusion capacity for O2 is approximately what fraction of that for CO?', '["Equal","2 times","0.5 times","1.2 times","3 times"]'::jsonb, '1.2 times', 'DO2/DLCO ≈ 1.2. CO diffuses slightly faster due to molecular weight differences, but both are diffusion-limited.'),
('20199ac5-e68b-5394-9cf0-cc48d8def0c6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The rate of diffusion of a gas across the alveolar-capillary membrane is inversely proportional to:', '["Membrane thickness","Surface area","Temperature","Solubility coefficient","Partial pressure gradient"]'::jsonb, 'Membrane thickness', 'Fick''s law: thicker membrane reduces diffusion rate. Seen in pulmonary fibrosis (alveolar-capillary block).'),
('5bb70f90-0547-5dcc-9089-e03a460f0a86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The most important factor determining the rate of gas diffusion for a given gas is:', '["Molecular weight of the gas","Membrane surface area","Temperature","Membrane thickness","Partial pressure gradient across membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Partial pressure gradient across membrane', 'The partial pressure gradient is the driving force for diffusion, as described by Fick''s law.'),
('397d8726-2cfa-5512-94a8-ada7b020c2ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Graham''s law states that the rate of diffusion is inversely proportional to:', '["Square of molecular weight","Molecular weight","Log of molecular weight","Square root of molecular weight","Cube root of molecular weight"]'::jsonb, 'Square root of molecular weight', 'Graham''s law: Rate ∝ 1/√MW. Lighter gases (He) diffuse faster than heavier ones.'),
('b817dafb-9d22-5184-b760-62de36daa6c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'O2 diffusion across the alveolar-capillary membrane is considered:', '["Flow-limited","Both diffusion and perfusion limited","Diffusion-limited under normal conditions","Perfusion-limited","Neither diffusion nor perfusion limited"]'::jsonb, 'Diffusion-limited under normal conditions', 'O2 uptake is normally diffusion-limited at rest, meaning it depends on the diffusion properties of the membrane.'),
('06533ee0-b06f-52b0-b2b1-f405ebedb7ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'CO2 diffusion across the alveolar-capillary membrane is:', '["Negligible","Slower than O2","Equal to O2","Similar to CO","Very rapid"]'::jsonb, 'Very rapid', 'CO2 is ~20x more soluble than O2, so it diffuses much more rapidly despite similar molecular weights.'),
('b511263a-284f-5329-84f0-0b1d7ffb4a66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The alveolar gas equation calculates:', '["A-a gradient","PAO2 = PIO2 -","VD/VT","Shunt fraction","PaCO2"]'::jsonb, 'PAO2 = PIO2 -', 'Alveolar gas equation: PAO2 = PIO2 - PaCO2/RQ (with correction factor F). Critical for calculating A-a gradient.'),
('7a985913-4a00-5708-9ac7-c1df79aec9f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal A-a gradient (P[A-a]O2) on room air is:', '["0-5 mmHg","<40-50 mmHg","<100 mmHg","<60-70 mmHg","<10-20 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '<10-20 mmHg', 'Normal A-a gradient is <10-20 mmHg on room air. It increases with age and in disease states causing V/Q mismatch.'),
('efcecb10-62ce-5718-be31-50f289876b51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'An increased A-a gradient indicates:', '["Normal lungs mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoventilation alone mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperventilation mediated physiological mechanism","V/Q mismatch, shunt, or diffusion impairment","Increased CO2 production mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'V/Q mismatch, shunt, or diffusion impairment', 'A-a gradient widens when gas exchange across alveoli is impaired. Isolated hypoventilation does NOT increase A-a gradient.'),
('4be7c78a-c4e3-52ac-a541-a8c06c26b297'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A normal A-a gradient with hypoxemia suggests:', '["Hypoventilation","V/Q mismatch","Right-to-left shunt","Pulmonary embolism","Diffusion impairment"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoventilation', 'Pure hypoventilation increases PaCO2 which lowers PAO2 but A-a gradient remains normal (e.g., opioid overdose).'),
('88aa0f7f-8df0-5fdc-bfea-ed7ec965bb19'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch is the most common cause of:', '["Alkalosis","Hypercapnia","Polycythemia","Pulmonary hypertension","Hypoxemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxemia', 'V/Q mismatch accounts for most clinical hypoxemia (e.g., COPD, asthma, pneumonia).'),
('29d62843-1457-54ca-91c0-3ca859cdc267'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal V/Q ratio for the whole lung is:', '["1.2-1.5","0.8-1.0","0.2-0.3","1.5-2.0","0.4-0.5"]'::jsonb, '0.8-1.0', 'Normal V/Q is ~0.85 (alveolar ventilation ~4.2 L/min / cardiac output ~5 L/min).'),
('6893dc49-f658-5cf6-93e0-b6fbeacb6b6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In West zone 1 of the lung, what happens?', '["Venous > alveolar > arterial pressure","Pulmonary arterial > alveolar > venous pressure","Equal pressures","No blood flow at apex","Alveolar pressure exceeds pulmonary arterial and venous pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar pressure exceeds pulmonary arterial and venous pressure', 'Zone 1 (apex in upright lung): PA > Pa > Pv. Alveolar pressure collapses capillaries, creating dead space (normally minimal).'),
('b543a417-c04e-5fcf-9562-82f569dc1444'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In West zone 2, blood flow is determined by:', '["Alveolar pressure alone","Gravity alone","Venous pressure alone","Difference between arterial and venous pressure","Difference between arterial and alveolar pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Difference between arterial and alveolar pressure', 'Zone 2 (waterfall effect): Pa > PA > Pv. Flow = (Pa - PA)/R.'),
('50409156-5094-5aa7-b2ee-4264711e7c0b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In West zone 3, blood flow is determined by:', '["Venous pressure","Alveolar pressure","Arterial-venous pressure difference","Gravity","Arterial-alveolar pressure difference"]'::jsonb, 'Arterial-venous pressure difference', 'Zone 3 (bases): Pa > Pv > PA. Both pressures exceed alveolar, so flow = (Pa - Pv)/R, like continuous flow.'),
('308f65a8-e605-5c74-8f37-db50a909d897'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is a response to:', '["Sympathetic stimulation","Low alveolar PO2","High PCO2","Low cardiac output","Low blood pH"]'::jsonb, 'Low alveolar PO2', 'Alveolar hypoxia constricts nearby pulmonary arterioles to divert blood to better-oxygenated regions (Euler-Liljestrand mechanism).'),
('1c5df283-c5a7-5092-90ce-509a5953690f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Shunt refers to blood that passes through the lungs without:', '["Returning to left atrium","Entering pulmonary capillaries","Participating in gas exchange","Being oxygenated at all","Being filtered"]'::jsonb, 'Participating in gas exchange', 'Shunt is blood reaching arterial system without undergoing gas exchange (e.g., anatomical shunt, intrapulmonary shunt).'),
('69468d5b-a9ac-5c20-bf32-640db061d8d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'True shunt is characterized by:', '["Normal PaO2","Normal A-a gradient","Hypoxemia that does not correct with 100% O2","Hypoxemia that corrects with 100% O2","Hypercapnia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxemia that does not correct with 100% O2', 'True shunt is refractory to 100% O2 because shunted blood completely bypasses ventilated alveoli.'),
('17d75d93-9208-5cb3-9885-a01be0229ad6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'V/Q mismatch hypoxemia typically corrects with:', '["Steroids","Antibiotics","Bronchodilators","Diuretics","Supplemental oxygen"]'::jsonb, 'Supplemental oxygen', 'Increasing inspired O2 can compensate for areas with low V/Q (unlike shunt, which is O2-refractory).'),
('c87d42d2-dd82-5996-9649-2644abca1931'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The venous admixture equation (shunt equation) uses which formula?', '["Qs/Qt = A-a gradient/PaO2","Qs/Qt = PAO2 - PaO2","Qs/Qt = CaO2/","Qs/Qt = (CcO2 - CaO2)/","Qs/Qt = VD/VT"]'::jsonb, 'Qs/Qt = (CcO2 - CaO2)/', 'Shunt equation quantifies fraction of cardiac output that bypasses gas exchange.'),
('80378aa7-d6cb-5724-ab4d-01f12c1b653a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A-a gradient on 100% O2 helps distinguish:', '["Dead space from shunt","Shunt from V/Q mismatch","Diffusion impairment from V/Q mismatch","Hypoventilation from shunt","Obstructive from restrictive disease"]'::jsonb, 'Shunt from V/Q mismatch', 'Breathing 100% O2 eliminates V/Q mismatch as cause of hypoxemia; residual gradient indicates shunt.'),
('f3740e93-fe0e-5151-955f-c07d96b54436'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal PAO2 at sea level on room air is approximately:', '["50 mmHg","80 mmHg","150 mmHg","100 mmHg","120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '100 mmHg', 'PAO2 ≈ 150 - (40/0.8) = 100 mmHg by alveolar gas equation (with PIO2 = 150 mmHg at sea level).'),
('a8df5931-9870-5262-a4cf-8784063c3ef8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'PAO2 minus PaO2 (A-a gradient) is normally less than:', '["30-40 mmHg on room air","10-20 mmHg on room air","5 mmHg on room air","50 mmHg on room air","100 mmHg on room air"]'::jsonb, '10-20 mmHg on room air', 'A-a gradient increases with age but normally <20 mmHg on room air in young adults.'),
('8e1b52e4-e748-5382-99a1-892cedc680e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Gas exchange in the lung is most efficient when V/Q is:', '["Uniformly matched ~0.8-1.0","Low in all zones","Variable from 0 to infinity","Maximally high","High in all zones"]'::jsonb, 'Uniformly matched ~0.8-1.0', 'Optimal gas exchange occurs when ventilation and perfusion are well-matched regionally.'),
('b7187315-ed22-54a7-8690-a9fe80128e63'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Decreased DLCO is characteristic of:', '["Chronic bronchitis","Pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema","Pleural effusion","Pneumothorax","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema', 'DLCO is reduced in conditions that disrupt the alveolar-capillary interface (fibrosis thickens, emphysema destroys).'),
('6f39579e-efdb-5284-be60-ce5f3b51984f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'High V/Q units (e.g., >1) contribute to:', '["Shunt effect","Improved gas exchange","Decreased work of breathing","Increased oxygen delivery","Dead space effect"]'::jsonb, 'Dead space effect', 'High V/Q regions receive more ventilation than perfusion, resulting in wasted ventilation (alveolar dead space).'),
('ce02da19-f0c0-5048-9038-e2a6d6fc92c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Low V/Q units (e.g., <0.8) contribute to:', '["Hypocapnia","Alkalemia","Shunt-like effect","Dead space effect","Improved CO2 elimination"]'::jsonb, 'Shunt-like effect', 'Low V/Q regions are underventilated relative to perfusion, causing venous admixture and hypoxemia.'),
('bdfdfd51-a440-56ec-860e-e30d9b8632b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Oxygen-hemoglobin affinity is expressed by:', '["CaO2","A-a gradient","SaO2%","PaO2","P50"]'::jsonb, 'P50', 'P50 is the PO2 at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated. Normal P50 is ~26 mmHg. ↑P50 = ↓affinity (right shift).'),
('72591494-e584-59c7-8472-93265c412d94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which of the following will increase the P50 (right-shift the O2-Hb curve)?', '["Increased 2,3-BPG","Decreased PCO2","Decreased temperature","Increased pH","Carboxyhemoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Increased 2,3-BPG', '2,3-BPG binds to deoxyhemoglobin and stabilizes the T state, reducing O2 affinity (right shift, ↑P50).'),
('9b1e2fb7-6343-5d6f-adaa-a414900e4360'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Haldane effect describes:', '["CO2 combining with Hb","CO2 transport enhancement by deoxygenated hemoglobin","Bicarbonate transport","O2 transport enhancement by low pH","Carbamino compound formation"]'::jsonb, 'CO2 transport enhancement by deoxygenated hemoglobin', 'Haldane effect: Deoxygenated Hb binds more CO2, facilitating CO2 uptake in tissues and release in lungs.'),
('2184e5b6-02cc-5bf8-b4b2-08ae7292a12c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Venous blood normally has a PO2 of:', '["40 mmHg","26 mmHg","60 mmHg","80 mmHg","100 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '40 mmHg', 'Mixed venous PO2 is ~40 mmHg at rest, reflecting tissue O2 extraction.'),
('6a1c6755-37db-51a2-9d3c-97e97b0c8370'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Oxygen content (CaO2) in arterial blood is calculated as:', '["Hb x 1.34 x SaO2 only","SaO2 x 0.003","Hb x SaO2","(Hb x 1.34 x SaO2) +","PaO2 x 0.003 only"]'::jsonb, '(Hb x 1.34 x SaO2) +', 'CaO2 = Hb x 1.34 x SaO2 + dissolved O2 (0.003 x PaO2). Normal ~20 mL O2/dL blood.'),
('0f042d04-00a3-595c-851d-88855b0f1617'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In anemia, which O2 parameter is most affected?', '["SaO2","PaO2","Oxygen content","A-a gradient","P50"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygen content', 'Anemia reduces Hb, decreasing O2 content. PaO2 and SaO2 remain normal because dissolved O2 is unaffected.'),
('7381d7d3-cf66-532a-b5ed-045ed4062378'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The arterial-venous oxygen content difference [C(a-v)O2] normally is:', '["~10 mL O2/dL","~5 mL O2/dL","~20 mL O2/dL","~15 mL O2/dL","~25 mL O2/dL"]'::jsonb, '~5 mL O2/dL', 'CaO2 ~20 mL/dL, CvO2 ~15 mL/dL. The ~5 mL/dL difference reflects normal O2 extraction by tissues.'),
('bd23f0a8-e4b2-539e-9c06-c55b630b0725'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The majority of oxygen in blood is transported:', '["Bound to hemoglobin in RBCs","As carbamino compounds","Bound to albumin","As bicarbonate","Dissolved in plasma"]'::jsonb, 'Bound to hemoglobin in RBCs', '>98% of O2 is transported bound to hemoglobin; only 1-2% is dissolved in plasma.'),
('d9af6f6e-19d0-561a-9c7f-5fbbbceea2c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Each gram of hemoglobin can bind approximately how much O2?', '["3.0 mL O2","2.0 mL O2","1.34 mL O2","0.5 mL O2","1.0 mL O2"]'::jsonb, '1.34 mL O2', 'Hb theoretical capacity: 1 g Hb binds 1.34-1.39 mL O2.'),
('1b21b836-9aeb-54a5-86d3-b1fa2dc02c9b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve is sigmoidal because of:', '["Passive diffusion","First-order kinetics","Competitive inhibition","Non-cooperative binding","Cooperative binding of O2 to Hb"]'::jsonb, 'Cooperative binding of O2 to Hb', 'Cooperativity: binding of one O2 molecule increases affinity of remaining heme sites for O2 (positive cooperativity).'),
('56299e9f-380b-5237-a389-967174da0811'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) has a higher O2 affinity than adult Hb because:', '["It has a higher P50","It is monomeric","It has a different alpha chain","It binds 2,3-BPG less strongly","It lacks heme groups"]'::jsonb, 'It binds 2,3-BPG less strongly', 'HbF binds 2,3-BPG less avidly than HbA, shifting the O2 dissociation curve left (higher affinity).'),
('75c3aeb0-59b9-52de-a47d-b72775c9105b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Bohr effect describes:', '["Right shift of O2-Hb curve by increased H+ and CO2","O2-dependent CO2 transport","Effect of 2,3-BPG on Hb","Effect of CO2 on pH","Effect of temperature on O2-Hb curve"]'::jsonb, 'Right shift of O2-Hb curve by increased H+ and CO2', 'Bohr effect: acidosis and hypercapnia decrease Hb-O2 affinity, promoting O2 unloading in metabolically active tissues.'),
('90fd0839-b9b3-5d4d-a059-ec20b6ca12b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Methemoglobinemia impairs O2 transport because:', '["Iron is in Fe3+ (ferric) state and cannot bind O2","Iron is absent from heme","Hb structure is completely denatured","Red cell count is decreased","Hb binds CO2 instead of O2"]'::jsonb, 'Iron is in Fe3+ (ferric) state and cannot bind O2', 'Methemoglobin contains oxidized ferric iron (Fe3+) that cannot bind O2, reducing O2-carrying capacity.'),
('968d4c72-c501-5dd5-aff4-10a7a8a89710'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning reduces O2 transport by:', '["Competing with O2 for Hb binding with 250x greater affinity","Denaturing hemoglobin mediated physiological mechanism","Causing methemoglobinemia mediated physiological mechanism","Decreasing RBC count mediated physiological mechanism","Causing vasoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Competing with O2 for Hb binding with 250x greater affinity', 'CO binds Hb ~250x more avidly than O2, forming carboxyhemoglobin and reducing O2-carrying capacity.'),
('1c78c144-9d4e-559c-abf4-24243bd18d87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A 50% left shift in the O2-Hb curve means:', '["Hb holds O2 more tightly","P50 is increased","2,3-BPG is elevated","Temperature is increased","Hb unloads O2 more readily"]'::jsonb, 'Hb holds O2 more tightly', 'Left shift (↓P50) means increased O2 affinity — Hb holds O2 tighter, reducing O2 unloading to tissues.'),
('5f625cd1-b8c0-5a6a-aa50-1a3ffa5ec452'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Temperature increase causes the O2-Hb curve to:', '["No change","Shift left","Become linear","Shift downward only","Shift right"]'::jsonb, 'Shift right', 'Increased temperature decreases Hb-O2 affinity (right shift), promoting O2 unloading in exercising tissues.'),
('fc0a55e3-8f5c-5625-9deb-df28a8e2e25b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The condition that causes a left shift of the O2-Hb curve is:', '["Hypercapnia","Alkalosis","Acidosis","Increased 2,3-BPG","Hyperthermia"]'::jsonb, 'Alkalosis', 'Increased pH (alkalosis) increases Hb-O2 affinity — left shift, reduced O2 unloading (e.g., hyperventilation).'),
('db820357-c6dd-5192-b91e-b2338c1da3a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', '2,3-BPG is produced in RBCs via which metabolic pathway?', '["Krebs cycle","Rapoport-Luebering shunt of glycolysis","Electron transport chain","Hexose monophosphate shunt","Beta-oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapoport-Luebering shunt of glycolysis', '2,3-BPG is synthesized from 1,3-BPG via bisphosphoglycerate mutase in a side branch of Embden-Meyerhof glycolysis.'),
('8580248a-87f8-5ef9-9696-7c3c8f2baf5f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'At a PaO2 of 60 mmHg, SaO2 is approximately:', '["75%","50%","95%","90%","99%"]'::jsonb, '90%', 'On the steep portion of the O2-Hb curve, PaO2 60 mmHg corresponds to ~90% saturation (the "knee" of the curve).'),
('45e25c5c-9daf-5ebc-8233-77a26a64df33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Below which PaO2 does oxygen saturation fall sharply (steep portion)?', '["100 mmHg","60 mmHg","30 mmHg","80 mmHg","40 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '60 mmHg', 'The O2-Hb curve is relatively flat above 60 mmHg (safe reserve) and steep below 60 mmHg, where small PaO2 drops cause large SaO2 falls.'),
('b82b3f23-69b0-5a65-bcc7-5868fb26159b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal P50 value is approximately:', '["16 mmHg","56 mmHg","36 mmHg","46 mmHg","26 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '26 mmHg', 'Normal P50 is ~26 mmHg. A P50 > 26 indicates right shift (decreased affinity); < 26 indicates left shift (increased affinity).'),
('d627f2e0-e6cc-55a5-a95e-37f5d43369e5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In a patient with COPD and chronic hypoxemia, 2,3-BPG is:', '["Decreased","Unchanged","Normal","Absent","Increased"]'::jsonb, 'Increased', 'Chronic hypoxemia stimulates 2,3-BPG production, right-shifting the curve to improve O2 unloading in tissues.'),
('db4131e6-8a0a-5d58-86c5-1e3073447aa3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The main advantage of the sigmoid shape of the O2-Hb curve is:', '["Maximum O2 binding at low PO2","Enables H+ buffering","Stable SaO2 over wide PaO2 range in lungs; large unloading in tissues","Linear relation allows simple calculation","Prevents CO2 transport"]'::jsonb, 'Stable SaO2 over wide PaO2 range in lungs; large unloading in tissues', 'Flat upper portion maintains SaO2 despite PaO2 falls; steep lower portion facilitates O2 unloading in tissues.'),
('5607a590-8cda-56d8-a34e-e56efc621b9e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The O2 content of blood is most dependent on:', '["Hemoglobin concentration","pH","P50","Cardiac output","PaO2"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoglobin concentration', 'O2 content = (Hb x 1.34 x SaO2) + dissolved O2. Hb level is the dominant factor.'),
('ae0f4907-9670-57d7-858e-6a03f42dc8bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In polycythemia, arterial O2 content is:', '["Increased","Normal","Decreased","Unchanged","Variable"]'::jsonb, 'Increased', 'Increased Hb concentration raises O2-carrying capacity and O2 content despite normal PaO2.'),
('3f7fee7d-ec4c-523f-babf-b4f4ea9305d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The dissolved oxygen fraction of total O2 content is approximately:', '["20-25%","10-15%","0.1-0.5%","5-10%","1-2%"]'::jsonb, '1-2%', 'Dissolved O2 (0.003 x PaO2) contributes <2% of total content; Hb-bound O2 provides >98%.'),
('797d1e0b-53d9-5e56-ba52-4a04b7d9b414'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Myoglobin has a higher O2 affinity than hemoglobin because:', '["It does not exhibit cooperative binding; P50 ~ 2-3 mmHg","It binds 2,3-BPG mediated physiological mechanism","It has four heme groups mediated physiological mechanism","It has alpha-beta chains mediated physiological mechanism","It denatures easily mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'It does not exhibit cooperative binding; P50 ~ 2-3 mmHg', 'Myoglobin is a monomer with very high O2 affinity (P50 ~2-3 mmHg), acting as an O2 reservoir in muscle.'),
('a327d4f6-c02c-52c9-b395-c746c15f7b8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Carbon monoxide exposure shifts the O2-Hb curve to the:', '["Curve flattens","No effect","Left","Right","Linear shape"]'::jsonb, 'Left', 'CO binding increases O2 affinity of remaining heme sites (left shift), further impairing O2 unloading.'),
('fa8ea5ed-dda2-57ac-9d25-69df992d46b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The most common cause of an acquired right shift of the O2-Hb curve is:', '["Methemoglobinemia","Hypothermia","CO poisoning","Increased 2,3-BPG in hypoxia","Alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Increased 2,3-BPG in hypoxia', 'Hypoxia stimulates 2,3-BPG production, right-shifting the curve — a compensatory mechanism.'),
('9be697e5-18cc-5086-9f09-bc89157cb368'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The P50 is decreased (left shift) in all EXCEPT:', '["Hypothermia","CO poisoning","Hyperthermia","Alkalosis","HbF"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperthermia', 'Hyperthermia right-shifts the curve (increased P50). Left shift occurs in alkalosis, hypothermia, HbF, and CO poisoning.'),
('cee85d1a-356f-519c-aaa2-9302198138a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'At high altitude, the compensatory increase in 2,3-BPG helps by:', '["Increasing Hb-O2 affinity to enhance loading in lungs","Increasing Hb concentration","Left-shifting the curve","Right-shifting the curve to unload O2 more readily to tissues","Decreasing P50"]'::jsonb, 'Right-shifting the curve to unload O2 more readily to tissues', 'Right shift enhances O2 unloading despite hypobaric hypoxia.'),
('8c5b8a58-cbf9-511d-9d15-84ebf2fc065d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The O2 content in a patient with Hb = 10 g/dL, SaO2 = 95%, PaO2 = 100 mmHg is approximately:', '["20 mL O2/dL","10 mL O2/dL","15 mL O2/dL","18 mL O2/dL","13.4 mL O2/dL"]'::jsonb, '13.4 mL O2/dL', 'CaO2 = (10 x 1.34 x 0.95) + (0.003 x 100) = 12.73 + 0.3 ≈ 13.0-13.4 mL/dL.'),
('29326b50-6973-51a8-9ccb-0cda0f30034b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Oxygen extraction ratio (O2ER) is calculated as:', '["(CvO2 - CaO2)/CvO2","CaO2/CvO2","(CaO2 - CvO2)/CaO2","VO2/DO2","DO2/VO2"]'::jsonb, '(CaO2 - CvO2)/CaO2', 'Normal O2ER is ~0.25 (25% of delivered O2 is extracted by tissues).'),
('a1322161-c1f2-5885-aed8-348c1bc0e985'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2) normally is:', '["~95%","~50%","~75%","~85%","~60%"]'::jsonb, '~75%', 'SvO2 ~75% reflects the balance between O2 delivery and consumption. Values <65% suggest increased extraction (low CO or high demand).'),
('bb959056-0993-5a20-80f3-2f0e809127cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Oxygen delivery (DO2) to tissues is calculated as:', '["CO x SaO2","CaO2 x 10","CO x Hb","CO x CaO2 x 10","CaO2 x HR"]'::jsonb, 'CO x CaO2 x 10', 'DO2 = cardiac output x arterial O2 content x 10 (to convert to mL/min). Normal ~1000 mL/min.'),
('afb4cc00-72cd-5459-8265-1349cf78a474'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A right shift of the O2-Hb curve is beneficial in:', '["High altitude ascent without acclimatization","Hypoventilation","Methemoglobinemia","CO poisoning","Exercise"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise', 'Active tissues have higher temperature, lower pH, and more CO2 — all right-shift the curve to facilitate O2 unloading.'),
('07c6708e-9b21-512b-bdb5-fc7744f36349'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which of the following is NOT a cause of left-shifted O2-Hb curve?', '["Alkalosis","HbF","Hypothermia","CO poisoning","Fever"]'::jsonb, 'Fever', 'Fever increases temperature, which right-shifts the curve. All others cause left shift.'),
('c552304d-4281-535b-b81a-fbc9aa96a8ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Cyanide poisoning causes histotoxic hypoxia because:', '["It increases Hb-O2 affinity mediated physiological mechanism","It reduces cardiac output mediated physiological mechanism","It blocks cytochrome oxidase (complex IV), preventing O2 utilization","It denatures Hb mediated physiological mechanism","It causes bronchoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'It blocks cytochrome oxidase (complex IV), preventing O2 utilization', 'Cyanide inhibits mitochondrial respiration, making O2 unavailable for oxidative phosphorylation despite normal PaO2.'),
('1f4c044a-3ce1-50fc-9e61-4c85b2719471'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The majority of CO2 in blood is transported as:', '["Bicarbonate","Carbamino compounds","Carbonic acid","Dissolved CO2","Bound to hemoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Bicarbonate', '~70% of CO2 is transported as bicarbonate (HCO3-) formed in RBCs via carbonic anhydrase.'),
('53fdd24e-b38b-53aa-9ad2-e664feb3054a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Carbonic anhydrase in RBCs catalyzes:', '["HbCO2 -> CO2 + Hb","CO2 binding to Hb","HCO3- -> CO2 + OH-","CO2 + H2O -> H2CO3","CO2 + OH- -> HCO3-"]'::jsonb, 'CO2 + H2O -> H2CO3', 'Carbonic anhydrase rapidly hydrates CO2 to carbonic acid, which dissociates to H+ and HCO3-.'),
('ac8146d9-8d23-5c38-b957-5411edcdc485'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Approximately what percentage of CO2 is transported as dissolved CO2 in plasma?', '["30-40%","5-10%","1-2%","20-25%","50%"]'::jsonb, '5-10%', 'Dissolved CO2 accounts for ~5-10% of total CO2 transport (much more soluble than O2).'),
('adba0164-c2be-561e-bfa4-5db80e217ef8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Carbamino compounds account for what percentage of CO2 transport?', '["~20%","~10%","~5%","~30%","~40%"]'::jsonb, '~20%', '~20-25% of CO2 is transported as carbamino compounds (CO2 bound to amino groups of Hb and other proteins).'),
('5510dd80-62d8-5c59-ba0c-35a9daa940e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Haldane effect states that:', '["HCO3- is formed in plasma","CO2 increases Hb-O2 affinity","Deoxygenated Hb can carry more CO2","Oxygenated Hb carries more CO2","CO2 decreases RBC pH"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated Hb can carry more CO2', 'Deoxyhemoglobin (T state) has higher affinity for CO2 and H+, enhancing CO2 loading in tissues.'),
('9d70acc4-7123-5780-9c73-1415b929fecc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The chloride shift (Hamburger phenomenon) involves:', '["Cl- moving out of RBC, HCO3- moving in","Water moving into RBC","K+ moving out of RBC","Na+ moving into RBC","HCO3- moving out of RBC, Cl- moving in"]'::jsonb, 'HCO3- moving out of RBC, Cl- moving in', 'HCO3- exits RBC via band 3 anion exchanger; Cl- enters to maintain electrical neutrality.'),
('eb87c9f2-6ff4-51df-8572-e5bed3d47bc4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In the lungs, the reaction HCO3- + H+ -> H2CO3 -> CO2 + H2O occurs mainly:', '["In interstitial fluid","In alveolar cells","In RBCs","In pulmonary capillaries","In plasma"]'::jsonb, 'In RBCs', 'In pulmonary capillaries, HCO3- diffuses into RBCs where carbonic anhydrase drives CO2 formation for exhalation.'),
('164a44f6-b368-5a82-b1bb-da6732a31fe4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'CO2 is transported from tissues to lungs primarily in which form in plasma?', '["Carbonate ions","Carbamino compounds","Bicarbonate","Carbonic acid","Dissolved CO2"]'::jsonb, 'Bicarbonate', 'Most CO2 from tissues enters RBCs, is converted to HCO3-, which diffuses to plasma for transport to lungs.'),
('a8824c7a-2fe2-5ec7-a665-c9b0ac7b2545'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The PaCO2 is primarily determined by:', '["Hemoglobin level mediated physiological mechanism","Cardiac output mediated physiological mechanism","O2 consumption mediated physiological mechanism","Alveolar ventilation and CO2 production","DLCO mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar ventilation and CO2 production', 'PaCO2 ∝ VCO2 / VA. Hypoventilation increases PaCO2; hyperventilation decreases it.'),
('de006402-c3f8-526a-b264-a8effd7b6603'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'An increase in PaCO2 by 10 mmHg causes the pH to decrease by approximately:', '["0.08","0.02","0.20","0.15","0.01"]'::jsonb, '0.08', 'Acute hypercapnia: ΔpH ≈ -0.008 per 1 mmHg increase in PaCO2 (i.e., ~0.08 for 10 mmHg rise).'),
('4ad9a97d-bdc3-577e-936b-d0189ac6369a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal mixed venous PCO2 is approximately:', '["45-46 mmHg","40 mmHg","55 mmHg","35 mmHg","50 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '45-46 mmHg', 'Mixed venous PCO2 is ~45-46 mmHg, reflecting tissue CO2 production.'),
('bb0c8c7b-e4f2-59bc-a9b7-155ca6cb51c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Most CO2 in arterial blood is in the form of:', '["Carbamino compounds","None","Carbonic acid","Bicarbonate","Dissolved CO2"]'::jsonb, 'Bicarbonate', 'In arterial blood too, ~90% of CO2 is as HCO3-, ~5% dissolved, ~5% carbamino.'),
('70bdcf03-d824-5092-8cf8-d0a7115fd8e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The conversion of CO2 to carbonic acid in RBCs is catalyzed by:', '["Catalase","Superoxide dismutase","Carbonic anhydrase I","Hemoglobin","Carbonic anhydrase II"]'::jsonb, 'Carbonic anhydrase II', 'CA II in RBCs catalyzes CO2 hydration. Inhibition by acetazolamide causes bicarbonate diuresis.'),
('c793c074-4eb3-548b-acb6-3a8d9f4b68dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Compared to O2, CO2 diffuses across the alveolar-capillary membrane:', '["About 20x faster","About 10x faster","Slower","About 2x faster","About equal"]'::jsonb, 'About 20x faster', 'CO2''s high solubility in biological membranes gives it ~20x the diffusion capacity of O2.'),
('58c27d1c-3c2c-5fff-a00b-91947ec4c836'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'High altitude-induced hyperventilation causes:', '["No acid-base change mediated physiological mechanism","Metabolic alkalosis mediated physiological mechanism","Respiratory acidosis mediated physiological mechanism","Respiratory alkalosis with reduced PaCO2","Metabolic acidosis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory alkalosis with reduced PaCO2', 'Hypoxia drives hyperventilation, lowering PaCO2 and causing respiratory alkalosis (initial response to altitude).'),
('efaeeb7e-1074-5a6a-81cf-271b7f0d497f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The primary respiratory center in the brainstem is located in the:', '["Medulla oblongata","Cerebral cortex","Pons","Midbrain","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata', 'The medullary respiratory center (dorsal and ventral groups) generates the basic rhythm of breathing.'),
('87755333-759b-52bc-88f8-3d544e943a54'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The dorsal respiratory group (DRG) of the medulla primarily functions in:', '["Both inspiration and expiration","Inspiration","Expiration","Cough","Swallowing"]'::jsonb, 'Inspiration', 'DRG contains inspiratory neurons that send rhythmic signals to inspiratory muscles via phrenic and intercostal nerves.'),
('4c0f416b-bf17-5ae5-baec-119faf316f37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The ventral respiratory group (VRG) is involved in:', '["Only quiet inspiration","Only forced expiration","Apreustic breathing","None","Forced expiration and inspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Forced expiration and inspiration', 'VRG contains both inspiratory and expiratory neurons, active during forced breathing.'),
('a80a520a-de99-536e-b6fa-5bb3d9783a1c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The pneumotaxic center is located in the:', '["Lower pons","Cerebellum","Midbrain","Upper pons","Medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Upper pons', 'The pneumotaxic center (nucleus parabrachialis) in the upper pons inhibits inspiration, regulating respiratory rate and tidal volume.'),
('969d1695-21ea-5d0b-b2e5-4601d2b601d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The apneustic center is located in the:', '["Midbrain","Medulla","Lower pons","Upper pons","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Lower pons', 'The apneustic center in the lower pons promotes inspiration; its activity is modulated by vagal input and the pneumotaxic center.'),
('fd663778-4803-5ef9-8369-10d400f85704'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Central chemoreceptors are located:', '["On the ventrolateral surface of the medulla","In the carotid bodies","In the aortic bodies","In the hypothalamus","In the cerebellar vermis"]'::jsonb, 'On the ventrolateral surface of the medulla', 'Central chemoreceptors are close to the ventral medullary surface, sensitive to CSF H+ concentration (CO2).'),
('579c3bb6-af4d-58ab-b3f7-4a72247d3aa7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Central chemoreceptors respond primarily to changes in:', '["Arterial H+","CSF PO2","Arterial PCO2 only directly","Arterial PO2","CSF H+ concentration"]'::jsonb, 'CSF H+ concentration', 'CO2 diffuses across blood-brain barrier, hydrates to form H+ which stimulates central chemoreceptors.'),
('7ec21ac6-4dd4-5895-ad45-59a57cb03fd8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The ventilatory response to CO2 is mediated primarily by:', '["Peripheral chemoreceptors","J-receptors","Irritant receptors","Stretch receptors","Central chemoreceptors"]'::jsonb, 'Central chemoreceptors', 'Central chemoreceptors account for ~80% of the ventilatory response to CO2; peripheral account for ~20%.'),
('002b66e0-c7cf-5a6c-b706-22a8ff858106'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies) are most sensitive to:', '["Arterial PO2","Arterial PCO2","Venous PO2","Arterial pH","CSF pH"]'::jsonb, 'Arterial PO2', 'Carotid bodies respond primarily to hypoxemia, especially when PaO2 falls below 60 mmHg, with a steep response curve.'),
('678b221d-20d0-5a36-9fc6-46e475a84351'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The carotid body receives its blood supply from:', '["Internal jugular vein","Coronary artery","Carotid artery branch","Pulmonary artery","Vertebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid artery branch', 'Carotid bodies have extremely high blood flow (~2000 mL/min/100g), allowing them to sense arterial rather than venous chemistry.'),
('1a672154-7121-55e4-b77b-0299d28da057'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Glomus type I cells in carotid bodies release which neurotransmitter in response to hypoxia?', '["Norepinephrine","Dopamine","Substance P","Serotonin","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine', 'Glomus cells (chief cells) release dopamine in response to hypoxia, which excites afferent nerve endings.'),
('f553abd3-2572-51aa-9a9c-722f4599b7f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The afferent pathway from peripheral chemoreceptors travels via:', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve', 'Carotid body afferents course through the carotid sinus nerve (of Hering) to CN IX. Aortic body afferents travel via CN X.'),
('0ec26566-f536-5495-9089-de5da1f9ef56'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The peripheral chemoreceptor response to hypoxia is minimal above PaO2 of:', '["80 mmHg","50 mmHg","100 mmHg","40 mmHg","60 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '60 mmHg', 'The hypoxic ventilatory response is weak until PaO2 drops below ~60 mmHg, below which ventilation increases exponentially.'),
('b416103b-e7a0-5c46-a26b-48d7f8083cdd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Acute exposure to high altitude increases ventilation mainly due to:', '["Central chemoreceptor stimulation","Decreased pH","Increased CO2 production","Peripheral chemoreceptor stimulation","Cold air"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral chemoreceptor stimulation', 'Hypobaric hypoxia at altitude stimulates carotid bodies, driving hyperventilation.'),
('3c4d47ee-2ea1-58b7-ae4c-c41b28a5fde4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex is activated by:', '["Hypoxia","Atelectasis","Overinflation of lungs","Hypercapnia","Pulmonary edema"]'::jsonb, 'Overinflation of lungs', 'Slowly adapting stretch receptors in airways transmit via vagus to inhibit inspiration when lungs are overdistended.'),
('a5fa8cb7-073b-5333-b1f9-fc4840678ceb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Hering-Breuer reflex is absent in:', '["Infants mediated physiological mechanism","Neonates mediated physiological mechanism","Animals mediated physiological mechanism","Adult humans at normal tidal volumes","Preterm infants mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Adult humans at normal tidal volumes', 'The reflex is weak in adult humans during quiet breathing (TV ~500 mL) but may be activated at high tidal volumes (>1 L).'),
('1398cc31-dbd9-5fad-9323-144cc421bdd1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Irritant receptors in airways respond to:', '["Stretch mediated physiological mechanism","Noxious gases, dust, and cold air","Hypercapnia mediated physiological mechanism","Edema mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoxia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Noxious gases, dust, and cold air', 'Irritant receptors (rapidly adapting) cause bronchoconstriction, cough, and hyperventilation in response to irritants.'),
('ecb4e0cb-7d8b-590d-9795-b06fb14ee75f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'J-receptors (juxtacapillary receptors) are stimulated by:', '["Airway stretch mediated physiological mechanism","Cold air mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary congestion and interstitial edema","Irritant gases mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoxia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary congestion and interstitial edema', 'J-receptors in alveolar walls near capillaries are activated by congestion, edema, and inflammation, causing dyspnea.'),
('927430e8-ef6c-5804-9aa2-39812ed0cd18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The immediate response to a sudden decrease in PaO2 to 40 mmHg is:', '["Decreased ventilation","Apnea","No change","Central chemoreceptor activation","Increased ventilation via peripheral chemoreceptors"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ventilation via peripheral chemoreceptors', 'Hypoxia stimulates carotid bodies, increasing ventilation within seconds.'),
('2768eb32-7796-504c-af0e-14bef7c2fda2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The ventilatory response to hypercapnia is blunted in:', '["Infants","Athletes","Women","Chronic CO2 retainers","Normal individuals"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic CO2 retainers', 'Chronic hypercapnia reduces central chemoreceptor sensitivity to CO2, making these patients rely on hypoxic drive.'),
('16b718f4-b3ee-5e08-ae2d-355c13081975'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In a patient with COPD and chronic CO2 retention, administration of high-flow O2 can cause:', '["Increased ventilation","Improved pH","No effect","Decreased ventilation","Decreased PaCO2"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased ventilation', 'Correcting hypoxia removes hypoxic drive; with blunted CO2 sensitivity, ventilation falls, worsening hypercapnia.'),
('4151cfdd-0ff2-5459-b26c-c2e4c5584386'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal ventilatory response to exercise is driven primarily by:', '["Acidosis mediated physiological mechanism","Neurogenic signals from motor cortex and limb movement","Hypercapnia mediated physiological mechanism","Carotid body stimulation mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoxia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Neurogenic signals from motor cortex and limb movement', 'Exercise hyperpnea is largely due to parallel activation of respiratory centers from motor cortex and feedback from muscle/joint receptors.'),
('c1880995-25ba-58b4-95ec-64ebcb15b543'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The cerebrospinal fluid pH is more dependent on PaCO2 than plasma pH because:', '["CSF has high HCO3-","CSF lacks carbonic anhydrase","CSF is less permeable to CO2","CSF has low buffering capacity","CSF has high protein content"]'::jsonb, 'CSF has low buffering capacity', 'CSF has little protein buffer, so PaCO2 changes cause larger pH swings than in plasma.'),
('bc212d60-873d-5bab-92c4-90d07f2e4467'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The isocapnic hypoxia test assesses:', '["Lung mechanics","Diffusion capacity","Peripheral chemoreceptor function","Airway reactivity","Central chemoreceptor function"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral chemoreceptor function', 'By maintaining isocapnia (constant CO2), the hypoxic response isolates peripheral chemoreceptor function.'),
('6f7f9a8a-1db9-5b16-8851-06002317ea29'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Hyperoxia (breathing 100% O2) in a normal person causes:', '["Marked hyperventilation","Increased PaCO2","No change","Apnea","Minimal decrease in ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Minimal decrease in ventilation', 'Normal individuals have minimal hypoxic drive at rest, so hyperoxia only slightly reduces ventilation (Haldane effect).'),
('d4d7563a-4310-54dd-b749-d7b44f916b34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'A patient with bilateral carotid body resection shows:', '["Apnea","Loss of hypercapnic response","Increased hypercapnic response","No change in breathing","Loss of hypoxic ventilatory response"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of hypoxic ventilatory response', 'Carotid bodies are the primary peripheral O2 sensors. Bilateral resection abolishes hypoxic ventilatory drive.'),
('96015fd7-d27e-5560-9ab5-3beb6fdc7dba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The medullary respiratory center receives input from the vagus nerve regarding:', '["Pain mediated physiological mechanism","Blood pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Body temperature mediated physiological mechanism","Lung stretch and airway irritation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung stretch and airway irritation', 'Vagus carries afferent signals from stretch receptors and irritant receptors in the airways and lungs.'),
('40587bb3-d2ee-5ca7-aa8e-ab0e578902cd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'During sleep, the ventilatory response to CO2 and hypoxia is:', '["Variable mediated physiological mechanism","Increased mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased compared to wakefulness","Unchanged mediated physiological mechanism","Absent mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased compared to wakefulness', 'During NREM sleep, both central and peripheral chemosensitivity decrease, with further blunting in REM sleep.'),
('33a41d65-5e0d-5dba-8ed6-ededf840e389'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The hypercapnic ventilatory response is mediated by detecting changes in:', '["Arterial H+ concentration","CSF H+ concentration","Venous PCO2","CSF PCO2 directly","Arterial PCO2 directly"]'::jsonb, 'CSF H+ concentration', 'CO2 crosses BBB, forms H2CO3 via CA (in choroid plexus), dissociates to H+ which stimulates medullary chemoreceptors.'),
('72dc5c09-41e8-5a77-b16a-302812545a21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Chronic mountain sickness (CMS) is associated with:', '["Metabolic alkalosis","Low hemoglobin","Blunted hypoxic ventilatory response","Excessive hyperventilation","Increased hypoxic ventilatory response"]'::jsonb, 'Blunted hypoxic ventilatory response', 'CMS is characterized by loss of hypoxic ventilatory drive, leading to severe hypoxemia and polycythemia.'),
('cc84d047-d76d-594f-91d6-4c006b1216ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Apreustic breathing is characterized by:', '["Prolonged inspiratory cramps with shortened expiration","Deep sighing breaths","Cheyne-Stokes respiration","Rapid shallow breathing","Apnea alternating with hyperpnea"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged inspiratory cramps with shortened expiration', 'Apneustic breathing results from damage to the pneumotaxic center or vagal afferents, causing prolonged inspiratory holds.'),
('ba721b79-40df-5128-a275-45efb05dbc3e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Cheyne-Stokes respiration is characterized by:', '["Constant hyperventilation mediated physiological mechanism","Irregular breathing mediated physiological mechanism","Apnea without hyperpnea mediated physiological mechanism","Gradual increase then decrease in ventilation alternating with apnea","Prolonged expiration mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual increase then decrease in ventilation alternating with apnea', 'Cheyne-Stokes is seen in heart failure and CNS disease; related to prolonged circulation time and altered chemosensitivity.'),
('b925df93-d6a4-56f6-96c0-626555c78864'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Biot''s respiration (ataxic breathing) suggests:', '["Anxiety mediated physiological mechanism","Sleep apnea mediated physiological mechanism","Damage to the medullary respiratory center","Damage to the pons mediated physiological mechanism","Normal variant mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Damage to the medullary respiratory center', 'Biot''s (ataxic) breathing is irregular with unpredictable apnea, indicating medullary damage (e.g., compression, stroke).'),
('c47c9a2e-6b18-5a8c-9e5c-80cea06956de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Kussmaul breathing is associated with:', '["Metabolic acidosis","Brainstem stroke","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Anxiety"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic acidosis', 'Kussmaul respirations are deep, rapid breaths compensating for metabolic acidosis by lowering PaCO2.'),
('328781b8-4012-5fb7-a41f-84b310fbee47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Central sleep apnea is characterized by:', '["Normal breathing","Lack of respiratory effort due to CNS dysfunction","Cough","Airway obstruction despite effort","Increased hypoxic response"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of respiratory effort due to CNS dysfunction', 'Central apnea involves loss of ventilatory drive; no chest wall movement during apnea (unlike obstructive apnea).'),
('9d323c77-8c09-5429-a67b-c29e373d7781'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Hypoxic hypoxia is characterized by:', '["Normal O2 content","Normal arterial PO2 with reduced O2 content","Low arterial PO2","Cellular poisoning of oxidative enzymes","Decreased cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'Low arterial PO2', 'Hypoxic hypoxia (e.g., high altitude, hypoventilation) features decreased PaO2 leading to reduced O2 saturation.'),
('9d7c567f-7cd0-5801-a17c-229ed3e04d1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Anemic hypoxia is characterized by:', '["Decreased blood flow","Low SaO2","Normal PaO2 with reduced O2-carrying capacity","Low PaO2","Impaired cellular utilization"]'::jsonb, 'Normal PaO2 with reduced O2-carrying capacity', 'In anemia, PaO2 is normal but Hb level is low, reducing O2 content.'),
('7e86bbd9-fe6d-5308-a4ac-f527715c7059'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Stagnant (circulatory) hypoxia is caused by:', '["Low PaO2","Low SaO2","Cytotoxic poisoning","Low Hb concentration","Reduced blood flow to tissues"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced blood flow to tissues', 'Stagnant hypoxia (e.g., heart failure, shock) occurs when adequate O2 content but insufficient delivery due to low flow.'),
('008880bf-2c49-563f-9993-a5946ed2befc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Histotoxic hypoxia is characterized by:', '["Low O2 content mediated physiological mechanism","Low arterial PO2 mediated physiological mechanism","Impaired cellular O2 utilization despite normal O2 delivery","Normal mitochondrial function mediated physiological mechanism","Reduced perfusion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired cellular O2 utilization despite normal O2 delivery', 'Histotoxic hypoxia (e.g., cyanide poisoning) involves failure of cells to use O2 due to mitochondrial toxin.'),
('8de0f47b-293a-5683-9070-6696f7eec613'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Central cyanosis is best observed in:', '["Tongue and oral mucosa","Nail beds only","Fingertips only","Palms","Earlobes"]'::jsonb, 'Tongue and oral mucosa', 'Central cyanosis (from arterial desaturation) is best seen in warm, well-perfused areas like tongue and oral mucosa.'),
('024705e6-ddca-5146-b07c-cffd2b396953'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Peripheral cyanosis is due to:', '["Anemia mediated physiological mechanism","Reduced blood flow with normal arterial O2 saturation","CO poisoning mediated physiological mechanism","Methemoglobinemia mediated physiological mechanism","Low arterial O2 saturation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced blood flow with normal arterial O2 saturation', 'Peripheral cyanosis results from vasoconstriction and O2 extraction in tissues (e.g., cold exposure), not arterial desaturation.'),
('a4f631ef-a3af-5f40-a1a4-b5a7f5e5adc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Cyanosis becomes clinically detectable when reduced Hb exceeds:', '["5 g/dL","3 g/dL","2 g/dL","10 g/dL","1 g/dL"]'::jsonb, '5 g/dL', 'Cyanosis appears when deoxygenated Hb > 5 g/dL in capillary blood. Polycythemia may show cyanosis at higher PaO2.'),
('30c1109a-07c8-5cf1-baa4-afe88d928812'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In severe anemia, cyanosis may not be apparent even with low SaO2 because:', '["Total Hb is too low to reach 5 g/dL reduced Hb","O2 content is too low","Peripheral perfusion is increased","PaO2 is normal","Hb is fully oxygenated"]'::jsonb, 'Total Hb is too low to reach 5 g/dL reduced Hb', 'Even if 50% of Hb is deoxygenated, 5 g/dL threshold may not be reached if total Hb is very low.'),
('416f3396-d22b-5600-a0ae-ded6cecbc8bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Chronic hypoxemia leads to compensatory:', '["Anemia","Leukocytosis","Polycythemia","Pancytopenia","Thrombocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Polycythemia', 'Chronic hypoxia increases erythropoietin production, stimulating RBC production to increase O2-carrying capacity.'),
('15c10579-ba43-5a17-bba8-026d050188e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Hypercapnia is defined as PaCO2 above:', '["35 mmHg","40 mmHg","50 mmHg","45 mmHg","55 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '45 mmHg', 'Normal PaCO2 is 35-45 mmHg. Hypercapnia: PaCO2 > 45 mmHg.'),
('b0135d8f-80d9-5df0-92af-b45cb8084477'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The most sensitive indicator of hypoventilation is:', '["Low SaO2","Low PaO2","High HCO3-","Elevated PaCO2","Low pH"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated PaCO2', 'PaCO2 is the best indicator of alveolar ventilation adequacy. Elevated PaCO2 = alveolar hypoventilation.'),
('8acbca43-913f-5405-ae7c-2fbfde16eb76'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Symptoms of hypercapnia (CO2 narcosis) include all EXCEPT:', '["Asterixis","Headache","Drowsiness","Confusion","Hypothermia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothermia', 'CO2 narcosis causes headache, confusion, drowsiness, asterixis, and eventually coma. Hypothermia is not a direct effect.'),
('7c15cf97-0bf6-5270-a9a5-c396e13fafd4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Hypocapnia (PaCO2 < 35 mmHg) is most commonly caused by:', '["Metabolic acidosis","Exercise","Hyperventilation","Hypoventilation","Increased CO2 production"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperventilation', 'Hypocapnia results from alveolar hyperventilation, which may be due to anxiety, hypoxia, pain, or central stimulation.'),
('793c0cf9-ee2a-5872-af46-8ccb53c3c418'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Acute hypocapnia can cause:', '["Seizures","Cerebral vasodilation","Cerebral vasoconstriction and lightheadedness","Coma","Increased intracranial pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral vasoconstriction and lightheadedness', 'Low CO2 causes cerebral vasoconstriction, reducing cerebral blood flow and causing lightheadedness, tingling, and tetany.'),
('6a20cc14-815c-5e7c-8144-fae8256f0a4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which type of hypoxia is present in carbon monoxide poisoning?', '["Mixed","Hypoxic hypoxia","Histotoxic hypoxia","Stagnant hypoxia","Anemic hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Anemic hypoxia', 'CO reduces O2-carrying capacity (functional anemia) and impairs O2 unloading, classified as anemic hypoxia.'),
('ac63e531-c10c-57c1-b123-b8283f1b2956'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which type of hypoxia is present in cyanide poisoning?', '["Mixed","Anemic hypoxia","Histotoxic hypoxia","Hypoxic hypoxia","Stagnant hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Histotoxic hypoxia', 'Cyanide inhibits cytochrome c oxidase, preventing cells from using O2 despite normal O2 delivery.'),
('6479233e-9440-56c1-a3ba-032444d60871'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Which type of hypoxia is present in heart failure with pulmonary edema?', '["None","Anemic hypoxia only","Pure hypoxic","Histotoxic only","Mixed hypoxic and stagnant hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed hypoxic and stagnant hypoxia', 'Pulmonary edema impairs gas exchange (hypoxic) and low cardiac output impairs delivery (stagnant).'),
('ad2ffac9-b342-53de-8084-4a1d8e3bce65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Arterial blood gas analysis showing PaO2 55 mmHg, PaCO2 50 mmHg, pH 7.30 indicates:', '["Normal","Metabolic acidosis","Hypoxemia with hypercapnic respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Compensated respiratory alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxemia with hypercapnic respiratory acidosis', 'Low PaO2, high PaCO2, low pH = hypoxemia + hypercapnic respiratory acidosis (e.g., COPD exacerbation).'),
('1d3d7769-3a58-5710-93d6-fc5c30c820e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In a patient with PaO2 of 70 mmHg on room air, oxygen saturation is expected to be:', '["~88-90%","~70-75%","~93-95%","~98-99%","~80-85%"]'::jsonb, '~93-95%', 'At PaO2 70 mmHg, SaO2 is ~93-95% (on the flatter portion just above the steep part of the O2-Hb curve).'),
('37b1aa6d-a0a1-5f39-bd63-59eba5849920'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The PaO2/FiO2 ratio (P/F ratio) is used to assess:', '["Ventilatory capacity","Severity of hypoxemia","Airway obstruction","Diffusion capacity","CO2 retention"]'::jsonb, 'Severity of hypoxemia', 'P/F ratio < 300 indicates mild, < 200 moderate, < 100 severe hypoxemia. ARDS: P/F < 200 (moderate) or < 100 (severe).'),
('cbad2ca3-ce7f-5c76-af99-cc60ed80f3bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Normal arterial blood pH range is:', '["7.00-7.35","7.25-7.35","7.45-7.55","7.35-7.50","7.35-7.45"]'::jsonb, '7.35-7.45', 'Normal pH is 7.35-7.45. Acidemia: pH < 7.35; Alkalemia: pH > 7.45.'),
('73b9b398-87b0-546b-af7c-50a4ba340295'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Normal PaCO2 range is:', '["30-40 mmHg","45-55 mmHg","25-35 mmHg","35-45 mmHg","40-50 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '35-45 mmHg', 'Normal PaCO2 is 35-45 mmHg. Values > 45 indicate hypercapnia/hypoventilation.'),
('721e47b3-ed5f-576c-b0b4-5e05f176b3a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Normal HCO3- range is:', '["15-20 mEq/L","10-15 mEq/L","22-26 mEq/L","30-35 mEq/L","26-30 mEq/L"]'::jsonb, '22-26 mEq/L', 'Normal HCO3- is 22-26 mEq/L. Decreased in metabolic acidosis; increased in metabolic alkalosis.'),
('a28d957d-0c8e-5b50-8070-2b9e2ae08912'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Normal PaO2 in a young adult on room air is:', '["50-60 mmHg","120-140 mmHg","80-100 mmHg","100-120 mmHg","60-80 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '80-100 mmHg', 'Normal PaO2 decreases with age. A young adult should have PaO2 80-100 mmHg on room air at sea level.'),
('f65acea6-2cde-5f19-a3c3-ffa17f200518'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Respiratory acidosis is characterized by:', '["pH > 7.45, PaCO2 < 35 mmHg","pH < 7.35, PaCO2 > 45 mmHg","pH > 7.45, HCO3- > 26","pH < 7.35, HCO3- < 22","pH normal, PaCO2 high"]'::jsonb, 'pH < 7.35, PaCO2 > 45 mmHg', 'Respiratory acidosis results from CO2 retention (hypoventilation). If compensated, HCO3- rises.'),
('2b36364d-5fb5-5545-8691-aef5a6a02b6f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Respiratory alkalosis is characterized by:', '["pH normal, HCO3- high","pH < 7.35, PaCO2 > 45 mmHg","pH < 7.35, HCO3- < 22","pH > 7.45, HCO3- > 26","pH > 7.45, PaCO2 < 35 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 'pH > 7.45, PaCO2 < 35 mmHg', 'Respiratory alkalosis results from hyperventilation lowering PaCO2.'),
('a1dd232c-2b63-551f-a073-8870afa3cf1f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Metabolic acidosis is characterized by:', '["pH > 7.45, PaCO2 < 35","pH < 7.35, PaCO2 > 45","pH > 7.45, HCO3- > 26","pH < 7.35, HCO3- < 22 mEq/L","pH normal, PaCO2 low"]'::jsonb, 'pH < 7.35, HCO3- < 22 mEq/L', 'Metabolic acidosis: primary decrease in HCO3- (e.g., DKA, renal failure, diarrhea).'),
('79caa536-464e-57be-b9c3-6d66b7fca7b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Metabolic alkalosis is characterized by:', '["pH normal, HCO3- low","pH > 7.45, PaCO2 < 35","pH < 7.35, HCO3- < 22","pH > 7.45, HCO3- > 26 mEq/L","pH < 7.35, PaCO2 > 45"]'::jsonb, 'pH > 7.45, HCO3- > 26 mEq/L', 'Metabolic alkalosis: primary increase in HCO3- (e.g., vomiting, diuretics, excess alkali).'),
('e22c7222-7704-55dd-bb8a-d9d866705def'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The expected compensatory response to metabolic acidosis is:', '["Hypoventilation","Renal HCO3- retention","No compensation","Increased HCO3-","Hyperventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperventilation', 'Kussmaul breathing (hyperventilation) lowers PaCO2 to offset the metabolic acidosis: PaCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3- + 8 +/- 2.'),
('63c9b185-2347-53de-a35d-752f045393f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The expected compensatory response to metabolic alkalosis is:', '["No compensation","Hypoventilation","Increased PaO2","Renal HCO3- excretion","Hyperventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoventilation', 'Lungs compensate by hypoventilating to retain CO2: PaCO2 = 0.7 x HCO3- + 21 +/- 1.5.'),
('15a1640d-dc6d-5dfd-a5fd-7e61f41dcd3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The expected compensatory response to respiratory acidosis is:', '["Renal excretion of HCO3-","Hypoventilation","No renal compensation","Renal retention of HCO3-","Hyperventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal retention of HCO3-', 'Kidneys compensate by excreting H+ and retaining HCO3- over 24-48 hours.'),
('631d9163-c5d6-5b64-900e-b2e0cef09445'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In acute respiratory acidosis, the expected HCO3- increase is:', '["No change","2-3 mEq/L per 10 mmHg","10 mEq/L per 10 mmHg","Minimal","4-5 mEq/L per 10 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 'Minimal', 'Acute: ΔHCO3- = ΔPaCO2 x 0.1 (very small). Chronic: ΔHCO3- = ΔPaCO2 x 0.4 (renal compensation).'),
('12ff1374-fd1f-5c8d-b1be-8619ec154008'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Winter''s formula for appropriate compensation in metabolic acidosis is:', '["PaCO2 = 1.1 x HCO3- + 5","PaCO2 = HCO3- + 15","PaCO2 = HCO3- / 2 + 20","PaCO2 = 2 x HCO3- + 10","Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 x HCO3-) + 8 +/- 2"]'::jsonb, 'Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 x HCO3-) + 8 +/- 2', 'Winter''s formula predicts respiratory compensation. If measured PaCO2 matches, compensation is appropriate.'),
('6c76a1cb-d0e8-57bf-8cb2-8854e31dea53'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Anion gap is calculated as:', '["Na+ - Cl-","(Na+ + K+) - Cl-","Cl- + HCO3- - Na+","Na+ + K+ -","Na+ -"]'::jsonb, 'Na+ -', 'Normal anion gap is 8-12 mEq/L. Increased in metabolic acidosis from endogenous acid accumulation.'),
('53f64a6d-8331-5c3b-a742-136baed353a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Causes of high anion gap metabolic acidosis include all EXCEPT:', '["Diabetic ketoacidosis","Lactic acidosis","Salicylate poisoning","Renal failure","Hyperchloremic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperchloremic acidosis', 'Hyperchloremic (normal anion gap) acidosis occurs in diarrhea, RTA, and some renal conditions.'),
('25defbda-6f98-552b-93fe-85e61eebe71e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The delta-delta (delta AG/delta HCO3-) ratio helps assess:', '["Simple acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Mixed acid-base disorders","Anion gap reliability","Respiratory alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed acid-base disorders', 'delta AG/delta HCO3- ~1 indicates pure AGMA; >1 suggests concurrent metabolic alkalosis; <1 suggests concurrent NAGMA.'),
('7e2507c0-6808-5785-b468-ca218064ea41'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In a patient with pH 7.25, PaCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3- 26 mEq/L, the diagnosis is:', '["Chronic respiratory acidosis","Normal","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Acute respiratory acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Acute respiratory acidosis', 'pH 7.25 (acidemia), PaCO2 60 (↑), HCO3- 26 (slightly ↑). Acute = minimal HCO3- rise. Needs assessment of chronicity.'),
('b81fdadc-d986-520f-ad24-ef2e8a09ee5c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'ABG: pH 7.50, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 24 mEq/L. This indicates:', '["Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Chronic respiratory alkalosis","Normal","Acute respiratory alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Acute respiratory alkalosis', 'pH > 7.45, PaCO2 < 35, HCO3- normal = acute respiratory alkalosis (e.g., hyperventilation).'),
('eb9532a1-a08b-5d3e-9bb7-5ce4d76a0034'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'This indicates:', '["Mixed disorder mediated physiological mechanism","Metabolic acidosis mediated physiological mechanism","Respiratory alkalosis mediated physiological mechanism","Respiratory acidosis mediated physiological mechanism","Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation', 'High HCO3- (metabolic alkalosis) with compensatory hypoventilation (high PaCO2) and near-normal pH.'),
('77ad61ca-486e-5c91-bd61-5dc8fde9ddda'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'ABG: pH 7.40, PaCO2 65 mmHg, HCO3- 40 mEq/L. This indicates:', '["Metabolic alkalosis","Normal","Mixed acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis","Compensated respiratory acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Compensated respiratory acidosis', 'pH normalized with high PaCO2 and high HCO3- = renal compensation for chronic hypercapnia.'),
('7e8735f4-2e19-5863-83e4-611c5effb05e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The base excess (BE) in metabolic acidosis is:', '["+3 to -3","Positive","Normal","Variable","Negative"]'::jsonb, 'Negative', 'Base excess is negative in metabolic acidosis (base deficit) indicating excess acid or HCO3- loss.'),
('86f5b790-9c7b-5737-ad29-c90d584019cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Pulmonary artery pressure is normally:', '["~15/5 mmHg","~120/80 mmHg","~40/20 mmHg","~50/10 mmHg","~25/8 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '~25/8 mmHg', 'Pulmonary circulation is a low-pressure, low-resistance system. Mean PAP ~15 mmHg.'),
('8457a830-170f-50d8-a6d7-1023297add9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is mediated by:', '["Nitric oxide release mediated physiological mechanism","Direct effect of low alveolar PO2 on pulmonary artery smooth muscle","Endothelin release mediated physiological mechanism","Parasympathetic nervous system mediated physiological mechanism","Sympathetic nervous system mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Direct effect of low alveolar PO2 on pulmonary artery smooth muscle', 'HPV is an intrinsic response of pulmonary vascular smooth muscle to alveolar hypoxia, independent of innervation.'),
('a8316786-ecd8-515e-934d-f8668ce39083'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Pulmonary vascular resistance is normally what fraction of systemic vascular resistance?', '["~Equal","~1/5","~1/10","~1/2","~2x"]'::jsonb, '~1/10', 'PVR is ~1-2 mmHg/L/min vs SVR ~10-15 mmHg/L/min. Pulmonary circulation is ~1/10th resistance of systemic.'),
('3ba52ff3-b473-558b-a266-4af37c59b803'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The major factor maintaining low pulmonary artery pressure is:', '["Bronchial circulation mediated physiological mechanism","Recruitment and distension of pulmonary capillaries","Low blood volume mediated physiological mechanism","High alveolar pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Thick-walled arteries mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Recruitment and distension of pulmonary capillaries', 'Pulmonary capillaries can recruit and distend, accommodating increased flow without large pressure rise.'),
('eb77f9c9-9e2f-5d5c-92da-a76aa439320d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In West zone 2 of the lung, blood flow is:', '["Absent","Continuous","Reverse direction","Equal to zone 3","Intermittent"]'::jsonb, 'Intermittent', 'In zone 2, flow occurs only when pulmonary arterial pressure exceeds alveolar pressure (systolic flow predominates).'),
('408e8108-5dc9-56bf-98b1-f553257b8f27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In West zone 1, alveolar pressure exceeds pulmonary arterial pressure. This results in:', '["Edema","No blood flow","Venous congestion","Continuous flow","Increased flow"]'::jsonb, 'No blood flow', 'Zone 1 has no perfusion — unventilated alveoli are ventilated but not perfused (alveolar dead space).'),
('71545924-188d-54c2-a0e9-ffa0680b03cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The bronchial circulation supplies oxygenated blood to:', '["Pulmonary capillaries mediated physiological mechanism","Pleura only mediated physiological mechanism","Alveoli mediated physiological mechanism","Carina only mediated physiological mechanism","Airway walls down to terminal bronchioles"]'::jsonb, 'Airway walls down to terminal bronchioles', 'Bronchial arteries (systemic) supply airways, pulmonary vessels, nerves, and pleura. Alveoli are supplied by pulmonary circulation.'),
('805d1743-126e-51fd-8f6a-ca03fc4353af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In a patient with atrial septal defect (left-to-right shunt), pulmonary blood flow is:', '["Decreased","Increased","Normal","Absent","Variable"]'::jsonb, 'Increased', 'Left-to-right shunt increases pulmonary circulation volume; if severe, can cause pulmonary hypertension.'),
('3314bfe4-d2e3-5dd8-9011-987af8c2fca0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Pulmonary edema can result from:', '["High plasma oncotic pressure","Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure","Decreased capillary pressure","Low capillary permeability","Increased lymphatic drainage"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure', 'Left heart failure increases pulmonary venous pressure, causing hydrostatic pulmonary edema.'),
('2447d992-c8d5-5518-b8ca-38f5b576b418'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) is:', '["20-25 mmHg","25-30 mmHg","6-12 mmHg","1-5 mmHg","15-20 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '6-12 mmHg', 'PCWP reflects left atrial pressure. Normal 6-12 mmHg; >18-20 mmHg indicates left heart failure/cardiogenic edema.'),
('fa95dbac-9a6a-5abb-9ae1-ecfadb2d9c44'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'High-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE) is primarily caused by:', '["Left heart failure mediated physiological mechanism","Fluid overload mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary embolism mediated physiological mechanism","Pneumonia mediated physiological mechanism","Exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction', 'HAPE involves uneven HPV causing overperfusion of some capillaries, leading to stress failure and high-permeability edema.'),
('2ea58393-a810-574a-b992-0a0227dba6d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Pulmonary vasodilation is mediated by all except:', '["Nitric oxide","Prostacyclin","Hypoxia","Atrial natriuretic peptide","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxia', 'Hypoxia causes vasoconstriction (HPV). NO, PGI2, ANP, and ACh (via NO release) cause vasodilation.'),
('7d0964b9-e62c-510b-9e88-20249a2114c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The normal distribution of pulmonary blood flow in an upright lung is:', '["Greatest at the base, least at apex","Greatest at apex, least at base","Least at hilum","Greatest at hilum","Equal throughout"]'::jsonb, 'Greatest at the base, least at apex', 'Gravity increases perfusion at bases. Apical blood flow is least (zone 1/2 conditions).'),
('74a57c9e-b426-5f82-bfd8-e04650828425'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In acute pulmonary embolism, the ABG most commonly shows:', '["Hypercapnia mediated physiological mechanism","Metabolic alkalosis mediated physiological mechanism","Metabolic acidosis mediated physiological mechanism","Hypocapnia with respiratory alkalosis","Normal ABG mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypocapnia with respiratory alkalosis', 'Acute PE causes tachypnea and hyperventilation (hypocapnia, alkalosis) with hypoxemia and increased A-a gradient.'),
('299ee478-f499-5d38-a306-dd5f5296c90c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Euler-Liljestrand mechanism refers to:', '["Hyperoxic pulmonary vasodilation","Bohr effect","Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Ventilatory response to CO2","Distribution of ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction', 'Euler-Liljestrand: local alveolar hypoxia constricts pulmonary arterioles, diverting blood to better-oxygenated regions.'),
('5faa3558-78e4-564e-b166-40069a5204d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'VO2 max is defined as:', '["O2 consumed at rest","Maximum rate of O2 consumption during exhaustive exercise","Maximum minute ventilation","O2 consumption at anaerobic threshold","Maximum heart rate x stroke volume"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of O2 consumption during exhaustive exercise', 'VO2 max is the gold standard for aerobic fitness, indicating the maximum ability to deliver and utilize O2.'),
('a4d6ab27-afea-56fb-b467-e9cfb02d85ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'During incremental exercise, the anaerobic threshold (lactate threshold) occurs at approximately what percentage of VO2 max in untrained individuals?', '["~70-80% of VO2 max","~95% of VO2 max","~85-90% of VO2 max","~20-30% of VO2 max","~50-60% of VO2 max"]'::jsonb, '~50-60% of VO2 max', 'In untrained individuals, lactate threshold occurs at ~50-60% VO2 max; in elite athletes, it can be 75-85%.'),
('20d91433-b8b4-5e19-a966-0e6168939db5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'During exercise, the increase in O2 delivery to tissues is achieved primarily by:', '["Increase in cardiac output and O2 extraction","Shifting O2-Hb curve left","Increasing PaO2","Increasing Hb concentration acutely","Decreasing P50"]'::jsonb, 'Increase in cardiac output and O2 extraction', 'During exercise, cardiac output increases 4-5x and O2 extraction increases (widening C(a-v)O2 difference).'),
('40e473d4-ed2c-58fb-8bdb-63d8f2938b8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The main determinant of VO2 max in normal individuals is:', '["Pulmonary diffusion capacity","Hemoglobin concentration","Muscle mitochondrial density","Cardiac output","Ventilatory capacity"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output', 'In healthy individuals, VO2 max is limited by O2 delivery (cardiac output) rather than pulmonary or muscle factors.'),
('9316d6b4-384b-523c-87bc-913bf31f248f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'During exercise, ventilation increases primarily due to:', '["Increase in tidal volume only","Decrease in dead space","Increase in tidal volume and respiratory rate","Improved compliance","Increase in respiratory rate only"]'::jsonb, 'Increase in tidal volume and respiratory rate', 'At lower exercise intensity, tidal volume increases first; at higher intensity, respiratory rate also increases.'),
('bba38fd8-115a-5ab5-ae6a-65e7d981668b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The ventilatory threshold during exercise correlates with:', '["Dead space","Heart rate max","Lactate threshold","Respiratory rate","VO2 max"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate threshold', 'The ventilatory threshold (VT1) occurs when ventilation increases disproportionately to VO2, matching lactate buffering by HCO3-.'),
('294feb1c-fb73-53ea-a112-831568bdd419'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'In elite athletes, VO2 max can be as high as:', '["~40-50 mL/kg/min","~20-30 mL/kg/min","~30-40 mL/kg/min","~50-60 mL/kg/min","~70-85 mL/kg/min"]'::jsonb, '~70-85 mL/kg/min', 'Normal VO2 max is ~35-45 mL/kg/min. Elite endurance athletes can achieve 70-85 mL/kg/min.'),
('ba025e65-829f-5f6c-a909-5809dd848364'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The Fick principle relates VO2 to:', '["Cardiac output x (C(a-v)O2 difference)","Heart rate x stroke volume","Ventilation x","O2 saturation x Hb","Work rate x efficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output x (C(a-v)O2 difference)', 'Fick: VO2 = CO x (CaO2 - CvO2). Measured during cardiopulmonary exercise testing.'),
('b85edc8b-3d40-547f-9a34-517c2080c4f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'During maximal exercise, the normal ventilatory reserve (MVV - VE) should be:', '["> 20% of MVV","Negative","Zero","< 5% of MVV","Equal to MVV"]'::jsonb, '> 20% of MVV', 'Healthy individuals have ventilatory reserve; in lung disease, VE may approach MVV, limiting exercise.'),
('e8187014-aedf-5d32-88f4-829c165a63f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Chronic exercise training increases VO2 max by:', '["Increasing PaO2 mediated physiological mechanism","Improving pulmonary function mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing Hb concentration mediated physiological mechanism","Decreasing dead space mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing stroke volume and arteriovenous O2 difference"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing stroke volume and arteriovenous O2 difference', 'Training increases cardiac output (stroke volume) and O2 extraction by working muscles.'),
('b3bd00b7-5311-5b18-bd59-2eace106991b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The initial ventilatory response to high altitude is triggered by:', '["Hypercapnia mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebral edema mediated physiological mechanism","Cold air mediated physiological mechanism","Barotrauma mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoxia stimulating peripheral chemoreceptors"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxia stimulating peripheral chemoreceptors', 'Hypobaric hypoxia at altitude stimulates carotid body chemoreceptors, increasing ventilation within minutes.'),
('ed3b35d6-454d-5340-acc8-0d143b446a46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Acclimatization to high altitude includes all EXCEPT:', '["Decreased mitochondria","Increased 2,3-BPG","Increased diffusing capacity","Increased pulmonary vasoconstriction","Increased hemoglobin concentration"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased mitochondria', 'Acclimatization increases 2,3-BPG, Hb, pulmonary vasoconstriction, and mitochondrial density (not decrease).'),
('ac0fbdaf-1a6a-503e-9e74-164a6acf87ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Acute mountain sickness (AMS) typically occurs above:', '["1500 m","3000 m","3500 m","2000 m","2500 m"]'::jsonb, '2500 m', 'AMS generally begins above 2500 m with headache, nausea, fatigue, and dizziness within hours of ascent.'),
('f240c353-d240-5934-875e-a0c6488f69fe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The most important intervention for acute mountain sickness is:', '["Hydration","Oxygen therapy","Descent to lower altitude","Acetazolamide","Rest"]'::jsonb, 'Descent to lower altitude', 'Definitive treatment for AMS is descent. Acetazolamide aids prevention and mild cases.'),
('f35b2cca-63e4-5172-9032-f7de0182da26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'HAPE (high-altitude pulmonary edema) is characterized by:', '["Cerebral edema symptoms mediated physiological mechanism","Pleuritic chest pain mediated physiological mechanism","Headache and vomiting only mediated physiological mechanism","Hemoptysis mediated physiological mechanism","Exertional dyspnea, cough, frothy sputum, and hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Exertional dyspnea, cough, frothy sputum, and hypoxia', 'HAPE occurs 2-5 days after rapid ascent; features dyspnea, cough, pink frothy sputum, and severe hypoxemia.'),
('185ba505-8c1f-52c4-ba83-6691b40c8f34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'HACE (high-altitude cerebral edema) presents with:', '["Headache alone mediated physiological mechanism","Ataxia, altered consciousness, and papilledema","Peripheral edema mediated physiological mechanism","Tachycardia mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary edema mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ataxia, altered consciousness, and papilledema', 'HACE is a life-threatening condition with cerebellar ataxia, confusion, and eventually coma from cerebral edema.'),
('a8e1b1cf-5478-51db-bfb3-4af0d0c112fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Acetazolamide prevents AMS by:', '["Bronchodilating airways","Causing metabolic alkalosis","Causing metabolic acidosis","Increasing Hb-O2 affinity","Inducing diuresis"]'::jsonb, 'Causing metabolic acidosis', 'Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase, producing HCO3- diuresis and mild metabolic acidosis, which stimulates ventilation.'),
('76a66696-4276-570a-b7df-4e5138d2953f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The ''Haldane effect'' at high altitude facilitates:', '["Renal HCO3- retention mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Increased Hb synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","CO2 unloading in lungs due to oxygenation of Hb","O2 unloading in tissues mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'CO2 unloading in lungs due to oxygenation of Hb', 'Haldane effect: oxygenated Hb binds less CO2, promoting CO2 unloading in lungs — important at altitude.'),
('a0caa917-70e5-5789-ac2a-d218785bc807'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'Chronic mountain sickness (Monge disease) is characterized by:', '["Excessive polycythemia with blunted hypoxic ventilatory response","Acute confusion mediated physiological mechanism","Cardiac failure mediated physiological mechanism","Severe pulmonary edema mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoventilation at altitude mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive polycythemia with blunted hypoxic ventilatory response', 'Monge disease: extreme erythrocytosis, pulmonary hypertension, and cor pulmonale due to loss of hypoxic ventilatory drive.'),
('e7e6c257-e71d-5a07-98db-840fb22c7699'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'The risk of HAPE is increased in individuals with:', '["High VO2 max mediated physiological mechanism","Frequent altitude exposure mediated physiological mechanism","Canadian ancestry mediated physiological mechanism","Absent left pulmonary artery or pulmonary hypertension","High altitude natives mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Absent left pulmonary artery or pulmonary hypertension', 'Absence of one pulmonary artery or preexisting pulmonary hypertension increases HAPE risk due to overperfusion of remaining vessels.'),
('57a98d14-b5a6-51f7-a2b3-53993787dc9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'According to Fick''s law, doubling the thickness of the alveolar-capillary membrane will:', '["Have no effect","Double gas diffusion","Increase diffusion four-fold","Reduce gas diffusion by quarter","Reduce gas diffusion by half"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce gas diffusion by half', 'Fick''s law: Vgas ∝ 1/thickness. Doubling thickness halves the diffusion rate (e.g., in pulmonary fibrosis).'),
('8d10c2ad-6a7c-56c5-a627-205d709d0844'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System', 'At the lactate threshold during incremental exercise, which of the following occurs?', '["O2 consumption plateaus","PaCO2 increases","Heart rate decreases","Blood lactate rises sharply and HCO3- falls","Respiratory exchange ratio (RER) falls below 0.7"]'::jsonb, 'Blood lactate rises sharply and HCO3- falls', 'The lactate threshold marks the point where lactate production exceeds clearance, buffered by HCO3- producing extra CO2.'),
('b44cd972-6f63-54e3-96b6-f5fc71ad174b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Mastication (chewing) is primarily controlled by which cranial nerve?', '["Vagus nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve', 'Masseter, temporalis, and pterygoids are innervated by mandibular division of CN V.'),
('46a21754-d66b-5082-a08b-60e71584046d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'How many phases does swallowing (deglutition) have?', '["pharyngeal","including a gastric phase","oral, esophageal","oral, pharyngeal, esophageal","oral, pharyngeal, esophageal, gastric"]'::jsonb, 'oral, pharyngeal, esophageal', 'Oral phase is voluntary; pharyngeal and esophageal phases are reflex.'),
('a44afd11-ef43-5e33-afde-bc47fcf71b25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The oral phase of swallowing is:', '["Voluntary","Involuntary reflex","Mediated solely by glossopharyngeal nerve","Mediated solely by vagus nerve","Autonomic"]'::jsonb, 'Voluntary', 'Voluntary oral phase: tongue elevates and retracts to propel bolus posteriorly.'),
('30b99c80-63e8-5f03-a415-dfe7a613e605'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The swallowing center is located in the:', '["Cerebral cortex","Cerebellum","Medulla oblongata","Midbrain","Pons"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata', 'The medullary swallowing center coordinates pharyngeal constrictors and laryngeal elevation.'),
('18f3d26a-9134-597d-b850-823f26ba1012'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'During the pharyngeal phase, the epiglottis:', '["Does nothing mediated physiological mechanism","Covers the laryngeal opening to prevent aspiration","Relaxes completely mediated physiological mechanism","Moves anteriorly mediated physiological mechanism","Opens the trachea mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the laryngeal opening to prevent aspiration', 'Epiglottis retroflexes; vocal cords adduct; breathing is inhibited.'),
('e58395ce-f636-53c1-999a-310c49027717'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The upper esophageal sphincter (UES) is formed by:', '["Diaphragm","Pylorus","Lower esophageal sphincter","Ileocecal valve","Cricopharyngeus muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Cricopharyngeus muscle', 'UES (cricopharyngeus) relaxes during swallowing to allow bolus entry into esophagus.'),
('3885c6ad-2019-55da-a623-9a002c25794e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The lower esophageal sphincter (LES) is:', '["Non-existent in humans mediated physiological mechanism","Controlled by somatic nerves mediated physiological mechanism","A true anatomical sphincter mediated physiological mechanism","Part of the pylorus mediated physiological mechanism","A physiological sphincter preventing gastric reflux"]'::jsonb, 'A physiological sphincter preventing gastric reflux', 'LES tone is maintained by intrinsic myogenic and hormonal factors.'),
('38b226f6-95f3-5482-95a8-5055d972d7a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'LES relaxation during swallowing is mediated by:', '["VIP and nitric oxide","Serotonin","Histamine","Acetylcholine","Noradrenaline"]'::jsonb, 'VIP and nitric oxide', 'NO and VIP are non-adrenergic, non-cholinergic (NANC) inhibitory transmitters.'),
('a4e26739-dade-5dec-a471-6cf015339e60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) primarily involves:', '["Increased salivation","Rapid esophageal transit","Increased LES tone","Delayed gastric emptying only","Incompetent LES causing acid reflux"]'::jsonb, 'Incompetent LES causing acid reflux', 'Transient LES relaxations or low basal LES pressure permit gastric acid reflux.'),
('0443af48-77d2-510d-88f7-c9f23c2f5796'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The esophageal phase of swallowing involves:', '["Bolus dissolving in saliva mediated physiological mechanism","Peristaltic contractions propelling bolus toward stomach","Voluntary muscle action mediated physiological mechanism","Sphincter contraction mediated physiological mechanism","Segmentation movements mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Peristaltic contractions propelling bolus toward stomach', 'Primary peristalsis follows the bolus; secondary peristalsis clears residual material.'),
('9824d01a-4eee-5bec-a457-cca6772abcf7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Which nerve provides sensory innervation to the pharynx for the gag reflex?', '["Facial nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve', 'CN IX senses the posterior pharyngeal wall; motor response via CN X.'),
('acd55d6b-3aac-55c7-a30b-b5517837366d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Failure of UES relaxation is characteristic of:', '["GERD","Esophageal spasm","Hiatal hernia","Cricopharyngeal achalasia","Achalasia cardia"]'::jsonb, 'Cricopharyngeal achalasia', 'Lack of UES relaxation causes hypopharyngeal pouch (Zenker diverticulum).'),
('3177eb67-627b-52d3-be0e-87c6b0701884'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Achalasia cardia involves:', '["Failure of LES relaxation and loss of peristalsis","Incompetent LES mediated physiological mechanism","Hyper-contractile esophagus mediated physiological mechanism","Increased peristalsis mediated physiological mechanism","UES spasm mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of LES relaxation and loss of peristalsis', 'Achalasia: loss of inhibitory neurons (NO) in LES; treated by myotomy or pneumatic dilation.'),
('572c16b5-0267-5609-a1ec-5c99d68a616b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Primary peristalsis in the esophagus is:', '["Tonic contraction","Segmentation movement","Local enteric reflex","Initiated by swallowing","Myogenic contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Initiated by swallowing', 'Primary peristalsis is a continuation of the pharyngeal phase; secondary peristalsis is local.'),
('29763c27-d282-5db0-95a9-5f2fea9723ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The pharyngeal phase of swallowing is:', '["Peripheral mediated physiological mechanism","An involuntary reflex mediated by medulla","Spinal mediated physiological mechanism","Voluntary mediated physiological mechanism","Cortical mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'An involuntary reflex mediated by medulla', 'The swallowing center coordinates pharyngeal constrictors, laryngeal elevation, and airway protection.'),
('671b1ecc-4575-5150-9d35-2d347b27dfc2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Which structure prevents reflux of food into the nasopharynx during swallowing?', '["Epiglottis","Laryngeal elevation","Soft palate elevation","UES contraction","Vocal cord adduction"]'::jsonb, 'Soft palate elevation', 'Soft palate rises to seal off nasopharynx during pharyngeal phase.'),
('e9ebcc97-cc00-5dc6-a31e-31aac47c03f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The LES is tonically contracted due to:', '["Somatic input","Sympathetic input only","Inhibitory input only","Myogenic tone plus excitatory cholinergic input","Absence of any neural input"]'::jsonb, 'Myogenic tone plus excitatory cholinergic input', 'LES has intrinsic myogenic tone; vagal cholinergic fibers increase tone; NO/VIP relax it.'),
('bf30f962-ab4d-58ec-a7d4-2e1f28b8aa97'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Hiatal hernia predisposes to GERD by:', '["Displacing LES above diaphragm, reducing its pressure","Increasing LES tone mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing saliva production mediated physiological mechanism","Decreasing gastric volume mediated physiological mechanism","Delaying esophageal transit mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Displacing LES above diaphragm, reducing its pressure', 'In hiatal hernia, LES may not be at diaphragmatic hiatus, losing crural support.'),
('a5cd283f-f81f-5f46-bd8e-55a26cfdbf58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Salivary secretion is primarily controlled by:', '["Parasympathetic (CN VII, IX) cholinergic input","Enteric reflexes mediated physiological mechanism","Sympathetic input only mediated physiological mechanism","Somatic motor input mediated physiological mechanism","Hormonal control mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (CN VII, IX) cholinergic input', 'Parasympathetic stimulation produces copious watery saliva; sympathetic produces thick mucus.'),
('42bfcdb1-5a83-5a11-9c89-d11720aa3619'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Which salivary glands contribute most to unstimulated saliva?', '["Sublingual only","Submandibular only","All equally","Submandibular (~70%) and parotid","Parotid only"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular (~70%) and parotid', 'Submandibular glands produce the majority of baseline saliva.'),
('df7503ad-55c4-5df1-86fe-51b2cdd5d1a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Saliva contains which antibacterial enzyme?', '["Trypsin","Pepsin","Lipase","Lysozyme","Amylase"]'::jsonb, 'Lysozyme', 'Lysozyme in saliva lyses bacterial cell walls.'),
('e62cdaac-641a-5d1f-83f6-0c86921b8668'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The swallowing reflex is initiated by tactile stimulation of the:', '["Esophagus","Soft palate","Epiglottis","Posterior pharyngeal wall","Tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior pharyngeal wall', 'Sensory afferents from pharynx to medulla trigger the swallowing reflex.'),
('ed1d8698-5946-5b7a-a775-368eb8da46a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'During swallowing, respiration is:', '["Irregular","Increased","Stimulated","Unchanged","Inhibited"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibited', 'Breathing is briefly inhibited during pharyngeal phase to prevent aspiration.'),
('7b1c3165-3abd-5bef-8862-55797e9a143c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Bolus movement through the esophagus takes approximately:', '["30-60 seconds","5-10 seconds","2-5 minutes","1-2 seconds","<1 second"]'::jsonb, '5-10 seconds', 'Peristaltic wave takes ~6-8 sec to reach stomach; gravity helps upright.'),
('dc7d1a2d-bfaf-597a-aeb6-8b87096faa62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The cricopharyngeus muscle acts as:', '["Pharyngeal constrictor","Pyloric sphincter","Laryngeal elevator","Upper esophageal sphincter","Lower esophageal sphincter"]'::jsonb, 'Upper esophageal sphincter', 'Cricopharyngeus is a striated muscle sphincter at the pharyngoesophageal junction.'),
('121272d2-d5a3-59dd-af7c-2601a15c41a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Peristalsis is defined as:', '["Tonic contraction of circular muscle","Coordinated contraction proximal and relaxation distal to propel contents","Rhythmic spasm","Independent contractions of smooth muscle","Segmentation movements"]'::jsonb, 'Coordinated contraction proximal and relaxation distal to propel contents', 'Peristalsis: circular muscle contracts oral to bolus and relaxes aboral to it.'),
('9946638f-ee64-56d8-8f97-6a6c80ff384f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Segmentation contractions primarily function to:', '["Empty the stomach","Mix chyme and enhance absorption","Inhibit peristalsis","Open sphincters","Propel contents forward"]'::jsonb, 'Mix chyme and enhance absorption', 'Segmentation in small intestine mixes contents and exposes them to mucosa.'),
('3a965143-2022-5357-a55b-ea27ec0e6ef8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The migrating motor complex (MMC) is:', '["A cyclic pattern of GI motility during fasting","Postprandial motility pattern","Peristalsis after meals","Tonic contraction of colon","Segmentation during meals"]'::jsonb, 'A cyclic pattern of GI motility during fasting', 'MMC cycles every 90-120 min during fasting; clears debris and bacteria.'),
('df55a0fd-e56f-5a6a-b3b9-8b57ebbbceb2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The MMC is disrupted by:', '["Stress","Sleep","Food intake","Exercise","Fasting"]'::jsonb, 'Food intake', 'Eating converts MMC to fed pattern (irregular but sustained contractions).'),
('45244112-7ab9-5c04-bc7f-8b6eecab1db8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Gastric emptying of liquids is primarily regulated by:', '["Pressure gradient between stomach and duodenum","Hormones only mediated physiological mechanism","Neural factors only mediated physiological mechanism","Gastric volume only mediated physiological mechanism","Duodenal pH only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Pressure gradient between stomach and duodenum', 'Liquid emptying occurs rapidly; regulated by gastric-duodenal pressure gradient.'),
('686316aa-ab0d-50a3-90ae-731b26bdb3e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Fat in the duodenum delays gastric emptying via:', '["Gastrin release","Increased gastric motility","CCK and secretin","Acetylcholine release","Histamine release"]'::jsonb, 'CCK and secretin', 'Fat stimulates CCK and secretin, which inhibit gastric motility and emptying.'),
('45470fa9-3a10-51c9-bf71-0de66c814755'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The ileocecal sphincter:', '["Opens only during meals","Is a true anatomical sphincter","Prevents reflux from colon into ileum","Closes permanently","Relaxes during fasting"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents reflux from colon into ileum', 'Ileocecal valve maintains one-way flow and prevents bacterial backflow into ileum.'),
('3f85fd2a-8636-511a-b4e3-8afdefc0289d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The gastrocolic reflex is:', '["Gastric relaxation","Increased colonic motility after a meal","Decreased colonic motility after a meal","Colonic relaxation","Ileal contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Increased colonic motility after a meal', 'Gastrocolic reflex mediated by gastrin, CCK, and neural pathways.'),
('ba99ffb8-6185-50cc-9e19-8a686df55ee7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The defecation reflex is initiated by:', '["Increase in intra-abdominal pressure","Relaxation of internal anal sphincter","Contraction of abdominal muscles","Voluntary relaxation of external sphincter","Stretch of rectal wall"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch of rectal wall', 'Rectal distension triggers intrinsic myenteric reflex and parasympathetic defecation reflex.'),
('dd4b7279-84c6-5d97-801c-fcfb1e1bdecb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The internal anal sphincter is composed of:', '["Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Elastic tissue","Striated muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle', 'Internal sphincter is smooth muscle; external sphincter is striated (voluntary).'),
('423a1728-9cb4-5179-aba0-d510a5dc736a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The external anal sphincter is controlled by:', '["Sympathetic chain","Enteric nervous system","Pudendal nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve', 'External sphincter is voluntarily controlled via the pudendal nerve.'),
('3ccec404-6011-5e11-909c-fbfb0dcb5b61'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The fed state motility pattern is characterized by:', '["MMC cycling mediated physiological mechanism","Irregular but sustained contractions","No contractions mediated physiological mechanism","Segmentation only mediated physiological mechanism","Tonic spasm mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Irregular but sustained contractions', 'After eating, MMC stops and is replaced by the fed pattern of mixing and propulsion.'),
('42f412b0-b4b9-5c2b-a62e-04d38541576d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Diarrhea may result from:', '["Increased water absorption","Decreased secretion","Decreased motility and decreased absorption","Increased motility with decreased absorption","Increased segmentation only"]'::jsonb, 'Increased motility with decreased absorption', 'Rapid transit reduces time for water absorption; toxin-induced secretion also contributes.'),
('09a03943-7448-5bf0-95c1-4fb98905bb13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Constipation is commonly associated with:', '["Rapid motility mediated physiological mechanism","Increased colonic transit time and excessive water absorption","Increased secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased water absorption mediated physiological mechanism","Shortened transit time mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased colonic transit time and excessive water absorption', 'Slow colonic transit allows more water extraction, hardening stool.'),
('8f4e7b2f-efc5-5a69-93d8-d939e62c1ee2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The basic electrical rhythm (slow waves) of GI smooth muscle originates from:', '["Sympathetic ganglia","Enteric neurons","Smooth muscle cells","Interstitial cells of Cajal","Vagal fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Interstitial cells of Cajal', 'ICC generate slow wave potentials that determine frequency of contractions.'),
('c43aa00b-9475-5d5f-a8df-d9eb4ff48c57'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Slow waves in GI smooth muscle:', '["Are fast sodium spikes mediated physiological mechanism","Are action potentials mediated physiological mechanism","Do not themselves cause contractions but trigger action potentials","Directly cause strong contractions mediated physiological mechanism","Are absent in the colon mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Do not themselves cause contractions but trigger action potentials', 'Slow waves depolarize threshold; action potentials fire on slow wave peaks.'),
('dd9b0442-b340-59d7-8bd7-e591de595a11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Action potentials in GI smooth muscle are mediated by:', '["Chloride influx","Sodium influx","Calcium influx","Magnesium influx","Potassium efflux"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium influx', 'GI smooth muscle action potentials are Ca2+-dependent; cause contraction.'),
('49f61be2-d73d-5491-94e2-8c0e0c6863d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pyloric sphincter relaxation allows:', '["Bile to enter duodenum","Esophageal contents to enter stomach","Colonic contents to enter ileum","Pancreatic juice to enter duodenum","Gastric chyme to enter duodenum"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric chyme to enter duodenum', 'Pylorus regulates gastric emptying; neural and hormonal signals control it.'),
