('5c807710-db80-5004-8101-f82940f4a707'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The most common causes of acute pancreatitis are:', '["Hypertriglyceridemia","Autoimmune","Medications","Viral infections","Gallstones and alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Gallstones and alcohol', 'Gallstones obstruct ampulla; alcohol sensitizes acinar cells to CCK.'),
('f0495226-cedb-5221-a4e3-13b7c66ca6c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic ductal cells secrete HCO3- via:', '["GLUT2 transporter mediated physiological mechanism","SGLT1 transporter mediated physiological mechanism","H+/K+ ATPase mediated physiological mechanism","Na+/K+ ATPase mediated physiological mechanism","CFTR-dependent Cl-/HCO3- exchange"]'::jsonb, 'CFTR-dependent Cl-/HCO3- exchange', 'CFTR secretes Cl- which is exchanged for HCO3- via pendrin.'),
('4b63d77f-8e94-551b-8d03-b077799fd9e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Cystic fibrosis affects pancreatic function by:', '["Defective CFTR causing thick secretions and ductal obstruction","Defective amylase secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Increased HCO3- secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Defective lipase secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Defective trypsinogen activation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Defective CFTR causing thick secretions and ductal obstruction', 'CF leads to pancreatic insufficiency and maldigestion.'),
('b8ef92e8-fd4e-57f3-a0b0-348136d68c88'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic colipase functions to:', '["Activate trypsinogen mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibit proteases mediated physiological mechanism","Anchorage of lipase at lipid-water interface in presence of bile salts","Directly digest triglycerides mediated physiological mechanism","Emulsify fat mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Anchorage of lipase at lipid-water interface in presence of bile salts', 'Bile salts strip colipase; colipase binds both lipase and lipid surface.'),
('02a75e36-c100-5cdd-a4de-bb7d7451654d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic phospholipase A2 digests:', '["Phospholipids into lysophospholipids and fatty acids","Starch mediated physiological mechanism","Proteins mediated physiological mechanism","Nucleic acids mediated physiological mechanism","Triglycerides mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Phospholipids into lysophospholipids and fatty acids', 'Phospholipase A2 is activated by trypsin; important in pathophysiology of pancreatitis.'),
('7a368043-4daa-58f6-b731-a4c3ce6c8383'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic elastase digests:', '["Nucleic acids mediated physiological mechanism","Lipids mediated physiological mechanism","Disaccharides mediated physiological mechanism","Elastin and other connective tissue proteins","Starch mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Elastin and other connective tissue proteins', 'Elastase is a serine protease with specificity for elastic fibers.'),
('e9fcf50c-c5de-57b0-a085-b8a048bc60a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic carboxypeptidase digests:', '["Nucleic acids mediated physiological mechanism","Peptides at the carboxy-terminal end","Starch mediated physiological mechanism","Disaccharides mediated physiological mechanism","Lipids mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Peptides at the carboxy-terminal end', 'Carboxypeptidase A and B are exopeptidases activated by trypsin.'),
('37a15a88-e51c-562c-894c-a56ab9078870'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic ribonuclease and deoxyribonuclease digest:', '["Carbohydrates","Fats","Proteins","Phospholipids","Nucleic acids"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleic acids', 'Pancreatic nucleases digest nucleic acids into nucleotides.'),
('07839cf9-92e0-531f-bf95-6768a53c2ebd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Trypsin inhibitor is produced by:', '["Duodenum","Pancreas","Liver","Stomach","Gallbladder"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreas', 'Pancreatic trypsin inhibitor binds trypsin to prevent autodigestion.'),
('d6db85cd-6f28-5ee1-b7d1-d59d0f2d1eaf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Chronic pancreatitis leads to:', '["Progressive loss of exocrine and endocrine function","Increased insulin secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Gallstone formation mediated physiological mechanism","Exocrine hyperfunction mediated physiological mechanism","Increased bile production mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive loss of exocrine and endocrine function', 'Fibrosis destroys acinar cells and later islets -> diabetes.'),
('a1a932a2-c413-5046-88a2-ce9a10685954'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy uses:', '["Only lipase supplements mediated physiological mechanism","Only protease supplements mediated physiological mechanism","Only amylase supplements mediated physiological mechanism","Lipase, amylase, and protease supplements taken with meals","Bile acid supplements mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Lipase, amylase, and protease supplements taken with meals', 'Enteric-coated microspheres release enzymes at duodenal pH.'),
('d21f4358-bf67-5613-96fc-7ff893ad6d17'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Maximal pancreatic HCO3- concentration is about:', '["100 mEq/L","145 mEq/L","25 mEq/L","200 mEq/L","50 mEq/L"]'::jsonb, '145 mEq/L', 'Secretin stimulates ductal cells to secrete HCO3- at high concentration.'),
('2d5ddcf6-db3a-5e75-b374-7fac5aee4584'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'With respect to management, The sphincter of Oddi controls:', '["Rectal emptying mediated physiological mechanism","Ileocecal flow mediated physiological mechanism","Esophageal transit mediated physiological mechanism","Flow of bile and pancreatic juice into duodenum","Gastric emptying mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Flow of bile and pancreatic juice into duodenum', 'Sphincter of Oddi at ampulla of Vater; relaxes with CCK.'),
('00c050f5-b72f-5f20-9a32-2f8209bda092'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic secretion occurs in which phases?', '["Gastric and intestinal only","Cephalic only","Cephalic, gastric, and intestinal","Intestinal only","Cephalic and gastric only"]'::jsonb, 'Cephalic, gastric, and intestinal', 'Intestinal phase (via CCK and secretin) accounts for ~80% of pancreatic secretion.'),
('5e3af0a3-9de7-56eb-af0f-0a410ccdfbcf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The vagal (cephalic) phase of pancreatic secretion produces:', '["Mucus only","Small volume of enzyme-rich juice","Large volume of HCO3--rich juice","Bile only","No secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Small volume of enzyme-rich juice', 'Vagal ACh stimulates acinar cells; small volume but enzyme rich.'),
('3b3ec2e8-fd0e-51d8-837e-e4cf3d39761a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic secretion in the intestinal phase is:', '["Large volume with high enzyme and HCO3- content","Large volume with low enzyme content","Small volume with high enzyme content","Low volume with high HCO3-","No secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Large volume with high enzyme and HCO3- content', 'CCK and secretin together produce maximal pancreatic secretion.'),
('fe258e67-6ed3-56b4-865b-9b7ff067338b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Cholinergic stimulation (vagus) of pancreatic acinar cells is via:', '["Histamine","Serotonin","Noradrenaline","ACh acting on nicotinic receptors","ACh acting on M3 receptors"]'::jsonb, 'ACh acting on M3 receptors', 'Vagal ACh binds M3 receptors on acinar cells -> IP3/Ca2+ -> enzyme secretion.'),
('a006a885-0d55-5e66-9fdd-983cabda3c2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Sympathetic stimulation of the pancreas:', '["No effect","Stimulates secretion","Inhibits exocrine secretion","Stimulates only HCO3-","Stimulates only enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits exocrine secretion', 'Sympathetic activation reduces blood flow and inhibits pancreatic secretion.'),
('b3483db8-a44b-5c5e-8192-77ab0b0c246e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic polypeptide is secreted by:', '["Alpha cells mediated physiological mechanism","Ductal cells mediated physiological mechanism","Beta cells mediated physiological mechanism","PP cells (F cells) in pancreatic islets","Acinar cells mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'PP cells (F cells) in pancreatic islets', 'Pancreatic polypeptide inhibits pancreatic exocrine secretion; released after protein meals.'),
('4b148691-6e01-5a80-941c-e83a7ed1c4f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'In pancreatic insufficiency, stool examination shows:', '["Mucus","Steatorrhea","Excess starch","Blood","Excess protein"]'::jsonb, 'Steatorrhea', 'Fecal fat >7g/24h indicates pancreatic insufficiency.'),
('41d1f648-42f1-53e7-a94c-103faf3e4748'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Bile is produced by:', '["Gallbladder","Stomach","Duodenum","Hepatocytes","Pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocytes', 'Hepatocytes produce bile (800-1000 mL/day); modified by cholangiocytes.'),
('54818b16-1296-5c2c-aa66-d36583176e3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The main components of bile are:', '["Pancreatic enzymes mediated physiological mechanism","Gastric juice mediated physiological mechanism","Digestive enzymes mediated physiological mechanism","Mucus mediated physiological mechanism","Water, bile salts, bilirubin, cholesterol, phospholipids"]'::jsonb, 'Water, bile salts, bilirubin, cholesterol, phospholipids', 'Bile salts aid fat digestion; bilirubin is a waste product; cholesterol may form stones.'),
('b18b9b98-3304-5b0b-9160-f58d8c87e8aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Bile is concentrated (5-10 fold) in the:', '["Duodenum","Liver","Gallbladder","Common bile duct","Pancreatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Gallbladder', 'Gallbladder concentrates bile by absorbing water and electrolytes.'),
('77f15a39-ff5e-5eaa-9d54-776534212421'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Gallbladder contraction is stimulated primarily by:', '["Secretin","VIP","Motilin","Gastrin","CCK"]'::jsonb, 'CCK', 'CCK contracts gallbladder and relaxes sphincter of Oddi.'),
('efff1b49-f8a6-53d8-b468-b7c9e15a39bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Bile salts emulsify fats by:', '["Neutralizing gastric acid","Dissolving fat","Chemically digesting fat","Precipitating fat","Breaking large fat droplets into smaller ones"]'::jsonb, 'Breaking large fat droplets into smaller ones', 'Emulsification increases surface area for pancreatic lipase action.'),
('d8950074-a861-5e7f-bd34-95ab6a031344'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Bile salts are synthesized from:', '["Amino acids","Phospholipids","Bilirubin","Fatty acids","Cholesterol in the liver"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol in the liver', 'Primary bile salts (cholic and chenodeoxycholic) are conjugated with glycine or taurine.'),
('4ac9ed43-905c-577e-9d9b-382dd9df389b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The enterohepatic circulation of bile salts:', '["Synthesizes new bile salts","Recycles ~95% of bile salts between liver and ileum","Converts bilirubin","Stores bile in gallbladder","Excretes all bile salts in feces"]'::jsonb, 'Recycles ~95% of bile salts between liver and ileum', 'Bile salts are reabsorbed in terminal ileum and returned to liver via portal vein.'),
('543f3d3c-9ed2-5b45-a40f-7be0461a272b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Cholelithiasis (gallstones) are most commonly:', '["Pigment stones","Calcium bilirubinate stones only","Cholesterol stones","Struvite stones","Mixed stones only"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol stones', 'Risk factors: female, fertile, fat, forty (plus obesity, rapid weight loss).'),
('8b96ab7b-1b2d-545b-a77d-7b70fc8b44db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Bilirubin is derived from:', '["Fat catabolism","Carbohydrate metabolism","Protein catabolism","Cholesterol metabolism","Heme breakdown"]'::jsonb, 'Heme breakdown', 'Heme oxygenase converts heme to biliverdin; biliverdin reductase to bilirubin.'),
('eefe5cc8-3e05-51af-972b-a150402d51e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin is:', '["Water-soluble mediated physiological mechanism","Conjugated in bile mediated physiological mechanism","Albumin-bound in bile mediated physiological mechanism","Water-insoluble, bound to albumin in plasma","Excreted in urine mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Water-insoluble, bound to albumin in plasma', 'Unconjugated bilirubin travels to liver for conjugation with glucuronic acid.'),
('57be26b8-1144-5943-baf3-5100ebb2f7f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Conjugated (direct) bilirubin is:', '["Toxic to brain mediated physiological mechanism","Water-insoluble mediated physiological mechanism","Not excreted mediated physiological mechanism","Bound to albumin mediated physiological mechanism","Water-soluble and excreted into bile"]'::jsonb, 'Water-soluble and excreted into bile', 'Conjugation via UGT1A1 makes bilirubin soluble for biliary excretion.'),
('6a1ef923-33af-5f0c-942e-85c7197187a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Crigler-Najjar syndrome is due to:', '["Obstructive jaundice mediated physiological mechanism","Hemolysis mediated physiological mechanism","Biliary atresia mediated physiological mechanism","UGT1A1 deficiency causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Hepatocellular injury mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'UGT1A1 deficiency causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia', 'Type I (severe) vs type II (partial) deficiency of glucuronidation.'),
('e07f0367-5a3b-5c22-b3aa-7f5f1f8600bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Gilbert syndrome presents with:', '["Severe bilirubin encephalopathy mediated physiological mechanism","Hemolytic jaundice mediated physiological mechanism","Mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia from reduced UGT activity","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia mediated physiological mechanism","Obstructive jaundice mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia from reduced UGT activity', 'Benign; bilirubin rises with fasting/stress; common (5-10% population).'),
('ed858e37-ec0c-5365-9206-c091e38e8e88'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Obstructive jaundice shows:', '["Normal colored urine","Elevated conjugated bilirubin, dark urine, pale stools","Hemolysis","Dark stools","Elevated unconjugated bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated conjugated bilirubin, dark urine, pale stools', 'Obstruction prevents bilirubin reaching intestine; conjugated bilirubin spills into urine.'),
('19d64003-4727-5065-8643-f6b5cd36a2d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Urobilinogen is formed by:', '["Renal filtration","Hepatocyte conjugation","Pancreatic secretion","Bacterial reduction of bilirubin in the colon","Gallbladder concentration"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial reduction of bilirubin in the colon', 'Urobilinogen is partly reabsorbed (enterohepatic circulation) and partly excreted in feces (stercobilin).'),
('98dbd179-916a-5ddc-961a-23117530f281'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Van den Bergh test measures:', '["Direct (conjugated) and indirect (unconjugated) bilirubin","Albumin mediated physiological mechanism","Only conjugated bilirubin mediated physiological mechanism","Only unconjugated bilirubin mediated physiological mechanism","Total protein mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Direct (conjugated) and indirect (unconjugated) bilirubin', 'Direct reacts with diazo reagent without alcohol; indirect requires alcohol.'),
('fd9b055e-c352-50c0-9145-59bb8b8912a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Primary bile acids are:', '["All of these","Deoxycholic acid and lithocholic acid","Ursodeoxycholic acid","Glycocholic acid and taurocholic acid","Cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid', 'Primary bile acids are synthesized in liver; secondary (deoxycholic, lithocholic) are formed in colon.'),
('efa2d6ef-c76a-5824-8dcb-06434f3c20cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Secondary bile acids are produced by:', '["Pancreatic secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Bacterial deconjugation and dehydroxylation in colon","Gallbladder modification mediated physiological mechanism","Liver synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Duodenal mucosa mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial deconjugation and dehydroxylation in colon', 'Gut bacteria convert primary to secondary bile acids.'),
('8daf8416-c7cb-543a-8f26-90cd79990b67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Ursodeoxycholic acid is used therapeutically to:', '["Dissolve cholesterol gallstones","Treat pancreatitis","Treat jaundice","Stimulate pancreatic enzymes","Reduce gastric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Dissolve cholesterol gallstones', 'UDCA reduces cholesterol saturation of bile and promotes dissolution.'),
('062d2fd0-5c3f-5897-ae3e-05c5463b05f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Bile salt export pump (BSEP) deficiency causes:', '["Rotor syndrome mediated physiological mechanism","Crigler-Najjar syndrome mediated physiological mechanism","Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis","Dubin-Johnson syndrome mediated physiological mechanism","Gilbert syndrome mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis', 'BSEP transports bile salts across canalicular membrane; mutation leads to cholestasis.'),
('b6d061e2-37a0-59e5-9112-0225859ef7da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Bilirubin encephalopathy (kernicterus) in neonates:', '["Unconjugated bilirubin deposits in basal ganglia","Occurs only in adults","Conjugated bilirubin deposits in brain","Is always benign","Does not cause sequelae"]'::jsonb, 'Unconjugated bilirubin deposits in basal ganglia', 'Unconjugated bilirubin crosses immature BBB; toxic to neurons.'),
('fd977e72-21e4-550e-aa2a-bf75751eca87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Phototherapy for neonatal jaundice:', '["Converts bilirubin to water-soluble isomers","Enhances hepatic uptake mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulates bile flow mediated physiological mechanism","Removes heme mediated physiological mechanism","Increases conjugation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Converts bilirubin to water-soluble isomers', 'Light (blue) converts bilirubin to lumirubin which is excreted without conjugation.'),
('19d3f1f1-721e-5642-9b65-5b49d649b0e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Dubin-Johnson syndrome is:', '["Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to defective canalicular MRP2 transporter","UGT deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Biliary obstruction mediated physiological mechanism","Hemolytic jaundice mediated physiological mechanism","Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to defective canalicular MRP2 transporter', 'Black liver from pigment accumulation; benign condition.'),
('1812f33c-278f-5d8f-bac7-3eff212c3964'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Rotor syndrome is:', '["Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia mediated physiological mechanism","Hemolysis mediated physiological mechanism","Bile duct obstruction mediated physiological mechanism","Biliary atresia mediated physiological mechanism","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to defective hepatic storage"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to defective hepatic storage', 'Similar to Dubin-Johnson but without liver pigmentation.'),
('3b5e920e-0ef4-5220-8337-de699ee59c24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Lecithin (phosphatidylcholine) in bile:', '["Absorbs vitamins mediated physiological mechanism","Serves to solubilize cholesterol in mixed micelles","Conjugates bilirubin mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulates gallbladder mediated physiological mechanism","Digests fat mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Serves to solubilize cholesterol in mixed micelles', 'Lecithin and bile salts form mixed micelles keeping cholesterol in solution.'),
('6dcb687b-13e0-501d-b1ab-0087d890bf25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Cholesterol gallstone formation results from:', '["Increased HCO3- mediated physiological mechanism","Excess lecithin mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased bilirubin mediated physiological mechanism","Excess bile salts mediated physiological mechanism","Supersaturation of bile with cholesterol relative to bile salts and lecithin"]'::jsonb, 'Supersaturation of bile with cholesterol relative to bile salts and lecithin', 'When cholesterol exceeds solubilizing capacity, it precipitates as crystals.'),
('c1bf3d7d-65cb-5e1a-8572-3770929308d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pigment gallstones are associated with:', '["Estrogen therapy mediated physiological mechanism","Hemolysis (black stones) or infection","Rapid weight loss mediated physiological mechanism","High fat diet mediated physiological mechanism","Obesity mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolysis (black stones) or infection', 'Black stones: unconjugated bilirubin; brown stones: bacterial deconjugation.'),
('16ab59b2-94ba-5a97-ae0a-a369a114ff0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Cholestyramine is a bile acid-binding resin used for:', '["Reducing cholesterol synthesis","Gallstone dissolution","Pruritus in cholestasis","Treating pancreatitis","Increasing bile flow"]'::jsonb, 'Pruritus in cholestasis', 'Cholestyramine binds bile acids in gut, preventing reabsorption.'),
('945ad7cf-8b35-517c-a4b7-70495341fa80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The gallbladder can store approximately:', '["1000 mL","5-10 mL","500 mL","30-50 mL of concentrated bile","150-200 mL"]'::jsonb, '30-50 mL of concentrated bile', 'Liver produces ~600-1000 mL bile/day; gallbladder concentrates it 5-10 fold.'),
('3ce3baee-47b7-5243-80ea-6970f5318ffd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Cholecystokinin (CCK) causes relaxation of:', '["Pyloric sphincter","Sphincter of Oddi","Ileocecal sphincter","Internal anal sphincter","Lower esophageal sphincter"]'::jsonb, 'Sphincter of Oddi', 'CCK relaxes Sphincter of Oddi allowing bile and pancreatic juice to enter duodenum.'),
('0e8a5590-f122-5698-8032-e693429d7b8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver has a dual blood supply from:', '["Superior mesenteric artery","Hepatic artery (~25%) and portal vein","Inferior vena cava","Hepatic artery only","Portal vein only"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic artery (~25%) and portal vein', 'Portal vein brings nutrient-rich blood from GI tract; hepatic artery delivers oxygenated blood.'),
('14c122bb-c1cb-547a-9630-3cfd205c118d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Kupffer cells in the liver are:', '["Bile duct epithelial cells","Endothelial cells","Hepatocytes","Stellate cells","Macrophages"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophages', 'Kupffer cells phagocytose bacteria, endotoxins, and debris from portal blood.'),
('e589f946-fd0a-5e3f-a960-3103afffe181'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Hepatic stellate cells (Ito cells) primarily store:', '["Bile","Glycogen","Copper","Vitamin A","Iron"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin A', 'Stellate cells store vitamin A; in liver injury they transform into collagen-producing myofibroblasts.'),
('6a8fd23a-d723-596f-a663-0966c34e04be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Liver regeneration after partial hepatectomy is driven by:', '["Fibroblast division mediated physiological mechanism","Bile duct proliferation mediated physiological mechanism","Stem cell differentiation mediated physiological mechanism","Hepatocyte growth factor (HGF), EGF, and TGF-alpha","Scar formation only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocyte growth factor (HGF), EGF, and TGF-alpha', 'Liver can regenerate to original size within weeks after resection.'),
('f9bdc9b4-974e-5bb3-a96b-147a67b7b5a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver synthesizes which plasma proteins?', '["Only clotting factors mediated physiological mechanism","Only globulins mediated physiological mechanism","Only albumin mediated physiological mechanism","Only immunoglobulins mediated physiological mechanism","Albumin, clotting factors, complement proteins, and carrier proteins"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin, clotting factors, complement proteins, and carrier proteins', 'Liver produces most plasma proteins except immunoglobulins (from plasma cells).'),
('602ab1c1-55dd-5c98-824f-987648035ad7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Albumin is synthesized by the liver and functions as:', '["Enzyme mediated physiological mechanism","Immune protein mediated physiological mechanism","Hormone mediated physiological mechanism","Main determinant of plasma oncotic pressure and carrier of substances","Clotting factor mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Main determinant of plasma oncotic pressure and carrier of substances', 'Albumin maintains colloid osmotic pressure; transports bilirubin, drugs, hormones.'),
('94e6b766-b66c-5085-b3ca-8c839b02309f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver is responsible for gluconeogenesis which:', '["Stores glucose as glycogen mediated physiological mechanism","Converts glucose to fat mediated physiological mechanism","Breaks down glycogen mediated physiological mechanism","Oxidizes glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Produces glucose from lactate, amino acids, and glycerol"]'::jsonb, 'Produces glucose from lactate, amino acids, and glycerol', 'Gluconeogenesis maintains blood glucose during fasting.'),
('47b090ec-a852-5e25-b234-eb85c1d2e760'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Glycogenolysis in the liver is stimulated by:', '["Insulin mediated physiological mechanism","Somatostatin mediated physiological mechanism","Thyroid hormone mediated physiological mechanism","Glucagon (via cAMP) and epinephrine","Cortisol mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon (via cAMP) and epinephrine', 'Glucagon from alpha cells activates glycogen phosphorylase.'),
('df43ccf8-b3f7-5534-9d04-129818d41115'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver is the main site of:', '["Fat absorption","Mineral absorption","Urea cycle","Protein digestion","Vitamin activation in the gut"]'::jsonb, 'Urea cycle', 'Hepatic failure elevates ammonia causing hepatic encephalopathy.'),
('c35abdc8-da55-5a22-a293-e54898b607a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'In hepatic failure, coagulopathy results from:', '["Vitamin K deficiency only","DIC","Decreased synthesis of clotting factors","Factor consumption","Increased platelet consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased synthesis of clotting factors', 'PT (INR) is prolonged early in liver failure.'),
('b56d7722-5add-5508-a1da-99379d35a165'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Portal hypertension causes:', '["Increased albumin synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Increased clotting factors mediated physiological mechanism","Esophageal varices, ascites, splenomegaly, and portosystemic shunts","Improved synthetic function mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased portal pressure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal varices, ascites, splenomegaly, and portosystemic shunts', 'Portal HTN: blood shunts through collaterals -> varices, hemorrhoids, caput medusae.'),
('60ceadda-4550-57f5-beca-d3b569852eef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Ascites in cirrhosis results from:', '["Hypoalbuminemia alone mediated physiological mechanism","Portal hypertension, hypoalbuminemia, increased aldosterone, and renal Na+ retention","Renal failure alone mediated physiological mechanism","Portal hypertension alone mediated physiological mechanism","Heart failure alone mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension, hypoalbuminemia, increased aldosterone, and renal Na+ retention', 'Also increased hepatic lymph formation and decreased Na+ excretion.'),
('cb5c28e1-77a4-5c35-897d-832288cf91a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Hepatic encephalopathy is attributed to:', '["Increased bilirubin","Decreased urea","Increased clotting factors","Hypoglycemia","Accumulation of ammonia and other neurotoxins"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulation of ammonia and other neurotoxins', 'Ammonia is detoxified via urea cycle; failure leads to cerebral dysfunction.'),
('8f737d7e-56b6-5abe-853f-1443c67f2189'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Lactulose is used in hepatic encephalopathy because it:', '["Stimulates liver function mediated physiological mechanism","Acidifies colon, traps NH4+, and reduces ammonia absorption","Increases urea production mediated physiological mechanism","Increases bile flow mediated physiological mechanism","Decreases ammonia production mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Acidifies colon, traps NH4+, and reduces ammonia absorption', 'Also acts as an osmotic laxative to clear nitrogenous wastes.'),
('4ee72517-a7db-5097-8d13-ab22935d5090'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver conjugates bilirubin with:', '["Glucuronic acid","Acetyl-CoA","Sulfate","Glutathione","Glycine"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronic acid', 'UGT1A1 catalyzes glucuronidation of bilirubin.'),
('53d3f777-c485-568d-8816-8bf16babe0de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver metabolizes alcohol via:', '["Catalase only","Aldehyde dehydrogenase only","Xanthine oxidase","MEOS only","Alcohol dehydrogenase (cytosol) and CYP2E1"]'::jsonb, 'Alcohol dehydrogenase (cytosol) and CYP2E1', 'MEOS (CYP2E1) is induced in chronic alcoholics.'),
('9c52dad5-de64-5752-9076-5ec9ca53a2cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver''s role in lipid metabolism includes:', '["Synthesis of VLDL, HDL, and cholesterol metabolism","Absorption of fats only mediated physiological mechanism","Excretion of lipids mediated physiological mechanism","Digestion of fats only mediated physiological mechanism","Storage of fat only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesis of VLDL, HDL, and cholesterol metabolism', 'Liver packages and exports lipids as lipoproteins.'),
('79ca6a5c-e4c2-5276-a3e5-e254690e4f2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Fatty liver (steatosis) can result from:', '["Increased bile flow","Low carbohydrate diet","Excess alcohol, obesity, diabetes, and toxins","High protein diet","Increased physical activity"]'::jsonb, 'Excess alcohol, obesity, diabetes, and toxins', 'NAFLD is the most common cause of elevated liver enzymes in Western countries.'),
('28a9ae3a-8453-54b9-a609-58437174733f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver detoxifies drugs via:', '["Biliary excretion only mediated physiological mechanism","Direct renal excretion mediated physiological mechanism","Phase I (oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis) and Phase II (conjugation) reactions","Lung excretion mediated physiological mechanism","Skin excretion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Phase I (oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis) and Phase II (conjugation) reactions', 'CYP450 enzymes catalyze Phase I; glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation for Phase II.'),
('b7f50ef3-887b-5564-bfce-64f398f52fb9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Cytochrome P450 (CYP) enzymes in liver:', '["Mediate Phase I drug metabolism","Transport bile","Synthesize proteins","Store vitamins","Conjugate drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Mediate Phase I drug metabolism', 'CYP3A4 metabolizes ~50% of all drugs.'),
('95dee111-5b93-5c4e-ae21-9d0af96c6bc3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver stores:', '["Only vitamins mediated physiological mechanism","Only glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Only glycogen mediated physiological mechanism","Only iron mediated physiological mechanism","Glycogen, iron, copper, and vitamins A, D, B12"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen, iron, copper, and vitamins A, D, B12', 'Liver is the main storage depot for many micronutrients.'),
('7f57266a-3761-550a-8c86-a2533c8a6b4b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Ferritin in the liver stores:', '["Bilirubin","Vitamin A","Glycogen","Iron","Copper"]'::jsonb, 'Iron', 'Ferritin sequesters iron; release is regulated by hepcidin.'),
('4e5b14ab-3d4a-5295-93ee-827ed4c4a6bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Hepcidin is produced by the liver and regulates:', '["Calcium metabolism","Copper metabolism","Bile flow","Iron homeostasis","Bilirubin conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Iron homeostasis', 'Hepcidin is the master regulator of systemic iron balance.'),
('80c54d33-9368-5fc4-b8dc-733e5ff07e82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Ceruloplasmin is synthesized by the liver and:', '["Transports calcium","Transports bilirubin","Transports iron","Transports copper","Transports zinc"]'::jsonb, 'Transports copper', 'Ceruloplasmin oxidizes Fe2+ to Fe3+ (ferroxidase) and carries copper.'),
('fd8d1454-bba6-5e0e-b512-4f8928677e8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Wilson disease involves:', '["Bile salt transport defect mediated physiological mechanism","Defective copper excretion causing hepatic and neurological copper accumulation","Bilirubin conjugation defect mediated physiological mechanism","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Iron overload mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Defective copper excretion causing hepatic and neurological copper accumulation', 'ATP7B mutation; Kayser-Fleischer rings in cornea.'),
('970855b0-8972-53ce-84d9-d42be2cd549a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency causes:', '["Emphysema (lung) and cirrhosis","Bile duct obstruction","Iron overload","Copper overload","Bilirubin conjugation defect"]'::jsonb, 'Emphysema (lung) and cirrhosis', 'Deficient protease inhibitor leads to unopposed elastase activity and liver accumulation.'),
('920ac02b-242e-5c3c-9d3a-5665d21cf954'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Cirrhosis is characterized by:', '["Bile duct proliferation only mediated physiological mechanism","Hepatocyte necrosis only mediated physiological mechanism","Inflammation only mediated physiological mechanism","Fatty change only mediated physiological mechanism","Fibrosis, nodular regeneration, and loss of liver architecture"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrosis, nodular regeneration, and loss of liver architecture', 'Cirrhosis is end-stage liver disease with irreversible fibrosis.'),
('97bf3e4f-84ce-5eba-b9c8-ec7e346d868f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'In cirrhosis, spider angiomas result from:', '["Hyperestrogenism due to decreased hepatic metabolism of estrogen","Portal hypertension mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoalbuminemia mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperaldosteronism mediated physiological mechanism","Vitamin K deficiency mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperestrogenism due to decreased hepatic metabolism of estrogen', 'Spider telangiectasias are characteristic of chronic liver disease.'),
('e95f02ce-eead-58f6-b258-e4ad7a389d96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Hepatorenal syndrome is:', '["Functional renal failure in cirrhosis due to splanchnic vasodilation and renal vasoconstriction","Glomerulonephritis mediated physiological mechanism","Structural kidney disease mediated physiological mechanism","Obstructive uropathy mediated physiological mechanism","Pyelonephritis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Functional renal failure in cirrhosis due to splanchnic vasodilation and renal vasoconstriction', 'Type 1: rapid; Type 2: slower; treatment: TIPS or liver transplant.'),
('c17f6f19-6c58-5fcf-90fb-c0ac1bb71f3c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Hepatopulmonary syndrome involves:', '["Pulmonary hypertension mediated physiological mechanism","COPD mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary fibrosis mediated physiological mechanism","Asthma mediated physiological mechanism","Intrapulmonary vascular dilatation causing hypoxemia in liver disease"]'::jsonb, 'Intrapulmonary vascular dilatation causing hypoxemia in liver disease', 'Orthodeoxia (oxygenation worsens on sitting up) is characteristic.'),
('2a63575a-af90-5cc5-8c34-f51f615eaa5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The liver synthesizes cholesterol and:', '["Stores cholesterol only mediated physiological mechanism","Absorbs cholesterol mediated physiological mechanism","Converts excess cholesterol to bile salts for excretion","Excretes cholesterol in urine mediated physiological mechanism","Digests cholesterol mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Converts excess cholesterol to bile salts for excretion', 'Bile salt synthesis is the major route of cholesterol elimination.'),
('8f86c25d-22da-518c-81c3-53f1979b0ce6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Vitamin D is hydroxylated in the liver at position:', '["None","1","25","All positions","24"]'::jsonb, '25', '25-hydroxylation in liver; 1-alpha hydroxylation in kidney.'),
('4a1029c3-fbb9-5c39-b6f1-5966576ce7d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Vitamin K is essential for hepatic synthesis of:', '["Complement proteins only mediated physiological mechanism","Albumin only mediated physiological mechanism","Clotting factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C, S","Cytokines only mediated physiological mechanism","Globulins only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Clotting factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C, S', 'Vitamin K is a cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylation of these factors.'),
('c6c30177-9868-50bb-a772-3f3358c444cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) positivity indicates:', '["Vaccination","Liver cirrhosis only","Past resolved infection","Hepatocellular carcinoma only","Active HBV infection"]'::jsonb, 'Active HBV infection', 'HBsAg is the first serologic marker to appear after HBV infection.'),
('741b1e45-7754-5444-857f-b861094003dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is strongly associated with:', '["Rotor syndrome","Chronic HBV, HCV, and alcoholic cirrhosis","Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Crigler-Najjar syndrome","Gilbert syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic HBV, HCV, and alcoholic cirrhosis', 'HCC is the most common primary liver malignancy; cirrhosis is a major risk factor.'),
('7d2063a5-9f81-5090-8499-7c9cd0f7efe9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Carbohydrate digestion begins in the:', '["Duodenum","Colon","Jejunum","Stomach","Mouth"]'::jsonb, 'Mouth', 'Salivary amylase (ptyalin) hydrolyzes starch; inactivated by gastric acid.'),
('a74aa300-179a-5617-bfb3-a2912f407aa9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Lactase deficiency causes:', '["Lactose intolerance","Constipation","Fat malabsorption","Protein malabsorption","Vitamin deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose intolerance', 'Undigested lactose causes osmotic diarrhea and bacterial fermentation producing gas.'),
('1f088918-5fd3-516e-b6c8-2745b864f346'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The brush border enzyme sucrase-isomaltase digests:', '["Lactose only","Maltose only","Cellulose","Starch only","Sucrose and isomaltose"]'::jsonb, 'Sucrose and isomaltose', 'Sucrase breaks sucrose to glucose + fructose; isomaltase breaks alpha-1,6 bonds.'),
('9541671b-74b7-52f6-8f9f-d324187eac69'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Maltase (glucoamylase) on the brush border:', '["Cleaves maltose and maltotriose to glucose","Digests starch mediated physiological mechanism","Digests lactose mediated physiological mechanism","Digests cellulose mediated physiological mechanism","Digests sucrose mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Cleaves maltose and maltotriose to glucose', 'Maltase removes terminal glucose from oligosaccharides.'),
('2b9aaa30-eeb6-598d-85f2-2d1e0518e8c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The final products of carbohydrate digestion are:', '["Glucose, galactose, and fructose","Starch and glycogen","Lactose and maltose","Dextrins only","Maltose and sucrose"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose, galactose, and fructose', 'Monosaccharides are the only absorbable form of carbohydrates.'),
('5af0e3e9-1a57-5700-b431-2f93b897c61e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Glucose and galactose are absorbed across enterocytes via:', '["GLUT5","GLUT2","Active transport of Na+ only","SGLT1","Simple diffusion"]'::jsonb, 'SGLT1', 'SGLT1 is secondary active transport coupled to Na+ gradient.'),
('4d68a4ad-8ef0-518c-a66d-31ef4a8e5f4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Fructose absorption occurs via:', '["SGLT1","GLUT5","Simple diffusion","Active transport","GLUT2"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT5', 'GLUT5 is a facilitated diffusion transporter specific for fructose.'),
('9bfeaf46-37f5-5aea-99b2-cbec9b8754cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Glucose exits enterocytes into blood via:', '["GLUT5","GLUT2","Na+/K+ ATPase","SGLT1","GLUT4"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT2', 'GLUT2 on the basolateral membrane transports glucose and galactose into portal blood.'),
('c3da2a84-ba0b-5136-a6e2-29347cc856d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Protein digestion begins in the:', '["Mouth","Colon","Stomach","Jejunum","Duodenum"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach', 'Pepsin cleaves proteins into peptides; optimal at pH 2-3.'),
('22a2697d-9a31-51cb-92aa-f6b753835724'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Trypsin and chymotrypsin are:', '["Endopeptidases","Exopeptidases","Carboxypeptidases","Aminopeptidases","Phosphatases"]'::jsonb, 'Endopeptidases', 'They produce oligopeptides of varying lengths.'),
('0279181e-de01-593a-8995-d11bd7d76238'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Carboxypeptidase A and B are:', '["Amylases","Endopeptidases","Lipases","Nucleases","Exopeptidases"]'::jsonb, 'Exopeptidases', 'Carboxypeptidase A prefers aromatic/aliphatic; B prefers basic amino acids.'),
('f24cddb2-56ba-53d9-b527-0d7ca26cedbd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Aminopeptidase is located on:', '["Enterocyte brush border","Salivary gland","Colonic mucosa","Gastric mucosa","Pancreatic zymogen"]'::jsonb, 'Enterocyte brush border', 'Aminopeptidase completes protein digestion to free amino acids.'),
('eb0fed8d-be36-5778-9c91-40bd47acfc8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Dipeptides and tripeptides are absorbed via:', '["SGLT1","GLUT5","PEPT1","GLUT2","Simple diffusion"]'::jsonb, 'PEPT1', 'PEPT1 transports small peptides into enterocytes; then hydrolyzed by cytoplasmic peptidases.'),
('60c00ca5-d425-5a0a-be20-d44815e59b2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Fat digestion begins in the:', '["Colon","Mouth","Duodenum","Stomach","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum', 'Gastric lipase initiates some digestion, but most occurs in duodenum.'),
('674a9643-356b-5417-b4db-b9dfe1e385d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pancreatic lipase digests triglycerides into:', '["Glycerol and three fatty acids","Fatty acids only","Monoglycerides only","2-monoglyceride and two free fatty acids","Fatty acids and bile salts"]'::jsonb, '2-monoglyceride and two free fatty acids', 'Lipase acts at the lipid-water interface with colipase.'),
('0a2990ea-5a97-5728-b0be-5e899bd71f52'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Colipase is secreted by the:', '["Gallbladder","Duodenum","Stomach","Pancreas","Liver"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreas', 'Colipase binds both lipase and lipid droplet, anchoring lipase.'),
('de5ce237-3a70-5f10-b631-62d57f03feb0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Micelles function to:', '["Excrete fats mediated physiological mechanism","Store fats mediated physiological mechanism","Digest fats mediated physiological mechanism","Emulsify fats mediated physiological mechanism","Transport lipid digestion products across the unstirred water layer to enterocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Transport lipid digestion products across the unstirred water layer to enterocytes', 'Micelles are water-soluble; they deliver lipids to brush border for absorption.'),
('a4dcfb02-3976-5bcc-b3af-b0ab2843c14b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Inside enterocytes, absorbed fatty acids and monoglycerides are:', '["Excreted back into lumen","Directly released into portal blood","Converted to bile salts","Stored as lipid droplets","Re-esterified to triglycerides in the smooth ER"]'::jsonb, 'Re-esterified to triglycerides in the smooth ER', 'Triglycerides are then packaged into chylomicrons in the Golgi.'),
('51d7ceaa-53b5-5ee5-a94e-ee26779066a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Chylomicrons consist of:', '["Only phospholipids mediated physiological mechanism","Triglycerides, cholesterol, phospholipids, apolipoprotein B-48","Only triglycerides mediated physiological mechanism","Only cholesterol mediated physiological mechanism","Only apolipoproteins mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Triglycerides, cholesterol, phospholipids, apolipoprotein B-48', 'ApoB-48 is essential for chylomicron assembly in enterocytes.'),
('60ec2a3a-1397-5f52-be35-7cfb35c3689b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Chylomicrons are transported via:', '["Mesenteric veins mediated physiological mechanism","Hepatic artery mediated physiological mechanism","Portal vein directly mediated physiological mechanism","Lacteals (lymphatic system) into thoracic duct","Bile duct mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Lacteals (lymphatic system) into thoracic duct', 'Chylomicrons are too large for blood capillaries; enter lymphatic lacteals.'),
('2034d423-118b-5657-ac41-c823dcfc0cd7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs) are:', '["Must be emulsified by bile","Absorbed via lacteals","Require bile salts for absorption","Cannot be absorbed","Directly absorbed into portal blood"]'::jsonb, 'Directly absorbed into portal blood', 'MCTs are water-soluble and enter portal vein directly.'),
('b389b37c-02c0-50ee-9e11-f8bef5266c64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The primary site of nutrient absorption is the:', '["Jejunum","Colon","Duodenum","Ileum","Stomach"]'::jsonb, 'Jejunum', 'Jejunum has long villi, high brush border enzyme activity, and abundant transporters.'),
('bf82f35b-3008-5564-8009-283c8f6afaa0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The duodenum is primarily responsible for:', '["Water absorption mediated physiological mechanism","Vitamin B12 absorption mediated physiological mechanism","Mixing chyme with digestive secretions and initial digestion","Bile storage mediated physiological mechanism","Nutrient absorption mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Mixing chyme with digestive secretions and initial digestion', 'Duodenum receives gastric chyme, bile, and pancreatic juice.'),
('6bc795c6-50a3-58d0-82d3-2c571865994d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The ileum is specialized for:', '["Water absorption","Lipid absorption","Carbohydrate absorption","Vitamin B12 and bile salt absorption","Protein absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 and bile salt absorption', 'Terminal ileum has receptors for intrinsic factor-B12 complex and bile salt transporters.'),
('8e34768c-540e-5e14-a0a9-ac6a317fd75f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'The colon primarily absorbs:', '["Glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Vitamins mediated physiological mechanism","Fatty acids mediated physiological mechanism","Amino acids mediated physiological mechanism","Water, electrolytes (Na+, Cl-), and short-chain fatty acids"]'::jsonb, 'Water, electrolytes (Na+, Cl-), and short-chain fatty acids', 'Colon absorbs ~1.5L of water per day; SCFAs from fiber fermentation.'),
('ca4632d8-d1dc-58ac-bfc7-b85aef8378be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Vitamin B12 requires for absorption:', '["Vitamin D mediated physiological mechanism","Pancreatic enzymes mediated physiological mechanism","Gastric acid only mediated physiological mechanism","Bile salts mediated physiological mechanism","Intrinsic factor (from parietal cells) and terminal ileum"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic factor (from parietal cells) and terminal ileum', 'Intrinsic factor-B12 complex is endocytosed in terminal ileum.'),
('77e0223f-730e-5502-baac-1ec41750525c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Iron is absorbed primarily in the:', '["Ileum","Rectum","Colon","Duodenum and proximal jejunum","Stomach"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum and proximal jejunum', 'Heme iron (via HCP1) and non-heme iron (via DMT1) are absorbed in duodenum.'),
('d023a212-0c53-575c-98ed-7b9ee20b33b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Hepcidin regulates iron absorption by:', '["Decreasing ferritin mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing iron absorption mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulating DMT1 mediated physiological mechanism","Degrading ferroportin, thus trapping iron in enterocytes and macrophages","Increasing transferrin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Degrading ferroportin, thus trapping iron in enterocytes and macrophages', 'High hepcidin reduces iron absorption; low hepcidin increases it.'),
('680733a0-5ba4-51ca-b844-9675ba1afd95'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Calcium absorption in the duodenum:', '["Requires bile salts","Requires intrinsic factor","Is enhanced by 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","Is independent of vitamin D","Occurs only in colon"]'::jsonb, 'Is enhanced by 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D', 'Calcitriol induces calbindin-D28k expression in enterocytes.'),
('02180905-8b7e-56e9-a7c1-10ac9066a737'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Vitamin D deficiency in adults causes:', '["Osteomalacia","Neuropathy","Dermatitis","Anemia","Rickets"]'::jsonb, 'Osteomalacia', 'Vitamin D is also a secosteroid hormone regulating calcium and bone metabolism.'),
('34e04810-03cc-5600-90f2-a59f88a3f1cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Folate (vitamin B9) is absorbed in the:', '["Stomach","Colon","Duodenum","Ileum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Jejunum', 'Folate absorption is via PCFT (proton-coupled folate transporter).'),
('7f3bfb07-f2d6-5b04-ad79-4f80100eb381'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Vitamin A (retinol) is absorbed:', '["Via micelles and chylomicrons","Via intrinsic factor","Via water diffusion","Via bile salts only","Directly into portal blood"]'::jsonb, 'Via micelles and chylomicrons', 'Vitamin A follows the lipid absorption pathway.'),
('ba902d1d-78b5-5e90-b6ab-95d505255875'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) functions primarily as:', '["A hormone precursor","A clotting factor","An antioxidant","A pro-oxidant","A coenzyme"]'::jsonb, 'An antioxidant', 'Vitamin E is a chain-breaking antioxidant in cell membranes.'),
('a7b2fb36-0199-51bb-958a-b164b443c967'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Vitamin K is necessary for:', '["Gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C, S","Collagen synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Nucleotide synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Antioxidant defense mediated physiological mechanism","Energy metabolism mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C, S', 'Vitamin K is a cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase.'),
('a9763dcb-d3a1-52c5-bc9c-060e63691879'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is absorbed via:', '["Simple diffusion","GLUT2","PEPT1","SVCT1","SGLT1"]'::jsonb, 'SVCT1', 'Vitamin C is a water-soluble vitamin; also acts as an antioxidant and collagen cofactor.'),
('1e166392-196f-595c-bcbf-013bb22ee5e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Thiamine (vitamin B1) is absorbed in the:', '["Colon","Ileum","Duodenum","Stomach","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Jejunum', 'Thiamine deficiency causes beriberi and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.'),
('b4aec15c-1db4-5762-bd01-29f84107c988'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Niacin (vitamin B3) is synthesized from:', '["Tryptophan","Methionine","Lysine","Leucine","Phenylalanine"]'::jsonb, 'Tryptophan', '60 mg tryptophan produces 1 mg niacin.'),
('42246a17-c066-5656-b799-2477459a2653'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT and Liver', 'Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is a cofactor for:', '["Glycolysis","Oxidative phosphorylation","Fatty acid oxidation","Gluconeogenesis","Aminotransferases and decarboxylases"]'::jsonb, 'Aminotransferases and decarboxylases', 'Vitamin B6 is involved in >100 enzymatic reactions.'),
